window.LESSONS=window.LESSONS||{};window.LESSONS.en={"01":"# Day 1 – Foundations of Horoscope Compatibility\n\n## Learn\n\n### Day 1 introduction\n**Module 1: Philosophy, History and Architecture of Milan (मिलान).** Day 1 of 14 · Foundation to Advanced · about 140 minutes (self-paced).\n\n> **Central theme: understand the philosophy before learning the formula.** Milan is not merely the mathematical matching of two Moon Nakshatras. It is a traditional framework for examining different dimensions of compatibility, which must ultimately be studied alongside the individual horoscopes.\n\nDay 1 builds the intellectual framework on which every later Koota is understood. Nothing is scored today. By the end you should be able to identify the Moon-related parameters of two charts and describe the architecture of traditional matching.\n\n### Instructor's welcome\nWelcome. I teach this course on four habits: understand before memorising; rules before predictions; mathematical clarity before interpretation; compare schools rather than accepting one blindly. Today you will meet each habit in small form. Please keep the Learning Journal open while you study; it is where good students record rules to revise and questions to ask.\n\n### Learning objectives\nBy the end of this module you should be able to:\n1. Explain the meaning and traditional purpose of Milan (मिलान).\n2. Describe the philosophical foundations of horoscope compatibility.\n3. Explain, with appropriate caution, the historical development of marriage matching in Jyotisha.\n4. Distinguish Nakshatra-based from Rashi-based matching.\n5. Explain why the Moon holds an important position in traditional compatibility assessment.\n6. Distinguish Ashtakoota Milan from Dashakoota Milan.\n7. Recognise regional differences in matching systems.\n8. Explain why accurate birth details matter.\n9. Separate a compatibility score from the individual marriage promise.\n10. State the limitations of numerical compatibility systems.\n11. Distinguish traditional astrological compatibility from practical interpersonal compatibility.\n12. Prepare the basic astrological information needed before any Koota calculation.\n\n### Suggested plan for 140 minutes (self-paced; adjust freely)\n| Minutes | Block | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| 20 | Philosophy and history | Meaning, purpose, development of Milan |\n| 25 | Architecture of compatibility | Nakshatra, Rashi and several dimensions |\n| 25 | Ashtakoota versus Dashakoota | Systems and regional differences |\n| 20 | The Moon and birth accuracy | Nakshatra, Pada, Rashi, ayanamsha |\n| 30 | Practical identification | The Workshop tab: two sample charts |\n| 20 | Assessment and discussion | Quiz, flashcards, written questions |\n\n### Your Day 1 progress\n{{status}}\n\n### How this module is organised\n- **Foundation Theory:** philosophy, history, architecture, Nakshatra, Rashi, the Moon.\n- **Detailed Rules:** birth accuracy and ayanamsha, the identification routine, score versus promise, limitations.\n- **Different Schools:** Ashtakoota versus Dashakoota, regional systems and the comparison reference.\n- **Examples, Practice, Workshop:** worked examples, exercises and the interactive sample-chart workshop.\n- **Diagrams and Explorer:** ten accurate figures and a Rashi-Nakshatra explorer.\n- **Instructor Notes:** teaching guidance. **Rule Register:** see the Rule Verification Register in the sidebar.\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### 1. The meaning and philosophy of Milan\n**Definition.** Milan (मिलन / मिलान) means union, meeting, matching. In Jyotisha (ज्योतिष), horoscope Milan is the comparison of selected birth-chart factors of two prospective partners to assess their compatibility before marriage. A **Koota** (कूट, factor or group) is one such factor; a **Guna** (गुण) is a point awarded under it.\n\n**Conceptual foundation.** The purpose is not to produce a number but to examine several symbolic dimensions of a relationship: temperament, mutual attraction, emotional disposition, mental harmony, the tradition's physical and biological symbolism, family and social compatibility, and long-term considerations. Different Kootas represent different dimensions inside the selected matching tradition.\n\n**Why it is practised.** Traditionally, marriage joined two families as well as two people and carried duties of lineage, household and society. Matching offered an additional structured framework for judging suitability. Because that framework examines several dimensions, it uses several parameters.\n\n**Key conceptual questions** (answer them in your journal): Why compare two charts at all? Why the Moon? Why Nakshatras? Why are some Kootas Rashi-based? Why do some traditions add classifications? Why can the same pair score differently under different traditions?\n\n> **Teaching principle.** A Koota score is an astrological classification under a particular rule system. It is not a scientific measurement of character, emotional intelligence, fertility, health or the ability to sustain a marriage.\n\n**Common misconceptions.** \"The score is a percentage of success.\" (No: it is a sum of classifications.) \"More Kootas mean more accuracy.\" (No: several overlap.) \"There is one correct table.\" (No: traditions differ.)\n\n### 2. Historical background (what can and cannot be said)\nMarriage-related matters appear in several strands of Jyotisha, and they should not be merged:\n| Strand | What it covers |\n|---|---|\n| Classical natal astrology | Marriage indications in an individual chart |\n| Muhurta | Choosing auspicious times, including for marriage |\n| Nakshatra-based compatibility | Methods comparing two Nakshatras |\n| Regional matching traditions | Local ten-factor and eight-factor practices |\n| Later matchmaking manuals | Printed manuals that standardised tables |\n| Contemporary software | Guna Milan as implemented by programs |\n\n**What our research could verify.** *Muhurta Chintamani* is a real Sanskrit work on muhurta by Rama Daivajna (Daivajñarāma). Library catalogues list manuscripts, and publishers list translated editions with commentaries; sources disagree on its date (some say 16th century, others 17th). *Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra* is a major classical text attributed to Parashara; its composition date is debated. Both are commonly cited in this field.\n\n**What our research could not verify.** Which of the ten Kootas, if any, a given verse or chapter of these works prescribes; the origin and dating of the 36-point Ashtakoota table; the origin of the Tamil ten-porutham list; and the contents of *Kalaprakashika* (named in the course brief, not verified here). **Therefore the course makes no claim that any Koota originates in a particular text.**\n\n> **Research integrity.** For every historical assertion the course records the source name, author (where known), chapter or verse (only if verified), translation or commentary, period (only where reliable) and whether the reference is direct or interpretative. At present no chapter or verse has been verified. Never attribute all ten Kootas to one classical authority without documentary evidence.\n\n### 3. The architecture of compatibility\n**Why several parameters?** A relationship has several dimensions, and one classification cannot represent them all. A favourable Moon Nakshatra relationship does not establish favourable planetary relationships; a favourable Rashi relationship does not establish favourable marriage indications in either chart. The Kootas therefore act as separate parameters, although some share the same underlying information.\n\n**The four layers used in this course** (see Figure 9):\n| Layer | Looks at | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| A – Nakshatra-based | The Moon's Nakshatra in each chart | Janma Nakshatra, lord, Pada, relative position |\n| B – Rashi-based | The Moon's Rashi in each chart | Janma Rashi, lord, sign distance, lord friendship |\n| C – Classification-based | Symbolic groupings of Nakshatras or Rashis | Gana, Yoni, Nadi, Vashya, Varna, Rajju, Mahendra, Stree Deergha (profile-dependent) |\n| D – Complete horoscope | Both charts, singly and together | Marriage promise, 7th house, Venus, Jupiter, Navamsha, dasha, mutual factors |\nLayer D must not be confused with the numerical Dashkoot score.\n\n**Three different comparisons.** Moon-to-Moon matching (relative Nakshatra and Rashi positions); Moon-lord matching (relationship of the two sign lords); complete horoscope matching (individual and mutual factors beyond the Moon).\n\n### 4. Nakshatra foundation (नक्षत्र)\n**Definition.** A Nakshatra is one of 27 divisions of the sidereal zodiac (निरयण राशिचक्र). 360° ÷ 27 = **13°20'** each; each has four Padas (पद / चरण): 13°20' ÷ 4 = **3°20'**.\n\n**Janma Nakshatra (जन्म नक्षत्र)** is the Nakshatra occupied by the Moon at birth. Many matching systems use it as the primary reference for the Nakshatra-based Kootas.\n\n**Data attached to each Nakshatra:** number; name; start and end longitude; lord; four Padas; the Rashi portions it covers; symbol and deity (according to tradition); and the classifications used in matching: Gana, Yoni, Nadi, Rajju and others. The Explorer tab shows all of these for any Pada.\n\n**Why the Moon's Nakshatra rather than the ascendant?** Tradition associates the Moon with the mind (मनस्), perception and experience, and the Nakshatra gives a finer position of the Moon than the sign does. This is a **philosophical convention of the tradition**; it should be kept distinct from empirically established psychology.\n\n**Nakshatra-based Kootas, introduced only (calculations from Day 4):**\n| Koota | General reference |\n|---|---|\n| Tara | Relative Nakshatra counting |\n| Yoni | Symbolic classification of the Nakshatra |\n| Gana | Temperament classification |\n| Nadi | Three-fold classification |\n| Mahendra | Counting, in traditions that use it |\n| Stree Deergha | Relative distance, in traditions that use it |\n| Rajju | Body-part grouping, in traditions that use it |\n\n### 5. Rashi foundation (राशि)\n**Janma Rashi (जन्म राशि)** is the Moon's sidereal sign at birth. There are twelve Rashis of 30° each:\n| # | Rashi | Sanskrit | Lord |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Aries | Mesha (मेष) | Mars |\n| 2 | Taurus | Vrishabha (वृषभ) | Venus |\n| 3 | Gemini | Mithuna (मिथुन) | Mercury |\n| 4 | Cancer | Karka (कर्क) | Moon |\n| 5 | Leo | Simha (सिंह) | Sun |\n| 6 | Virgo | Kanya (कन्या) | Mercury |\n| 7 | Libra | Tula (तुला) | Venus |\n| 8 | Scorpio | Vrishchika (वृश्चिक) | Mars |\n| 9 | Sagittarius | Dhanu (धनु) | Jupiter |\n| 10 | Capricorn | Makara (मकर) | Saturn |\n| 11 | Aquarius | Kumbha (कुम्भ) | Saturn |\n| 12 | Pisces | Meena (मीन) | Jupiter |\nRashi matching examines the sign relationship of the two Moons and, in some Kootas, the relationship of the two sign lords: Rashi distance, Rashi lord, friendship, Bhakoot, Vashya, and Varna where applicable.\n\n**Important distinction.** Nakshatra and Rashi are not independent positions. They are different divisions of the **same sidereal longitude** of the Moon. One longitude fixes both. Several Nakshatra-based and Rashi-based Kootas therefore contain overlapping information, which matters when you read an aggregate score (Figures 4, 5 and 6).\n\n### 6. The role of the Moon in Koota Milan (चन्द्र)\n**Traditional significance.** The Moon is associated with Manas (मनस्, mind), emotional experience, perception, mental disposition and day-to-day experience, and it fixes the Nakshatra-based classifications. That is why it is central to many matching systems.\n\n**Three levels of matching.**\n| Level | Examines |\n|---|---|\n| Moon-to-Moon | Relative Nakshatra and Rashi positions of the two Moons |\n| Moon-lord | The planetary relationship between the lords of the two Moon signs |\n| Complete horoscope | Individual and mutual factors beyond the Moon |\nMoon-based compatibility is one part of a wider analysis.\n\n**Discussion questions** (explored later): Can two Moons be favourably placed while the full charts show other difficulties? Can the Koota score be low while other chart factors are supportive? Should Moon-based matching override the 7th-house analysis? What is the role of the Navamsha (D-9)?\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### 7. Birth accuracy and ayanamsha (अयनांश)\n**Essential birth information:** date of birth; exact time; place; time zone; and any historical daylight-saving adjustment.\n\n**Why it matters.** The Moon moves continuously (about 12°–15° a day, roughly 0.55° an hour; one Pada in about six hours). Its position fixes Rashi, Nakshatra, Pada and Nakshatra lord. A time error matters most when the Moon is near a classification boundary. **Not every small error changes the Nakshatra or Pada**: the effect depends on the Moon's distance from the boundary.\n\n**Tropical and sidereal.** The tropical (सायन) zodiac is measured from the equinox point; the sidereal (निरयण) zodiac is fixed against the stars. **Sidereal longitude = tropical longitude − ayanamsha.** Lahiri (Chitrapaksha) is widely used; other recognised ayanamshas exist and give slightly different sidereal positions. A difference matters only in boundary-sensitive cases; do not claim that every ayanamsha difference changes a Koota result.\n\n**Software verification.** Check and record: the Moon longitude, the ayanamsha, the sidereal Rashi, the Nakshatra, the Pada and the birth time zone. For professional work always record the calculation settings.\n\n### 8. The identification routine (do this before any scoring)\nFrom the sidereal Moon longitude L, in arc-minutes M = L × 60:\n- Rashi number = floor(M ÷ 1800) + 1 · Nakshatra number = floor(M ÷ 800) + 1 · Pada = floor((M mod 800) ÷ 200) + 1.\n- Rashi lord from the Rashi table; Nakshatra lord from the Vimshottari order Ketu, Venus, Sun, Moon, Mars, Rahu, Jupiter, Saturn, Mercury (repeated three times).\n- The start of each division belongs to the new division; 360° = 0°.\nDay 2 treats the mathematics in full.\n\n### 9. Compatibility score versus marriage promise\n**What a Koota score represents.** The result of a defined classification and scoring procedure. It is **not** a measurement of love, commitment, emotional maturity, communication, respect, financial responsibility, relationship skill or guaranteed marital longevity.\n\n**What marriage promise (विवाह योग) is.** In traditional chart analysis it is the assessment of marriage-related indications in an individual's own chart: relevant houses, lords, planets, divisional charts and timing. It is separate from Koota Milan.\n\n**Four analytical situations** (Figure 10):\n| Situation | Koota score | Individual marriage indications |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | Favourable | Supportive |\n| B | Favourable | Challenging |\n| C | Unfavourable | Supportive |\n| D | Unfavourable | Challenging |\nWhy can these not be reduced to one number? Because the two axes measure different things from different material. **The course draws no deterministic conclusion from any of the four.**\n\n**The professional astrologer's responsibility.** Explain the calculation system used; identify specific favourable and unfavourable factors; explain school-specific exceptions; avoid fear-based statements and absolute guarantees; separate traditional belief from scientifically validated conclusions; encourage practical discussion between the partners.\n\n### 10. Limitations of numerical matching\n1. Different regional systems use different rules.\n2. Different software implements different rule profiles.\n3. A high score cannot establish a successful marriage.\n4. A low score cannot independently establish an unsuccessful marriage.\n5. Several Kootas share underlying information.\n6. Cancellation rules are not universally accepted.\n7. Birth-time inaccuracy can change a classification.\n8. Astrological interpretations are not scientifically established predictors of relationship outcomes.\n9. Interpersonal compatibility needs considerations beyond astrology.\n10. An astrologer must never replace personal consent and informed decision-making with a score.\n\n> **Conclusion.** The purpose of this course is to teach the traditional system accurately, not to encourage blind acceptance of numerical matching.\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### 11. Ashtakoota versus Dashakoota\n**Ashtakoota (अष्टकूट)** is the commonly used North Indian eight-factor system. The conventional maxima:\n| Koota | Max | Reference point |\n|---|---|---|\n| Varna | 1 | Moon sign |\n| Vashya | 2 | Moon sign |\n| Tara | 3 | Nakshatra counting |\n| Yoni | 4 | Nakshatra |\n| Graha Maitri | 5 | Lords of the two Moon signs |\n| Gana | 6 | Nakshatra |\n| Bhakoot | 7 | Moon-sign pair |\n| Nadi | 8 | Nakshatra |\n| **Total** | **36** | |\nThese are the conventional values of one widely used profile, **not universal values for every tradition**.\n\n**Dashakoota (दशकूट)** names a ten-factor framework, but the ten factors and the scoring depend on the tradition. Some South Indian and regional systems use factors such as Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Rajju and Vedha; the composition is not uniform. **Do not add two Kootas to Ashtakoota and call the result a universal Dashakoota.** This course freezes **Tradition A**: the eight Ashtakoota Kootas (36 points) plus Rajju and Vedha as pass/Dosha checks (Figure 8).\n\n**Why different systems exist.** Regional schools; different source traditions; different priorities in assessing marriage; different counting and classification; the evolution of matchmaking practice; and modern standardisation through software.\n\n**Comparative table (Tradition A against Ashtakoota).**\n| Koota | In Ashtakoota? | In Tradition A? | Reference | Scoring in Tradition A |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Varna | Yes | Yes | Moon sign | 1 or 0 |\n| Vashya | Yes | Yes | Moon sign | 0–2 |\n| Tara | Yes | Yes | Nakshatra | 3, 1.5 or 0 |\n| Yoni | Yes | Yes | Nakshatra | 0–4 |\n| Graha Maitri | Yes | Yes | Sign lords | 0–5 |\n| Gana | Yes | Yes | Nakshatra | 0–6 |\n| Bhakoot | Yes | Yes | Moon-sign pair | 7 or 0 |\n| Nadi | Yes | Yes | Nakshatra | 8 or 0 |\n| Rajju | No | Yes | Nakshatra | pass / Dosha |\n| Vedha | No | Yes | Nakshatra | pass / Dosha |\n\n### 12. Regional matching systems\nResearch and document, for each: traditional name, Kootas included, calculation reference, maximum score, special considerations, regional variation, and documentary references. Expand each tradition below. Where documentation is inadequate the entry says so and no rule is stated.\n{{comparison}}\n\n**Discussion questions.** (1) Why might one region value Rajju while another does not score it? (2) Why can the same pair score differently? (3) Can two different systems both be internally consistent? (4) Is it acceptable to combine the favourable rules of one system with the cancellation rules of another? (The course answer to (4): no, never.)\n\n> **Teaching conclusion.** First understand the complete logic of one chosen tradition; then compare it with another.\n\nUse the selector below to note which school you are studying.\n\n## Exceptions\nDay 1 contains no scoring rules, so there are no Dosha exceptions yet. Three principles apply from now on:\n- A Dosha **identified** is not a Dosha **cancelled**.\n- A cancellation must name its rule, school and conditions.\n- A good score in one Koota never cancels a problem in another automatically.\n\n### Common misconceptions to correct today\n| Misconception | Correction |\n|---|---|\n| \"Rashi and Nakshatra are two separate positions.\" | They are two divisions of the same longitude. |\n| \"Dashakoota = Ashtakoota + 2.\" | Ten-factor sets differ by tradition. |\n| \"A high score means a good marriage.\" | The score is not a promise. |\n| \"The ayanamsha never matters.\" | It matters near boundaries. |\n| \"Nadi or Yoni describe real biology.\" | They are symbolic classifications. |\n\n## Examples\n\n### Worked example A – Moon at 43°20' (illustrative, not a real birth)\nM = 43 × 60 + 20 = 2,600'. Rashi: 2600 ÷ 1800 = 1.44, floor 1, number 2 = **Taurus (Vrishabha)**, lord **Venus**. Nakshatra: 2600 ÷ 800 = 3.25, floor 3, number 4 = **Rohini**. Pada: 2600 mod 800 = 200; 200 ÷ 200 = 1, floor 1, **Pada 2**. Nakshatra lord: **Moon**. Note: 43°20' is exactly the start of Pada 2; one arc-second earlier it is Pada 1.\n\n### Worked example B – Moon at 100°30' (illustrative, not a real birth)\nM = 6,030'. Rashi: 6030 ÷ 1800 = 3.35, number 4 = **Cancer (Karka)**, lord **Moon**. Nakshatra: 6030 ÷ 800 = 7.54, number 8 = **Pushya**. Pada: 6030 mod 800 = 430; 430 ÷ 200 = 2.15, floor 2, **Pada 3**. Nakshatra lord: **Saturn**.\n\n### Correction recorded in the audit\nThe supplied illustrative value for Chart B, 113°10', actually lies in **Ashlesha Pada 2** (lord Mercury), not Pushya Pada 3. This module uses 100°30', which is Pushya Pada 3 as intended.\n\n### What can be said about the pair?\nOnly the input data: a Taurus Moon in Rohini Pada 2 and a Cancer Moon in Pushya Pada 3. Counting both ways (inclusively), the signs are 3 and 11 apart and the Nakshatras 5 and 24 apart. What those counts mean is the subject of Days 4 and 8. **No score is calculated today.**\n\n## Practice\n\n### Exercise 2 – Conceptual analysis\nWrite brief answers in your journal: (1) Why does traditional Milan use several Kootas? (2) Why is the Moon important? (3) Why are both Nakshatra and Rashi considered? (4) Why should individual marriage promise be examined separately? (5) Why can two schools produce different results?\n\n### Exercise 3 – Research assignment\nFind **two documented** traditional marriage-matching systems. Compare their Kootas: which factors are common, which differ, how the scoring differs, and what interpretative principles differ. Give a reference for every rule. If you cannot document a rule, mark it \"requires further source verification\".\n\n### Exercise 4 – Learning Journal prompt\nRecord in the Learning Journal: three new Sanskrit terms, one rule to revise, one different opinion you met, and one question for the instructor.\n\nThe interactive identification exercise (Exercise 1) is in the **Workshop** tab.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What does Milan (मिलान) mean?\nA: Matching or meeting. In Jyotisha, comparing two charts' factors to assess compatibility before marriage.\n\nQ: What is Janma Nakshatra?\nA: The Nakshatra occupied by the Moon at birth.\n\nQ: What is Janma Rashi?\nA: The sidereal sign occupied by the Moon at birth.\n\nQ: What is a Nakshatra lord?\nA: The planet ruling a Nakshatra, from the Vimshottari order Ketu, Venus, Sun, Moon, Mars, Rahu, Jupiter, Saturn, Mercury repeated three times.\n\nQ: What is a Rashi lord?\nA: The planet ruling a sign, for example Cancer is ruled by the Moon.\n\nQ: How wide is one Nakshatra?\nA: 13°20' (360° ÷ 27).\n\nQ: How wide is one Nakshatra Pada?\nA: 3°20' (13°20' ÷ 4); there are 108 Padas.\n\nQ: What is Ashtakoota?\nA: The conventional eight-factor, 36-point matching profile widely used in North India.\n\nQ: Is Dashakoota Ashtakoota plus two Kootas?\nA: No. Ten-factor systems differ by tradition, and each must be a separate rule profile.\n\nQ: What is ayanamsha?\nA: The offset between the tropical and sidereal zodiacs; sidereal = tropical − ayanamsha.\n\nQ: What does a Koota score represent?\nA: The result of a defined classification procedure, not a measurement of character, love or marital success.\n\nQ: What is marriage promise (विवाह योग)?\nA: What an individual chart indicates about marriage, studied separately from Koota matching.\n\nQ: Why is the Moon central in many matching systems?\nA: Tradition associates it with mind and experience, and it fixes the Nakshatra classifications. It is a convention, not proof.\n\nQ: Why do regional systems differ?\nA: Different schools, sources, priorities and counting conventions, plus later standardisation by manuals and software.\n\nQ: What are the limits of numerical matching?\nA: Rules differ by system, Kootas overlap, birth time can shift classes, and no score can replace the complete horoscope or personal decision.\n\nQ: Nakshatra and Rashi: how are they related?\nA: Both are divisions of the same sidereal longitude of the Moon.\n\nQ: What is the Navamsha (नवांश)?\nA: The ninth divisional chart (D-9); a supporting layer for marriage study.\n\nQ: What are the four layers of compatibility in this course?\nA: Nakshatra-based, Rashi-based, classification-based, and complete horoscope.\n\nQ: What does \"requires further source verification\" mean in this course?\nA: A rule is commonly stated but no primary source has been checked; it is not presented as verified.\n\nQ: Why record the calculation settings in a professional report?\nA: Ayanamsha, time zone and software can change boundary-sensitive results, so the report must be reproducible.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: What does Janma Nakshatra generally refer to?\nA) Ascendant Nakshatra\nB) Moon's Nakshatra at birth\nC) Sun's Nakshatra\nD) Seventh-house Nakshatra\nANS: B\nWHY: Janma Nakshatra is the Nakshatra occupied by the Moon at birth. The ascendant, Sun and 7th house are not its reference.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: What is the zodiacal span of one Nakshatra in the 27-Nakshatra system?\nA) 10°\nB) 12°\nC) 13°20'\nD) 15°\nANS: C\nWHY: 360° ÷ 27 = 13°20'. The other values do not divide the zodiac into 27 equal parts.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many Padas are assigned to each Nakshatra?\nA) Two\nB) Three\nC) Four\nD) Nine\nANS: C\nWHY: Each Nakshatra has four Padas of 3°20'. Nine Padas make one Rashi, not one Nakshatra.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: What is the maximum score of the commonly used Ashtakoota system?\nA) 28\nB) 32\nC) 36\nD) 40\nANS: C\nWHY: 1+2+3+4+5+6+7+8 = 36.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Does a favourable Koota score independently establish marriage success?\nA) Always\nB) No, it is one component of traditional matching\nC) Only when Nadi is favourable\nD) Only when Bhakoot is favourable\nANS: B\nWHY: A score is a classification result, not a promise; no single Koota changes that.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Nakshatra and Rashi are best described as:\nA) Two independent astronomical positions\nB) Two divisions of the same sidereal longitude\nC) Positions of different planets\nD) Tropical and sidereal versions of one another\nANS: B\nWHY: One longitude fixes both, so some Kootas overlap in information.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Dashakoota is best described as:\nA) Ashtakoota plus Varna and Rajju\nB) A ten-factor framework whose factors depend on the tradition\nC) The same everywhere in India\nD) A 40-point scale\nANS: B\nWHY: Different regional ten-factor systems use different sets.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which layer should NOT be confused with the numerical Dashkoot score?\nA) Layer A\nB) Layer B\nC) Layer C\nD) Layer D (complete horoscope compatibility)\nANS: D\nWHY: Layer D studies both full charts, which the Moon-based score cannot replace.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Sidereal longitude equals:\nA) Tropical + ayanamsha\nB) Tropical − ayanamsha\nC) Tropical × ayanamsha\nD) Ayanamsha − tropical\nANS: B\nWHY: The sidereal zodiac lags behind the tropical by the ayanamsha.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A Moon at 100°30' lies in which Nakshatra and Pada?\nA) Punarvasu Pada 4\nB) Pushya Pada 3\nC) Ashlesha Pada 2\nD) Pushya Pada 1\nANS: B\nWHY: Pushya spans 93°20'–106°40'; (100.5 − 93.333) ÷ 3.333 = 2.15, so Pada 3.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A Moon at 113°10' lies in which Nakshatra?\nA) Pushya\nB) Ashlesha\nC) Magha\nD) Punarvasu\nANS: B\nWHY: Ashlesha spans 106°40'–120°. This is the corrected value of the supplied sample.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which statement about birth-time error is correct?\nA) Any error changes the Nakshatra\nB) An error matters most when the Moon is near a classification boundary\nC) Birth time is irrelevant to the Moon\nD) Only the Sun is affected\nANS: B\nWHY: The Moon moves about 0.55° an hour, so only boundary-adjacent cases flip.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: What should a research reference record?\nA) Only the title\nB) Source, author, verified chapter or verse, commentary, period, and whether the reference is direct or interpretative\nC) Only the verse\nD) Nothing\nANS: B\nWHY: The course's research integrity rule requires all of these, and never an invented verse.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Muhurta Chintamani, according to the research for this module, is:\nA) Proven to prescribe all ten Kootas\nB) A real Sanskrit muhurta work whose Koota content was not verified here\nC) A modern software program\nD) A text on gemstones\nANS: B\nWHY: Authorship and existence are documented; what it says on each Koota was not verified.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The Moon moves roughly how fast?\nA) 0.05° an hour\nB) 0.55° an hour\nC) 5.5° an hour\nD) 55° an hour\nANS: B\nWHY: About 13° a day; a Pada (3°20') takes about six hours.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: True or False: Nakshatra and Rashi positions of the Moon are independent of each other.\nA) True\nB) False\nANS: B\nWHY: Both come from the same longitude.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: True or False: Dashakoota is always Ashtakoota plus two extra Kootas.\nA) True\nB) False\nANS: B\nWHY: Ten-factor sets differ between traditions.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: True or False: A low Koota score alone proves an unsuccessful marriage.\nA) True\nB) False\nANS: B\nWHY: A low score cannot independently establish failure.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: True or False: The Moon's Nakshatra is the primary reference for several Kootas.\nA) True\nB) False\nANS: A\nWHY: Tara, Yoni, Gana and Nadi, among others, use it.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: True or False: Software always follows one universal rule profile.\nA) True\nB) False\nANS: B\nWHY: Different programs implement different profiles.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: State two differences between Nakshatra-based and Rashi-based matching.\nMA: Nakshatra-based matching compares the Moons' 13°20' Nakshatra positions (and classes such as Gana, Yoni, Nadi); Rashi-based matching compares the 30° signs and sign lords (Bhakoot, Graha Maitri, Vashya, Varna). Both come from the same longitude, so they overlap in information.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Why should marriage promise be examined separately from the Koota score?\nMA: The Koota score uses only the Moon and a rule system; marriage promise uses each full chart (7th house, lord, Venus, Jupiter, Navamsha, dashas). They measure different things, so one cannot replace the other.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Name three pieces of birth information needed before any calculation.\nMA: Date, exact time, and place with time zone (and any daylight-saving adjustment); the calculation settings such as ayanamsha must also be recorded.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Give one reason two systems can score the same pair differently.\nMA: They use different rule tables, factor sets, counting conventions or scoring scales; for example one treats Janma Tara as inauspicious and another does not.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Explain what \"requires further source verification\" tells a reader.\nMA: The rule is commonly stated, but the course has not checked a primary source; it is shown with its grade and not presented as verified.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual analysis 1. Explain why aggregate scores can mislead, using the idea of overlapping Kootas.\nMA: Several Kootas draw on the same Moon sign or Nakshatra, so they are not independent tests. Adding them counts some information more than once, and the total hides which individual Kootas are favourable or not. Read each Koota first, then the total, and then the full charts.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual analysis 2. Using the four situations A–D, explain why no single numerical decision rule is justified.\nMA: The Koota score and the individual marriage indications come from different material (Moon-based classification versus full-chart analysis). Combinations such as favourable score with challenging indications cannot be settled by arithmetic; they require reading the factors, the school's rules, timing and the people's circumstances.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Practical identification. For a Moon at 251°30' give the Rashi (and lord), the Nakshatra, the Pada and the Nakshatra lord.\nMA: M = 15,090'. Rashi: 15090 ÷ 1800 = 8.38, number 9 = Sagittarius (lord Jupiter). Nakshatra: 15090 ÷ 800 = 18.86, number 19 = Mula. Pada: 15090 mod 800 = 690; 690 ÷ 200 = 3.45, so Pada 4. Nakshatra lord: Ketu.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\n## References\n### Source status (Day 1)\nNo chapter or verse has been verified for any statement in this module.\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Milan, Koota and Guna as terms | B/C | Established vocabulary |\n| Ashtakoota 1-2-3-4-5-6-7-8 = 36 | C/D | Common convention |\n| Dashakoota systems differ | C | Regional variation documented in manuals; not verified here |\n| Moon as the basis of classification | B | Convention of the Koota scheme |\n| Moon speed and Pada duration | n/a | Astronomical arithmetic |\n| Muhurta Chintamani exists; author Rama Daivajna; date uncertain | Research (catalogues) | Koota content not verified |\n| Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra attributed to Parashara; dating debated | Established, dating unverified | Koota content not verified |\n\n### Research log for this module\n- Search: \"Muhurta Chintamani author date marriage chapter\" found: manuscripts in the University of Pennsylvania library catalogue (Ms. Coll. 390 items) and publisher listings naming Rama Daivajna (Daivajñarāma) as author; sources conflict on the date (16th or 17th century). It found nothing on Rajju or Vedha in the text.\n- Search: \"history of Ashtakoota guna milan Dasa Porutham\" returned no relevant scholarship.\n- Conclusion: the historical development of the Koota tables remains **unverified** and is marked grade E where claimed anywhere.\n\n### Further reading (to be completed with verified entries)\nA translated edition of *Muhurta Chintamani* with commentary (editions exist); regional matching manuals; peer-reviewed work on the history of Indian marriage customs and astrology. Each must be read directly before any rule is attributed to it.\n\n## Diagrams\n{{diagrams}}\n\n## Explorer\nChoose a Nakshatra and Pada, or browse by Rashi, or enter a longitude. The explorer uses exact arc-minute boundaries and handles 360° = 0°.\n{{explorer}}\n\n## Workshop\n### Exercise 1 – Identification of basic horoscope parameters\nFor each sample chart identify: Moon longitude, sidereal Rashi and lord, Nakshatra, Pada, Nakshatra lord. In your own notes also record the ayanamsha and the software you would use. Illustrative values only.\n{{workshop}}\n\n## Instructor Notes\n*For teachers and self-study students who want the teaching view.*\n\n### Suggested lecture sequence (140 minutes)\n1. (20) Philosophy and history of Milan. Start from the question \"why compare two charts?\".\n2. (25) Architecture: draw the four layers (Figure 9); show that Nakshatra and Rashi come from one longitude (Figures 4–6).\n3. (25) Ashtakoota versus Dashakoota: the comparison table; stress \"not universal\".\n4. (20) The Moon and birth accuracy: the Moon's speed; ayanamsha; boundary cases.\n5. (30) Practical identification: students do the Workshop alone, then compare.\n6. (20) Assessment and discussion.\n\n### Questions to ask students\n- \"If both Moons are in the same sign, what does that tell you? What does it not tell you?\"\n- \"Two programs give different totals for the same pair. List three possible reasons.\"\n- \"A friend says the score is 32, so the marriage is safe. How do you respond?\"\n\n### Misconceptions to correct\nRashi and Nakshatra as independent; Dashakoota as Ashtakoota plus two; score as success probability; ayanamsha as irrelevant; Yoni and Nadi as biology.\n\n### Board work\n1. 360° → 12 × 30° and 27 × 13°20'; 108 × 3°20'. 2. Convert 100°30' to 6,030' and divide by 1800, 800, 200. 3. Draw the 2 × 2 grid of Situations A–D with a \"no single number\" circle.\n\n### Practical demonstration\nMove the slider in Figures 4 and 5 across a Rashi boundary and a Nakshatra boundary; show a Nakshatra that straddles two signs (Figure 6); enter 360° in the Explorer.\n\n### Discussion topics\nThe Moon as a convention; responsible language for clients; what evidence would be needed to test any matching rule.\n\n### Homework\nJournal entries; Exercises 2 and 3; identification for two people you know (anonymised). Revise the flashcards marked \"needs revision\".\n\n### Rule Verification Register\nEvery rule in the course follows the permanent Rule Verification Register (sidebar): traditional rule, source, school, alternative opinion, ASTROSUTRAS implementation. The Lab and the lessons use the same register entries.\n\n### Audit log for Day 1\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Zodiac division 30°, 13°20', 3°20' | Verified arithmetic; 9 Padas per Rashi |\n| Nakshatra boundaries and lords | Verified against the exact division and Vimshottari order |\n| Rashi lordship | Standard seven-planet lordship |\n| Sample classifications | Chart A correct; Chart B as supplied (113°10') was wrong, corrected to 100°30' |\n| Ashtakoota table | Matches the conventional profile; flagged \"not universal\" |\n| Historical statements | Only existence and authorship verified; rest marked unverified |\n| Textual attributions | None invented |\n","02":"# Day 2 – Mathematical and Astronomical Foundations\n\n## Learn\n\n### Welcome to Day 2\n**Module 2: Nakshatra, Rashi and Calculation Architecture – from astronomical longitude to astrological classification.** Day 2 of 14 · Foundation to Advanced · 150–180 minutes (self-paced) · Core emphasis: mathematical calculation · Practical: five horoscope exercises.\n\n> **Central teaching principle.** Before interpreting a Nakshatra, understand how it is calculated. Before interpreting a Pada, understand its exact longitude. Before calculating compatibility, verify the astronomical position from which the entire classification originates.\n\nThis day is the mathematical backbone of the whole course. Every Koota you will learn from Day 3 onwards starts from the numbers you calculate today. If you master this day, the rest of the course becomes much easier; if you skip it, every later result rests on sand.\n\n### The one idea to keep in mind all day\n**Two different stages.**\n1. **Stage 1 – the astronomical position of the Moon.** This depends on the ephemeris, the exact time, the place's time zone and the calculation settings (ayanamsha, geocentric position).\n2. **Stage 2 – the astrological classification.** This is pure arithmetic on a single number: the sidereal longitude. It divides that number into Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada.\nSoftware can fail in Stage 1 (wrong time zone, wrong ayanamsha) while Stage 2 is perfectly correct. Learn to ask: \"Which stage went wrong?\"\n\n### What you will be able to do by the end of the day\n1. Explain the difference between the tropical and the sidereal zodiac.\n2. Explain the astronomical and mathematical basis of the sidereal zodiac.\n3. Describe the division into twelve Rashis.\n4. Describe the division into twenty-seven Nakshatras.\n5. Explain how Nakshatras and Padas relate mathematically.\n6. Calculate the Nakshatra number from a sidereal longitude.\n7. Calculate the Nakshatra Pada.\n8. Identify the Rashi and the Rashi lord.\n9. Identify the Nakshatra and the Nakshatra lord.\n10. Explain the role of the ayanamsha.\n11. Convert tropical longitude into sidereal longitude.\n12. Handle longitudes close to Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada boundaries.\n13. Explain rounding errors and precision.\n14. Explain how birth-time uncertainty affects the Moon's classification.\n15. Verify planetary positions in astrology software.\n16. Independently classify the Moon for sample horoscopes.\n\n### How to study this day (a learner's route map)\n| Step | What to do | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Read \"Foundation Theory\" slowly; do each \"Check yourself\" box | Foundation Theory |\n| 2 | Learn the Rashi and Nakshatra tables; then the formulas | Detailed Rules, Formula Library |\n| 3 | Watch the diagrams move with the slider | Diagrams |\n| 4 | Try the Calculator with your own numbers | Calculator |\n| 5 | Break the rules on purpose: boundaries and rounding | Boundary Lab |\n| 6 | See how time and ayanamsha shift the Moon | Time & Ayanamsha Lab |\n| 7 | Solve the five exercises by hand, then check | Workshop |\n| 8 | Study the 15 common errors | Common Errors |\n| 9 | Take the assessment and flashcards | Assessment, Flashcards |\n\n### Suggested pace (self-paced; adjust freely)\n| Block | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Revision of Day 1 | 15 |\n| Theory (astronomy, zodiacs, ayanamsha) | 35 |\n| Rashi, Nakshatra, Pada mathematics | 40 |\n| Boundaries, precision, time | 30 |\n| Five practical exercises | 35 |\n| Assessment and flashcards | 15 |\n\n### Your Day 2 progress\n{{status2}}\n\n### Day 2 completion requirement\nTo complete Day 2 you must **solve at least four of the five practical horoscope calculations correctly and pass the boundary-condition test** (both are in the Workshop tab). Day 2 is the first formal mathematical checkpoint of the course.\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### 1. The ecliptic (क्रांतिवृत्त)\n**In plain words.** Look at the Sun from Earth over a year: it appears to travel through the sky along a great circle against the background stars. That apparent annual path is the **ecliptic**. It exists because the Earth orbits the Sun in a plane (the orbital plane); the Sun's apparent motion is the Earth's real motion seen backwards.\n\n**What you need from it.** Positions along the ecliptic are measured as **ecliptic longitude**, an angle from a chosen starting direction, going round the full circle: **360 degrees**. Traditional astrology uses this angle as its basis. When we say \"the Moon is at 43°20' sidereal\", we mean 43°20' of ecliptic longitude measured from a particular zero point.\n\n**Important distinction.** A zodiac sign is a **mathematical division of the ecliptic** (equal arcs of 30°). It must not be confused with the physical constellations, which are unequal in size and overlap the signs unevenly.\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) What is the ecliptic? (b) What does \"longitude\" mean here? (c) Why are the 30° signs not the same as constellations?\n\n### 2. The 360-degree circle, minutes and seconds\n| Unit | Relation |\n|---|---|\n| 1 degree (°) | 60 arc-minutes (') |\n| 1 arc-minute | 60 arc-seconds (\") |\n| 1 degree | 3,600 arc-seconds |\n| Full circle | 360° = 21,600' = 1,296,000\" |\n**Why this matters.** The Moon moves about half a degree per hour, and the Nakshatra and Pada boundaries are only 3°20' apart. Near a boundary, a difference of a few arc-seconds can move the Moon into a different division. That is why we calculate in arc-minutes or finer units, never in rounded degrees.\n\n### 3. Tropical and sidereal zodiacs\n**Tropical zodiac (सायन राशिचक्र).** Its zero point is the **vernal (March) equinox**: where the Sun crosses the celestial equator going north. It is tied to the seasons. Because the equinox point slowly shifts against the stars (the **precession of the equinoxes**), the tropical signs slowly drift against the star background.\n\n**Sidereal zodiac (निरयन राशिचक्र).** Its zero point is fixed against the stars by a convention. Traditional Indian (Nirayana) astrology uses it, and **all Koota classification in this course uses sidereal longitude**.\n\n| Parameter | Tropical | Sidereal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reference | Vernal equinox | Adopted stellar reference |\n| Ayanamsha | Not subtracted | Applied |\n| Zodiac basis | Equinoctial (seasonal) | Stellar |\n| Typical use | Western tropical astrology | Traditional Nirayana Jyotisha |\nThe two are **different reference conventions for measuring the same planets**. They are not two sets of planets.\n\n### 4. The ayanamsha (अयनांश)\n**Definition.** The ayanamsha is the angular difference between the tropical and the sidereal zero points under a chosen convention. It grows slowly with time because of precession.\n\n**The conversion:**\n> **Lsid = MOD(Ltrop − A, 360)**\nwhere Lsid is sidereal longitude, Ltrop is tropical longitude, A is the ayanamsha, and MOD brings the result into the range 0° (included) up to 360° (excluded). If the subtraction is negative, add 360°; if it reaches 360° or more, subtract 360°.\n\n**Why the exact value varies.** It depends on the date and on which convention you adopt. This course does not give a fixed ayanamsha for every date; real values must come from an ephemeris or the ayanamsha routine of your software. Examples in this module that use an ayanamsha value are labelled **illustrative teaching values**, not ephemeris figures.\n\n**Check yourself.** If Ltrop = 14°00' and A = 24°00', what is Lsid? (Answer: 14° − 24° = −10°, so add 360°: 350°.)\n\n### 5. Different ayanamsha systems (an introduction)\n| System | What to know (cautious summary) |\n|---|---|\n| Lahiri (Chitrapaksha) | Widely used in India; commonly described as anchored to the star Chitra (Spica) near the middle of the Chitra Nakshatra |\n| Raman | A different zero-point convention associated with B. V. Raman |\n| Krishnamurti (KP) | Close to, but not identical with, Lahiri; used in the KP system |\n| Fagan-Bradley | Associated with Western sidereal astrology |\n| Yukteshwar | Based on Sri Yukteshwar's precession model |\n| Others | Supported by various programs |\nEach system defines its zero point differently, so each gives a slightly different sidereal longitude for the same instant. **The practical effect is felt only when the Moon is near a boundary.** In the Time & Ayanamsha Lab you can see the differences for any date, using this course's implementation (approximate).\n\n> **Source status.** The descriptions above are short, commonly stated summaries (grade C). They are not a substitute for each system's own published definition, and no numerical value is given here for any date.\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### 6. Division of the zodiac into twelve Rashis\n**Mathematical foundation.** 360° ÷ 12 = **30°** per Rashi.\n\n| No. | Rashi | Sanskrit | Starts | Ends | Lord |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Aries | Mesha (मेष) | 0° | 30° | Mars |\n| 2 | Taurus | Vrishabha (वृषभ) | 30° | 60° | Venus |\n| 3 | Gemini | Mithuna (मिथुन) | 60° | 90° | Mercury |\n| 4 | Cancer | Karka (कर्क) | 90° | 120° | Moon |\n| 5 | Leo | Simha (सिंह) | 120° | 150° | Sun |\n| 6 | Virgo | Kanya (कन्या) | 150° | 180° | Mercury |\n| 7 | Libra | Tula (तुला) | 180° | 210° | Venus |\n| 8 | Scorpio | Vrishchika (वृश्चिक) | 210° | 240° | Mars |\n| 9 | Sagittarius | Dhanu (धनु) | 240° | 270° | Jupiter |\n| 10 | Capricorn | Makara (मकर) | 270° | 300° | Saturn |\n| 11 | Aquarius | Kumbha (कुम्भ) | 300° | 330° | Saturn |\n| 12 | Pisces | Meena (मीन) | 330° | 360° | Jupiter |\nThe starting longitude in this table is 0° of **sidereal** Aries, not tropical Aries.\n\n**Formula.** Rashi index = FLOOR(L ÷ 30); Rashi number = FLOOR(L ÷ 30) + 1; degree within the Rashi = L − 30 × FLOOR(L ÷ 30).\n\n**Step by step (example).** Sidereal L = 113°10' = 113.1667°. 113.1667 ÷ 30 = 3.77, so FLOOR = 3 and the Rashi number is 4 = **Karka (Cancer)**. Degree within the sign: 113°10' − 90° = **23°10'**. Lord: **Moon**.\n\n**Boundary rules (half-open intervals).** Each division is [start, end): the start is included, the end is not.\n- 30°00' exactly belongs to **Taurus**, not Aries. 60°00' exactly belongs to **Gemini**.\n- 360°00' normalises to 0°00' = **Aries**; it is not a thirteenth sign.\n\n### 7. Division into twenty-seven Nakshatras\n**Mathematical foundation.** 360° ÷ 27 = 13.3333…° = **13°20'** (13 + 20/60). And 27 × 13°20' = 360°.\n\n| No. | Nakshatra | Starts | Ends | Lord |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini | 0°00' | 13°20' | Ketu |\n| 2 | Bharani | 13°20' | 26°40' | Venus |\n| 3 | Krittika | 26°40' | 40°00' | Sun |\n| 4 | Rohini | 40°00' | 53°20' | Moon |\n| 5 | Mrigashira | 53°20' | 66°40' | Mars |\n| 6 | Ardra | 66°40' | 80°00' | Rahu |\n| 7 | Punarvasu | 80°00' | 93°20' | Jupiter |\n| 8 | Pushya | 93°20' | 106°40' | Saturn |\n| 9 | Ashlesha | 106°40' | 120°00' | Mercury |\n| 10 | Magha | 120°00' | 133°20' | Ketu |\n| 11 | Purva Phalguni | 133°20' | 146°40' | Venus |\n| 12 | Uttara Phalguni | 146°40' | 160°00' | Sun |\n| 13 | Hasta | 160°00' | 173°20' | Moon |\n| 14 | Chitra | 173°20' | 186°40' | Mars |\n| 15 | Swati | 186°40' | 200°00' | Rahu |\n| 16 | Vishakha | 200°00' | 213°20' | Jupiter |\n| 17 | Anuradha | 213°20' | 226°40' | Saturn |\n| 18 | Jyeshtha | 226°40' | 240°00' | Mercury |\n| 19 | Mula | 240°00' | 253°20' | Ketu |\n| 20 | Purva Ashadha | 253°20' | 266°40' | Venus |\n| 21 | Uttara Ashadha | 266°40' | 280°00' | Sun |\n| 22 | Shravana | 280°00' | 293°20' | Moon |\n| 23 | Dhanishta | 293°20' | 306°40' | Mars |\n| 24 | Shatabhisha | 306°40' | 320°00' | Rahu |\n| 25 | Purva Bhadrapada | 320°00' | 333°20' | Jupiter |\n| 26 | Uttara Bhadrapada | 333°20' | 346°40' | Saturn |\n| 27 | Revati | 346°40' | 360°00' | Mercury |\nThe table uses the conventional 27-Nakshatra division starting at 0° of sidereal Aries.\n\n**The lordship cycle.** Ketu → Venus → Sun → Moon → Mars → Rahu → Jupiter → Saturn → Mercury, repeated three times across the 27 Nakshatras. It is the same sequence as the Vimshottari Dasha lords. This is a traditional lordship system; it does not mean every Nakshatra property comes from dasha lordship alone.\n\n**Formula.** In arc-minutes L: Nakshatra number N = FLOOR(L ÷ 800) + 1 (because 13°20' = 800'). Position within the Nakshatra = L MOD 800. Lord index = (N − 1) MOD 9, mapped to the cycle above.\n\n### 8. The Nakshatra Pada (चरण)\n**Foundation.** Each Nakshatra has four equal Padas: 13°20' ÷ 4 = **3°20'** = 200 arc-minutes. Total Padas: 27 × 4 = **108**.\n\n**Formula.** Pada number P = FLOOR((L MOD 800) ÷ 200) + 1. Use the **unrounded** longitude.\n\n| Pada | Position inside the Nakshatra |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | 0°00' up to (not including) 3°20' |\n| 2 | 3°20' up to 6°40' |\n| 3 | 6°40' up to 10°00' |\n| 4 | 10°00' up to 13°20' |\n\n**Step by step (43°20').** M = 43 × 60 + 20 = 2,600'. N = FLOOR(2600 ÷ 800) + 1 = 3 + 1 = **4 (Rohini)**. Position inside Rohini: 2600 − 3 × 800 = 200'. Pada = FLOOR(200 ÷ 200) + 1 = **2**. (Note: exactly at the start of Pada 2.)\n\n### 9. Pada and Navamsha (नवांश)\nEach Pada spans 3°20', and so does one Navamsha division. A Rashi contains **nine** Navamsha divisions (30° ÷ 9 = 3°20'), and the zodiac contains 108.\n\n**But they are not the same thing.** They have the same angular width but belong to different classification frameworks: the Pada belongs to the Nakshatra system; the Navamsha sign belongs to the divisional chart D-9. Never treat them as interchangeable.\n\n**Finding the Navamsha sign.** Traditional starting rules: for **movable** signs (Aries, Cancer, Libra, Capricorn) the Navamsha count starts from the same sign; for **fixed** signs (Taurus, Leo, Scorpio, Aquarius) it starts from the ninth sign from itself; for **dual** signs (Gemini, Virgo, Sagittarius, Pisces) it starts from the fifth sign from itself. All three rules give the same result as one short formula: **Navamsha sign index = FLOOR(L ÷ 3°20') MOD 12** (index 0 = Aries). The course engine uses this and the Workshop lets you confirm it by the traditional rules.\n\n**Example.** L = 43°20' (Taurus, a fixed sign). Traditional rule: start at the 9th from Taurus = Capricorn; the position 13°20' into Taurus is 13°20' ÷ 3°20' = 4 divisions, so count 4 signs on from Capricorn: Aquarius, Pisces, Aries, Taurus: **Taurus**. Formula: FLOOR(43.3333 ÷ 3.3333) = 13; 13 MOD 12 = 1 = Taurus ✓.\n\n### 10. Nakshatras that cross a Rashi boundary\nBecause 13°20' does not divide 30° evenly, nine Nakshatras cross a sign boundary: Krittika (Mesha/Vrishabha), Mrigashira (Vrishabha/Mithuna), Punarvasu (Mithuna/Karka), Uttara Phalguni (Simha/Kanya), Chitra (Kanya/Tula), Vishakha (Tula/Vrishchika), Uttara Ashadha (Dhanu/Makara), Dhanishta (Makara/Kumbha), Purva Bhadrapada (Kumbha/Meena). Since 9 Padas fit exactly in one Rashi, **a Pada never crosses a Rashi boundary**, but a Nakshatra can.\n\n**Why this matters in Koota Milan.** A Moon in Krittika may have the Moon sign Aries or Taurus; the Pada decides. A Rashi-based Koota and a Nakshatra-based Koota can therefore both be affected by the same small shift in longitude. The table below is computed live.\n{{nakrashi}}\n\n### 11. From the Moon's position to its classification\n**Why the Moon's longitude is needed.** It determines the Janma Rashi, Janma Nakshatra, Pada, Nakshatra lord, Rashi lord and the other Nakshatra-based classifications used later.\n\n**Birth data required.** Date; exact local time; place; geographic latitude and longitude (for the time zone and, if needed, topocentric work); the **historical** time-zone offset; daylight-saving information where it applied.\n\n**Time conversion.** UTC = local time − the offset that applied at that place and date. Use a date-specific time-zone source; **do not assume today's offset applied on a historical birth date**, and handle daylight saving where it was in force.\n\n**Ephemeris stage.** A planetary-position engine calculates the Moon's **geocentric ecliptic longitude** at the instant. The vocabulary: ephemeris; Julian Day; time scales; geocentric versus topocentric; apparent versus geometric; ecliptic versus equatorial coordinates. For standard traditional classification use the geocentric Moon longitude, unless the chosen calculation profile says otherwise. The topocentric position (as seen from the surface) can differ by up to about one degree because of parallax, which is enough to change a Pada.\n\n**Sidereal conversion.** Sidereal Moon longitude = MOD(tropical Moon longitude − ayanamsha, 360°). Every later classification uses this one number, and the report must record the ayanamsha and the calculation profile.\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### 12. Differences between ayanamsha systems\nDifferent ayanamshas place the sidereal zero point differently, so the same Moon gets slightly different sidereal longitudes. **For the same instant, the difference between two ayanamshas is a fixed angle; it only changes the classification if a boundary lies between the two results.**\n\n**How to compare responsibly** (use the Time & Ayanamsha Lab):\n1. Enter one birth instant.\n2. Calculate the sidereal Moon with each ayanamsha supported by this course's implementation.\n3. Record Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada for each.\n4. Highlight only the cases where the classification changes.\n5. Recalculate the affected Koota only in those cases.\nDo not suggest that every longitude difference changes a compatibility result.\n\n| Other conventions | What differs |\n|---|---|\n| Geocentric or topocentric Moon | Up to about 1° difference |\n| Apparent or geometric position | Small differences (arc-seconds to arc-minutes) |\n| Mean or true lunar node | Does not affect the Moon's own classification; matters for wider chart work |\n| 27 or 28 Nakshatras | Some muhurta (electional) traditions add Abhijit as a 28th Nakshatra; Koota matching in this course uses the 27-Nakshatra division |\nUse the selector below to record the ayanamsha school you follow.\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### 13. Special conditions\n- **Birth time unknown or only approximate.** The Moon's Pada, and sometimes Nakshatra and Rashi, cannot be fixed reliably; say so in the report and show the range of possible results.\n- **The Moon near a boundary.** Show a warning and the neighbouring result. **Do not silently choose one and do not assign two Nakshatras.**\n- **Historical time zones and daylight saving.** An error of one hour moves the Moon by about half a degree; a missing daylight-saving adjustment is a common cause of a wrong Pada.\n- **Different software giving different results.** First compare the settings (ayanamsha, geocentric/topocentric, time zone, date, place) before calling anything a calculation error.\n- **Configurable warning margin.** The Lab lets you choose how close to a boundary triggers the warning (default 60 arc-seconds).\n\n## Examples\n\n### Worked example 1 – Rohini (the boundary start of Pada 2)\nIllustrative: tropical Moon 67°20', illustrative ayanamsha 24°00'. Lsid = MOD(67°20' − 24°00', 360) = **43°20'** = 2,600'. Rashi: 2600 ÷ 1800 = 1.44 → number 2 = **Vrishabha (Taurus)**, 13°20' into the sign, lord **Venus**. Nakshatra: FLOOR(2600 ÷ 800) + 1 = **4, Rohini**, lord **Moon**. Pada: 2600 MOD 800 = 200; FLOOR(200 ÷ 200) + 1 = **2**. Navamsha: FLOOR(43.3333 ÷ 3.3333) = 13; 13 MOD 12 = 1 = **Taurus**.\n\n### Worked example 2 – Ashlesha (a correction to a common sample)\nSidereal Moon 113°10' = 6,790'. Rashi: 6790 ÷ 1800 = 3.77 → 4 = **Karka**, 23°10' into the sign, lord **Moon**. Nakshatra: FLOOR(6790 ÷ 800) + 1 = 8 + 1 = **9, Ashlesha**, lord **Mercury**. Pada: 6790 MOD 800 = 390; FLOOR(390 ÷ 200) + 1 = **2**. Navamsha: FLOOR(113.1667 ÷ 3.3333) = 33; 33 MOD 12 = 9 = **Capricorn**.\n> **Audit note.** This longitude is sometimes quoted as \"Pushya Pada 3\". It is not: Pushya ends at 106°40', so 113°10' is in Ashlesha Pada 2.\n\n### Worked example 3 – Chitra (another correction)\nSidereal Moon 180°30' = 10,830'. Rashi: 10830 ÷ 1800 = 6.02 → 7 = **Tula (Libra)**, 0°30' into the sign, lord **Venus**. Nakshatra: FLOOR(10830 ÷ 800) + 1 = 13 + 1 = **14, Chitra**, lord **Mars**. Pada: 10830 MOD 800 = 430; FLOOR(430 ÷ 200) + 1 = **3** (Pada 3, not 4). Navamsha: FLOOR(180.5 ÷ 3.3333) = 54; 54 MOD 12 = 6 = **Libra**.\n> **Audit note.** Chitra Pada 4 begins at 183°20'. A Moon at 180°30' is in Pada 3.\n\n### Boundary demonstration (computed by the course engine)\n| Longitude (sidereal) | Rashi | Nakshatra and Pada |\n|---|---|---|\n| 13°19'59.9\" | Aries | Ashwini Pada 4 |\n| 13°20'00.0\" | Aries | **Bharani Pada 1** |\n| 13°20'00.1\" | Aries | Bharani Pada 1 |\n| 26°39'59.9\" | Aries | Bharani Pada 4 |\n| 26°40'00\" | Aries | **Krittika Pada 1** |\n| 29°59'59.9\" | Aries | Krittika Pada 1 |\n| 30°00'00\" | **Taurus** | Krittika Pada 2 |\n| 39°59'59.9\" | Taurus | Krittika Pada 4 |\n| 40°00'00\" | Taurus | **Rohini Pada 1** |\n| 53°19'59.9\" | Taurus | Rohini Pada 4 |\n| 53°20'00\" | Taurus | **Mrigashira Pada 1** |\n| 59°59'59.9\" | Taurus | Mrigashira Pada 2 |\n| 60°00'00\" | **Gemini** | Mrigashira Pada 3 |\n| 93°19'59.9\" | Cancer | Punarvasu Pada 4 |\n| 93°20'00\" | Cancer | **Pushya Pada 1** |\n| 119°59'59.9\" | Cancer | Ashlesha Pada 4 |\n| 120°00'00\" | **Leo** | **Magha Pada 1** |\n| 179°59'59.9\" | Virgo | Chitra Pada 2 |\n| 180°00'00\" | **Libra** | Chitra Pada 3 |\n| 239°59'59.9\" | Scorpio | Jyeshtha Pada 4 |\n| 240°00'00\" | **Sagittarius** | **Mula Pada 1** |\n| 346°39'59.9\" | Pisces | Uttara Bhadrapada Pada 4 |\n| 346°40'00\" | Pisces | **Revati Pada 1** |\n| 359°59'59.9\" | Pisces | Revati Pada 4 |\n| 360°00'00\" | **Aries** | **Ashwini Pada 1** |\nEvery starting value belongs to the new division. At 360° the zodiac wraps to the beginning. Try your own values in the **Boundary Lab**.\n\n## Practice\n\n### Guided practice\n1. Learn the three constants: **1800'** (Rashi), **800'** (Nakshatra), **200'** (Pada).\n2. Convert 100°30' to arc-minutes: 6,030'. Divide by 1800, 800 and 200 and use FLOOR as shown.\n3. Check each result in the Calculator, then try a longitude within one arc-minute of a boundary.\n\n### Exercise set – ten mathematical problems (answers in the Assessment tab)\nClassify each sidereal Moon: Rashi (and lord), Nakshatra (and lord), Pada. (a) 7°45' (b) 29°59' (c) 58°20' (d) 95°41' (e) 126°50' (f) 175°10' (g) 222°35' (h) 268°05' (i) 312°48' (j) 341°20'.\n\n### Mini-project\nIn the Time & Ayanamsha Lab compare two ayanamshas for the same birth data and write a three-sentence note on whether the classification changed, and why. Then record the settings in your Learning Journal.\n\nThe five full horoscope exercises, the printable worksheets and the checkpoint are in the **Workshop** tab.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What is the ecliptic (क्रांतिवृत्त)?\nA: The apparent annual path of the Sun against the stars, seen from Earth; the great circle along which zodiacal longitude is measured.\n\nQ: What is the tropical zodiac (सायन)?\nA: The zodiac measured from the vernal equinox; it is tied to the seasons and drifts against the stars by precession.\n\nQ: What is the sidereal zodiac (निरयन)?\nA: The zodiac fixed against the stars by a chosen convention; the one used in traditional Indian classification.\n\nQ: What is the zodiac in this course?\nA: A 360° circle of ecliptic longitude divided mathematically: 12 Rashis of 30°, 27 Nakshatras of 13°20' and 108 Padas of 3°20'; not the physical constellations.\n\nQ: What is the ayanamsha (अयनांश)?\nA: The angular difference between the tropical and sidereal zero points under a chosen convention.\n\nQ: How many degrees, arc-minutes and arc-seconds in a full circle?\nA: 360° = 21,600' = 1,296,000\".\n\nQ: What is one Rashi in degrees?\nA: 30° (360° ÷ 12); nine Padas fit exactly in one Rashi.\n\nQ: What is one Nakshatra in degrees and arc-minutes?\nA: 13°20' = 800' (360° ÷ 27).\n\nQ: What is one Nakshatra Pada?\nA: 3°20' = 200' (13°20' ÷ 4); there are 108 Padas.\n\nQ: Nakshatra lord sequence?\nA: Ketu, Venus, Sun, Moon, Mars, Rahu, Jupiter, Saturn, Mercury, repeated three times; lord index = (N − 1) MOD 9.\n\nQ: Rashi lord of Aries, Taurus, Gemini, Cancer?\nA: Mars, Venus, Mercury, Moon.\n\nQ: Formula for sidereal longitude?\nA: Lsid = MOD(Ltrop − A, 360).\n\nQ: Why is the Moon's longitude needed for Koota Milan?\nA: It fixes Janma Rashi, Nakshatra, Pada and lords, from which the Kootas are calculated.\n\nQ: What is a boundary condition?\nA: A longitude exactly at, or very near, the edge of a Rashi, Nakshatra or Pada, where the classification changes.\n\nQ: What is the half-open interval rule?\nA: Each division is [start, end): the start belongs to the new division, the end does not.\n\nQ: What is longitude normalisation?\nA: Bringing a longitude into 0° ≤ L < 360° by adding or subtracting 360°; 360° becomes 0°.\n\nQ: What is a geocentric position?\nA: The position as seen from the centre of the Earth; the standard for traditional Rashi and Nakshatra classification.\n\nQ: Why does birth-time uncertainty matter?\nA: The Moon moves about 0.55° an hour, so a time error shifts the longitude and may move it across a boundary.\n\nQ: What is the Navamsha (नवांश)?\nA: A 3°20' division of the zodiac forming the D-9 chart; same width as a Pada but a different classification framework.\n\nQ: Why must classification come before display rounding?\nA: Rounding first can push a position across a boundary (13°19'59.9\" rounds to 13°20' but belongs to Ashwini).\n\n## Assessment\nQ: Sidereal longitude is calculated as:\nA) Tropical + ayanamsha\nB) MOD(tropical − ayanamsha, 360)\nC) Tropical × ayanamsha\nD) Ayanamsha − tropical\nANS: B\nWHY: The sidereal zero point lags the tropical by the ayanamsha, and the result is normalised to 0°–360°.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Tropical 14°00' with an ayanamsha of 24°00' gives a sidereal longitude of:\nA) −10°\nB) 10°\nC) 350°\nD) 38°\nANS: C\nWHY: 14° − 24° = −10°; adding 360° gives 350°.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A Pada is how many arc-minutes?\nA) 100\nB) 200\nC) 800\nD) 1,800\nANS: B\nWHY: 3°20' = 200'. A Nakshatra is 800' and a Rashi 1,800'.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: What is the Nakshatra number for L = 2,600'?\nA) 3\nB) 4\nC) 5\nD) 2\nANS: B\nWHY: FLOOR(2600 ÷ 800) + 1 = 4 (Rohini).\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A Moon at exactly 30°00' belongs to:\nA) Aries\nB) Taurus\nC) Both\nD) Neither\nANS: B\nWHY: Intervals are half-open: the start of a division belongs to the new division.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: What does a longitude of 360°00' normalise to?\nA) Pisces 30°\nB) A thirteenth sign\nC) 0°00' Aries (Ashwini Pada 1)\nD) Revati Pada 4\nANS: C\nWHY: The zodiac wraps; 360° = 0°.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Ashwini spans which interval?\nA) [0°, 13°20')\nB) (0°, 13°20']\nC) [0°, 13°20']\nD) (0°, 13°20')\nANS: A\nWHY: The start is included and the end excluded.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra crosses a Rashi boundary?\nA) Rohini\nB) Krittika\nC) Pushya\nD) Hasta\nANS: B\nWHY: Krittika covers Mesha and Vrishabha. The others lie wholly within one sign.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: How many Padas fit in one Rashi?\nA) 3\nB) 4\nC) 9\nD) 12\nANS: C\nWHY: 30° ÷ 3°20' = 9, so a Pada never crosses a Rashi boundary.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Nakshatra 10 (Magha) has which lord?\nA) Ketu\nB) Venus\nC) Sun\nD) Moon\nANS: A\nWHY: Lord index = (10 − 1) MOD 9 = 0, which is Ketu.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The Rashi lord of Libra is:\nA) Mars\nB) Venus\nC) Mercury\nD) Saturn\nANS: B\nWHY: Taurus and Libra are ruled by Venus.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A Navamsha division spans:\nA) 3°20'\nB) 13°20'\nC) 30°\nD) 9°\nANS: A\nWHY: 30° ÷ 9 = 3°20', the same width as a Pada but a different classification.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which stage of the calculation depends on the ephemeris, time zone and ayanamsha?\nA) Stage 2, the classification arithmetic\nB) Stage 1, the astronomical position\nC) Both equally\nD) Neither\nANS: B\nWHY: The classification arithmetic (Stage 2) only needs the sidereal longitude.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A longitude of 13°19'59.9\" is in:\nA) Bharani Pada 1\nB) Ashwini Pada 4\nC) Ashwini Pada 3\nD) Krittika Pada 1\nANS: B\nWHY: It is 0.1 arc-second before 13°20', so it still belongs to Ashwini.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Why must the longitude not be rounded before classification?\nA) Rounding is illegal\nB) Rounded values can cross a boundary and give a wrong class\nC) It makes the formula longer\nD) It changes the ayanamsha\nANS: B\nWHY: 13°19'59.9\" rounded to minutes becomes 13°20' and would be mislabelled Bharani.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: How is UTC obtained from local civil time?\nA) UTC = local + offset\nB) UTC = local − the offset that applied then and there\nC) UTC = local × offset\nD) UTC = local\nANS: B\nWHY: Local time is ahead of UTC by the offset in force on that date at that place.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Standard traditional classification uses which Moon position?\nA) Topocentric\nB) Geocentric\nC) Heliocentric\nD) Observed horizon position\nANS: B\nWHY: The topocentric position can differ by up to about one degree.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A different ayanamsha changes the Koota input only when:\nA) Always\nB) A classification boundary lies between the two sidereal longitudes\nC) Never\nD) The year is a leap year\nANS: B\nWHY: Only a crossed boundary changes Rashi, Nakshatra or Pada.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The Moon moves roughly how far in an hour?\nA) 0.05°\nB) 0.55°\nC) 5.5°\nD) 55°\nANS: B\nWHY: About 13° a day (range roughly 12°–15°); one Pada takes about six hours.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Sidereal longitude 43°20' belongs to which Nakshatra and Pada?\nA) Krittika Pada 4\nB) Rohini Pada 1\nC) Rohini Pada 2\nD) Mrigashira Pada 1\nANS: C\nWHY: Rohini starts at 40°00'; Pada 2 begins at 43°20'.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Problem 1. Classify the sidereal Moon at 7°45'.\nMA: M = 465'. Aries 7°45' (lord Mars); Ashwini (#1, lord Ketu); Pada: 465 ÷ 200 = 2.3, so Pada 3. Navamsha: Gemini.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Problem 2. Classify the sidereal Moon at 29°59'.\nMA: M = 1,799'. Aries 29°59' (lord Mars); Krittika (#3, lord Sun); position in Krittika 1,799 − 1,600 = 199', Pada 1 (just below 200'). Navamsha: Sagittarius.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Problem 3. Classify the sidereal Moon at 58°20'.\nMA: M = 3,500'. Taurus 28°20' (lord Venus); Mrigashira (#5, lord Mars); position 3,500 − 3,200 = 300', Pada 2. Navamsha: Virgo.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Problem 4. Classify the sidereal Moon at 95°41'.\nMA: M = 5,741'. Cancer 5°41' (lord Moon); Pushya (#8, lord Saturn); position 5,741 − 5,600 = 141', Pada 1. Navamsha: Leo.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Problem 5. Classify the sidereal Moon at 126°50'.\nMA: M = 7,610'. Leo 6°50' (lord Sun); Magha (#10, lord Ketu); position 7,610 − 7,200 = 410', Pada 3. Navamsha: Gemini.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Problem 6. Classify the sidereal Moon at 175°10'.\nMA: M = 10,510'. Virgo 25°10' (lord Mercury); Chitra (#14, lord Mars); position 10,510 − 10,400 = 110', Pada 1. Navamsha: Leo.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Problem 7. Classify the sidereal Moon at 222°35'.\nMA: M = 13,355'. Scorpio 12°35' (lord Mars); Anuradha (#17, lord Saturn); position 13,355 − 12,800 = 555', Pada 3. Navamsha: Libra.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Problem 8. Classify the sidereal Moon at 268°05'.\nMA: M = 16,085'. Sagittarius 28°05' (lord Jupiter); Uttara Ashadha (#21, lord Sun); position 16,085 − 16,000 = 85', Pada 1. Navamsha: Sagittarius.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Problem 9. Classify the sidereal Moon at 312°48'.\nMA: M = 18,768'. Aquarius 12°48' (lord Saturn); Shatabhisha (#24, lord Rahu); position 18,768 − 18,400 = 368', Pada 2. Navamsha: Capricorn.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Problem 10. Classify the sidereal Moon at 341°20'.\nMA: M = 20,480'. Pisces 11°20' (lord Jupiter); Uttara Bhadrapada (#26, lord Saturn); position 20,480 − 20,000 = 480', Pada 3. Navamsha: Libra.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Error detection 1. A student writes: \"Tropical Moon 124°20', so Nakshatra = FLOOR(124.333 ÷ 13.3333) + 1 = 10, Magha.\" Find the mistake.\nMA: The student classified the tropical longitude directly. The ayanamsha must be subtracted first. With an illustrative ayanamsha of 24°10', Lsid = 100°10', which is Pushya (#8), Pada 3, in Cancer, not Magha in Leo.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Error detection 2. A student writes: \"13°19'59.9\" is 13°20' to the nearest minute, so it is Bharani Pada 1.\" Find the mistake.\nMA: The longitude was rounded before classification. 13°19'59.9\" is 0.1 arc-second short of 13°20', so it is Ashwini Pada 4. Never classify a rounded display value.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Error detection 3. A student writes: \"360°00' is Pisces 30°, Revati Pada 4.\" Find the mistake.\nMA: 360° must be normalised. MOD(360, 360) = 0°, which is the start of Aries: Ashwini Pada 1. 360° is not a separate position.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Error detection 4. For 43°20' a student computes FLOOR((2600 MOD 800) ÷ 200) = 1 and states \"Pada 1\". Find the mistake.\nMA: The student forgot the +1. Pada = FLOOR((L MOD 800) ÷ 200) + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2. Index and number differ by one.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Error detection 5. A student uses the lord order Ketu, Venus, Sun, Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Saturn, Rahu and says Ardra (#6) is ruled by Mercury. Find the mistake.\nMA: The order is Ketu, Venus, Sun, Moon, Mars, Rahu, Jupiter, Saturn, Mercury. The sixth lord is Rahu, so Ardra is ruled by Rahu. Check with (6 − 1) MOD 9 = 5, which is Rahu.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. Why do Nakshatra and Rashi boundaries differ?\nMA: The zodiac is divided into 12 equal parts for Rashis (30°) and 27 for Nakshatras (13°20'). 13°20' does not divide 30° evenly, so the two sets of boundaries coincide only at 0°, 120° and 240°.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Why can a Nakshatra extend across two Rashis?\nMA: Because its width (13°20') does not fit a whole number of times into 30°; nine of the 27 Nakshatras straddle a sign boundary, so the Pada decides which Rashi applies.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 3. Why does the ayanamsha matter?\nMA: It converts tropical to sidereal longitude. Different ayanamshas give slightly different sidereal positions, which can change the Pada, Nakshatra or Rashi if a boundary lies between them; the report must state which one was used.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual 4. Why must classification occur before display rounding?\nMA: Rounding moves the value by up to half a display unit, which can cross a boundary and change the class. Classify the unrounded (integer arc-second or finer) value, then round only for display.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 5. Why should birth-time uncertainty be investigated?\nMA: The Moon moves about 0.55° an hour, so a time error shifts its longitude and can cross a boundary; the result depends on the distance to the nearest boundary. Calculate the Moon at the earliest and latest possible times and report any crossing.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\n## References\n### Source status (Day 2)\nThis module is arithmetic and astronomical convention. No classical verse is cited, and none has been verified.\n\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| 12 × 30°, 27 × 13°20', 108 × 3°20' | n/a | Geometric definition |\n| Nakshatra lords in the Vimshottari order | B | Established practice; no verse verified here |\n| Rashi lords (seven planets) | B | Standard |\n| Navamsha starting rules (movable, fixed, dual) | B | Established; also equals FLOOR(L ÷ 3°20') MOD 12 |\n| Ayanamsha definitions | C/D | Differ by system; this course's implementation is approximate |\n| Lahiri anchored near Chitra (Spica) | C | Commonly stated |\n| Moon speed about 12°–15° a day | n/a | Astronomical fact (approximate) |\n| 28-Nakshatra (Abhijit) scheme in muhurta | C | Used in some traditions; not used for Koota matching here |\n\n### Technical notes for professionals\n- Classification uses **integer milli-arcseconds** (1° = 3,600,000 units) in the course engine, so no rounding can move a position across a boundary.\n- The automatic Moon calculation in the Lab uses a shortened lunar series and approximate ayanamsha formulas (good to a few arc-minutes). For critical work take the longitude from your own ephemeris.\n- Sample longitudes in this module are teaching values, not birth records. The ayanamsha 24°00' used in the Workshop to construct tropical values is an **illustrative** figure.\n\n### Corrections made to supplied sample data\n| Supplied | Correct (verified by the engine) |\n|---|---|\n| 113°10' = Pushya Pada 3 | Ashlesha Pada 2 (lord Mercury) |\n| 180°30' = Chitra Pada 4 | Chitra Pada 3 |\n| Navamsha of 43°20' = Simha | Navamsha of 43°20' = Taurus |\n\n## Formula Library\nOpen each formula, change the longitude, and see it evaluate. All values come from the course engine; nothing is hard-coded.\n{{formulas}}\n\n## Diagrams\nThe divisions are drawn to exact scale. Angles run clockwise from 0° Aries at the top.\n{{d2diagrams}}\n\n## Calculator\n### Nakshatra and Rashi Calculator\nEnter a sidereal longitude, or a tropical longitude with an ayanamsha, or birth data. The outputs update at once. This is also a reverse-lookup tool.\n{{calculator}}\n### Reverse lookup\n{{explorer}}\n\n## Boundary Lab\nTest the half-open interval rule and the danger of rounding. Compare the exact classification with what you get if the longitude is rounded to the nearest arc-minute first.\n{{boundary}}\n\n## Time and Ayanamsha Lab\nTwo experiments: how a birth-time uncertainty moves the Moon, and how different ayanamshas change the classification. Positions are calculated at each candidate time; no average speed is assumed.\n{{timelab}}\n\n## Workshop\n### Five practical horoscope calculations\nTeaching examples, not birth records. The tropical longitude is built from the sidereal value with an **illustrative ayanamsha of 24°00'**, so you can practise the conversion. Work each exercise by hand first.\n{{d2workshop}}\n\n## Common Errors\nFifteen mistakes to recognise and avoid. For each: the wrong method, the right method, a short demonstration, the likely consequence and how to verify.\n\n### 1. Using tropical longitude directly\n**Wrong:** classify the tropical Moon. **Right:** subtract the ayanamsha first. **Demonstration:** tropical 124°20' read directly gives Leo, Magha Pada 2; with an illustrative ayanamsha of 24°10' the sidereal 100°10' is Cancer, Pushya Pada 3. **Consequence:** wrong Rashi and Nakshatra, so every Koota can be wrong. **Verify:** check that your software is set to sidereal.\n\n### 2. Forgetting the ayanamsha subtraction\n**Wrong:** carrying out the Nakshatra formula on the tropical value or on a value already shifted by a different amount. **Right:** Lsid = MOD(Ltrop − A, 360) exactly once. **Consequence:** a shift of about 24° moves the Moon by almost two Nakshatras. **Verify:** compare with a trusted program.\n\n### 3. Subtracting the ayanamsha twice\n**Demonstration:** 124°20' − 2 × 24°10' = 76°00', which is Gemini, Ardra Pada 3, instead of Cancer, Pushya Pada 3. **Verify:** the sidereal value should sit about 24° behind the tropical value (in the present era), not about 48°.\n\n### 4. Using the wrong birth time zone\n**Wrong:** treating the local time as UTC or using the wrong region. **Demonstration:** a local time of 10:30 at UTC+5:30 is 05:00 UTC; forgetting the offset is a 5.5-hour error, which moves the Moon by about 3°, close to a whole Pada. **Verify:** write down local time, offset and UTC side by side.\n\n### 5. Applying today's offset to a historical date\n**Wrong:** assuming the present time zone always applied. **Right:** use a date-specific source for the offset. **Consequence:** an hour's difference moves the Moon about 0.55°. **Verify:** look up the offset for that place and date.\n\n### 6. Mishandling daylight-saving time\n**Wrong:** ignoring that clocks were advanced in some periods. **Right:** subtract the offset **including** any daylight-saving advance that was in force. **Consequence:** a one-hour error. **Verify:** check whether daylight saving applied on the birth date at the birthplace.\n\n### 7. Using topocentric instead of geocentric Moon\n**Wrong:** an observed (horizon) position where the profile needs geocentric. **Consequence:** a difference of up to about one degree because of parallax. **Verify:** check the software's geocentric/topocentric setting.\n\n### 8. Rounding before classification\n**Demonstration:** 13°19'59.9\" rounded to 13°20' gives Bharani Pada 1; unrounded it is Ashwini Pada 4. **Right:** classify the unrounded value; round only for display. **Verify:** use the Boundary Lab.\n\n### 9. Wrong boundary treatment\n**Wrong:** letting an endpoint belong to the old division. **Right:** [start, end): the start belongs to the new division. **Demonstration:** exactly 40°00' is Rohini Pada 1, not Krittika Pada 4.\n\n### 10. Treating 360° as a separate sign\n**Wrong:** 360° = Pisces 30°. **Right:** 360° ≡ 0° = Aries, Ashwini Pada 1. **Verify:** normalise with MOD before any classification.\n\n### 11. Wrong Nakshatra numbering\n**Wrong:** numbering Ashwini as 0 and using that number in a table lookup, or starting at Krittika. **Right:** Ashwini is 1 and Revati is 27. **Demonstration:** FLOOR(L ÷ 800) is the zero-based index; add 1 for the number.\n\n### 12. Wrong Pada numbering\n**Wrong:** forgetting the +1, so a Pada 2 becomes \"Pada 1\". **Demonstration:** 43°20': FLOOR(200 ÷ 200) = 1, so P = 1 + 1 = 2.\n\n### 13. Wrong Nakshatra lord sequence\n**Wrong:** Ketu, Venus, Sun, Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Saturn, Rahu. **Right:** Ketu, Venus, Sun, Moon, Mars, **Rahu**, Jupiter, Saturn, **Mercury**. **Verify:** lord index (N − 1) MOD 9; Nakshatra 6 gives index 5 = Rahu.\n\n### 14. Confusing Pada with Navamsha sign\n**Wrong:** saying \"Pada 2 means the second Navamsha sign\". **Right:** the Navamsha sign is found from FLOOR(L ÷ 3°20') MOD 12. **Demonstration:** 43°20' is Rohini Pada 2, but its Navamsha sign is Taurus.\n\n### 15. Using different ayanamshas in two programs without noticing\n**Wrong:** comparing two programs and calling a difference an error. **Right:** compare the settings first. **Demonstration (illustrative):** two ayanamshas about 1.5° apart can put the same Moon in different Nakshatras. **Verify:** use the Time & Ayanamsha Lab to see where two settings diverge.\n\n### Discrepancy investigation checklist (software verification)\nRecord and compare, in this order: birth date; birth time; birthplace; time zone (and daylight saving); ayanamsha; geocentric/topocentric setting; tropical or sidereal setting; Moon longitude; Rashi; Nakshatra; Pada. Only after the settings agree should a remaining difference be treated as an error. Compare at least two programs (for example JHora and another reference program) and this course's Calculator; state which ones you used.\n\n## Instructor Notes\n*For teachers and self-study students.*\n\n### Suggested lecture sequence (150–180 minutes)\n1. (15) Revise Day 1; pose \"two stages\" (position versus classification).\n2. (30) Ecliptic, tropical and sidereal zodiac, ayanamsha formula.\n3. (35) Rashi, Nakshatra, Pada mathematics; formula library.\n4. (25) Boundaries, half-open intervals, precision.\n5. (20) Time conversion, birth-time uncertainty, ayanamsha comparison.\n6. (35) Five practical exercises.\n7. (15) Assessment and discussion.\n\n### Questions to ask students\n- \"If your software and mine disagree on the Pada, what do we check first?\"\n- \"Why does 360° not exist as a position?\"\n- \"Two ayanamshas differ by 1.5°. Does that change the Koota score? Always? Never?\"\n\n### Misconceptions to correct\nConstellations equal signs; ayanamsha is a constant; rounding is harmless; Pada and Navamsha are identical; an ayanamsha difference always changes the result.\n\n### Board-work calculations\n1. 360 → 21,600 → 1,296,000. 2. 13°20' = 800'; 3°20' = 200'. 3. Classify 100°30' step by step. 4. Draw the half-open intervals on a number line and mark 13°20'. 5. Derive FLOOR(L ÷ 3°20') MOD 12 for Navamsha.\n\n### Practical demonstration\nDrag the Diagrams slider across 29°59'59\", 30°00'00\" and 360°; show the Boundary Lab rounding trap; run a birth time in the Time Lab with ±30 minutes.\n\n### Discussion topics\nWhat evidence would be needed to prefer one ayanamsha; whether a report should show two classifications when the Moon is near a boundary.\n\n### Homework\nComplete the five exercises and the boundary test; record the settings of one real calculation in your Learning Journal.\n\n### Audit log for Day 2\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada boundaries | Verified by engine at 26 boundary points (before, at, after) |\n| Nakshatra and Rashi lords | Verified |\n| Navamsha rules | Traditional rules and FLOOR/MOD formula agree for all exercises |\n| Supplied sample 113°10' | Corrected to Ashlesha Pada 2 |\n| Supplied sample 180°30' | Corrected to Pada 3 |\n| Supplied Navamsha of 43°20' | Corrected to Taurus |\n| Ayanamsha values | Illustrative only; none stated as fact |\n","03":"# Day 3 – Vashya Koota\n\n## Learn\n\n### Welcome to Day 3\n**Module 3: Vashya (वश्य) – mutual attraction, influence and traditional classification.** Day 3 of 14 · Foundation to Advanced · 180–210 minutes (self-paced) · Practical: a twelve-sign matrix and ten scoring cases.\n\n> **Central teaching philosophy.** Understand the nature of the relationship before assigning its score. Understand the direction of influence before interpreting compatibility.\n\nToday is the first day on which you **score a Koota**. It is deliberately slower than the days before, because Vashya contains three separate ideas that beginners often merge:\n1. **Classification.** How each Rashi (and, for two signs, each half of a Rashi) is placed in one of five Vashya categories.\n2. **Relationship.** How one category is traditionally related to another: who is \"responsive\" to whom.\n3. **Interpretation.** What a resulting score can, and cannot, be taken to mean.\n\n> **Warning to keep in mind.** Vashya is **not** simply a test of attraction. Its traditional framework is about influence, responsiveness, dominance and the symbolic relationship between sign classifications. And the published scoring tables **differ**; this course teaches the differences instead of hiding them.\n\n### What you will be able to do by the end of the day\n1. Explain the Sanskrit meaning and traditional significance of Vashya.\n2. Explain the philosophical foundation of Vashya Koota.\n3. Explain how Vashya relates to attraction, influence and control.\n4. Name the five traditional Vashya categories.\n5. Classify all twelve Rashis into Vashya groups.\n6. Explain why Sagittarius and Capricorn are split at 15°.\n7. Calculate the Vashya category from an exact Moon longitude.\n8. Tell mutual Vashya from one-sided Vashya.\n9. Explain the Vashya and Bhakshya concepts.\n10. Build a twelve-sign Vashya matrix.\n11. Explain the conventional two-point scoring system.\n12. Compare different scoring matrices and say why they differ.\n13. Explain directional and non-directional scoring.\n14. Recognise regional and textual variations.\n15. Explain exceptions and the limits of interpretation.\n16. Calculate Vashya scores for different horoscope pairs.\n17. Explain why a Vashya score is never a stand-alone marriage decision.\n18. Describe how Vashya is built as a configurable calculation module.\n\n### Your learning route (ten lessons)\nOpen the tabs in order. Each lesson has an estimated time, objectives, reading, key terms, worked examples and a knowledge check. Tick each lesson when you have finished it.\n{{vlessons}}\n\n### Suggested pace (self-paced)\n| Block | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Introduction and philosophy | 25 |\n| The five Vashya categories | 35 |\n| Rashi classification (with the 15° rule) | 25 |\n| Scoring matrices | 35 |\n| Different schools and exceptions | 25 |\n| Practical exercises | 40 |\n| Assessment and discussion | 25 |\n\n### Your Day 3 progress\n{{status3}}\n\n### Before you begin (two-minute self-check)\nFrom Day 2, can you find the Moon's Rashi **and the degree inside the Rashi** from a sidereal longitude? (Rashi = FLOOR(L ÷ 30) + 1; degree = L − 30 × FLOOR(L ÷ 30).) Vashya needs the degree for two signs. If you are unsure, revisit Day 2 first.\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### Lesson 1 – Introduction to Vashya (about 20 minutes)\n**Learning objectives.** Define Vashya; separate attraction, influence, dominance and accommodation; place Vashya among the Kootas.\n\n**1.1 What does Vashya mean?** The Sanskrit word *Vashya* (वश्य) carries the idea of being amenable, responsive, influenced, guided or brought under influence; also of being able to exercise influence. In traditional Jyotisha, Vashya Koota is one of the compatibility factors of the Ashtakoota scheme. It examines the traditional relationship between the **Moon signs** of two people through a system of sign classifications.\n\n**1.2 What is it trying to represent?** Mutual attraction; influence; responsiveness; mutual accommodation; dominance; willingness to yield; the relative strength of influence. These are **traditional astrological interpretations, not scientifically validated measurements of behaviour.**\n\n**1.3 In plain words.** Two people can be similar and still relate in different patterns: one may adapt more readily, one may lead more often. Traditional matching tries to give a symbolic picture of such patterns by comparing the *kinds* of creatures that the two Moon signs stand for.\n\n**1.4 Why is Vashya used in marriage matching?** Traditional matching does not only ask whether two people are alike. It also asks how two different temperaments may interact. Vashya is one of the ways the system represents that: both may be strongly independent; one may be more accommodating; one may traditionally be shown as influencing the other; the two classes may be mutually compatible or one-sided.\n\n**1.5 Five ideas that must not be confused.**\n| Idea | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Attraction | The inclination towards another person |\n| Influence | The capacity to affect another person's response or decisions |\n| Dominance | The tendency to exercise greater control or authority |\n| Accommodation | The willingness to adjust to another person |\n| Mutual compatibility | How far two dispositions are traditionally thought able to accommodate one another |\nVashya must **not** be read as a direct measure of romantic attraction, sexual compatibility, obedience or actual relationship behaviour.\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) Give the Sanskrit idea behind \"Vashya\". (b) Name two things Vashya is *not* measuring. (c) Why is \"attraction\" an incomplete summary?\n\n### Lesson 2 – Philosophy of Vashya (about 20 minutes)\n**2.1 The symbolic classification principle.** Vashya divides the twelve Rashis into **five** classes, each linked to a traditional symbolic image of a creature or mode of life: **Chatushpada** (quadruped), **Dwipada or Manava** (two-footed, human), **Jalachara** (water-dwelling), **Vanachara** (forest or wild), **Keeta** (insect).\n\n**2.2 Four layers of meaning.** Keep them apart: the *literal* meaning (an animal), the *symbolic* representation (a mode of being), the *astrological classification* (a label for a sign or half-sign), and the *interpretative application* (what a score is taken to say). The symbols are a classification device; **they are not descriptions of the people born under a sign.**\n\n**2.3 Why five classes for twelve signs?** Several signs share a class (for example Aries and Taurus). And two signs, **Sagittarius** and **Capricorn**, are internally divided at 15°. So Vashya depends on two things: the Moon's **Rashi** and, for those two signs, the Moon's **exact degree within the Rashi**. You cannot always classify from the sign name alone.\n\n**2.3 How Vashya differs from the other Kootas.**\n| Koota | Primary traditional reference |\n|---|---|\n| Vashya | Rashi-based influence classification |\n| Tara | Relative Nakshatra position |\n| Yoni | Nakshatra-based symbolic classification |\n| Gana | Nakshatra-based temperament classification |\n| Graha Maitri | Relationship of the Moon-sign lords |\n| Bhakoot | Relative Moon-sign relationship |\n| Nadi | Nakshatra-based classification |\n**Vashya versus Graha Maitri.** Vashya compares the *classes* of the two signs; Graha Maitri compares the *planetary friendship* of the two sign lords. They are different dimensions and must not be treated as identical.\n\n**Check yourself.** Why can Vashya not be calculated from the Rashi name alone?\n\n### Lesson 3 – The five Vashya categories (about 25 minutes; use the Categories tab)\n**3.1 Chatushpada – चतुष्पद (quadruped).** Mesha (Aries); Vrishabha (Taurus); Dhanu (Sagittarius) from 15°00' to 30°00'; Makara (Capricorn) from 0°00' to 15°00'. Symbolism: four-footed creatures: strength, stability, movement, instinctive response. *Do not turn these into fixed personality descriptions.*\n\n**3.2 Dwipada / Manava – द्विपद / मानव (two-footed, human).** Mithuna (Gemini); Kanya (Virgo); Tula (Libra); Kumbha (Aquarius); Dhanu (Sagittarius) from 0°00' to 15°00'. Some traditions call the class Nara or Manava. Symbolism: reasoning, social interaction, communication, conscious decision, adaptability. *These are symbolic associations, not established personality traits.*\n\n**3.3 Jalachara – जलचर (water-dwelling).** Karka (Cancer); Meena (Pisces); Makara (Capricorn) from 15°00' to 30°00'. Symbolism: adaptability, dependence on surroundings, movement through changing conditions, protective and responsive nature.\n\n**3.4 Vanachara – वनचर (forest or wild).** Simha (Leo) only. Symbolism: a wild animal; independent disposition, strength, dominance, authority. Vanachara holds a distinctive position in many scoring matrices (see the matrix lessons). *Not every Leo person is dominant or controlling.*\n\n**3.5 Keeta – कीट (insect or crawling creature).** Vrischika (Scorpio) only. Symbolism: protective and defensive response, instinct. *Do not give a person a negative label because of this class.*\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### Lesson 4 – The twelve-Rashi classification (about 25 minutes; use the Classification tab)\n**4.1 The classification table.** Use this as the primary teaching profile; every cell is configurable if a source profile says otherwise.\n| Rashi | Sanskrit | Longitude within the Rashi | Vashya |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Aries | Mesha (मेष) | 0°–30° | Chatushpada |\n| Taurus | Vrishabha (वृषभ) | 0°–30° | Chatushpada |\n| Gemini | Mithuna (मिथुन) | 0°–30° | Dwipada / Manava |\n| Cancer | Karka (कर्क) | 0°–30° | Jalachara |\n| Leo | Simha (सिंह) | 0°–30° | Vanachara |\n| Virgo | Kanya (कन्या) | 0°–30° | Dwipada / Manava |\n| Libra | Tula (तुला) | 0°–30° | Dwipada / Manava |\n| Scorpio | Vrishchika (वृश्चिक) | 0°–30° | Keeta |\n| Sagittarius | Dhanu (धनु) | 0°–15° | Dwipada / Manava |\n| Sagittarius | Dhanu (धनु) | 15°–30° | Chatushpada |\n| Capricorn | Makara (मकर) | 0°–15° | Chatushpada |\n| Capricorn | Makara (मकर) | 15°–30° | Jalachara |\n| Aquarius | Kumbha (कुम्भ) | 0°–30° | Dwipada / Manava |\n| Pisces | Meena (मीन) | 0°–30° | Jalachara |\n\n**4.2 Half-open intervals.** Use [start, end): Dhanu 0°00' up to but not including 15°00' = Dwipada; Dhanu 15°00' up to but not including 30°00' = Chatushpada; Makara 0°00' up to but not including 15°00' = Chatushpada; Makara 15°00' up to but not including 30°00' = Jalachara. **At exactly 15°00' the Moon belongs to the second category.** If a source profile specifies otherwise, the course engine lets the boundary be configured.\n\n### Lesson 5 – Sagittarius and Capricorn (about 20 minutes; use the Classification tab)\n**5.1 Why are they divided?** Both signs are traditionally pictured as composite figures: Dhanu as an archer (a human upper body on an animal body) and Makara as a composite aquatic-animal creature. The classification follows the image: the human part first for Dhanu, then the animal part; the land-animal part first for Makara, then the water part.\n\n**5.2 Sagittarius.** First half: 0°00' to just before 15°00': **Dwipada**. Second half: 15°00' to just before 30°00': **Chatushpada**.\n| Moon at | Category |\n|---|---|\n| 5° Sagittarius | Dwipada |\n| 14°59' Sagittarius | Dwipada |\n| 15°00' Sagittarius | Chatushpada |\n| 25° Sagittarius | Chatushpada |\n\n**5.3 Capricorn.** First half: **Chatushpada**. Second half: **Jalachara**.\n| Moon at | Category |\n|---|---|\n| 5° Capricorn | Chatushpada |\n| 14°59' Capricorn | Chatushpada |\n| 15°00' Capricorn | Jalachara |\n| 25° Capricorn | Jalachara |\n\n**5.4 Where does the degree come from?** From the Moon's calculated sidereal longitude (Day 2): degree in sign = L − 30 × FLOOR(L ÷ 30). In sidereal longitude, Sagittarius 15° is 255°00' and Capricorn 15° is 285°00'.\n\n### Lesson 6 – Calculating Vashya (about 25 minutes)\n**6.1 The calculation sequence.**\n1. Calculate the Moon's sidereal longitude (Day 2).\n2. Identify the Moon's Rashi.\n3. Calculate the degree within the Rashi.\n4. Identify the Vashya category.\n5. Compare the two categories.\n6. Apply the selected scoring matrix.\n7. Produce the interpretation.\n8. Record the rule profile and its source.\n\n**6.2 Formula.** Let L be the normalised sidereal longitude in degrees. R = FLOOR(L ÷ 30) (zero-based Rashi index); D = L − 30R (degree within the Rashi). Look up the category in the classification table; for Sagittarius (R = 8) and Capricorn (R = 9) use the 15° rule. **Never calculate Vashya from the Rashi name alone.**\n\n**6.3 Worked example.** Person A: Moon 43°20' (Taurus, Chatushpada). Person B: Moon 100°30' (Cancer, Jalachara). Under Profile A, Chatushpada (bride row) against Jalachara (groom column) = **1 point out of 2**. That is a **profile-specific** score, not a universal one.\n\n### Lesson 6b – Mutual and one-sided Vashya\n**Mutual Vashya.** Where the classes are compatible in both directions the relationship is traditionally seen as more favourable: mutual influence, responsiveness and accommodation; a balanced relationship; a shared ability to yield.\n\n**One-sided Vashya.** Some systems consider direction: A may be Vashya to B while B is not Vashya to A. Then the reading must distinguish: A influences B; B influences A; mutual influence; no recognised influence. **Do not confuse this directional idea with the bride-groom order used in some scoring tables.**\n\n**Bride-groom orientation.** Where a table puts the **bride's** category on the rows and the **groom's** on the columns, the order must be kept. If the matrix is asymmetric, swapping bride and groom can change the score. (The course also offers a gender-neutral view that simply shows the ordered pair, \"first person\" and \"second person\", without assumptions about roles.)\n\n### Lesson 6c – Vashya and Bhakshya\n*Vashya* is associated with being subject to influence. *Bhakshya* (भक्ष्य) is associated, in the traditional symbolic picture, with being eaten or preyed upon. Some traditional explanations use predator-prey symbolism to explain why certain class pairs score high, middling or zero: for example a wild animal and a quadruped.\n\n**Caution.** This is a commonly offered explanation (grade C/E); the course has not verified its textual source. Do **not** assume that every modern matrix is a direct mathematical picture of predator and prey. Always ask: is this a traditional explanation or a later interpretative model?\n\n### Lesson 7 – The scoring system (about 30 minutes; use the Scoring Matrices tab)\n**7.1 Maximum score.** In the widely used Ashtakoota scheme Vashya carries **2 points out of 36**. If Vashya appears in a Dashakoota profile, do not assume the same maximum: verify the profile.\n\n**7.2 Profile A: the primary teaching matrix.** Orientation: **bride's category = row, groom's category = column.**\n| Bride ↓ / Groom → | Chatushpada | Manava | Jalachara | Vanachara | Keeta |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Chatushpada | 2 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| Manava | 1 | 2 | 0.5 | 0 | 1 |\n| Jalachara | 1 | 0.5 | 2 | 1 | 1 |\n| Vanachara | 0 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 0 |\n| Keeta | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 2 |\n> **Source status.** This matrix was supplied by the course author as the primary teaching profile. Its attribution to a named classical text has **not been verified** (grade C/D). It is one profile among several; see the Different Schools tab.\n\n**7.3 Reading the matrix.** Same category = 2. Partial compatibility = 1 or 0.5. No credit = 0. **It is asymmetric in exactly one pair:** Manava bride with Keeta groom scores 1, but Keeta bride with Manava groom scores 0. Every other pair of cells is mirrored.\n\n**7.4 What the scores mean.** 2: full traditional score (same category). 1: intermediate. 0.5: lower intermediate; the table recognises a limited favourable relationship. 0: no points under this profile; **not** proof of incompatibility.\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### Lesson 8 – Different schools of thought (about 25 minutes)\n**8.1 Why the tables differ.** Sources agree that Vashya is worth 2 points but **disagree on the matrix.** Research for this course found: some published tables use **1.5** for certain pairs (for example Chatushpada with Vanachara); some describe a 2 / 1 / 0.5 / 0 scheme only in words; some state that Chatushpada and Vanachara score 0 as a predator-prey pair, and that Vanachara and Jalachara also score 0; category allocation also differs slightly between sources (for example the treatment of Aquarius). The tables on the web are **not authoritative**: none was traced to a verified classical text.\n\n**8.2 The three scoring profiles distinguished by this course.**\n| Profile | What it is | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | The primary five-category matrix above (2, 1, 0.5, 0; directional in one pair) | In use; unverified source (grade C/D) |\n| **A0** | The matrix used in earlier drafts of this course; differs from A in three cells | In use for comparison; unverified (grade C/D) |\n| **B** | Alternative fractional scoring with 1.5 values | **Not populated**: requires source verification |\n| **C** | Alternative category interpretation or a source-specific directional matrix | **Not populated**: requires source verification |\nNever combine different matrices into one.\n\n**8.3 Where A and A0 differ.**\n| Cell (bride row × groom column) | Profile A | Profile A0 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chatushpada × Vanachara | 0 | 0.5 |\n| Vanachara × Jalachara | 1 | 0 |\n| Keeta × Manava | 0 | 1 |\nEverything else is identical. Open the Calculation Lab to see the effect on a pair.\n\n**8.4 Comparative school register.** Fill a table like this for each profile you can document. Write \"Not verified\" where that is the truth; **never invent a cell.**\n| Parameter | Profile A | Profile A0 | Profile B | Profile C |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Five categories | As in 4.1 | As in 4.1 | Not verified | Not verified |\n| Sagittarius division | 15° | 15° | Not verified | Not verified |\n| Capricorn division | 15° | 15° | Not verified | Not verified |\n| Scoring matrix | See 7.2 | See 8.3 | Not verified | Not verified |\n| Directional scoring | One asymmetric pair (Manava / Keeta) | Two asymmetric pairs (Chatushpada / Vanachara and Vanachara / Jalachara) | Not verified | Not verified |\n| Fractional scores | 0.5 | 0.5 | 1.5 reported; not verified | Not verified |\n| Source | Course-supplied; unverified | Earlier draft; unverified | Not verified | Not verified |\n| Interpretation | As in 7.4 | As in 7.4 | Not verified | Not verified |\n\n**8.5 Four approaches to compare.** (1) *Classical sign-category approach:* the animal symbolism and Bhakshya/Vashya reasoning; (2) *Muhurta Chintamani-based practice:* investigate what that text actually says; **a software website's attribution is not proof of an original Sanskrit rule** (this course could not verify it); (3) *later matchmaking manuals:* fractional and directional scores, bride-groom order; (4) *contemporary software:* implementations differ.\n\n**8.6 Why two programs can give different Vashya scores for the same pair.** Different classification tables; different scoring matrices; different orientation (bride/groom order); different treatment of directional relationships; different treatment of Sagittarius and Capricorn; different rounding or boundary handling.\n\nUse the selector below to record the profile you are studying.\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### Lesson 9 – Vashya Dosha and exceptions (about 20 minutes)\n**9.1 Is there a separate \"Vashya Dosha\"?** Be careful with words. Distinguish: a **low** Vashya score; a **zero** Vashya score; a \"traditional incompatibility\"; and a separately **named** Vashya Dosha. **The sources consulted for this course did not establish a separately named Vashya Dosha with agreed conditions.** So the course does not label every zero as a severe Dosha: it records \"Vashya score 0 under Profile X\" and leaves the reading to the full discussion.\n\n**9.2 Exception questions to research and record (none applied automatically).**\n- Same Vashya category.\n- Different categories with mutual influence.\n- One-sided Vashya and directional exceptions.\n- Friendly sign relationships.\n- Alternative scoring tables.\n- Same-Rashi cases.\n- Sagittarius and Capricorn boundary cases.\n- Conflicting results from different schools.\n\n**9.3 Does Graha Maitri cancel a low Vashya?** Do not cancel Vashya points merely because the sign lords are friends. Distinguish three things: an **actual cancellation rule** (named school, source, conditions); a **general interpretative mitigation**; and **a modern astrologer's judgement**. In this course the first is not available (no source has been verified), so the status is \"identified, not applied\".\n\n**9.4 Does Bhakoot affect the reading of Vashya?** They are different Kootas: Vashya compares categories; Bhakoot compares the relative Moon signs. They can be read together, but **one score must not mechanically erase the other.**\n\n## Examples\n\n### Worked example 1 – category from a longitude\nMoon at 270°30'. Rashi = FLOOR(270.5 ÷ 30) = 9 (zero-based), so Capricorn; degree in sign = 270.5 − 270 = 0°30'. First half of Capricorn, so **Chatushpada**.\n\n### Worked example 2 – Sagittarius at the boundary\nMoon at 254°59' (Sagittarius 14°59'): **Dwipada**. Moon at 255°00' (Sagittarius 15°00'): **Chatushpada**. Moon at 255°01': Chatushpada. Rounding 254°59'59.9\" to 255° would give the wrong class: classify unrounded.\n\n### Worked example 3 – a score, with the direction shown\nBride: Mithuna (Manava). Groom: Karka (Jalachara). Manava row, Jalachara column = **0.5**. Swap them: Jalachara row, Manava column = **0.5**. Symmetric here.\n\n### Worked example 4 – an asymmetric pair\nBride: Kanya (Manava), groom: Vrishchika (Keeta): Manava row, Keeta column = **1**. Swap: Keeta row, Manava column = **0**. Swapping the people changes the score.\n\n### Worked example 5 – the same pair under two profiles\nBride: Vrishchika (Keeta), groom: Kanya (Manava): Profile A = **0**, Profile A0 = **1**. Not an error: two profiles. Report both, side by side, never combined.\n\n### The ten practice cases (Profile A)\n| # | Bride | Groom | Category pair | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Mesha | Vrishabha | Chatushpada / Chatushpada | 2 |\n| 2 | Mithuna | Karka | Manava / Jalachara | 0.5 |\n| 3 | Karka | Mithuna | Jalachara / Manava | 0.5 |\n| 4 | Meena | Simha | Jalachara / Vanachara | 1 |\n| 5 | Kanya | Vrishchika | Manava / Keeta | 1 |\n| 6 | Vrishchika | Kanya | Keeta / Manava | 0 |\n| 7 | Vrishabha | Simha | Chatushpada / Vanachara | 0 |\n| 8 | Simha | Mesha | Vanachara / Chatushpada | 0 |\n| 9 | Dhanu 10° | Karka | Manava / Jalachara | 0.5 |\n| 10 | Dhanu 20° | Karka | Chatushpada / Jalachara | 1 |\nThe Workshop tab recalculates every one of these from the matrix in the course engine; the scores above are not copied into the calculator.\n\n## Practice\n\n### Lesson 9b – Practical horoscope matching (about 40 minutes)\n**Guided steps.**\n1. Do Exercise 1 (the twelve-Rashi classification) on paper, then check it.\n2. Build the twelve-sign matrix by looking each sign up in the category matrix.\n3. Do the ten worked cases yourself before opening the answers.\n4. Test the Sagittarius and Capricorn boundaries.\n5. Do the reverse-order test and write why direction matters.\n6. Compare Profile A with Profile A0 for the same pairs.\n\n**Exercises (all in the Workshop tab).**\n- **Exercise 1.** Complete the twelve-Rashi classification worksheet (Sagittarius and Capricorn as two rows each): Rashi, degree, Vashya category, symbolic classification, short explanation.\n- **Exercise 2.** The interactive 12 × 12 matrix (with exact degree input for Sagittarius and Capricorn), the category pair, the score and the interpretation; and the 5 × 5 category matrix.\n- **Exercise 3.** The ten worked combinations.\n- **Exercise 4.** The Sagittarius and Capricorn boundary test: 14°59', 15°00', 15°01' in each.\n- **Exercise 5.** The reverse-order test: pick an asymmetric pair, compute both orders, explain.\n\n**Written task.** In your Learning Journal, write a six-line interpretation of one sample pair that (a) names the profile and the orientation, (b) gives the categories and score, (c) states the limits, (d) avoids deterministic claims.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What does Vashya (वश्य) mean?\nA: Amenable, responsive, brought under influence; also able to exercise influence. In matching, a classification of influence between Moon signs.\n\nQ: How many Vashya categories are there?\nA: Five: Chatushpada, Dwipada (Manava), Jalachara, Vanachara, Keeta.\n\nQ: What is Chatushpada (चतुष्पद)?\nA: The four-footed class: Mesha, Vrishabha, Dhanu 15°–30°, Makara 0°–15°.\n\nQ: What is Dwipada / Manava (द्विपद / मानव)?\nA: The two-footed, human class: Mithuna, Kanya, Tula, Kumbha, Dhanu 0°–15°.\n\nQ: What is Manava?\nA: Another name for the human (Dwipada, Nara) class.\n\nQ: What is Jalachara (जलचर)?\nA: The water-dwelling class: Karka, Meena, Makara 15°–30°.\n\nQ: What is Vanachara (वनचर)?\nA: The forest or wild-animal class: Simha only.\n\nQ: What is Keeta (कीट)?\nA: The insect or crawling-creature class: Vrishchika only.\n\nQ: What is the difference between Vashya and Bhakshya?\nA: Vashya concerns being subject to influence; Bhakshya concerns being preyed upon in the symbolic picture. The Bhakshya explanation is commonly offered but its source is unverified.\n\nQ: What is mutual Vashya?\nA: Classes compatible in both directions: mutual influence and accommodation.\n\nQ: What is one-sided Vashya?\nA: A relationship considered in one direction only: A is responsive to B but not the reverse.\n\nQ: What is the maximum Vashya score in Ashtakoota?\nA: 2 points out of 36.\n\nQ: In Profile A, which category is the row and which is the column?\nA: The bride's category is the row, the groom's category is the column.\n\nQ: Why is Sagittarius divided?\nA: Its classification changes at 15°: 0°–15° Dwipada (human part), 15°–30° Chatushpada.\n\nQ: Why is Capricorn divided?\nA: 0°–15° Chatushpada, 15°–30° Jalachara, following the composite land-and-water image.\n\nQ: Is there a separate, agreed \"Vashya Dosha\"?\nA: Not established by the sources consulted; a zero score is recorded as a zero, not automatically as a severe Dosha.\n\nQ: How does Vashya differ from Graha Maitri?\nA: Vashya compares the classes of the two signs; Graha Maitri compares the friendship of the two sign lords.\n\nQ: Name two reasons different software gives different Vashya scores.\nA: Different scoring matrices, and different bride-groom orientation or boundary handling.\n\nQ: Which Profile A pair is asymmetric?\nA: Manava bride with Keeta groom scores 1; Keeta bride with Manava groom scores 0.\n\nQ: What is the score for Chatushpada with Chatushpada?\nA: 2.\n\nQ: Where exactly does the second Sagittarius category begin?\nA: At 15°00' (255°00' sidereal); the start belongs to the new category.\n\nQ: Why must the Moon's longitude not be rounded before classification?\nA: Rounding 14°59'59.9\" to 15° would change the category.\n\nQ: Can a favourable Vashya score guarantee compatibility?\nA: No. It is a traditional classification result, one factor among many.\n\nQ: Which profile is the primary teaching profile?\nA: Profile A, supplied by the course author; its source attribution is unverified.\n\nQ: What must you do when two profiles disagree?\nA: Show both, separately, and give the reason; never average or merge.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: The Sanskrit term Vashya (वश्य) is associated with:\nA) Being amenable or brought under influence\nB) A planetary period\nC) A divisional chart\nD) A lunar day\nANS: A\nWHY: Vashya carries the idea of responsiveness and influence.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Vashya Koota is best described as:\nA) A direct measure of romantic attraction\nB) A traditional classification of influence between Moon-sign dispositions\nC) A scientific measure of dominance\nD) A test of fertility\nANS: B\nWHY: It is symbolic classification, not a measurement of behaviour or attraction.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many Vashya categories are there?\nA) Three\nB) Four\nC) Five\nD) Twelve\nANS: C\nWHY: Chatushpada, Dwipada (Manava), Jalachara, Vanachara and Keeta.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which sign alone is Vanachara?\nA) Aries\nB) Leo\nC) Scorpio\nD) Pisces\nANS: B\nWHY: Leo is the wild-animal class; Scorpio is Keeta.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which sign alone is Keeta?\nA) Leo\nB) Cancer\nC) Scorpio\nD) Taurus\nANS: C\nWHY: Vrishchika is the insect class.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which signs are Chatushpada in full?\nA) Aries and Taurus\nB) Gemini and Virgo\nC) Cancer and Pisces\nD) Libra and Aquarius\nANS: A\nWHY: Mesha and Vrishabha are wholly Chatushpada (plus parts of Dhanu and Makara).\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A Moon at Sagittarius 14°59' is:\nA) Chatushpada\nB) Dwipada (Manava)\nC) Jalachara\nD) Keeta\nANS: B\nWHY: The first 15° of Sagittarius is Dwipada.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A Moon at Sagittarius 15°00' is:\nA) Dwipada\nB) Chatushpada\nC) Jalachara\nD) Vanachara\nANS: B\nWHY: Half-open intervals: 15°00' starts the second category.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A Moon at Capricorn 15°00' is:\nA) Chatushpada\nB) Dwipada\nC) Jalachara\nD) Keeta\nANS: C\nWHY: Capricorn 15°–30° is Jalachara.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Why must the Moon's degree be known for Sagittarius and Capricorn?\nA) Their class changes at 15°\nB) They are the largest signs\nC) They have no lord\nD) They are tropical signs\nANS: A\nWHY: One sign name can map to two Vashya categories.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Under Profile A, the maximum Vashya score is:\nA) 1\nB) 2\nC) 3\nD) 4\nANS: B\nWHY: Vashya carries 2 of the 36 Ashtakoota points.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Profile A, bride Chatushpada and groom Jalachara scores:\nA) 0\nB) 0.5\nC) 1\nD) 2\nANS: C\nWHY: Chatushpada row, Jalachara column = 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Profile A, bride Manava and groom Keeta scores:\nA) 0\nB) 0.5\nC) 1\nD) 2\nANS: C\nWHY: Manava row, Keeta column = 1.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Profile A, bride Keeta and groom Manava scores:\nA) 0\nB) 0.5\nC) 1\nD) 2\nANS: A\nWHY: Keeta row, Manava column = 0: the one asymmetric pair.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Swapping bride and groom changes the score when the matrix is:\nA) Symmetric\nB) Asymmetric for that pair\nC) Always\nD) Never\nANS: B\nWHY: Direction matters only in cells that are not mirrored.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which statement about published Vashya tables is correct?\nA) All sources give one identical matrix\nB) Sources agree on 2 points but differ on the matrix\nC) Vashya has no points\nD) All tables use 1.5\nANS: B\nWHY: Matrices differ; some use 1.5 for some pairs.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Profile A and Profile A0 differ in how many cells?\nA) 0\nB) 3\nC) 12\nD) 25\nANS: B\nWHY: Chatushpada×Vanachara, Vanachara×Jalachara and Keeta×Manava.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A zero Vashya score should be read as:\nA) A severe Dosha always\nB) Zero points under the chosen profile, not proof of incompatibility\nC) A medical finding\nD) A cancelled Dosha\nANS: B\nWHY: No separately named, agreed Vashya Dosha was established.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A favourable Graha Maitri and low Vashya: the course treats this as:\nA) An automatic cancellation\nB) Identified; any cancellation needs a documented rule, school and conditions\nC) Not allowed to mention\nD) A scoring rule\nANS: B\nWHY: Cancellations must not become shortcuts.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which is a valid reason two applications give different Vashya scores?\nA) Different matrices or orientation\nB) The Sun's position\nC) The weekday\nD) The birth place only\nANS: A\nWHY: Different tables, orientation, boundary handling and rounding all matter.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Classification 1. Vashya category of Mesha 12°?\nMA: Chatushpada (Aries is wholly Chatushpada).\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Classification 2. Vashya category of Mithuna 29°?\nMA: Dwipada / Manava (Gemini is wholly human class).\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Classification 3. Vashya category of Karka 3°?\nMA: Jalachara.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Classification 4. Vashya category of Simha 20°?\nMA: Vanachara.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Classification 5. Vashya category of Vrishchika 25°?\nMA: Keeta.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Classification 6. Vashya category of Dhanu 14°59'?\nMA: Dwipada / Manava (first half, before 15°00').\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Classification 7. Vashya category of Dhanu 15°00'?\nMA: Chatushpada (the start of the second half belongs to the new category).\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Classification 8. Vashya category of Makara 14°59'?\nMA: Chatushpada (first half).\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Classification 9. Vashya category of Makara 15°00'?\nMA: Jalachara (second half begins at 15°00').\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Classification 10. Vashya category of Kumbha 7°?\nMA: Dwipada / Manava (Aquarius is wholly human class).\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Scoring 1. Profile A: bride Vrishchika (Keeta), groom Simha (Vanachara).\nMA: Keeta row, Vanachara column = 0.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Scoring 2. Profile A: bride Vrishabha (Chatushpada), groom Mithuna (Manava).\nMA: Chatushpada row, Manava column = 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Scoring 3. Profile A: bride Karka (Jalachara), groom Kumbha (Manava).\nMA: Jalachara row, Manava column = 0.5.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Scoring 4. Profile A: bride Simha (Vanachara), groom Karka (Jalachara).\nMA: Vanachara row, Jalachara column = 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Scoring 5. Profile A: bride Mesha (Chatushpada), groom Vrishchika (Keeta).\nMA: Chatushpada row, Keeta column = 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Scoring 6. Profile A: bride Tula (Manava), groom Vrishchika (Keeta).\nMA: Manava row, Keeta column = 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Scoring 7. Profile A: bride Vrishchika (Keeta), groom Tula (Manava).\nMA: Keeta row, Manava column = 0. The reverse of scoring 6: the asymmetric pair.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Scoring 8. Profile A: bride Makara 20° (Jalachara), groom Meena (Jalachara).\nMA: Same category: 2.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Scoring 9. Profile A: bride Dhanu 20° (Chatushpada), groom Simha (Vanachara).\nMA: Chatushpada row, Vanachara column = 0.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Scoring 10. Profile A: bride Kumbha (Manava), groom Dhanu 5° (Manava).\nMA: Same category: 2.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Error detection 1. A student writes: \"Sagittarius 20° is Manava, because Sagittarius is the human-archer sign.\" Correct it.\nMA: The second half of Sagittarius (15°–30°) is Chatushpada, not Manava. 20° is in the second half. The class depends on the degree, not only the sign name.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Error detection 2. A student reads the matrix for bride Keeta and groom Manava as 1 (using the Manava row and Keeta column). Correct it.\nMA: The student reversed bride and groom. The bride is the row: Keeta row, Manava column = 0 in Profile A. The reversed reading (1) belongs to bride Manava, groom Keeta.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Error detection 3. A student writes: \"Capricorn 10° is Jalachara because Makara is an aquatic sign.\" Correct it.\nMA: The first half of Capricorn (0°–15°) is Chatushpada; Jalachara begins at 15°. At 10° the class is Chatushpada.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Error detection 4. A student rounds a Moon at Sagittarius 14°59'59.9\" to 15°00' and says Chatushpada. Correct it.\nMA: Rounding before classification crossed the boundary. Unrounded, 14°59'59.9\" is before 15°00', so the class is Dwipada (Manava). Classify first, round only for display.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Error detection 5. A student averages Profile A (0) and Profile A0 (1) for Keeta bride and Manava groom and reports 0.5. Correct it.\nMA: Scores from different profiles must never be averaged or combined. Report Profile A = 0 and Profile A0 = 1 separately, with the reason (the cell differs).\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Analytical 1. Why is Vashya not a simple measurement of mutual attraction?\nMA: Its traditional framework concerns influence, responsiveness, dominance and the symbolic relationship between sign classes, not attraction alone. It is a classification result under a rule profile, and it is not a scientifically validated measurement of behaviour.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Analytical 2. Why must the exact degree be considered in Sagittarius and Capricorn?\nMA: Each of these signs is split at 15° into two Vashya categories, so the sign name alone gives two possible classes. The degree in the sign, from the sidereal longitude, selects the correct one.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Analytical 3. Why can two software applications produce different Vashya scores?\nMA: They may use different category tables, different scoring matrices, different bride-groom orientation, different treatment of directional pairs, different handling of the Sagittarius and Capricorn split, or different rounding. Identify each program's profile before treating the difference as an error.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\n## References\n### Source status (Day 3)\nNo classical verse or chapter has been verified for any Vashya rule in this module.\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Five Vashya categories and their symbolism | C | Widely stated in manuals and web explanations; no verse verified |\n| Sign allocation including the 15° split of Sagittarius and Capricorn | C | Commonly stated; allocation varies slightly (for example Aquarius) in some sources |\n| Maximum 2 points | C/D | Common to the sources consulted |\n| Profile A matrix | C/D | Supplied by the course author as the primary teaching profile; unverified |\n| Profile A0 matrix | C/D | Earlier draft; unverified |\n| Profiles B and C | E | Not populated; requires source verification |\n| Bhakshya (predator-prey) explanation | C/E | Commonly offered; textual source unverified |\n| Separately named Vashya Dosha | E | Not established by the sources consulted |\n\n### Research log for this module\n- Search for published Vashya matrices found web pages that agree on 2 points and the five categories but **disagree on the matrix**: some show 1.5 for certain pairs, some describe a 2 / 1 / 0.5 / 0 scheme in words, and some state that Chatushpada with Vanachara, and Vanachara with Jalachara, score 0. Pages consulted (all contemporary web sources, grade D, not authoritative): [Steve Hora, Marriage Compatibility](https://stevehora.substack.com/p/marriage-compatibility); [AstroYogi](https://www.astroyogi.com/blog/vasya-koota-in-kundli-matching.aspx); [InstaAstro](https://instaastro.com/blog/?p=69941); [OnlineJyotish](https://www.onlinejyotish.com/articles/marriage-matching/english/vashya-kootam.php); [kundli.muthu.co](https://kundli.muthu.co/koot/vashya); [VedTara](https://vedtara-website.pages.dev/learn/compatibility); [Hindutone](https://hindutone.com/astrology/kundli-matching-ashtakoota-guna-milan-36-gunas/).\n- **Not verified:** what *Muhurta Chintamani* (or *Saravali*, or any commentary) says about Vashya classes or scores; the original Sanskrit terms for any rule. These are open research tasks, not claims.\n- **Conclusion:** the course freezes a primary **teaching** profile (A), keeps another (A0) for comparison, leaves B and C unpopulated, and shows the student why a result changes with the profile.\n\n### Corrections made to supplied material\n| Supplied | Treatment |\n|---|---|\n| Profile A matrix (3 cells differ from the table used in earlier drafts) | Adopted as Profile A; the earlier table kept as Profile A0 |\n| \"Profile B uses 1.5\" and \"Profile C differs in categories\" | Recorded as research findings; matrices not populated because no verified table was obtained |\n\n## Categories\n### Lesson 3 (interactive) – the five Vashya categories\nTap a card for its signs, symbolism and the cautions. Then see which signs belong to it.\n{{vcards}}\n\n## Classification\n### Lessons 4 and 5 (interactive) – the twelve-Rashi wheel and the 15° split\nThe wheel is drawn to scale: Sagittarius and Capricorn each show two colours. Move the slider to place a Moon; the Vashya category updates, with the exact degree in the sign.\n{{vwheel}}\n\n## Scoring Matrices\n### Lessons 6 and 7 (interactive) – the 5 × 5 category matrix and the 12 × 12 sign matrix\nChoose a profile. The bride is always the row and the groom the column; asymmetric cells are marked.\n{{vmatrix}}\n\n## Calculation Lab\n### Vashya Koota Calculator\nEnter a bride and a groom (by Moon longitude, or by birth details). Choose a profile. The result highlights the matrix cell, shows the other profiles side by side, and explains itself in words. Scores from different profiles are never added together.\n{{vcalc}}\n\n## Workshop\n### Lesson 9 – practical workshop\nExercises 1, 3, 4 and 5. Every expected value is calculated by the engine from the selected profile; none is hard-coded in the interface.\n{{vworkshop}}\n\n## Practical Exam\n### Lesson 10 – the Day 3 practical examination\nComplete the components, mark the self-assessed ones honestly, and see your weighted result. Pass mark: 70%.\n{{vexam}}\n\n## FAQ\n### What does Vashya mean?\nVashya (वश्य) means amenable, responsive, brought under influence; also able to influence. In matching it is a classification of influence between two Moon signs.\n\n### Why is Vashya used in horoscope matching?\nTraditional matching asks not only whether two people are alike, but how two temperaments may interact. Vashya is one of the ways the scheme represents patterns of influence and accommodation.\n\n### How many Vashya categories exist?\nFive: Chatushpada, Dwipada (Manava), Jalachara, Vanachara and Keeta.\n\n### What is Chatushpada?\nThe four-footed class (Mesha, Vrishabha, the second half of Dhanu and the first half of Makara).\n\n### What is Dwipada, and what is Manava?\nDwipada means two-footed; Manava (or Nara) means human. They are two names for the same class: Mithuna, Kanya, Tula, Kumbha and the first half of Dhanu.\n\n### What is Jalachara?\nThe water-dwelling class: Karka, Meena and the second half of Makara.\n\n### What is Vanachara?\nThe forest or wild-animal class: Simha (Leo).\n\n### What is Keeta?\nThe insect or crawling-creature class: Vrishchika (Scorpio).\n\n### Why is Leo classified as Vanachara?\nBy traditional symbolism: Simha is the lion, a wild forest animal. The course records this as a classification label, not a trait of people born under Leo.\n\n### Why is Scorpio classified as Keeta?\nBy traditional symbolism: Vrishchika is the scorpion, a crawling creature. It is a label for the sign, not a negative judgement of anyone.\n\n### Why are Sagittarius and Capricorn divided?\nBoth signs are pictured as composite figures (Dhanu, an archer; Makara, a land-and-water creature), so the classification changes at 15°.\n\n### Does the Moon's exact degree matter?\nFor Sagittarius and Capricorn, yes: the category changes at 15° of the sign. For the other ten signs the degree does not change the Vashya category.\n\n### Is Vashya based on the Ascendant or the Moon sign?\nOn the Moon sign, in the Ashtakoota scheme. Using the Ascendant would need a source-specific rule.\n\n### What is the maximum Vashya score?\n2 points of the 36 in Ashtakoota. In a Dashakoota profile, verify the profile's own maximum.\n\n### Why do different software applications produce different scores?\nDifferent matrices, different bride-groom orientation, different treatment of directional pairs, different handling of the 15° split, or different rounding. Find each program's profile before calling a difference an error.\n\n### Can Vashya scoring be directional?\nYes, in some tables: the score can depend on which person's category is the row. In Profile A exactly one pair is asymmetric (Manava bride with Keeta groom).\n\n### What is Bhakshya?\nIn the traditional symbolic picture, being preyed upon. Some explanations use predator-prey symbolism to account for scores. This is a commonly offered explanation whose textual source is unverified.\n\n### Is a zero Vashya score a serious Dosha?\nNot by itself. This course found no agreed, separately named Vashya Dosha. A zero means no points under the chosen profile.\n\n### Can Graha Maitri influence the reading of Vashya?\nIt can be discussed alongside, but it does not cancel Vashya points automatically. Any cancellation would need a documented rule, school and conditions.\n\n### Can two different Rashis belong to the same Vashya group?\nYes. For example Mesha and Vrishabha are both Chatushpada; Karka and Meena are both Jalachara.\n\n### Is Vashya the same as Yoni?\nNo. Vashya is Rashi-based; Yoni is Nakshatra-based. Both use animal symbolism, but they classify different things.\n\n### Is Vashya the same as psychological compatibility?\nNo. It is a traditional symbolic classification, not a psychological measurement.\n\n### Can Vashya independently determine marriage suitability?\nNo. It is 2 of 36 points, one factor among many, and it never replaces the full horoscope study.\n\n### Which scoring system should an astrologer follow?\nThe one chosen and recorded for the report, with its source and status stated. If sources are disputed, show the alternatives and explain the difference. This course teaches Profile A and compares Profile A0.\n\n### How should two different profiles be reported?\nSide by side, each under its own name, with a short statement of where and why they differ. Never average or merge them.\n\n## Common Mistakes\nFourteen mistakes to recognise. For each: the incorrect approach, the correct approach, a demonstration and the consequence.\n| # | Mistake | Correct approach | Demonstration | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | One category for all of Sagittarius | Split at 15° | Sagittarius 20° is Chatushpada, not Manava | Wrong category and score |\n| 2 | One category for all of Capricorn | Split at 15° | Capricorn 10° is Chatushpada, not Jalachara | Wrong category and score |\n| 3 | Ignoring the 15° boundary | Compute the degree in the sign | 254°59' is Dwipada; 255°00' is Chatushpada | Wrong class near 15° |\n| 4 | Using the Ascendant instead of the Moon sign | Use the Moon sign unless a source says otherwise | Moon in Taurus, Ascendant Leo: use Taurus | Wrong class |\n| 5 | Confusing Vashya with Graha Maitri | Vashya = classes; Graha Maitri = lord friendship | Aries/Scorpio: same lord (Mars), different classes | Mixing two Kootas |\n| 6 | Confusing Vashya with Yoni | Vashya is Rashi-based, Yoni is Nakshatra-based | Both use animals, different inputs | Wrong input used |\n| 7 | Assuming all matrices are symmetric | Check each pair | Manava/Keeta: 1 one way, 0 the other | Wrong score |\n| 8 | Reversing bride and groom | Bride = row, groom = column | Keeta bride, Manava groom = 0 | Reading the wrong cell |\n| 9 | Combining different matrices | One profile per result | Profile A (0) with A0 (1): never average | Meaningless number |\n| 10 | Treating zero as automatic rejection | Zero means no points under the profile | Several zeros are normal | Unfair conclusion |\n| 11 | Treating Vashya as a measurement of dominance | It is symbolic classification | A Leo Moon does not make a person dominant | Unsupported claim |\n| 12 | Assuming a favourable score guarantees compatibility | It is 2 of 36 points | Score 2 does not settle anything | False reassurance |\n| 13 | Ignoring the chosen tradition | State the profile | Two programs, two matrices | Confusion about \"errors\" |\n| 14 | Rounding the longitude before classifying | Classify unrounded | 254°59'59.9\" rounded to 255° flips the class | Wrong category |\n\n## Instructor Guide\n*For teachers and self-study students.*\n\n### Suggested lecture time (210 minutes)\n| Block | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Introduction and philosophy | 25 |\n| The five Vashya categories | 35 |\n| Rashi classification | 25 |\n| Scoring matrices | 35 |\n| Different schools and exceptions | 25 |\n| Practical exercises | 40 |\n| Assessment and discussion | 25 |\n\n### Suggested teaching sequence\n1. Open with \"attraction versus influence\" (Lesson 1). 2. Draw the five categories as five boxes and place the twelve signs in them. 3. Show the 15° split on the wheel. 4. Fill the 5 × 5 matrix together, noting the one asymmetric pair. 5. Compare Profile A and A0 and ask why. 6. Do the ten cases. 7. Discuss limitations.\n\n### Important explanations\nSymbolism is a classification device, not a personality description; direction (bride row, groom column) is part of the rule; a zero is zero points, not a verdict; two profiles are two rulebooks.\n\n### Historical and textual research notes\nExistence of the five classes and the 2-point weight is common to all sources consulted; the **matrix is disputed**; no verse for any rule has been verified; do not accept a website's attribution as proof.\n\n### Board-work demonstrations\n1. Number line 240°–300° with the 255° and 285° markers. 2. The 5 × 5 matrix with arrows showing which cell is read for (bride, groom). 3. The three cells where A and A0 differ.\n\n### Questions to ask students\n\"If two programs disagree, what do you check first?\" \"Why is Keeta/Manava different from Manava/Keeta?\" \"Does Leo Moon mean a dominant person?\"\n\n### Common misunderstandings\nOne category per sign; the matrix is symmetric; zero means rejection; Vashya equals attraction; Graha Maitri cancels Vashya.\n\n### Sagittarius and Capricorn boundary demonstration\nMove the wheel slider across 254°59' → 255°00' → 255°01' and 284°59' → 285°00' → 285°01'.\n\n### Practical assessment instructions\nStudents complete the five exercises and the exam widget; they mark the written interpretation against the checklist; pass mark 70%.\n\n### Homework\nJournal entry on \"attraction versus influence\"; the six-line interpretation of a sample pair; list three questions for source verification.\n\n## Reference Library\n### Vashya reference library (every rule has a source record)\nSearch the records below. Each rule shows its source status and its implementation in the course engine.\n{{vlibrary}}\n","04":"# Day 4 – Tara Koota\n\n## Learn\n\n### Welcome to Day 4\n**Module 4: Tara Koota (तारा कूट) – Nakshatra-based compatibility and the nine-fold Tara classification.** Day 4 of 14 · Foundation to Advanced · 180–210 minutes (self-paced) · Instructor: Anoopam Shukla.\n\nToday you learn Tara as a **two-direction Nakshatra relationship**, not as a nine-name lookup table. You will count from Nakshatra A to Nakshatra B, count again from B to A, understand why the two answers differ, and only then apply a chosen tradition's reading and scoring rule.\n\n> **Central teaching principle.** Count first, classify second, interpret third. The same two Nakshatras give two different counts; the arithmetic never changes with the scoring profile.\n\n### The one idea to keep in mind all day\nThree things must stay separate:\n1. **The calculation:** count inclusively, divide by nine, read the remainder (a remainder of 0 is Tara 9). This is pure arithmetic.\n2. **The interpretation:** what each of the nine Taras *traditionally symbolises*.\n3. **The scoring:** how many points a chosen profile gives. Changing the profile changes the points, **never** the Tara.\n\n### What you will be able to do by the end of the day\n1. Explain the meaning of Tara (तारा) and its relevance in Nakshatra compatibility.\n2. Name the nine Taras in order and state the three Nakshatra positions of each.\n3. Explain Janma, Sampat, Vipat, Kshema, Pratyak, Sadhaka, Naidhana, Mitra and Parama Mitra.\n4. Calculate the forward Tara between two Janma Nakshatras with inclusive counting.\n5. Calculate the reverse Tara.\n6. Divide by nine and interpret the remainder, including the zero remainder.\n7. Explain why one pair gives two different Tara results.\n8. Distinguish Tara Koota from Tara Bala.\n9. Explain Tara Dosha and its limits.\n10. Compare scoring conventions and rule profiles.\n11. Use the calculator and the 27 × 27 matrix.\n12. Complete the exercises and the final assessment.\n13. Explain why Tara must never be treated as a stand-alone verdict.\n\n### Your learning route (ten stages)\nProgress → Introduction → Nine Taras → Mathematics → Directional Counting → Dosha → Scoring → Matrix → Calculator → Practical → Assessment. Tick each stage when you finish it; the tracker remembers where you stopped.\n{{tdash}}\n\n### Prerequisites and quick revision\nYou should already know: the 27 Nakshatras in order; the Janma Nakshatra (जन्म नक्षत्र); the Moon's sidereal longitude; how to identify the Nakshatra from a longitude; basic inclusive counting; and the difference between Nakshatra, Rashi and Pada. If any of these is shaky, revise Day 2 first. Tap a Nakshatra below to see its number, longitude range and lord.\n{{trevise}}\n\n### Suggested pace (self-paced)\n| Block | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Foundation and the nine Taras | 35 |\n| The mathematical method | 30 |\n| Forward and reverse counting | 30 |\n| Tara Dosha and scoring variations | 25 |\n| Matrix and calculator practice | 35 |\n| Assessment and revision | 25 |\n| Total | 180 |\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### Lesson 1 – Introduction to Tara Koota\n**1.1 Meaning of Tara.** *Tara* (तारा) means \"star\". In matching it refers to the position of one person's Janma Nakshatra counted from the other's, grouped into a nine-fold cycle. Traditional associations: fortune, well-being, support, obstacles, accomplishment. In some marriage-matching traditions the same Koota is called **Dina Koota**.\n\n**1.2 Tara Koota and Tara Bala.** *Tara Bala* is the use of the same counting principle in **Muhurta and Panchanga-based timing**: it counts from a person's birth Nakshatra to the Nakshatra of a chosen day. The counting idea is shared; the context is different, and Tara Bala must not automatically inherit the scoring rules of marriage compatibility. (Lesson 8 compares them.)\n\n**1.3 What does Tara Koota examine?** (a) The first person's Janma Nakshatra is the reference. (b) The second person's Janma Nakshatra is counted from it. (c) The count is classified into one of nine Taras. (d) The same is repeated in the reverse direction. (e) The two results are considered together. **A relationship between two Nakshatras cannot be read from one direction only.**\n\n**1.4 Philosophical reading of the nine meanings.** Birth and personal nature; wealth and resources; obstacles and adversity; protection and well-being; resistance and opposition; accomplishment and progress; loss and obstruction; friendship and cooperation; deeper friendship and support. These are **traditional symbolic assessments, not predictions** of health, longevity, prosperity or marital success. Never present them as guaranteed outcomes.\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) Give the literal meaning of Tara. (b) Name the other name for Tara Koota. (c) Why is one direction not enough?\n\n### Lesson 2 – The nine Taras at a glance\nOpen the **Nine Taras** tab for one learning page per Tara. Here is the master table.\n| No. | Tara | Hindi | Traditional meaning | Common classification |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Janma | जन्म | Birth, identity | Neutral or context-dependent |\n| 2 | Sampat | संपत | Wealth, resources | Favourable |\n| 3 | Vipat | विपत | Adversity | Unfavourable |\n| 4 | Kshema | क्षेम | Welfare, protection | Favourable |\n| 5 | Pratyak / Pratyari | प्रत्यक / प्रत्यारी | Opposition | Unfavourable |\n| 6 | Sadhaka | साधक | Accomplishment | Favourable |\n| 7 | Naidhana / Vadha | नैधन / वध | Loss, obstruction | Unfavourable |\n| 8 | Mitra | मित्र | Friendship | Favourable |\n| 9 | Parama Mitra / Ati Mitra | परम मित्र / अति मित्र | Supreme friendship | Favourable |\n> **Note.** Janma Tara's *interpretative* status (neutral or mixed) differs from its treatment in some *numerical scoring* systems, where remainder 1 still earns points. Keep the two apart (Lesson 7).\n\n**The two names for some Taras.** Pratyak and Pratyari; Naidhana and Vadha; Parama Mitra and Ati Mitra: the same Tara under different names in different presentations.\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### Lesson 3 – The mathematics of Tara\n**3.1 27 Nakshatras, nine Taras.** Total Nakshatras = 27; Taras = 9; so each Tara occurs **three times**, and the cycle repeats after every nine positions. The 27 Nakshatras in order: 1 Ashwini, 2 Bharani, 3 Krittika, 4 Rohini, 5 Mrigashira, 6 Ardra, 7 Punarvasu, 8 Pushya, 9 Ashlesha, 10 Magha, 11 Purva Phalguni, 12 Uttara Phalguni, 13 Hasta, 14 Chitra, 15 Swati, 16 Vishakha, 17 Anuradha, 18 Jyeshtha, 19 Mula, 20 Purva Ashadha, 21 Uttara Ashadha, 22 Shravana, 23 Dhanishta, 24 Shatabhisha, 25 Purva Bhadrapada, 26 Uttara Bhadrapada, 27 Revati.\n\n**3.2 Grouping the Nakshatras by Tara, counted from Ashwini.**\n| Tara | First | Second | Third |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Janma | Ashwini | Magha | Mula |\n| Sampat | Bharani | Purva Phalguni | Purva Ashadha |\n| Vipat | Krittika | Uttara Phalguni | Uttara Ashadha |\n| Kshema | Rohini | Hasta | Shravana |\n| Pratyak | Mrigashira | Chitra | Dhanishta |\n| Sadhaka | Ardra | Swati | Shatabhisha |\n| Naidhana | Punarvasu | Vishakha | Purva Bhadrapada |\n| Mitra | Pushya | Anuradha | Uttara Bhadrapada |\n| Parama Mitra | Ashlesha | Jyeshtha | Revati |\n> **Important.** This grouping is **relative to the reference Nakshatra**, here Ashwini. If the reference is Rohini, Rohini itself becomes Janma, Mrigashira becomes Sampat, and so on. The Wheel tab lets you move the reference and watch the groups shift.\n\n**3.3 The formula.** Use **one-based inclusive counting**. Let B be the starting Nakshatra number, T the target number, 27 the cycle length.\n> **C = ((T − B + 27) MOD 27) + 1** (inclusive count) and **R = ((C − 1) MOD 9) + 1** (Tara number, 1 to 9).\nAlternative form: R = C MOD 9, **except** that a remainder of 0 is read as 9. Both forms always agree (the engine checks this for all 729 pairs).\n\n**3.4 Why divide by nine?** The 27 Nakshatras form three repeating cycles of nine. The remainder after dividing by nine identifies the Tara: counts 1, 10, 19 are Janma; 2, 11, 20 Sampat; 3, 12, 21 Vipat; 4, 13, 22 Kshema; 5, 14, 23 Pratyak; 6, 15, 24 Sadhaka; 7, 16, 25 Naidhana; 8, 17, 26 Mitra; 9, 18, 27 Parama Mitra.\n\n**3.5 Five rules to remember.** (1) Counting begins with the start Nakshatra as 1. (2) The start is included. (3) The target is included. (4) After Revati, counting returns to Ashwini. (5) A zero remainder is Tara 9, **never** \"Tara 0\". Do not use zero-based counting when you show your working.\n\n### Lesson 4 – Directional counting\n**4.1 Why two directions.** Let Person A's Nakshatra be the start and Person B's the target. Calculate A → B, then B → A. The first describes the Tara of B's Nakshatra seen from A's reference; the second describes the reverse. Never swap them and never report only one.\n\n**4.2 A property of the arithmetic (this corrects a common statement).** For two **different** Nakshatras the two counts **always add up to 29**, not 28. Proof: the forward count goes round part of the circle and the reverse count the rest, and both endpoints are counted in both, so C₁ + C₂ = 27 + 2 = 29. Since 29 leaves remainder 2 when divided by 9, the two Tara numbers always sum to 2 (mod 9). Consequently **both directions can never both be in {3, 5, 7}**. And if one direction is Janma (remainder 1), the other is Janma too. For the same Nakshatra, both counts are 1.\n\n**4.3 Forward example.** Rohini (4) → Anuradha (17): Rohini 1, Mrigashira 2, Ardra 3, Punarvasu 4, Pushya 5, Ashlesha 6, Magha 7, Purva Phalguni 8, Uttara Phalguni 9, Hasta 10, Chitra 11, Swati 12, Vishakha 13, Anuradha 14. Count 14; 14 = 1 × 9 + 5; remainder 5 = **Pratyak**.\n\n**4.4 Reverse example.** Anuradha (17) → Rohini (4): Anuradha 1, Jyeshtha 2, Mula 3, Purva Ashadha 4, Uttara Ashadha 5, Shravana 6, Dhanishta 7, Shatabhisha 8, Purva Bhadrapada 9, Uttara Bhadrapada 10, Revati 11, Ashwini 12, Bharani 13, Krittika 14, Rohini 15. Count 15; remainder 6 = **Sadhaka**. Check: 14 + 15 = 29 ✓.\n\n**4.5 Reading the pair.** Rohini → Anuradha = Pratyak; Anuradha → Rohini = Sadhaka. The results differ. Under the common three-point Ashtakoota convention, one unfavourable and one favourable direction give **1.5 out of 3**: a *conventional* scoring example, not a rule for every Dashakoota tradition.\n\n### Lesson 4b – Conventions and edge cases\n**Starting Nakshatra.** The mathematics does not know \"bride\" or \"groom\": the engine uses Person A and Person B. Role labels (Bride → Groom, Groom → Bride, Person A → Person B) are display choices; the profile may decide which direction is shown first or how points are assigned. The result of the arithmetic never depends on the label.\n\n**Inclusive versus exclusive counting.** Inclusive (standard): Ashwini = 1, Bharani = 2: count 2 = Sampat. Exclusive counting would start at 0 and give 1 = Janma, which is **wrong** for the traditional procedure. The calculator lets you toggle exclusive counting *for demonstration only*.\n\n**\"Reverse counting\" does not mean backwards.** It means swapping the reference and target Nakshatras and **continuing forward** through the normal sequence. See it on the wheel.\n\n**Repeated and special cases.**\n| Case | Example | Count | Remainder | Tara |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A. Identical Nakshatra | Rohini → Rohini | 1 (both ways) | 1 | Janma both ways |\n| B. Nine apart | Ashwini → Magha | 10 | 1 | Janma |\n| C. Eighteen apart | Ashwini → Mula | 19 | 1 | Janma |\n| D. Full cycle | Ashwini → Revati | 27 | 0, read as 9 | Parama Mitra |\nCase A needs nuance: identical Nakshatras may be treated in a particular way by some traditions. Do not label the pair favourable or unfavourable until you consult the selected profile.\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### Lesson 7 – Scoring conventions and school differences\n**7.1 Five different things.** Tara **classification** (which of nine); Tara **interpretation** (what it traditionally symbolises); Tara Koota **numerical scoring**; **regional Dashkoota** application; **Tara Bala** for Muhurta. Do not assume every regional Dashkoota system scores Tara as North Indian Ashtakoota does.\n\n**7.2 The common Ashtakoota three-point model (Profile A).** Total Tara Koota = 3 points; each direction contributes 1.5; remainders 3, 5 and 7 are unfavourable; other remainders are acceptable for scoring. Both acceptable = 3; one acceptable and one unfavourable = 1.5; neither acceptable = 0 *if a profile defines that result*.\n> **Mathematical qualification.** Because two different Nakshatras never both fall in {3, 5, 7} (Lesson 4), Profile A gives **3 or 1.5, never 0** for different Nakshatras. The software must not manufacture a zero merely because a generic table allows it.\n\n**7.3 Janma Tara: interpretation versus scoring.** Some teaching materials describe Janma as neutral or mixed; the common three-point method nevertheless treats remainder 1 as acceptable for points. The software keeps **two separate fields**: the *interpretative classification* and the *scoring eligibility*. Do not force them into one.\n\n**7.4 The four rule profiles.** These are **software configuration profiles for transparent comparison, not four established classical schools.**\n| Profile | What it does | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | Standard three-point model; Janma acceptable for scoring | Common convention; unverified |\n| B | Janma interpretative-caution model: Janma earns no points and is shown with a caution | Configuration for comparison; unverified |\n| C | User-defined regional profile (editable in the calculator) | User-defined |\n| D | Research / custom profile (editable in the calculator) | User-defined |\nEach profile stores: name, source reference, counting convention, Tara sequence, Janma treatment, favourable and unfavourable lists, directional scoring method, maximum score, notes, version and verification status. Editing a profile does not touch the calculation engine.\n\n**7.5 Where Profile A and Profile B differ.** Only for Janma. And Janma can occur **only in both directions at once**: the two Tara numbers always sum to 2 (mod 9), so if one is 1 the other is 1 as well. That happens for the same Nakshatra and for Nakshatras exactly nine or eighteen positions apart (such as Ashwini and Magha): 81 of the 729 ordered pairs. For those pairs Profile A scores **3** and Profile B scores **0**. Every other pair scores the same under A and B. That is the only way a zero can arise under B.\n\n**7.6 Interpretative differences among schools.** Each is either interpretative or mathematical; none has a verified source in this course.\n| Area | Differences | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| Janma Tara | Neutral versus acceptable for scoring | Interpretative |\n| Names | Pratyak or Pratyari; Naidhana or Vadha; Parama Mitra or Ati Mitra | Terminology |\n| Identical Janma Nakshatras | Special treatment in some traditions | Interpretative |\n| Weight of the two directions | Equal in the common model; others differ | Interpretative |\n| Numerical or qualitative | Some use points, some only a qualitative reading | Interpretative |\n| Integration | How Tara fits a larger Dashkoota or regional framework | Structural |\n| Counting | Inclusive (standard here); one-direction counting in some traditions (for example the Dina Porutham as commonly described) | Mathematical |\nUse the selector below to record the profile you are studying.\n\n**7.7 Tara Koota versus Tara Bala.**\n| Feature | Tara Koota | Tara Bala |\n|---|---|---|\n| Context | Compatibility assessment | Muhurta and timing |\n| Reference | One partner's Janma Nakshatra | The native's Janma Nakshatra |\n| Target | The other partner's Janma Nakshatra | The Nakshatra of the event day |\n| Counting | Both directions in common marriage matching | Birth star to the selected day's star |\n| Purpose | Relative Nakshatra compatibility | Traditional assessment of day suitability |\nTara Bala has its own reading (commonly, Vipat, Pratyak and Naidhana days are avoided for important work, and Janma is treated with caution; grade C, not verified here) and **must not inherit** marriage-matching scores.\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### Lesson 5 – Tara Dosha\n**5.1 What is Tara Dosha (तारा दोष)?** In traditional compatibility assessment, an unfavourable Tara result: commonly Vipat (3), Pratyak (5) or Naidhana (7) in one or both directions. The calculation is repeated in both directions because the two results can differ. **One unfavourable direction is not the same as two.** And Janma Tara needs special care (Lesson 7).\n\n**5.2 Common classification.** Janma: neutral, mixed or context-dependent. Sampat, Kshema, Sadhaka, Mitra, Parama Mitra: favourable. Vipat, Pratyak, Naidhana: unfavourable.\n\n**5.3 The three commonly unfavourable Taras.** *Vipat:* adversity, disruption, difficulty. *Pratyak:* resistance, opposition, obstruction. *Naidhana:* loss, destruction or impediment (also called Vadha). These are symbolic descriptions. **Never use them to frighten a student or client**, and never read Naidhana as a prediction of death.\n\n**5.4 Exceptions and mitigations.** No agreed, sourced cancellation of Tara Dosha was found for this course. Any exception category must follow the Day 12 rule: identified, with school, source and conditions, and **not applied** without them. A good score in another Koota does not cancel a Tara result.\n\n**5.5 Required caution (shown by the software).**\n> *Tara Koota is one component of traditional compatibility analysis. It should not independently determine marriage suitability or predict unavoidable life events.*\n\n## Examples\n\n### Worked examples (all calculated live by the engine)\nOpen each pair to see every Nakshatra counted. The scores use Profile A; switch profile in the Calculator to see the difference.\n{{texamples}}\n\n### Example comparison table (Profile A)\n| Pair | A → B | B → A | Common score |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ashwini – Bharani | Sampat | Parama Mitra | 3 |\n| Ashwini – Rohini | Kshema | Naidhana | 1.5 |\n| Rohini – Anuradha | Pratyak | Sadhaka | 1.5 |\n| Pushya – Hasta | Sadhaka | Pratyak | 1.5 |\n| Mula – Revati | Parama Mitra | Sampat | 3 |\n| Rohini – Rohini | Janma | Janma | 3 under the common convention; interpretation requires nuance |\n\n### Why Example 6 is shown separately\nFor the identical pair the counts are 1 and 1. Under Profile A the score is 3; under Profile B it is 0 (Janma earns no points); the *interpretation* in both is \"neutral or context-dependent\". That is the clearest demonstration of why Janma's interpretative status must not be confused with its scoring treatment.\n\n## Practice\n\n### Lesson 9 – Practical work\n**Guided steps.** (1) Learn the counts 1–9 and the Tara names by heart. (2) Do ten counts by hand before using the tools. (3) Always do both directions. (4) Verify with the calculator and read the matrix. (5) Do the exercises and the numerical test below. (6) Take the final assessment.\n\n### Numerical practice test (10 problems, both directions)\nFor each pair give the count and the Tara in both directions. Answers appear only after you submit.\n{{tnumeric}}\n\n### Written task\nIn your Learning Journal, write a six-line professional reading of one pair that (a) names the profile, (b) gives both counts and Taras, (c) states the score and the Dosha flag, (d) names the limits, (e) avoids deterministic claims.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What does Tara (तारा) mean?\nA: Star. In matching, a Nakshatra-counting relationship classified into nine Taras.\n\nQ: What is Dina Koota?\nA: Another name for Tara Koota in some marriage-matching traditions.\n\nQ: Name the nine Taras in order.\nA: Janma, Sampat, Vipat, Kshema, Pratyak, Sadhaka, Naidhana, Mitra, Parama Mitra.\n\nQ: Which counts belong to which Tara?\nA: Remainder = Tara number: counts 1, 10, 19 = Janma; 2, 11, 20 = Sampat; and so on to 9, 18, 27 = Parama Mitra.\n\nQ: What is Janma Tara (जन्म तारा)?\nA: Count 1, 10 or 19: the birth-star relationship; interpreted as neutral or context-dependent, but acceptable for scoring in the common three-point model.\n\nQ: What is Sampat Tara (संपत तारा)?\nA: Count 2, 11 or 20: wealth, resources; favourable.\n\nQ: What is Vipat Tara (विपत तारा)?\nA: Count 3, 12 or 21: adversity; commonly unfavourable.\n\nQ: What is Kshema Tara (क्षेम तारा)?\nA: Count 4, 13 or 22: welfare, protection; favourable.\n\nQ: What is Pratyak Tara (प्रत्यक तारा)?\nA: Count 5, 14 or 23 (also Pratyari): opposition; commonly unfavourable.\n\nQ: What is Sadhaka Tara (साधक तारा)?\nA: Count 6, 15 or 24: accomplishment; favourable.\n\nQ: What is Naidhana Tara (नैधन तारा)?\nA: Count 7, 16 or 25 (also Vadha): loss, obstruction; commonly unfavourable; never read as a prediction of death.\n\nQ: What is Mitra Tara (मित्र तारा)?\nA: Count 8, 17 or 26: friendship; favourable.\n\nQ: What is Parama Mitra Tara (परम मित्र तारा)?\nA: Count 9, 18 or 27 (also Ati Mitra): supreme friendship; favourable.\n\nQ: What is inclusive counting?\nA: The starting Nakshatra is 1 and the target is also counted: Ashwini to Bharani is 2.\n\nQ: What is the formula for the count?\nA: C = ((T − B + 27) MOD 27) + 1; Tara = ((C − 1) MOD 9) + 1.\n\nQ: What does \"reverse counting\" mean?\nA: Swap start and target and count forward again; it does not mean counting backwards through the zodiac.\n\nQ: What does a remainder of zero mean?\nA: Tara 9 (Parama Mitra), never \"Tara 0\": for example count 9, 18 or 27.\n\nQ: What happens when both partners have the same Nakshatra?\nA: Count 1 both ways: Janma in both directions; read with nuance and the chosen profile.\n\nQ: What is Tara Dosha (तारा दोष)?\nA: An unfavourable Tara, commonly Vipat, Pratyak or Naidhana, in one or both directions; not a marriage verdict.\n\nQ: What is the three-point Ashtakoota scoring?\nA: 1.5 per direction unless the Tara is 3, 5 or 7: both acceptable = 3, one = 1.5.\n\nQ: What do the two counts of a pair add up to?\nA: 29 for two different Nakshatras, so the pair never scores 0 under Profile A.\n\nQ: Tara Bala versus Tara Koota?\nA: Tara Bala times events from the native's birth star to the day's star; Tara Koota compares two people's birth stars in both directions.\n\nQ: What is the 27 × 27 matrix?\nA: All 729 forward Tara results, generated by the formula, with rows as the start and columns as the target.\n\nQ: Why keep Janma interpretation separate from scoring?\nA: Some sources call Janma neutral while common scoring still gives points; mixing the two hides the difference.\n\nQ: What are rule profiles?\nA: Software configurations (A, B, C, D) for transparent comparison; not four established classical schools.\n\nQ: What are the limits of Tara scoring?\nA: It is one factor of the Moon-based matching; it never decides suitability by itself and its tables are not source-verified.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: Tara (तारा) literally means:\nA) Planet\nB) Star\nC) Sign\nD) Moon\nANS: B\nWHY: Tara means star; here the relationship between two birth stars.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Another name for Tara Koota in some traditions is:\nA) Nadi Koota\nB) Dina Koota\nC) Gana Koota\nD) Yoni Koota\nANS: B\nWHY: Dina Koota is the alternative name; its counting and scoring conventions can differ.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many Taras are there?\nA) Seven\nB) Eight\nC) Nine\nD) Twelve\nANS: C\nWHY: Janma, Sampat, Vipat, Kshema, Pratyak, Sadhaka, Naidhana, Mitra and Parama Mitra.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which three Taras are commonly treated as unfavourable?\nA) Sampat, Kshema, Mitra\nB) Vipat, Pratyak, Naidhana\nC) Janma, Sadhaka, Parama Mitra\nD) Janma, Sampat, Vipat\nANS: B\nWHY: Remainders 3, 5 and 7 are the commonly unfavourable ones.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Tara Bala differs from Tara Koota mainly because:\nA) It has a different arithmetic\nB) It is used for timing events rather than comparing two people\nC) It uses the ascendant\nD) It has twelve Taras\nANS: B\nWHY: The counting idea is shared; the context and reading differ.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Why must Janma's interpretation and scoring be kept separate?\nA) They are always identical\nB) Some sources call it neutral while scoring tables still give it points\nC) Janma has no Nakshatra\nD) Janma is always a Dosha\nANS: B\nWHY: Interpretative status and scoring eligibility are different fields.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Why is Tara calculated in both directions?\nA) Tradition is arbitrary\nB) The two directional counts differ, so one direction does not describe the pair\nC) Both counts are always equal\nD) To double the score\nANS: B\nWHY: For different Nakshatras the counts are different (they add up to 29).\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Mitra means:\nA) Enemy\nB) Friend\nC) Teacher\nD) Wealth\nANS: B\nWHY: Mitra is friend; Parama Mitra is supreme friend.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Sadhaka is associated with:\nA) Loss\nB) Accomplishment\nC) Birth\nD) Adversity\nANS: B\nWHY: Sadhaka means one who accomplishes.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Tara Dosha should be treated as:\nA) A final marriage verdict\nB) One traditional indicator that never decides suitability by itself\nC) A medical finding\nD) A guarantee of misfortune\nANS: B\nWHY: The required caution says it must not independently determine suitability.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The inclusive count from Rohini (4) to Anuradha (17) is:\nA) 12\nB) 13\nC) 14\nD) 15\nANS: C\nWHY: ((17 − 4 + 27) MOD 27) + 1 = 14.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A count of 14 gives which Tara?\nA) Kshema\nB) Pratyak\nC) Sadhaka\nD) Mitra\nANS: B\nWHY: 14 = 9 + 5; remainder 5 is Pratyak.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A remainder of 0 is read as:\nA) Tara 0\nB) Tara 1\nC) Tara 9\nD) An error\nANS: C\nWHY: Count 9, 18 or 27 is Parama Mitra, Tara 9.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Ashwini → Revati (count 27) is:\nA) Janma\nB) Mitra\nC) Parama Mitra\nD) Naidhana\nANS: C\nWHY: 27 MOD 9 = 0, read as 9.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Ashwini → Magha (count 10) is:\nA) Sampat\nB) Janma\nC) Vipat\nD) Kshema\nANS: B\nWHY: 10 MOD 9 = 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Hasta (13) → Pushya (8) has count:\nA) 6\nB) 22\nC) 23\nD) 24\nANS: C\nWHY: ((8 − 13 + 27) MOD 27) + 1 = 23; 23 MOD 9 = 5, Pratyak.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: For two different Nakshatras the two inclusive counts add up to:\nA) 27\nB) 28\nC) 29\nD) 30\nANS: C\nWHY: The two counts cover the circle once and both endpoints twice.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Mula (19) → Revati (27) gives:\nA) Count 9, Parama Mitra\nB) Count 8, Mitra\nC) Count 10, Janma\nD) Count 20, Sampat\nANS: A\nWHY: ((27 − 19 + 27) MOD 27) + 1 = 9; remainder 0 is Tara 9.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Two people with the same Nakshatra have forward and reverse counts of:\nA) 0 and 0\nB) 1 and 1\nC) 9 and 9\nD) 27 and 27\nANS: B\nWHY: The start is counted as 1 in both directions: Janma both ways.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Exclusive counting for Ashwini → Bharani would give:\nA) Sampat (correct)\nB) Janma (wrong, because the start is not counted)\nC) Vipat\nD) Kshema\nANS: B\nWHY: Exclusive counting gives 1 instead of 2; it is not the standard method.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Profile A score for Ashwini and Rohini (Kshema, Naidhana) is:\nA) 0\nB) 1.5\nC) 3\nD) 4.5\nANS: B\nWHY: One acceptable direction (1.5) and one unfavourable (0).\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Can Profile A give 0 for two different Nakshatras?\nA) Yes, often\nB) No, because both directions can never both be in {3, 5, 7}\nC) Yes, for every Vipat\nD) Only for the same Nakshatra\nANS: B\nWHY: The two Tara numbers always sum to 2 mod 9.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Under Profile B (Janma earns no points), a pair scores 0 when:\nA) Any direction is Vipat\nB) Both directions are Janma, for example the same Nakshatra or Ashwini and Magha\nC) Never\nD) Whenever the counts are equal\nANS: B\nWHY: Only both-Janma pairs have no point-earning direction.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Changing the scoring profile changes:\nA) The Tara classification\nB) The count\nC) Only scoring and flags, never the Tara classification\nD) The Nakshatra\nANS: C\nWHY: The calculation engine is independent of profiles.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which statement is correct?\nA) Tara Bala automatically uses marriage-matching scores\nB) Tara Bala has its own interpretative context and does not inherit those scores\nC) Tara Bala has no counting\nD) Tara Bala uses 12 Taras\nANS: B\nWHY: The two share counting but not their scoring rules.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. Why must Tara be read in both directions?\nMA: The forward and reverse counts of two different Nakshatras are different (they add up to 29), so each direction gives its own Tara; reading only one would describe only half of the relationship.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Why must Janma's interpretation be separated from its scoring eligibility?\nMA: Some explanations call Janma neutral or mixed while common scoring tables still award points for remainder 1. If the two are merged, the difference between schools is hidden and the report may misstate what was done.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual 3. State two limitations of Tara scoring.\nMA: The tables and profiles are not source-verified, and Tara is one factor of the Moon-based matching: it cannot decide suitability alone or predict life events.\nLVL: Basic\n\n## References\n### Source status (Day 4)\nNo classical verse or chapter has been verified for any Tara rule in this module.\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nine Tara names and the remainder rule | C | Widely stated in manuals and web explanations; no verse verified |\n| Inclusive counting, both directions, zero read as 9 | C/D | Commonly stated for Ashtakoota |\n| Remainders 3, 5, 7 unfavourable; 1.5 per direction | C/D | Common published three-point model |\n| Janma as neutral or mixed in interpretation | C/E | Varies by presentation |\n| Tara Koota called Dina Koota | C | Terminology varies; the Tamil Dina Porutham is commonly described as a one-direction count and is a separate profile |\n| Tara Bala in Muhurta | C | Commonly stated; reading not verified here |\n| Counts add up to 29 and the minimum score under Profile A | n/a | Arithmetic consequence |\n| Profiles A–D | n/a | Software configurations, not classical schools |\n\n### Corrections to supplied material\n| Supplied | Correct |\n|---|---|\n| \"The sum of the two directional counts is 28\" | It is **29** (for example 14 + 15, 2 + 27) |\n| Grouping of Nakshatras by Tara (positions 1–27) | Correct only relative to the chosen reference Nakshatra (here Ashwini); it shifts with the reference |\n\n### Research and source policy\nOnline explanatory pages may be used only as secondary support. For every classical passage the course would cite it must record: text name, author or attribution, chapter, verse, edition or translator, the exact rule supported, and confidence of verification. Where the exact source cannot be verified, the statement is labelled **\"Traditional practice: source attribution requires verification.\"** Leads still to be checked: *Muhurta Chintamani* (the marriage and Tara passages), *Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra* (edition, chapter and verse), regional matching manuals. No quotation, verse or chapter number is given here.\n\n## Nine Taras\n### Lesson 2 (interactive) – one learning page for each Tara\nChoose a Tara. Choose a reference Nakshatra to see which three Nakshatras fall in that Tara.\n{{ttaras}}\n\n## Wheel and Counting\n### Lessons 3 and 4 (interactive) – the 27-Nakshatra wheel, the nine-Tara wheel and the counting animation\nChoose a reference Nakshatra and watch the nine Tara groups; or choose two Nakshatras and play the forward or reverse count step by step.\n{{twheel}}\n\n## Matrix\n### The complete 27 × 27 Tara matrix\nAll 729 cells are generated by the formula (nothing is typed in). Rows are the start Nakshatra, columns the target. Search a Nakshatra, tap a cell for the full working, switch to the both-directions view, export CSV or print.\n{{tmatrix}}\n\n## Calculator\n### Tara Compatibility Calculator\nChoose two Nakshatras and a rule profile. The calculation is always shown in full; no profile ever changes the Tara itself.\n{{tcalc}}\n\n## Exercises\n### Lessons 9 – five practical exercises\nIdentify the Tara; reverse counting; identify the Dosha; matrix reading; a case study.\n{{texercise}}\n\n## Final Assessment\n### Lesson 10 – the 30-question final assessment\n10 theory, 10 calculation, 5 reverse-counting and 5 interpretation questions. Pass mark 70%. A failed attempt never erases your completed lessons.\n{{tfinal}}\n\n## Common Mistakes\nTen mistakes to recognise: the incorrect method, the corrected method and a worked example.\n| # | Mistake | Correct method | Worked example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Forgetting to count the start as 1 | Start = 1 | Ashwini → Bharani is 2, not 1 |\n| 2 | Counting exclusively | Inclusive | Exclusive would call Sampat \"Janma\" |\n| 3 | Counting backwards in the zodiac for \"reverse\" | Swap start and target; keep counting forward | Anuradha → Rohini is 15 (forward, wrapping), not 13 |\n| 4 | Forgetting the second direction | Always calculate both | Rohini–Anuradha: Pratyak and Sadhaka |\n| 5 | Treating remainder 0 as Tara 0 | Read 0 as 9 | Count 27 is Parama Mitra |\n| 6 | Confusing the Tara number with the Nakshatra number | They are different | Pushya is Nakshatra 8; a count of 8 is Mitra |\n| 7 | Confusing Tara Koota with Tara Bala | Different contexts | Koota compares two people; Bala times an event |\n| 8 | Treating Janma as automatically favourable or unfavourable | Interpretation and scoring are separate | Same Nakshatra: Janma both ways; Profile A scores 3, Profile B scores 0 |\n| 9 | Applying Ashtakoota scoring to another regional system unverified | Check that profile | Dina Porutham is described as a one-direction count |\n| 10 | Treating Tara Dosha as a marriage verdict | It is one indicator | One unfavourable direction in the Ashwini–Rohini pair (1.5 of 3) |\n\n## Instructor Guide\n*For teachers and self-study students.*\n\n### Suggested time allocation (180 minutes)\n| Section | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Foundation and nine Taras | 35 |\n| Mathematical method | 30 |\n| Forward and reverse counting | 30 |\n| Tara Dosha and scoring variations | 25 |\n| Matrix and calculator practice | 35 |\n| Assessment and revision | 25 |\n\n### Teaching sequence\n1. Open with \"star counted from star\". 2. Draw the circle of 27 and mark nine groups. 3. Count Ashwini → Bharani on the board both ways. 4. Prove 29. 5. Introduce the nine meanings carefully. 6. Compare profiles A and B. 7. Matrix and calculator. 8. Assessment.\n\n### Core definitions\nInclusive count; remainder; Tara number; Janma; Dosha flag; profile; Tara Koota versus Tara Bala.\n\n### Common student mistakes\nSee the Common Mistakes tab.\n\n### Calculation demonstrations\nRohini → Anuradha and back; Ashwini → Revati (zero remainder); Rohini → Rohini; Ashwini → Magha.\n\n### Discussion questions\n\"Why do the counts add to 29?\" \"If Janma is neutral, why does the scoring table give it points?\" \"What would you tell a client whose pair shows Naidhana in one direction?\"\n\n### Advanced interpretation notes\nDirectional asymmetry; the effect of the reference Nakshatra on the grouping; why a profile can change points but never the Tara.\n\n### Case-study instructions\nUse Exercise 5: students write a reading that names the profile, both counts and Taras, the score, the limits and avoids certainty.\n\n### Assessment answer key\nFinal assessment questions are generated and marked by the engine; the theory questions carry explanations after submission.\n\n## Validation\n### Technical validation report\nAutomatic tests of the Tara engine. Every row runs when this page opens.\n{{ttests}}\n","05":"# Day 5 – Yoni Koota\n\n## Learn\n\n### Welcome to Day 5\n**Module 5: Yoni Koota (योनि कूट) – the animal-symbol classification of the Nakshatras.** Day 5 of 14 · Foundation to Advanced · 180–210 minutes (self-paced) · Instructor: Anoopam Shukla.\n\nToday you learn Yoni as a **table-driven classification**, not as a list of animals to memorise. You will find the Yoni of each Nakshatra, read a score from a 14 × 14 matrix (bride = row, groom = column), apply the separate rule for sworn-enemy pairs, and see exactly where the published tables differ.\n\n> **Central teaching principle.** Identify the Nakshatra first, then its Yoni, then the matrix cell, then the Dosha flag, and only last the interpretation. The animal is a **symbolic classification label**; it is never a description of a person.\n\n### Required teaching note\nYoni is treated here strictly as a **traditional symbolic classification** used in Nakshatra compatibility. It is not a biological, medical or psychological assessment. Words such as \"male\" and \"female\" attached to a Nakshatra are **designations inside the traditional table**; they do not describe anyone's sex or gender identity. Yoni must not be used to make statements about sexual conduct, intimacy, health, fertility, children or the outcome of a marriage. It is one traditional indicator among many and can never decide suitability alone.\n\n### The one idea to keep in mind all day\nFour things must stay separate:\n1. **The allocation:** which Yoni a Nakshatra carries (a lookup table).\n2. **The relationship:** how two Yonis relate (same, friendly, neutral, unfriendly, sworn enemy).\n3. **The scoring:** how many of the 4 points a chosen profile gives (a matrix).\n4. **The Dosha flag:** whether the pair is a sworn-enemy pair. This comes from the seven-pair list, **never** from the numbers in the matrix.\n\n### What you will be able to do by the end of the day\n1. Explain what Yoni Koota (योनि कूट) is and where it sits among the eight Ashtakoota factors (4 of 36 points).\n2. Name the fourteen Yonis in Sanskrit, Hindi and English.\n3. State the Yoni of each of the 27 Nakshatras, and which Yoni has only one Nakshatra.\n4. Explain the traditional male/female designation of a Nakshatra without confusing it with a person's sex.\n5. Name the seven sworn-enemy pairs.\n6. Read a score from the 14 × 14 matrix with bride as row and groom as column.\n7. Explain why the same pair can score differently in the two directions under Profile A.\n8. Distinguish the scoring profiles A, A0, B, C, D and E and say what differs.\n9. Explain Yoni Dosha and its limits.\n10. Use the calculator, the matrix and the comparison tool.\n11. Complete five exercises, fifteen numerical questions and the 30-question final assessment.\n12. Write a responsible professional interpretation that never over-claims.\n\n### Your learning route (twelve stages)\nProgress → Introduction → Fourteen Yonis → Nakshatra Allocation → Animal Symbolism → Friendship and Enmity → Scoring → Rule Variations → Yoni Dosha → Calculator → Matrix → Practical → Assessment. Tick each stage when you finish it; the tracker remembers where you stopped.\n{{ydash}}\n\n### Prerequisites and quick revision\nYou should already know: the 27 Nakshatras in order and their numbers; how to find a Janma Nakshatra from the Moon's sidereal longitude; the difference between Nakshatra, Rashi and Pada; and the Ashtakoota point table. If any of these is shaky, revise Days 1 and 2 first. Tap a Nakshatra below to see its number, longitude range, lord and Yoni.\n{{yrevise}}\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### Lesson 1 – What Yoni means\n**Yoni (योनि)** is a Sanskrit word with several meanings. In Koota Milan it is the name of a classification: each Nakshatra is assigned one of fourteen **animal symbols**, and two people's animal symbols are compared. The comparison asks, in traditional language, how well two natures \"fit\". Read literally the question is symbolic, not biological.\n\n| Item | Statement |\n|---|---|\n| Koota name | Yoni Koota (योनि कूट) |\n| Points | 4 of the 36 Ashtakoota points |\n| Input | The Janma Nakshatra (the Nakshatra of the natal Moon) of each person |\n| Output | A relationship category, a score from 0 to 4, and a sworn-enemy flag |\n| Direction | Bride's Yoni and groom's Yoni are looked up separately; under Profile A the score depends on which one is the row |\n\n### Where Yoni sits in Ashtakoota\nThe eight factors in the usual order are Varna 1, Vashya 2, Tara 3, **Yoni 4**, Graha Maitri 5, Gana 6, Bhakoot 7, Nadi 8: total 36. Yoni therefore carries about 11% of the total. It is a Nakshatra-based factor (like Tara, Gana and Nadi), not a Rashi-based one.\n\n### Why animals?\nTraditional teaching uses animals as a compact symbolic vocabulary: each animal stands for a cluster of qualities (for example the elephant for steadiness, the monkey for activity). Two animals that are natural opponents (cat and rat, dog and deer) stand for a pair of qualities that are said to clash. The course states these as **illustrative teaching themes only**. They are metaphors for memory, not descriptions of how a person behaves, and they must never be said to a client as a personality reading.\n\n### Symbolic, not biological\nThree misunderstandings are common and all are wrong:\n1. \"Yoni describes physical or sexual compatibility.\" **It does not.** The word Yoni in this context is a classification label.\n2. \"A person with the Dog Yoni behaves like a dog.\" **No.** The label belongs to the Nakshatra.\n3. \"A zero score means the marriage will fail.\" **No.** It is a traditional indicator, read with every other factor and both full horoscopes.\n\n### Traditional male and female designation\nIn the usual allocation table each of the 27 Nakshatras is marked **male (M)** or **female (F)**; for thirteen Yonis the two Nakshatras sharing a Yoni are marked one M and one F, which is why the table is often called a \"pairing\" of Nakshatras. This marker is a **field of the traditional table**. It is not a person's sex. Day 5 shows it because some regional conventions use it for the same-Yoni case (Profile D), and because you will meet it in other books. The Mongoose Yoni has only Uttara Ashadha in the 27-Nakshatra system, so it has one designation and no pair.\n\n### Three views of the same fact\n| View | Question | Where you see it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Allocation | Which animal does this Nakshatra carry? | Nakshatra Allocation tab |\n| Relationship | Are the two animals same, friendly, neutral, unfriendly or sworn enemies? | Matrix tab |\n| Score | How many points does this profile give? | Calculator tab |\n\n### Source status in brief\nThe allocation of Nakshatras to Yonis and the seven enemy pairs are widely published and agree across common manuals, but **no classical verse or chapter has been verified in this module**. The numbers inside the matrix are the weakest part: published tables differ. See the References tab and the Rule Variations lessons.\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### Lesson 2 – The fourteen Yonis\n| # | Sanskrit | Hindi | English |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwa | अश्व | Horse |\n| 2 | Gaja | गज | Elephant |\n| 3 | Mesha | मेष | Sheep |\n| 4 | Sarpa | सर्प | Serpent |\n| 5 | Shwana | श्वान | Dog |\n| 6 | Marjara | मार्जार | Cat |\n| 7 | Mushaka | मूषक | Rat |\n| 8 | Gau | गौ | Cow |\n| 9 | Mahisha | महिष | Buffalo |\n| 10 | Vyaghra | व्याघ्र | Tiger |\n| 11 | Mriga | मृग | Deer |\n| 12 | Vanara | वानर | Monkey |\n| 13 | Nakula | नकुल | Mongoose |\n| 14 | Simha | सिंह | Lion |\n\nSome books translate Mesha as \"ram\" or \"goat\", and Mriga as \"antelope\". The English label is a convention; the Sanskrit name identifies the Yoni.\n\n### Lesson 3 – Allocation of Nakshatras to Yonis (the 27-Nakshatra table)\n| Yoni | Nakshatras (designation) |\n|---|---|\n| 1 Horse | Ashwini (M), Shatabhisha (F) |\n| 2 Elephant | Bharani (M), Revati (F) |\n| 3 Sheep | Pushya (M), Krittika (F) |\n| 4 Serpent | Rohini (M), Mrigashira (F) |\n| 5 Dog | Mula (M), Ardra (F) |\n| 6 Cat | Ashlesha (M), Punarvasu (F) |\n| 7 Rat | Magha (M), Purva Phalguni (F) |\n| 8 Cow | Uttara Phalguni (M), Uttara Bhadrapada (F) |\n| 9 Buffalo | Swati (M), Hasta (F) |\n| 10 Tiger | Vishakha (M), Chitra (F) |\n| 11 Deer | Jyeshtha (M), Anuradha (F) |\n| 12 Monkey | Purva Ashadha (M), Shravana (F) |\n| 13 Mongoose | Uttara Ashadha only |\n| 14 Lion | Purva Bhadrapada (M), Dhanishta (F) |\n\nThirteen Yonis have two Nakshatras each (26) and the Mongoose has one: 27 in all. The complete, filterable table with the source note for every row is in the Nakshatra Allocation tab.\n\n### Lesson 4 – The matrix and its orientation\nThe Yoni score is read from a **14 × 14 matrix**. In this course the convention is:\n\n> **Bride = row, groom = column.** Find the bride's Yoni down the left side and the groom's Yoni along the top.\n\nOrientation matters because the Profile A matrix is **asymmetric** in two places. If a different book places the groom in the rows, its numbers must be read transposed. Always state which orientation a table uses.\n\n### Lesson 5 – The five relationship levels\n| Level | Symbol | Score (Profile A) | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Same Yoni | ★ | 4 | Both people carry the same animal |\n| Friendly | ✓ | 3 | A traditionally friendly pair |\n| Neutral | ○ | 2 | No marked friendship or opposition |\n| Unfriendly | △ | 1 | A traditionally poor fit, but not a sworn-enemy pair |\n| Sworn enemy | ✗ | 0 | One of the seven enemy pairs |\n\nThe category is a plain-language label; colour is always paired with the symbol and the name so nothing depends on colour alone.\n\n### Lesson 6 – The seven sworn-enemy (Vaira) pairs\n| # | Pair | Score both ways |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Horse – Buffalo | 0 |\n| 2 | Elephant – Lion | 0 |\n| 3 | Sheep – Monkey | 0 |\n| 4 | Serpent – Mongoose | 0 |\n| 5 | Dog – Deer | 0 |\n| 6 | Cat – Rat | 0 |\n| 7 | Cow – Tiger | 0 |\n\nEach Yoni has exactly one enemy, so seven pairs cover all fourteen Yonis. The list is symmetric and every common table scores these pairs 0 in both directions.\n\n### Lesson 7 – Yoni Dosha\n**Yoni Dosha** is, in this course, the flag raised when the two Yonis form a sworn-enemy pair. The flag is **derived from the seven-pair list**, not from the number 0 in a matrix, so editing a custom matrix can never remove it. A score of 1 (unfriendly) is a low score but is **not** a Dosha. Some books define Dosha differently; where they do the course will say so under Different Schools.\n\n> **Reading rule.** A flagged pair is a traditional incompatibility indicator. Name it, name the profile, and say what else must be looked at. Do not say that the marriage will fail, and do not make claims about intimacy, health or children.\n\n### Lesson 8 – The calculation procedure\n1. Identify each person's Janma Nakshatra (Day 2).\n2. Look up each Nakshatra's Yoni in the allocation table.\n3. State the profile and the orientation (bride row, groom column).\n4. Read the matrix cell: row = bride's Yoni, column = groom's Yoni.\n5. Check the seven-pair list for the Dosha flag.\n6. Record the category and the score (0–4).\n7. Read the reversed cell as well, and note whether it differs.\n8. Interpret: one indicator, with its limits.\n\n### Lesson 9 – The complete Profile A matrix (bride row, groom column)\nOrder of rows and columns: Horse, Elephant, Sheep, Serpent, Dog, Cat, Rat, Cow, Buffalo, Tiger, Deer, Monkey, Mongoose, Lion.\n\n| Bride \\ Groom | Hor | Ele | She | Ser | Dog | Cat | Rat | Cow | Buf | Tig | Dee | Mon | Mgs | Lio |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Horse | 4 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 3 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n| Elephant | 2 | 4 | 3 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 0 |\n| Sheep | 2 | 3 | 4 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 3 | 3 | 1 | 2 | 0 | 3 | 1 |\n| Serpent | 3 | 3 | 2 | 4 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n| Dog | 2 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 4 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 2 | 1 | 1 |\n| Cat | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 4 | 0 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 3 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n| Rat | 2 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 4 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 2 |\n| Cow | 1 | 2 | 3 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 4 | 3 | 0 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 1 |\n| Buffalo | 0 | 3 | 3 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 |\n| Tiger | 1 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 0 | 1 | 4 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 1 |\n| Deer | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 0 | 3 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 1 | 4 | 2 | 2 | 1 |\n| Monkey | 3 | 3 | 0 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 4 | 3 | 2 |\n| Mongoose | 2 | 2 | 3 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 2 |\n| Lion | 1 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 4 |\n\nThis matrix is a software configuration called **Profile A**, supplied for the course and labelled a \"Maitreya-style\" reference matrix. **The attribution is unverified.** It is symmetric except for two pairs: Horse/Deer and Buffalo/Lion (see Different Schools).\n\n### Lesson 10 – The numerical scale and its consequences\nScores run from 0 to 4. A score of 0 is reached only through a sworn-enemy pair, 4 only through a same-Yoni pair. When Yoni is added to the other seven Kootas the Ashtakoota total changes by at most 4 points; Day 14 shows the five full worked pairs.\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### Why the tables differ\nThe allocation of Nakshatras to Yonis and the seven enemy pairs are stable across common manuals. The **intermediate scores** (the 1, 2 and 3 cells) are not standardised: published tables differ in a few cells, and some regional systems score by category instead of by cell. The course therefore keeps several **rule profiles** side by side. They are software configurations, not claimed classical schools, and they are **never averaged or merged**.\n\n### The six profiles\n| Profile | Method | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | The supplied 14 × 14 \"Maitreya-style\" matrix; directional (bride row, groom column) | Default. Attribution unverified |\n| **A0** | The earlier symmetric table used in previous drafts of this course | Variant; differs from A in exactly two pairs |\n| **B** | Five-level simplified model derived from the A0 classification (same 4, friendly 3, neutral 2, unfriendly 1, enemy 0) | Provisional, pending researcher approval |\n| **C** | Three-level model: same Yoni 4, enemy 0, every other pair 2 | Simplified teaching model |\n| **D** | Profile A with a sex-aware same-Yoni rule: complementary designations (M with F) 4, identical designation 3 | Regional-style variant; use only where that convention is intended |\n| **E** | Custom 14 × 14 matrix that you edit and save in your browser | Your own regional table |\n\n### Exactly where Profile A and Profile A0 differ\n| Cell | Profile A | Profile A0 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Horse (bride) × Deer (groom) | 3 | 3 |\n| Deer (bride) × Horse (groom) | **1** | **3** |\n| Buffalo (bride) × Lion (groom) | 1 | 1 |\n| Lion (bride) × Buffalo (groom) | **2** | **1** |\n\nOnly these two cells differ, so Profile A is asymmetric in exactly two pairs. That means Horse–Deer and Buffalo–Lion give a **different score depending on who is the bride**. Compare with a symmetric table, where it would not matter.\n\n### What an asymmetric cell does and does not mean\nA directional difference is a feature of the **scoring table**. It is not proof that the traditional friendship or enmity itself is one-sided, and it is not a statement about either person. Never transpose or \"symmetrise\" a directional matrix to make it look neater.\n\n### Same-sex and non-traditional pairings\nThe row/column convention assumes the traditional \"bride and groom\" framing of the source tables. For any other pairing, label the two people **Person A (row)** and **Person B (column)**, state the orientation, and show both directions. The calculator uses these neutral labels in its output.\n\n### Other regional systems\nSome South-Indian and other regional systems use a similar animal idea with their own tables and their own point counts. Their rules are **not** part of this course unless verified; do not apply an Ashtakoota table to a regional system without checking that system's profile. Each regional claim here is marked \"requires further source verification\".\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### Lesson 11 – Special cases\n1. **Same Nakshatra for both people.** The Yoni is the same, so the pair scores 4 (Profiles A, A0, B, C), or follows the Profile D sex-aware rule. Under Profile D a pair with the same Nakshatra has identical designations and so scores 3.\n2. **Same Yoni, different Nakshatra.** Example: Ashwini and Shatabhisha are both Horse and score 4. Under Profile D they have complementary designations (M with F) and also score 4.\n3. **A sworn-enemy pair always flags**, whichever matrix is selected, even a custom one containing a different number.\n4. **Score 1 is not Dosha.** Unfriendly pairs such as Sheep–Tiger (Krittika–Chitra) score 1 but are not flagged.\n5. **The Mongoose Yoni** has only Uttara Ashadha. Two Uttara Ashadha people are the only pair with a Mongoose × Mongoose cell in the 27-Nakshatra system.\n6. **Abhijit (the 28th Nakshatra).** Some systems add Abhijit between Uttara Ashadha and Shravana. If you enable the Abhijit variant, Abhijit is given the Mongoose Yoni with the designation **not verified**, because sources disagree. The 28-Nakshatra mode is explicit and never mixed silently with the 27-Nakshatra table.\n7. **Cancellation rules.** Some traditions describe conditions under which a Yoni defect is considered reduced. None is verified for this course, so none is applied by the engine. If a client cites one, record it as \"requires further source verification\".\n\n### Professional limits\nNever state a medical, sexual, fertility, financial or marriage-outcome prediction from Yoni. Never present a Yoni score as the \"compatibility\" of two people; it is one traditional indicator for two Moon-Nakshatras. Where a book gives a Yoni remedy, record it as a traditional practice, not as advice.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Ten worked pairs (Profile A)\nChoose a profile to see how each example changes. Open any example for the step-by-step working; asymmetric pairs show the reverse direction explicitly.\n{{yexamples}}\n\n### Worked example 1 – Same Yoni\nBride Ashwini, groom Shatabhisha. Ashwini is Horse (M), Shatabhisha is Horse (F). Row Horse, column Horse = 4. Same Yoni, no Dosha. Reverse also 4.\n\n### Worked example 2 – An asymmetric pair\nBride Ashwini (Horse), groom Anuradha (Deer). Row Horse, column Deer = **3** (friendly). Reverse: Deer row, Horse column = **1**. A different book that scores symmetrically (Profile A0) gives 3 both ways. Reading only the first number would hide a real difference between tables.\n\n### Worked example 3 – A sworn-enemy pair\nBride Ardra (Dog), groom Anuradha (Deer). Dog and Deer are the fifth enemy pair. Row Dog, column Deer = 0; Dosha flag raised. The professional sentence: \"Under Profile A the Yoni factor scores 0 of 4 and is a sworn-enemy pair, a traditional incompatibility indicator; it is read together with all the other factors.\"\n\n### Worked example 4 – A neutral pair\nBride Bharani (Elephant), groom Uttara Phalguni (Cow). Row Elephant, column Cow = 2. Neutral, no Dosha.\n\n## Practice\n\n### Fifteen numerical questions (Profile A)\nFor each pair give the two Yonis, the score and the category. The answers appear only after you submit. Use the allocation table if you need it, and aim to need it less each time.\n{{ynumeric}}\n\n### Study route\n1. Learn the fourteen Yonis in order first, with their enemy partner. 2. Learn the allocation as pairs of Nakshatras (Ashwini and Shatabhisha, Bharani and Revati and so on). 3. Practise reading matrix cells until the row–column order is automatic. 4. Practise the reverse cell for the two asymmetric pairs.\n\n### Self-check before the flashcards\nWithout looking: what is Rohini's Yoni? Which Yoni has one Nakshatra? What is Dog's enemy? What does bride = row mean? What is the Dosha flag derived from?\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What is Yoni Koota (योनि कूट)?\nA: The Koota that compares the animal-symbol classifications of the two Janma Nakshatras; 4 of the 36 Ashtakoota points. It is a symbolic classification, not a biological assessment.\n\nQ: How many Yonis are there?\nA: Fourteen.\n\nQ: Name the fourteen Yonis in order.\nA: Horse, Elephant, Sheep, Serpent, Dog, Cat, Rat, Cow, Buffalo, Tiger, Deer, Monkey, Mongoose, Lion (Ashwa, Gaja, Mesha, Sarpa, Shwana, Marjara, Mushaka, Gau, Mahisha, Vyaghra, Mriga, Vanara, Nakula, Simha).\n\nQ: How many points does Yoni carry?\nA: 4 of the 36 Ashtakoota points.\n\nQ: Which input does Yoni use?\nA: The Janma Nakshatra of each person.\n\nQ: Which Yoni has only one Nakshatra in the 27-Nakshatra system?\nA: Mongoose (Nakula), with Uttara Ashadha.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Horse?\nA: Ashwini and Shatabhisha.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Elephant?\nA: Bharani and Revati.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Sheep?\nA: Krittika and Pushya.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Serpent?\nA: Rohini and Mrigashira.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Dog?\nA: Ardra and Mula.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Cat?\nA: Punarvasu and Ashlesha.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Rat?\nA: Magha and Purva Phalguni.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Cow?\nA: Uttara Phalguni and Uttara Bhadrapada.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Buffalo?\nA: Swati and Hasta.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Tiger?\nA: Vishakha and Chitra.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Deer?\nA: Anuradha and Jyeshtha.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Monkey?\nA: Purva Ashadha and Shravana.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Lion?\nA: Dhanishta and Purva Bhadrapada.\n\nQ: Name the seven sworn-enemy pairs.\nA: Horse–Buffalo, Elephant–Lion, Sheep–Monkey, Serpent–Mongoose, Dog–Deer, Cat–Rat, Cow–Tiger.\n\nQ: What is the enemy of the Serpent?\nA: The Mongoose.\n\nQ: What does the male/female marker on a Nakshatra mean?\nA: It is a field of the traditional allocation table. It is not a person's sex or gender identity.\n\nQ: In this course, which axis is the bride and which the groom?\nA: Bride = row, groom = column.\n\nQ: What are the five relationship levels?\nA: Same Yoni (4), Friendly (3), Neutral (2), Unfriendly (1), Sworn enemy (0) under Profile A.\n\nQ: How is Yoni Dosha determined?\nA: From the seven-pair sworn-enemy list, never from the number in the matrix.\n\nQ: Is a score of 1 a Yoni Dosha?\nA: No. It is an unfriendly pair with a low score; only the seven sworn-enemy pairs are flagged.\n\nQ: In which two pairs is the Profile A matrix asymmetric?\nA: Horse/Deer (Horse row × Deer column 3; Deer row × Horse column 1) and Buffalo/Lion (Buffalo × Lion 1; Lion × Buffalo 2).\n\nQ: What is Profile C?\nA: A three-level model: same Yoni 4, sworn enemy 0, every other pair 2.\n\nQ: What is Profile D?\nA: Profile A with a sex-aware same-Yoni rule: complementary designations score 4, identical designations score 3.\n\nQ: What is Profile E?\nA: A custom editable 14 × 14 matrix saved in the learner's own browser. It cannot remove the enemy flag.\n\nQ: What is Abhijit's role in Yoni?\nA: In the optional 28-Nakshatra variant Abhijit takes the Mongoose Yoni with an unverified designation; it is never mixed silently with the 27-Nakshatra table.\n\nQ: Why must Yoni never be described as biological or sexual?\nA: It is a symbolic classification label of the Nakshatra. Statements about intimacy, health or fertility are outside what the tradition's table can support.\n\nQ: Can Yoni alone decide a marriage?\nA: No. It is one traditional indicator among many.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: Yoni (योनि) in Koota Milan is best described as:\nA) A medical test\nB) A traditional symbolic animal classification of the Nakshatra\nC) A measure of fertility\nD) The ascendant\nANS: B\nWHY: It is a classification label assigned to each Nakshatra; it is symbolic, not biological.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many points does Yoni Koota carry in Ashtakoota?\nA) 2\nB) 3\nC) 4\nD) 5\nANS: C\nWHY: Yoni carries 4 of the 36 points.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Yoni is assigned from:\nA) The Sun sign\nB) The ascendant\nC) The Janma Nakshatra\nD) The Navamsha\nANS: C\nWHY: Each Nakshatra carries a Yoni; the input is each person's Janma Nakshatra.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many Yonis are there?\nA) Seven\nB) Twelve\nC) Fourteen\nD) Twenty-seven\nANS: C\nWHY: There are fourteen animal Yonis.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which Yoni has only one Nakshatra in the 27-Nakshatra system?\nA) Lion\nB) Mongoose\nC) Cow\nD) Monkey\nANS: B\nWHY: Only Uttara Ashadha is Mongoose.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Rohini's Yoni is:\nA) Horse\nB) Serpent\nC) Cat\nD) Rat\nANS: B\nWHY: Rohini and Mrigashira are Serpent.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Ashwini and Shatabhisha share which Yoni?\nA) Elephant\nB) Sheep\nC) Horse\nD) Dog\nANS: C\nWHY: Both are Horse, so a pair of them scores 4.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Mula's Yoni is:\nA) Dog\nB) Cat\nC) Lion\nD) Deer\nANS: A\nWHY: Mula and Ardra are Dog.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Hasta's Yoni is:\nA) Cow\nB) Tiger\nC) Buffalo\nD) Deer\nANS: C\nWHY: Hasta and Swati are Buffalo.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many sworn-enemy pairs are listed?\nA) Three\nB) Five\nC) Seven\nD) Fourteen\nANS: C\nWHY: Seven pairs cover all fourteen Yonis.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The sworn enemy of the Dog is the:\nA) Cat\nB) Deer\nC) Tiger\nD) Lion\nANS: B\nWHY: Dog–Deer is the fifth pair.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The sworn enemy of the Lion is the:\nA) Elephant\nB) Buffalo\nC) Cow\nD) Horse\nANS: A\nWHY: Elephant–Lion is the second pair.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A traditional \"male\" or \"female\" marker on a Nakshatra is:\nA) A person's sex\nB) A person's gender identity\nC) A field in the traditional allocation table\nD) An animal\nANS: C\nWHY: It labels the Nakshatra in the table and says nothing about a person.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which statement is responsible teaching?\nA) Yoni predicts fertility\nB) Yoni can be used to infer sexual conduct\nC) Yoni must not be used to infer sexual, medical or fertility matters\nD) A low Yoni score proves the marriage will fail\nANS: C\nWHY: Yoni is a symbolic classification; such inferences are outside what it can support.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: With bride as row and groom as column, Profile A gives Horse (row) × Deer (column) =\nA) 0\nB) 1\nC) 2\nD) 3\nANS: D\nWHY: Horse row, Deer column is 3.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Under Profile A, Deer (row) × Horse (column) =\nA) 3\nB) 2\nC) 1\nD) 0\nANS: C\nWHY: The Profile A matrix is asymmetric here: the reverse of 3 is 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Profile A is asymmetric in:\nA) No pair\nB) Exactly two pairs\nC) All pairs\nD) Only the enemy pairs\nANS: B\nWHY: Horse/Deer and Buffalo/Lion are the only asymmetric pairs.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Under Profile A, Lion (bride) × Buffalo (groom) =\nA) 0\nB) 1\nC) 2\nD) 3\nANS: C\nWHY: Lion row, Buffalo column is 2, while Buffalo row, Lion column is 1.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The Yoni Dosha flag is derived from:\nA) The numeric matrix only\nB) The seven-pair sworn-enemy list\nC) The Rashi\nD) The Nakshatra number\nANS: B\nWHY: Even a custom matrix cannot remove the flag.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Krittika (Sheep) as bride and Chitra (Tiger) as groom score 1 under Profile A. Is Yoni Dosha flagged?\nA) Yes, always\nB) No, because Sheep–Tiger is not a sworn-enemy pair\nC) Yes, because 1 is low\nD) Only if the groom is older\nANS: B\nWHY: Only the seven listed pairs flag; 1 is an unfriendly score.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Ardra (Dog) as bride and Anuradha (Deer) as groom score:\nA) 0, sworn enemy\nB) 1, unfriendly\nC) 2, neutral\nD) 4, same Yoni\nANS: A\nWHY: Dog–Deer is a sworn-enemy pair; the cell is 0 and the flag is raised.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Pushya (Sheep) as bride and Revati (Elephant) as groom score, under Profile A:\nA) 4\nB) 3\nC) 2\nD) 0\nANS: B\nWHY: Sheep row, Elephant column is 3; Profile C would give 2.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Profile C scores every non-same, non-enemy pair as:\nA) 1\nB) 2\nC) 3\nD) It varies by pair\nANS: B\nWHY: Profile C has only three levels: same 4, enemy 0, other 2.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Two people with Uttara Ashadha as the Janma Nakshatra score:\nA) 0\nB) 2\nC) 3\nD) 4\nANS: D\nWHY: Same Yoni (Mongoose). Under Profile D the identical designation would give 3.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Why can published Yoni matrices differ?\nA) The animals change from book to book\nB) The intermediate scores are not standardised across sources\nC) The zodiac moves\nD) Yoni has no matrix\nANS: B\nWHY: Allocation and enemy pairs are stable; cells scoring 1, 2 and 3 vary.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: If Profile A and Profile C disagree for a pair, the correct approach is to:\nA) Average them\nB) Show both separately with the reason\nC) Choose the higher\nD) Hide one\nANS: B\nWHY: Profiles are shown side by side and are never merged or averaged.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The \"Maitreya-style\" label on the Profile A matrix means:\nA) A verified classical citation\nB) A name for a supplied reference matrix whose attribution is unverified\nC) A medical standard\nD) A Panchang rule\nANS: B\nWHY: The course records the attribution as unverified; it is not cited as a classical source.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Abhijit in the optional 28-Nakshatra variant:\nA) Is silently included in the 27-table\nB) Takes the Mongoose Yoni with an unverified designation, in an explicit mode\nC) Has the Lion Yoni\nD) Cancels Yoni Dosha\nANS: B\nWHY: The variant is explicit and never mixed silently with the 27-Nakshatra table.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A client has Yoni score 0. The responsible statement is:\nA) The marriage will fail\nB) A traditional incompatibility indicator, read with all other factors\nC) They will have no children\nD) They must not marry\nANS: B\nWHY: Yoni is one indicator among many; it never predicts outcomes.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: How many of the 36 Ashtakoota points come from Yoni plus Nadi together?\nA) 8\nB) 10\nC) 12\nD) 14\nANS: C\nWHY: Yoni 4 + Nadi 8 = 12.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. What does Yoni Koota classify, and why must the classification not be treated as biological?\nMA: It assigns each Nakshatra one of fourteen animal symbols and compares the two people's symbols. The animal is a label attached to a Nakshatra in a traditional table; it describes no person's body, behaviour or sexuality, so biological or sexual conclusions cannot be drawn from it.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Explain how the enemy flag is determined and why it is separate from the matrix.\nMA: The flag comes from the list of seven sworn-enemy pairs. Keeping it separate means that a scoring profile (or a learner's custom matrix) can change the numbers without silently hiding or inventing a Dosha.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 3. A pair scores 1 and another scores 0. Explain the difference in reading.\nMA: A score of 0 under the course's profiles marks a sworn-enemy pair and raises the Yoni Dosha flag; a score of 1 is an unfriendly pair with a low score but is not flagged. Both are traditional indicators, read with the other factors and not as verdicts.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 4. Why does Profile A require the orientation to be stated?\nMA: Profile A is directional: the Horse/Deer and Buffalo/Lion pairs score differently in the two directions. With the wrong orientation the learner would read the other cell, so the bride/groom (row/column) convention must be stated every time.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual 5. Name two limitations of Yoni scoring.\nMA: The intermediate scores are not standardised and the supplied matrix's attribution is unverified; and Yoni is one traditional indicator for two Moon-Nakshatras, so it cannot decide suitability or predict personal outcomes.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Calculation 1. Bride Rohini, groom Uttara Ashadha: give Yonis, score and Dosha status under Profile A.\nMA: Rohini is Serpent, Uttara Ashadha is Mongoose. Serpent row, Mongoose column = 0. Serpent–Mongoose is a sworn-enemy pair, so the Yoni Dosha flag is raised.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Calculation 2. Bride Ashwini, groom Anuradha: give both directional scores under Profile A and Profile A0.\nMA: Horse × Deer = 3 in both profiles. The reverse (Deer × Horse) is 1 under Profile A and 3 under Profile A0. The difference comes from the table, not from the pair.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Calculation 3. Bride Purva Bhadrapada, groom Dhanishta: give the Yonis, score and status under Profile A and Profile D.\nMA: Both are Lion, so the Yoni pair is same-Yoni and scores 4 under Profile A. Under Profile D the designations are M (Purva Bhadrapada) and F (Dhanishta): complementary, so also 4. No Dosha.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\n## References\n### Source status (Day 5)\nNo classical verse or chapter has been verified for any Yoni rule in this module.\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fourteen Yonis and their Sanskrit names | C | Widely stated in manuals and web explanations; no verse verified |\n| Allocation of the 27 Nakshatras to Yonis (13 pairs and Mongoose) | C | Stable across common manuals; no verse verified |\n| Male/female designation of each Nakshatra | C/D | Commonly published; source and meaning of the marker not verified |\n| Seven sworn-enemy pairs | C | Widely stated and consistent; no verse verified |\n| Yoni = 4 points in Ashtakoota | C | Commonly stated |\n| Profile A matrix (\"Maitreya-style\") | C/D | Supplied; attribution unverified; not claimed as a classical text |\n| Profile A0 matrix | C/D | Earlier draft table; differs from A in two cells |\n| Profile B | n/a | Provisional software model, pending researcher approval |\n| Profiles C, D, E | n/a | Software configurations, not classical schools |\n| Abhijit as 28th Nakshatra with Mongoose Yoni | D/E | Sources differ; designation not verified |\n| Same-Yoni sex-aware rule (Profile D) | D | Regional convention requiring further verification |\n\n### Corrections to supplied material\n| Supplied | Correct |\n|---|---|\n| Earlier draft: \"the Yoni matrix is symmetric\" | The supplied Profile A matrix is asymmetric in Horse/Deer and Buffalo/Lion; the earlier symmetric table is kept as Profile A0 |\n| Earlier draft: Deer row, Horse column = 3 and Lion row, Buffalo column = 1 | Under Profile A these cells are 1 and 2 respectively |\n| \"Enemy\" read from a zero in the matrix | Derived from the seven-pair list |\n\n### Research and source policy\nOnline explanatory pages may be used only as secondary support. For every classical passage the course would cite it must record: text name, author or attribution, chapter, verse, edition or translator, the exact rule supported, and confidence of verification. Where the exact source cannot be verified, the statement is labelled **\"Traditional practice: source attribution requires verification.\"** Leads still to be checked: texts commonly cited for Koota Milan (for example the *Muhurta Chintamani* and *Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra*, with edition, chapter and verse), the work to which the \"Maitreya\" attribution of the Profile A matrix refers, and regional matching manuals. No quotation, verse or chapter number is given here.\n\n## Fourteen Yonis\n### Lesson 2 (interactive) – one learning page for each Yoni\nChoose a Yoni to see its names, its Nakshatras with their traditional designation, the illustrative theme, its sworn enemy, an example of a compatible and an adverse combination, and its relationship with every other Yoni in both directions. Pick a profile, or add the Abhijit variant.\n{{yyonis}}\n\n## Nakshatra Allocation\n### Lesson 3 (interactive) – the allocation table\nThe complete table of the 27 Nakshatras with their Yoni, Sanskrit name, traditional designation and source note, plus the male/female pairing within each Yoni. Filter by Nakshatra or animal; switch on the Abhijit variant if you wish.\n{{ytable}}\n\n## Enemy Pairs\n### Lesson 5 (interactive) – the seven sworn-enemy pairs and the Dosha reading chart\nThe diagram shows the seven pairs; the table lists the scores in both directions under the profile you choose.\n{{yenemy}}\n\n## Matrix\n### The Yoni matrices\nFour views: the 27 × 27 Nakshatra matrix, the 14 × 14 Yoni matrix, the seven enemy pairs, and the asymmetry demonstration. Rows are the bride, columns the groom. Every number is generated from the stored profile (nothing is typed in). Search, tap a cell for its working, show both directions, export CSV or print.\n{{ymatrix}}\n\n## Profiles\n### Compare the scoring profiles\nChoose a bride's and a groom's Nakshatra to see the score under all six profiles side by side. The profiles are never merged or averaged.\n{{ycompare}}\n\n## Calculator\n### Yoni Compatibility Calculator\nChoose two Nakshatras, a rule profile and (if needed) the Abhijit variant. The full calculation is shown: allocation lookup, row, column, exact score, category and Dosha status.\n{{ycalc}}\n\n## Exercises\n### Five practical exercises\nIdentify the Yoni; identify the enemy; score a combination; the reverse-matrix test; a case study.\n{{yexercise}}\n\n## Final Assessment\n### The 30-question final assessment\n8 conceptual, 8 allocation, 7 scoring, 4 directional and 3 interpretation questions. Pass mark 70%. A failed attempt never erases your completed lessons, and the report names the topics to revise.\n{{yfinal}}\n\n## Common Mistakes\nTen mistakes to recognise: the incorrect method, the corrected method and a worked example.\n| # | Mistake | Correct method | Worked example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Describing Yoni as sexual or biological compatibility | It is a symbolic classification label | Say \"traditional symbolic indicator\", never \"physical compatibility\" |\n| 2 | Confusing the Nakshatra's M/F marker with a person's sex | It is a field of the traditional table | Krittika is marked F as a table entry; it says nothing about the person |\n| 3 | Swapping row and column | Bride = row, groom = column | Horse (row) × Deer (column) = 3, not 1 |\n| 4 | Assuming the matrix is symmetric | Profile A is asymmetric in two pairs | Buffalo × Lion = 1, Lion × Buffalo = 2 |\n| 5 | Reading the Dosha from the number 0 | Use the seven-pair list | A custom matrix with 0 for Sheep × Tiger does not create a Dosha |\n| 6 | Treating a score of 1 as Yoni Dosha | 1 is unfriendly, not a sworn enemy | Krittika–Chitra: Sheep × Tiger = 1, no flag |\n| 7 | Mixing the 27- and 28-Nakshatra systems silently | Abhijit is an explicit variant | Enable the Abhijit mode on purpose and say so |\n| 8 | Averaging profiles | Show each profile separately | Pushya–Revati: Profile A 3, Profile C 2; not 2.5 |\n| 9 | Forgetting that Mongoose has one Nakshatra | Only Uttara Ashadha | Two Uttara Ashadha people are the only Mongoose pair |\n| 10 | Using Yoni to approve or reject a marriage | It is one indicator among many | Name the score, the limits and what else must be read |\n\n## Instructor Guide\n*For teachers and self-study students.*\n\n### Suggested time allocation (180 minutes)\n| Section | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Foundation and fourteen Yonis | 30 |\n| Allocation and enemy pairs | 30 |\n| Matrix and scoring profiles | 35 |\n| Yoni Dosha, exceptions and responsible reading | 25 |\n| Calculator and practice | 35 |\n| Assessment and revision | 25 |\n\n### Teaching sequence\n1. Open with \"symbols for memory, not descriptions of people\". 2. Teach the fourteen Yonis with their enemy partner. 3. Teach the allocation as thirteen pairs and one single. 4. Read matrix cells aloud until bride-row, groom-column is automatic. 5. Show the two asymmetric pairs and why a profile can differ from another. 6. Teach the Dosha as a list-derived flag. 7. Calculator and exercises. 8. Assessment.\n\n### Core definitions\nYoni; Nakshatra designation; sworn-enemy pair; directional matrix; profile; Dosha flag; Abhijit variant.\n\n### Common student mistakes\nSee the Common Mistakes tab.\n\n### Calculation demonstrations\nAshwini and Shatabhisha (same Yoni); Ashwini and Anuradha (asymmetric); Ardra and Anuradha (enemy); Pushya and Revati (profile difference).\n\n### Discussion questions\n\"Why is a symbolic animal table safer to teach than a personality reading?\" \"What does it mean that Profile A is directional?\" \"How should a consultant explain a zero score to a client?\"\n\n### Advanced interpretation notes\nWhy a flag must come from the pair list; why symmetric and asymmetric tables should be shown side by side; how to record an unverified attribution.\n\n### Case-study instructions\nUse Exercise 5: students write a reading that names the profile, both Yonis, the score, the limits and avoids certainty and any claim about intimacy, health or children.\n\n### Assessment answer key\nFinal assessment questions are generated and marked by the engine; the theory questions carry explanations after submission.\n\n## Validation\n### Technical validation report\nAutomatic tests of the Yoni engine. Every row runs when this page opens.\n{{ytests}}\n","06":"# Day 6 – Graha Maitri Koota and Panchadha Maitri\n\n## Learn\n\n### Welcome to Day 6\n**Module 6: Graha Maitri Koota (ग्रह मैत्री कूट) – the friendship of the two Moon-sign lords, and Panchadha Maitri (पंचधा मैत्री) – the five-fold planetary relationship.** Day 6 of 14 · Foundation to Advanced · 190–220 minutes (self-paced) · Instructor: Anoopam Shukla.\n\nGraha Maitri is the Koota that asks one question: **are the two planets that rule the bride's and the groom's Moon signs friendly to each other?** It is worth up to **5 points** in the 36-point Ashtakoota scheme. Today you learn to answer that question step by step, and you also learn the larger system of planetary friendship (Panchadha Maitri) that astrologers use when they read a single chart.\n\n> **Central teaching principle.** Find the lord first, look up the friendship second, score third. Natural friendship, temporary friendship, Panchadha Maitri and the Koota score are **four different layers**. Mixing them is the most common mistake in this topic.\n\n### The one idea to keep in mind all day\nKeep six layers apart:\n1. **Rashi lordship** (राशि स्वामी): which planet rules which sign.\n2. **Natural friendship** (नैसर्गिक मैत्री): a fixed table, read **in each direction separately**.\n3. **Temporary friendship** (तात्कालिक मैत्री): depends on where two planets sit in a particular chart.\n4. **Panchadha Maitri**: natural plus temporary, giving five grades.\n5. **Koota scoring**: converts the Moon-sign lords' relationship to 0 to 5 points.\n6. **Interpretation**: what the score may symbolise. It never changes the score.\n\n### What you will be able to do by the end of the day\n1. Explain Graha Maitri and its purpose in Ashtakoota.\n2. Name the lord of each of the twelve Rashis.\n3. Read the natural friendship table and explain why it is directional.\n4. Give the example where two planets regard each other differently.\n5. Calculate temporary friendship from two sign positions.\n6. Combine natural and temporary friendship into the five Panchadha grades.\n7. Distinguish Panchadha Maitri from the Graha Maitri Koota score.\n8. Convert the two lords' relationship into the five-point score.\n9. Read the 7 × 7 and the 12 × 12 matrices.\n10. Explain why Rahu and Ketu are not in the standard seven-planet table.\n11. Compare the conventional Koota with advanced chart-specific observations without mixing them.\n12. Complete the exercises and the final assessment (pass mark 70%).\n\n### Your learning route (twelve stages)\nProgress → Introduction → Seven Planetary Lords → Natural Friendship → Asymmetric Relationships → Temporary Friendship → Panchadha Maitri → Koota Scoring → 7 × 7 Matrix → 12 × 12 Matrix → Full Chart Analysis → Practical → Assessment. Tick each stage when you finish it.\n{{gmdash}}\n\n### Prerequisites and quick revision\nYou should know: the twelve Rashis and their order; the Moon sign (Chandra Rashi) of a person; that the Ashtakoota has eight Kootas with 36 points; and the work of Days 1 to 5. Graha Maitri uses **only the Moon sign**, so you do not need a Nakshatra for this Koota.\n\n### Suggested pace (self-paced)\n| Block | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Foundation: lords and natural friendship | 40 |\n| Directional relationships | 25 |\n| Temporary friendship and Panchadha | 40 |\n| Koota scoring and matrices | 40 |\n| Full chart, nodes and calculator | 30 |\n| Exercises, assessment and revision | 35 |\n| Total | 210 |\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### Lesson 1 – What is Graha Maitri?\n**1.1 Meaning.** *Graha* (ग्रह) means planet and *Maitri* (मैत्री) means friendship. **Graha Maitri Koota** compares the lords of the two Moon signs. In some manuals it is called by related names; the content is the same idea.\n\n**1.2 Why the Moon sign?** In Ashtakoota the Moon is the main reference point (it also gave the Nakshatra for Tara, Yoni and Gana). The Moon sign shows the ruling planet whose friendship is used here. The Ascendant lord, Sun-sign lord, seventh lord and Navamsha lords are **not** used in the conventional Koota.\n\n**1.3 What is awarded.** Up to **5 points**. The theme is the mental wavelength, mutual regard and way of thinking between the two people, as a **traditional symbolic indicator**. It is not a measurement of psychology.\n\n**1.4 What it is not.** Graha Maitri is not the full planetary relationship of the two charts; for that you would use Panchadha Maitri and the actual planetary positions.\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) Which planets are compared? (b) What is the maximum score? (c) Which sign is used for each person?\n\n### Lesson 2 – The seven planetary lords\nSeven classical planets rule the twelve signs. The Sun and Moon rule one sign each; the other five rule two each.\n| Planet | Hindi | Signs ruled |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sun (Surya) | सूर्य | Leo |\n| Moon (Chandra) | चंद्र | Cancer |\n| Mars (Mangala) | मंगल | Aries, Scorpio |\n| Mercury (Budha) | बुध | Gemini, Virgo |\n| Jupiter (Guru) | गुरु | Sagittarius, Pisces |\n| Venus (Shukra) | शुक्र | Taurus, Libra |\n| Saturn (Shani) | शनि | Capricorn, Aquarius |\n\nThis is the standard seven-planet rulership. Rahu and Ketu are not used as Rashi lords in the conventional Koota. Use the interactive chart below to tap any sign.\n{{gmlords}}\n\n### Lesson 3 – Natural friendship (नैसर्गिक मैत्री)\nEach planet has a fixed attitude to each other planet: **Friend (मित्र), Neutral (सम), Enemy (शत्रु)**. This is called natural (permanent) friendship. The standard table follows.\n| Planet | Friends | Neutrals | Enemies |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sun | Moon, Mars, Jupiter | Mercury | Venus, Saturn |\n| Moon | Sun, Mercury | Mars, Jupiter, Venus, Saturn | none |\n| Mars | Sun, Moon, Jupiter | Venus, Saturn | Mercury |\n| Mercury | Sun, Venus | Mars, Jupiter, Saturn | Moon |\n| Jupiter | Sun, Moon, Mars | Saturn | Mercury, Venus |\n| Venus | Mercury, Saturn | Mars, Jupiter | Sun, Moon |\n| Saturn | Mercury, Venus | Jupiter | Sun, Moon, Mars |\n\n**Read each row as that planet's own view of the others.** The table is **not symmetric**, and that matters a great deal (Lesson 4).\n{{gmnatural}}\n\n### Lesson 4 – Directional (asymmetric) relationships\nFriendship here is a **viewpoint**. The Sun regards Mercury as neutral, but Mercury regards the Sun as a friend. The Moon regards Mercury as a friend, but Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy. A computer that stores one friendship value per pair would get these wrong. The Koota handles this by reading **both directions** and combining them:\n| Bride's lord regards groom's lord | Groom's lord regards bride's lord |\n|---|---|\n| Each of F, N, E | Each of F, N, E |\n\nNine direction combinations reduce to six categories (Lesson 6).\n\n### Lesson 5 – Temporary friendship (तात्कालिक मैत्री)\nTemporary friendship depends on the **signs in which two planets stand in a chart**. Count from the first planet's sign to the second planet's sign, **inclusive**, so the sign itself is 1:\n- relative position = ((B sign − A sign + 12) MOD 12) + 1\n- positions **2, 3, 4, 10, 11, 12** = temporary friend (तात्कालिक मित्र)\n- positions **1, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9** = temporary enemy (तात्कालिक शत्रु)\nTwo planets in the **same sign** are at position 1 and are therefore temporary enemies in this rule.\n\nFor two different signs the forward and reverse positions add up to 14, and under this rule they give the same result in both directions.\n{{gmtemp}}\n\n### Lesson 6 – Panchadha Maitri (पंचधा मैत्री) and the Koota score\n**Panchadha** means five-fold. Combine natural and temporary relationships:\n| Natural → | Temporary Friend | Temporary Enemy |\n|---|---|---|\n| Natural Friend | Adhimitra (great friend) | Sama (neutral) |\n| Natural Neutral | Mitra (friend) | Shatru (enemy) |\n| Natural Enemy | Sama (neutral) | Adhishatru (great enemy) |\n{{gmpanch}}\n\n**The Koota score is separate.** The conventional Graha Maitri Koota does **not** use temporary friendship or the Panchadha grade. It takes the **natural** relationship of the two Moon-sign lords in both directions and scores:\n| Relationship of the two lords | Score |\n|---|---|\n| Same planet | 5 |\n| Mutual friends (F + F) | 5 |\n| Friend + Neutral | 4 |\n| Neutral + Neutral | 3 |\n| Friend + Enemy | 1 |\n| Neutral + Enemy | 0.5 |\n| Mutual enemies (E + E) | 0 |\nThe conversion table is a common published convention (source grade C) and is editable as a custom profile.\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) What does Panchadha mean? (b) Does the Koota use temporary friendship? (c) Score for Friend + Enemy?\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### Rule 1 – Find the Moon-sign lords\nIdentify the bride's Moon sign and the groom's Moon sign (not the Sun sign, not the Ascendant). Convert each to its lord using the table in Lesson 2.\n\n### Rule 2 – Same lord\nIf both signs have the same lord (for example Aries and Scorpio, both Mars; Taurus and Libra, both Venus; Gemini and Virgo, both Mercury; Sagittarius and Pisces, both Jupiter; Capricorn and Aquarius, both Saturn), the pair scores **5**.\n\n### Rule 3 – Different lords: read both directions\nLook up the bride's lord's view of the groom's lord, and then the groom's lord's view of the bride's lord. Classify the pair into one of six categories: FF, FN, NN, FE, NE, EE.\n\n### Rule 4 – Convert to the score\nUse the scoring table in Lesson 6. FF = 5, FN = 4, NN = 3, FE = 1, NE = 0.5, EE = 0.\n\n### Rule 5 – Fractional points\nThe score 0.5 is allowed in the five-point scheme. A report must show the value exactly as 0.5, never rounded.\n\n### Rule 6 – Total contribution\nThe Graha Maitri score is added to the other seven Koota scores for the 36-point total. Its maximum is 5. It is never read in isolation.\n\n### Rule 7 – Order of reading\nFor a chart-specific reading, apply: (1) Rashi lordship, (2) natural table, (3) temporary rule, (4) Panchadha grade. The Koota itself stops after step 2. This order never changes.\n\n### Rule 8 – Directional report\nAlways show both directions in the report, for example \"Moon → Mercury: Friend; Mercury → Moon: Enemy; category Friend + Enemy; score 1\".\n\n### The 7 × 7 and 12 × 12 matrices\nThere are only seven planets, so the 12 × 12 matrix of Moon signs is a re-labelled version of the 7 × 7 matrix of lords. Both are generated from the engine, never typed by hand. In the **score view** each cell shows the score; in the **relationship view** it shows how each lord regards the other.\n{{gmpmatrix}}\n{{gmscore7}}\n{{gmmatrix12}}\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### Rule profiles in this module\nAll the profiles below are **software configuration profiles for transparent comparison, not classical schools**. Choose them in the lab; the lesson never merges or adds their results.\n| Profile | What differs |\n|---|---|\n| Natural table: standard seven-planet | The widely published table in Lesson 3 (grade C) |\n| Natural table: custom | Your own edits, saved on this device |\n| Temporary profile A | Positions 2, 3, 4, 10, 11, 12 friends |\n| Temporary profile B | As A, plus a research consideration of exaltation (explicitly experimental) |\n| Temporary profile C | Custom position list |\n| Scoring: standard five-point | 5, 4, 3, 1, 0.5, 0 |\n| Scoring: custom | Your own conversion |\n| Node profile A | Exclude Rahu and Ketu (standard) |\n| Node profile B | Dispositor-based (research) |\n| Node profile C | Custom table |\n| Node profile D | Compare A, B and C side by side |\n\n### Why do manuals differ?\nSome published natural tables differ in individual cells. Some manuals list slightly different scores. Some treat Rahu and Ketu by their own tables. The module never claims one of them is the correct classical text. Where the exact textual attribution is unknown, it is marked **unverified**.\n\n### How to use the profiles\nAlways state the profile in your report (for example \"standard natural table, standard five-point conversion\"). Two reports with different profiles must be compared, never added.\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### Exception 1 – Same lord\nSame-lord pairs score full marks even though the two planets are, in the planetary friendship sense, \"the same planet\", not a friend of itself. This is a convention.\n\n### Exception 2 – Neutral that behaves as friend or enemy\nIn Panchadha, a natural neutral becomes a Friend (Mitra) if temporary friendship applies, or an Enemy (Shatru) if temporary enmity applies. In the Koota score, neutral stays neutral.\n\n### Exception 3 – Same sign is temporary enmity\nTwo planets in the same sign are at position 1, which is temporary enmity under the standard rule. Students often assume \"same sign means friend\". It does not.\n\n### Exception 4 – Rahu and Ketu\nThey are not in the standard seven-planet table and are not Rashi lords in the conventional Koota. A node profile is optional, experimental and never affects the score.\n\n### Exception 5 – Exaltation\nPlanetary exaltation is **not** part of the standard rule. Profile B treats it as a research configuration only.\n\n### Exception 6 – Asymmetry\nA pair can be Friend in one direction and Enemy in the other. The score is still defined: Friend + Enemy = 1.\n\n### Exception 7 – Other corrective factors\nSome manuals mention extra cancellations (Parihara) for low Graha Maitri. Their sources are not verified here and no cancellation rule is applied in the engine.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Ten worked examples\nEach example takes the two Moon signs, identifies the lords, reads both directions and scores. The table and the step-by-step explanations are generated by the engine.\n{{gmexamples}}\n\n### Example sentence for a report\n\"Bride Cancer Moon (lord Moon), groom Virgo Moon (lord Mercury): Moon regards Mercury as a friend, Mercury regards Moon as an enemy; Friend + Enemy; Graha Maitri 1 of 5 (standard conversion).\"\n\n### Full chart comparison\nThe conventional Koota and the advanced, chart-specific observations are shown in separate blocks. Observations never add points.\n{{gmchart}}\n\n## Practice\n\n### Calculator\nChoose the two Moon signs, the scoring profile and the natural table. The page shows the lords, both directions, the category, the score and a full step-by-step working.\n{{gmcalc}}\n\n### Numerical practice (20 questions)\n{{gmnumeric}}\n\n### Learning Journal\nWrite a five-line reading of one pair that (a) names the profile, (b) shows both lords and both directions, (c) gives the category and score, (d) states what Graha Maitri does not measure, (e) avoids deterministic claims.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What does Graha Maitri (ग्रह मैत्री) mean?\nA: Planetary friendship. The Koota compares the friendship of the two Moon-sign lords.\n\nQ: What is the maximum Graha Maitri score?\nA: 5 points.\n\nQ: Which sign is used for each person?\nA: The Moon sign (Chandra Rashi).\n\nQ: Name the Rashi lord of Aries and Scorpio.\nA: Mars (Mangala).\n\nQ: Name the Rashi lord of Taurus and Libra.\nA: Venus (Shukra).\n\nQ: Name the Rashi lord of Gemini and Virgo.\nA: Mercury (Budha).\n\nQ: Name the Rashi lord of Cancer.\nA: Moon (Chandra).\n\nQ: Name the Rashi lord of Leo.\nA: Sun (Surya).\n\nQ: Name the Rashi lord of Sagittarius and Pisces.\nA: Jupiter (Guru).\n\nQ: Name the Rashi lord of Capricorn and Aquarius.\nA: Saturn (Shani).\n\nQ: How many planets are used in the standard table?\nA: Seven: Sun, Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus, Saturn.\n\nQ: Are Rahu and Ketu in the standard table?\nA: No. They are excluded; node handling is an optional research profile.\n\nQ: What is natural friendship (नैसर्गिक मैत्री)?\nA: The fixed Friend, Neutral or Enemy attitude of one planet to another.\n\nQ: Is natural friendship always mutual?\nA: No. It is directional and can be asymmetric.\n\nQ: How does the Sun regard Mercury, and Mercury the Sun?\nA: The Sun regards Mercury as neutral; Mercury regards the Sun as a friend.\n\nQ: How does the Moon regard Mercury, and Mercury the Moon?\nA: The Moon regards Mercury as a friend; Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy.\n\nQ: What is temporary friendship (तात्कालिक मैत्री)?\nA: A relationship that depends on the signs in which two planets stand in a chart.\n\nQ: What is the temporary friendship formula?\nA: Relative position = ((B sign − A sign + 12) MOD 12) + 1, counted inclusively.\n\nQ: Which positions give temporary friendship?\nA: 2, 3, 4, 10, 11 and 12.\n\nQ: Which positions give temporary enmity?\nA: 1, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9.\n\nQ: What do forward and reverse positions add up to (different signs)?\nA: 14.\n\nQ: Same sign: friend or enemy under the standard temporary rule?\nA: Enemy, because the position is 1.\n\nQ: What does Panchadha mean?\nA: Five-fold: Adhimitra, Mitra, Sama, Shatru, Adhishatru.\n\nQ: Natural Friend + temporary Friend gives what?\nA: Adhimitra (great friend).\n\nQ: Natural Friend + temporary Enemy gives what?\nA: Sama (neutral).\n\nQ: Natural Neutral + temporary Friend gives what?\nA: Mitra (friend).\n\nQ: Natural Neutral + temporary Enemy gives what?\nA: Shatru (enemy).\n\nQ: Natural Enemy + temporary Friend gives what?\nA: Sama (neutral).\n\nQ: Natural Enemy + temporary Enemy gives what?\nA: Adhishatru (great enemy).\n\nQ: Does the Koota use Panchadha Maitri?\nA: No. The conventional Koota uses only the natural relationship of the two Moon-sign lords.\n\nQ: What is the score for the same lord?\nA: 5.\n\nQ: What is the score for mutual friends?\nA: 5.\n\nQ: What is the score for Friend + Neutral?\nA: 4.\n\nQ: What is the score for Neutral + Neutral?\nA: 3.\n\nQ: What is the score for Friend + Enemy?\nA: 1.\n\nQ: What is the score for Neutral + Enemy?\nA: 0.5.\n\nQ: What is the score for mutual enemies?\nA: 0.\n\nQ: Why must the four layers be kept apart?\nA: Lordship, natural, temporary and Panchadha answer different questions; mixing them gives wrong scores.\n\nQ: What are the limits of Graha Maitri?\nA: It is a symbolic indicator of the two lords' friendship, not a measure of psychology or a prediction.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: Graha Maitri Koota compares:\nA) The two Ascendant lords\nB) The lords of the two Moon signs\nC) The two Sun-sign lords\nD) The two seventh lords\nANS: B\nWHY: The conventional Koota uses the Moon-sign lords only.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The maximum Graha Maitri score is:\nA) 3\nB) 4\nC) 5\nD) 6\nANS: C\nWHY: Graha Maitri carries up to five points.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The lord of Leo is:\nA) Moon\nB) Sun\nC) Mars\nD) Jupiter\nANS: B\nWHY: Leo is ruled by the Sun.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The lord of both Taurus and Libra is:\nA) Mercury\nB) Mars\nC) Venus\nD) Saturn\nANS: C\nWHY: Venus rules Taurus and Libra.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The lord of Sagittarius and Pisces is:\nA) Jupiter\nB) Venus\nC) Mars\nD) Mercury\nANS: A\nWHY: Jupiter rules both signs.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many planets are in the standard natural friendship table?\nA) 5\nB) 7\nC) 9\nD) 12\nANS: B\nWHY: Sun, Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus and Saturn.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Natural friendship between planets is:\nA) Always mutual\nB) Directional and can be asymmetric\nC) Decided by the signs they occupy\nD) Different for each person\nANS: B\nWHY: Each planet has its own view of the others; the reverse view may differ.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The Sun regards Mercury as:\nA) Friend\nB) Neutral\nC) Enemy\nD) Not defined\nANS: B\nWHY: In the standard table the Sun regards Mercury as neutral.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Mercury regards the Sun as:\nA) Friend\nB) Neutral\nC) Enemy\nD) Not defined\nANS: A\nWHY: Mercury regards the Sun as a friend, which makes the pair asymmetric.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Mercury regards the Moon as:\nA) Friend\nB) Neutral\nC) Enemy\nD) Not defined\nANS: C\nWHY: In the standard table Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy, though the Moon regards Mercury as a friend.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Temporary friendship depends on:\nA) The sign positions of the planets in a chart\nB) The Moon's Nakshatra\nC) The Ascendant degree\nD) The year of birth\nANS: A\nWHY: It is counted between the signs the two planets occupy.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Temporary friends stand in positions:\nA) 1, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9\nB) 2, 3, 4, 10, 11, 12\nC) 1, 2, 3, 4\nD) 5 and 9 only\nANS: B\nWHY: Positions 2, 3, 4, 10, 11 and 12 are temporary friends.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Two planets in the same sign are:\nA) Temporary friends\nB) Temporary enemies under the standard rule\nC) Neutral\nD) Not compared\nANS: B\nWHY: The same sign is position 1, which is classed as temporary enmity.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Planet A in Aries and planet B in Cancer: the relative position of B from A is:\nA) 3\nB) 4\nC) 5\nD) 10\nANS: B\nWHY: Aries = 1, Taurus = 2, Gemini = 3, Cancer = 4.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Planet A in Aries and planet B in Leo: the temporary relationship is:\nA) Friend\nB) Enemy\nC) Neutral\nD) Not defined\nANS: B\nWHY: Position 5 is a temporary enemy.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Panchadha means:\nA) Three-fold\nB) Five-fold\nC) Seven-fold\nD) Nine-fold\nANS: B\nWHY: Panchadha Maitri has five grades.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Natural Friend plus temporary Friend gives:\nA) Mitra\nB) Adhimitra\nC) Sama\nD) Shatru\nANS: B\nWHY: Friend plus Friend is Adhimitra (great friend).\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Natural Neutral plus temporary Friend gives:\nA) Mitra\nB) Adhimitra\nC) Sama\nD) Shatru\nANS: A\nWHY: A neutral relationship strengthened by temporary friendship becomes Mitra.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Natural Friend plus temporary Enemy gives:\nA) Adhishatru\nB) Shatru\nC) Sama\nD) Mitra\nANS: C\nWHY: Friend and enemy cancel to Sama.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Natural Enemy plus temporary Enemy gives:\nA) Shatru\nB) Sama\nC) Adhishatru\nD) Mitra\nANS: C\nWHY: Enemy plus Enemy is Adhishatru (great enemy).\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The conventional Graha Maitri Koota uses:\nA) Panchadha Maitri\nB) Temporary friendship\nC) Natural relationship of the two Moon-sign lords\nD) Exaltation and debilitation\nANS: C\nWHY: Only the natural relationship of the lords is used for the five-point score.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Two Moon signs with the same lord score:\nA) 0\nB) 3\nC) 4\nD) 5\nANS: D\nWHY: The same-lord rule gives the full five points.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Mutual friends score:\nA) 3\nB) 4\nC) 5\nD) 1\nANS: C\nWHY: Friend plus friend scores 5.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A Friend plus Neutral pair scores:\nA) 5\nB) 4\nC) 3\nD) 1\nANS: B\nWHY: The standard conversion gives 4.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A Neutral plus Neutral pair scores:\nA) 5\nB) 4\nC) 3\nD) 0.5\nANS: C\nWHY: The standard conversion gives 3.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A Friend plus Enemy pair scores:\nA) 1\nB) 3\nC) 0.5\nD) 0\nANS: A\nWHY: The standard conversion gives 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A Neutral plus Enemy pair scores:\nA) 1\nB) 0.5\nC) 0\nD) 3\nANS: B\nWHY: The standard conversion gives 0.5.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Mutual enemies score:\nA) 0\nB) 0.5\nC) 1\nD) 3\nANS: A\nWHY: Enemy plus enemy scores 0.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The Koota score for Aries Moon and Leo Moon (Mars and Sun) is:\nA) 5\nB) 4\nC) 3\nD) 1\nANS: A\nWHY: Mars and the Sun are mutual friends.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The Koota score for Leo Moon and Virgo Moon (Sun and Mercury) is:\nA) 5\nB) 4\nC) 3\nD) 0.5\nANS: B\nWHY: The Sun regards Mercury as neutral, Mercury regards the Sun as a friend: Neutral plus Friend = 4.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The Koota score for Cancer Moon and Virgo Moon (Moon and Mercury) is:\nA) 5\nB) 3\nC) 1\nD) 0\nANS: C\nWHY: The Moon regards Mercury as a friend, Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy: Friend plus Enemy = 1.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The Koota score for Leo Moon and Capricorn Moon (Sun and Saturn) is:\nA) 5\nB) 3\nC) 0.5\nD) 0\nANS: D\nWHY: The Sun and Saturn are mutual enemies.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Rahu and Ketu in the standard seven-planet table are:\nA) Included\nB) Excluded\nC) Included only as friends\nD) Included only as enemies\nANS: B\nWHY: The standard table has seven planets; nodes belong to separate, optional profiles.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Why must the natural table be read in both directions?\nA) Because the relationships are not always mutual\nB) Because the table is random\nC) Because there are twelve planets\nD) Because Rahu is included\nANS: A\nWHY: One planet's view of another may differ from the reverse view.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which statement about the Graha Maitri score is correct?\nA) It predicts the length of the marriage\nB) It is a symbolic indicator and not a prediction\nC) It measures personality exactly\nD) It replaces the other Kootas\nANS: B\nWHY: It is a traditional symbolic indicator and must never be presented as a prediction.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. Why must the natural friendship table be read in both directions?\nMA: Because natural friendship is a viewpoint and is not always mutual. For example the Moon regards Mercury as a friend but Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy; reading only one direction would give the wrong category.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Explain why temporary friendship is not part of the conventional Graha Maitri score.\nMA: The Koota compares only the Moon-sign lords' natural relationship, so no actual chart positions are used. Temporary friendship needs the signs where the planets stand in a chart and belongs to the Panchadha reading.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual 3. State two limits of Graha Maitri.\nMA: The natural table and the scoring conversion are published conventions (grade C) that are not source-verified here, and the score is a symbolic indicator of the Moon-sign lords' friendship; it cannot decide suitability alone or predict outcomes.\nLVL: Basic\n\n## References\n\n### Source status (Day 6)\nNo classical verse or chapter has been verified for any Graha Maitri rule in this module. The table records the status of each statement.\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Seven-planet rulership of the twelve signs | B | Established practice |\n| Standard natural friendship table | C | Common published table; individual cells can differ between manuals |\n| Directional reading of natural friendship | B | Established practice |\n| Temporary friendship positions 2, 3, 4, 10, 11, 12 | B | Established practice |\n| Panchadha combination table | B | Established practice |\n| Five-point Koota conversion (5, 4, 3, 1, 0.5, 0) | C | Common published convention |\n| Same lord scores 5 | C | Common published convention |\n| Cancellation (Parihara) rules | E | Unverified |\n| Exaltation in temporary friendship | E | Research configuration only |\n| Node handling (Rahu, Ketu) | E | Optional research profiles |\n| Software conversion tables in applications | D | Contemporary software |\n\n### Research leads (unverified)\nThe following works are commonly named when planetary friendship is discussed: Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra, Brihat Jataka, Jataka Parijata, Muhurta Chintamani, Bharatiya Jyotish, and B. V. Raman's Muhurtha. They are recorded here only as leads. **Exact textual attribution requires verification**, and no verse or chapter is quoted in this module.\n\n### Corrections to supplied material\n| Item | Correction |\n|---|---|\n| Title written with Telugu glyphs in the brief | The module title uses the Devanagari form ग्रह मैत्री |\n| Same sign described loosely as friendly | Same sign is position 1 in temporary friendship, which is temporary enmity; the same *lord* rule (score 5) is a different rule |\n| One value per pair of planets | The natural table is directional; each pair has two values |\n| Rahu and Ketu in the table | Excluded from the standard seven-planet profile |\n| Panchadha grade used as the Koota score | Kept separate; the score uses the natural relationship only |\n\n## Rashi Lordship\nUse the chart on Day 6 Foundation Theory, Lesson 2, and here test yourself. Tap any sign and read its lord, and the lord's natural friends, neutrals and enemies.\n{{gmlords}}\n\n## Natural Friendship\nRead the table as a directional matrix. The cell in row A and column B is how A regards B. Tap cells, planet buttons and the exercise.\n{{gmnatural}}\n\n## Temporary Friendship\nMove two planets around the sign wheel and watch the position, the reverse position and the result.\n{{gmtemp}}\n\n## Panchadha Maitri\nTap a cell in the combination table, then use the calculator. The grade is shown separately for both directions.\n{{gmpanch}}\n\n## Score Matrices\nThe 7 × 7 matrix of lords and the 12 × 12 matrix of Moon signs are generated from the engine. Use the toggles, search and export.\n{{gmscore7}}\n{{gmmatrix12}}\n\n## Calculator\n{{gmcalc}}\n\n## Full Chart\nThe Koota is conventional. The Panchadha matrix below uses planet positions and is an advanced, chart-specific reading that never adds points to the Koota.\n{{gmpmatrix}}\n{{gmchart}}\n\n## Rahu and Ketu\nThe standard table has seven planets. Rahu and Ketu are not Rashi lords in the conventional Koota. The tool below shows the optional research profiles side by side; none of them affects the score.\n{{gmnodes}}\n\n## Exercises\nSix exercises (marking is automatic; reaching 80% in a set marks it complete).\n{{gmexercise}}\n\n## Final Assessment\n35 questions: 8 conceptual, 8 natural friendship, 7 temporary friendship, 7 Panchadha Maitri and 5 Koota scoring. Pass mark 70%. A failed attempt does not erase any lesson progress.\n{{gmfinal}}\n\n## Common Mistakes\n1. Using the Ascendant lord or the Sun-sign lord instead of the Moon-sign lord.\n2. Treating natural friendship as always mutual.\n3. Believing that the same sign means friendship.\n4. Using temporary friendship or the Panchadha grade as the Koota score.\n5. Counting positions without including the starting sign.\n6. Adding Rahu and Ketu to the standard table.\n7. Rounding 0.5 to 1 or to 0.\n8. Mixing two natural tables or two scoring conversions in one report.\n9. Reading a low score as a prediction.\n\n## Instructor Guide\n**Teaching order.** Rashi lordship, natural table, asymmetric examples, temporary friendship, Panchadha, scoring, matrices, full chart, nodes, assessment.\n\n**Common difficulty.** Learners often feel that \"Moon–Mercury\" should have one answer. Ask them to say each planet's opinion aloud before giving the category.\n\n**Discussion prompts.** (1) Why would a tradition record a one-sided friendship? (2) What does a software engine lose if it stores one value per pair? (3) Why is the Koota limited to natural friendship?\n\n**Assessment evidence.** Look for the written both-direction reading in the Learning Journal. Do not credit a score without the two lords and both directions.\n\n**Boundaries.** This module teaches a traditional framework. It does not predict and it quotes no verse that has not been verified.\n\n## Validation\nThe test report runs the engine against the required cases, the full 7 × 7 and 12 × 12 matrices, the ten examples, layer independence, node exclusion and invalid input. It also compares all 144 sign pairs against the Calculation Lab's Graha Maitri row.\n{{gmtests}}\n","07":"# Day 7 – Gana Koota\n\n## Learn\n\n### Welcome to Day 7\n**Module 7: Gana Koota (गण कूट) – the temperament-category factor of Ashtakoota.** Day 7 of 14 · Foundation to Advanced · 180–210 minutes (self-paced) · Instructor: Anoopam Shukla.\n\nToday you learn Gana as a **three-way classification of the 27 Nakshatras, read in a direction**: the bride's Gana is the row of a 3 × 3 table and the groom's Gana is the column. You will assign Ganas, score all nine combinations, understand why the table is not symmetric, separate the **score** from the **Dosha flag** and from the **exception status**, and learn to describe the traditional temperament symbolism **without making claims about real people**.\n\n> **Central teaching principle.** Allocate first, relate second, score third, flag fourth, consider exceptions fifth, interpret last. A scoring profile can change the score; it can never change which Gana a Nakshatra belongs to.\n\n### The one idea to keep in mind all day\nSix layers must stay separate:\n1. **Allocation:** Nakshatra → Gana (nine Nakshatras in each of three Ganas). Fixed; no profile changes it.\n2. **Relationship:** the bride's Gana paired with the groom's Gana (nine ordered combinations).\n3. **Score:** the value a chosen profile's matrix gives to that combination (0 to 6).\n4. **Dosha flag:** a prompt for study, for example a low score or a Rakshasa-with-another-Gana mix. It is **not** the same thing as the score.\n5. **Exception status:** categories some manuals mention; here always \"identified, not applied\" unless a school, a source and conditions are recorded. An exception **never alters the raw score**.\n6. **Interpretation:** symbolic, responsible language that never describes a real person.\n\n### What you will be able to do by the end of the day\n1. Explain the meaning of Gana (गण) and why it carries 6 points.\n2. Name the three Ganas and state the nine Nakshatras of each.\n3. Describe the traditional symbolic temperament of each Gana, and say clearly what that symbolism is **not**.\n4. Read the 3 × 3 matrix with the correct direction (row = bride, column = groom).\n5. Score all nine combinations in the active profile (Profile A) and explain the asymmetry.\n6. Compare Profile A, the earlier draft (A0) and Profile B, and notice that A is B with the roles swapped.\n7. Explain Gana Dosha, its three flag types and why a flag is not a verdict.\n8. Describe the exception categories without applying them silently.\n9. Discuss the historical gendered framing of the table and how to use neutral labels.\n10. Use the 27 × 27 matrix and the calculator, including \"show calculation\".\n11. Complete the exercises and the final assessment.\n12. Explain why Gana can never be a stand-alone verdict.\n\n### Your learning route (ten stages)\nIntroduction → Three Ganas → Allocation → Nine Combinations → Scoring Matrix → Profiles → Dosha → Exceptions → Matrix and Calculator → Assessment. Tick each stage when you finish it; the tracker remembers where you stopped.\n{{g7dash}}\n\n### Prerequisites and quick revision\nYou should know: the 27 Nakshatras in order; how to find a person's Janma Nakshatra (Day 2); and that Day 3 to Day 6 each read one Koota from the Moon's position. Tap a Nakshatra to see its number, longitude range, lord and Gana.\n{{g7revise}}\n\n### Suggested pace (self-paced)\n| Block | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Meaning, the three Ganas and the allocation | 35 |\n| The nine combinations and the matrix | 35 |\n| Profiles and school differences | 25 |\n| Dosha, exceptions and responsible interpretation | 30 |\n| Matrix, calculator and exercises | 35 |\n| Assessment and revision | 20 |\n| Total | 180 |\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### Lesson 1 – Introduction to Gana Koota\n**1.1 Meaning.** *Gana* (गण) means \"group\", \"class\" or \"troop\". In matching it names one of three classes into which the 27 Nakshatras are divided: **Deva** (देव, divine), **Manushya** (मनुष्य, human) and **Rakshasa** (राक्षस, fierce, traditionally rendered \"demonic\").\n\n**1.2 What Gana Koota examines.** (a) Each person's **Janma Nakshatra** is assigned a Gana. (b) The bride's Gana and the groom's Gana are paired. (c) A matrix gives a score from 0 to 6. (d) Low scores, especially those involving Rakshasa, may raise a flag called Gana Dosha. Gana is scored out of **6**, the sixth of the eight Kootas in the common Ashtakoota total of 36.\n\n**1.3 Traditional symbolic descriptions.**\n| Gana | Traditional symbolic temperament |\n|---|---|\n| Deva | Gentle, generous, harmonious |\n| Manushya | Balanced, worldly, practical |\n| Rakshasa | Forceful, assertive, intense |\nThese are **symbolic labels from the tradition**. The course does not claim that people born under a Nakshatra have these traits, and it never uses a Gana to judge a real person's character. \"Rakshasa\" in particular is a technical label, not an accusation.\n\n**1.4 Why a temperament factor at all?** Traditional matching aimed to ask whether two people's dispositions would be comfortable together. Gana is the Koota in which that idea is expressed symbolically. A symbolic category cannot measure disposition, which is why this course teaches it as a **traditional analytical factor**, never as a personality test.\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) What does Gana mean? (b) Name the three Ganas. (c) Which Nakshatra reference does Gana use?\n\n### Lesson 2 – The allocation of the 27 Nakshatras\nEach Gana has exactly nine Nakshatras. This is the course's allocation (every value is read from the data file, nothing is typed into the interface).\n| Gana | The nine Nakshatras |\n|---|---|\n| Deva | Ashwini, Mrigashira, Punarvasu, Pushya, Hasta, Swati, Anuradha, Shravana, Revati |\n| Manushya | Bharani, Rohini, Ardra, Purva Phalguni, Uttara Phalguni, Purva Ashadha, Uttara Ashadha, Purva Bhadrapada, Uttara Bhadrapada |\n| Rakshasa | Krittika, Ashlesha, Magha, Chitra, Vishakha, Jyeshtha, Mula, Dhanishta, Shatabhisha |\nOpen the **Allocation** tab to explore and self-test. The allocation is widely consistent across manuals (grade B/C), but no verse is verified in this course.\n\n**A memory aid, not a rule.** Within the sequence 1 to 27 the Ganas do not follow a simple repeating pattern like Tara, so the allocation must be learned or looked up; the engine looks it up from the data file for every calculation.\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### Lesson 3 – The 3 × 3 scoring matrix (read the direction first)\n**3.1 Orientation.** Read the matrix as **row = bride's Gana, column = groom's Gana**. Before using any table, find out which axis is which; a matrix read the wrong way round gives the wrong answer for every directional cell.\n\n**3.2 The active profile (Profile A).** This is the table supplied by the course author and adopted as the active profile. Its attribution to *Muhurta Chintamani* and *Bharatiya Jyotish* is a **lead, not a verified citation**.\n| Bride ↓ / Groom → | Deva | Manushya | Rakshasa |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Deva | 6 | 5 | 1 |\n| Manushya | 6 | 6 | 0 |\n| Rakshasa | 0 | 0 | 6 |\n\n**3.3 The nine combinations (Profile A).**\n| # | Bride | Groom | Score | Kind |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Deva | Deva | 6 | same Gana |\n| 2 | Deva | Manushya | 5 | Deva and Manushya |\n| 3 | Deva | Rakshasa | 1 | involves Rakshasa |\n| 4 | Manushya | Deva | 6 | Deva and Manushya |\n| 5 | Manushya | Manushya | 6 | same Gana |\n| 6 | Manushya | Rakshasa | 0 | involves Rakshasa |\n| 7 | Rakshasa | Deva | 0 | involves Rakshasa |\n| 8 | Rakshasa | Manushya | 0 | involves Rakshasa |\n| 9 | Rakshasa | Rakshasa | 6 | same Gana |\n\n**3.4 What the matrix shows.** (a) The same Gana scores the full 6 every time. (b) The table is **asymmetric**: Deva bride with Manushya groom scores 5, but Manushya bride with Deva groom scores 6; Deva bride with Rakshasa groom scores 1, but Rakshasa bride with Deva groom scores 0. (c) Manushya with Rakshasa is 0 in both directions. (d) Across the 729 ordered Nakshatra pairs, the scores are 6 in 324 pairs, 5 in 81, 1 in 81 and 0 in 243.\n\n**3.5 The direction is part of the data.** Swapping bride and groom is **not** a neutral operation in this table. The calculator shows both readings side by side so the effect is never hidden.\n\n### Lesson 4 – From nine cells to 729 pairs\nBecause every Nakshatra belongs to exactly one Gana, the 27 × 27 matrix is just the 3 × 3 matrix expanded: each of the nine cells occupies a block of 9 × 9 = 81 Nakshatra pairs. The Matrix tab generates all 729 cells from the engine. Rows are the bride's Nakshatra, columns the groom's. The same Nakshatra (the diagonal) always scores 6.\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### Lesson 5 – Profiles and why published tables differ\n**5.1 The principle.** Gana tables are **not consistently reproduced** across manuals. This course therefore keeps several **software configuration profiles for transparent comparison**; they are not established classical schools, and none is source-verified.\n\n| Profile | Matrix (bride row, groom column) | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| A (active) | 6,5,1 / 6,6,0 / 0,0,6 | Supplied by the course author; attribution to *Muhurta Chintamani* / *Bharatiya Jyotish* is an unverified lead |\n| A0 | 6,6,1 / 5,6,0 / 0,0,6 | The earlier draft table used in this course before Profile A was supplied; kept for comparison |\n| B | 6,6,0 / 5,6,0 / 1,0,6 | Supplied \"Maitreya-style\" table; attribution unverified |\n| C | Editable copy of A | User-defined |\n| D | 6,5,0 / 5,6,0 / 0,0,6 | Research model with a symmetric matrix; research only |\n\n**5.2 A finding worth knowing.** Profile A is **exactly Profile B with bride and groom swapped** (the transpose). Under A the Deva bride / Manushya groom cell is 5; under B the same cell is 6. This suggests (but does not prove) that two sources may differ only in which axis they call the bride. That is a **lead to check against sources**, not a conclusion, and the course must not transpose a table silently to reconcile them.\n\n**5.3 How much do the profiles differ?** A and A0 differ in 2 of 9 cells (162 of 729 ordered pairs). A and B differ in 4 of 9 cells (324 of 729). The three tables each sum to 30 across their nine cells. Use the Profiles tab to compare any two.\n\n**5.4 Other differences among manuals.** Regional ten-factor systems (for example the Tamil \"ten Poruthams\") treat Gana differently and are separate profiles; they are not merged into Tradition A. Some manuals allow partial scores for particular pairs; some treat only certain Rakshasa combinations as Dosha; some give a different weight to the direction. None of these is verified here.\n\n**5.5 Rule for reports.** Always name the profile and its status. A report that gives a Gana score without naming the table is incomplete.\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### Lesson 6 – Gana Dosha\n**6.1 What is Gana Dosha (गण दोष)?** In traditional assessment, an unfavourable Gana combination, most commonly one in which a **Rakshasa** is paired with a Deva or a Manushya. The course uses three **separate** flags:\n| Flag | Meaning | Trigger |\n|---|---|---|\n| Low-score flag | The score is at or below a threshold | Default: score of 0 or 1 (configurable) |\n| Traditional flag | A Rakshasa is paired with a non-Rakshasa | Rakshasa mix |\n| Research warning | The profile is unverified or differs from another profile | Always shown with unverified profiles |\nUnder Profile A the first two flags coincide for the four mixed Rakshasa cells (1, 0, 0, 0). In another table or with another threshold they can differ, which is why they are stored separately.\n\n**6.2 Dosha is not the score.** A pair can have a low score and a flag, a flag and a moderate score, or neither. Keep the three fields apart in your calculation sheet.\n\n**6.3 Dosha is not a verdict.** A flag is a prompt for study and discussion. Never write that a flagged pair \"should not marry\", and never describe a person as a \"Rakshasa\".\n\n### Lesson 7 – Exceptions and mitigation\n**7.1 The rule.** No sourced, agreed cancellation of Gana Dosha was verified for this course. Categories that **some manuals mention**: friendly or identical Rashi lords (Graha Maitri favourable); a favourable Bhakoot; strong results in other Kootas; same Nakshatra with a different Pada or the same Rashi; supportive Navamsha and complete-chart factors (the Day 13 layer).\n\n**7.2 The status words.** Every category is reported as **\"identified, not applied\"** unless a school, a source and exact conditions are recorded. Even then, **the raw score is not changed**; the exception is reported in a separate field. A good score in another Koota does not cancel a Gana result.\n\n**7.3 Engine behaviour.** The engine identifies categories only when a flag is raised; it applies a rule only if the user supplies a rule with a school and a source; and the raw score is returned unchanged in every case (this is tested).\n\n**7.4 Required caution (shown by the software).**\n> *Gana Koota is one component of traditional compatibility analysis. It is a symbolic temperament factor and it should not independently determine marriage suitability or describe the real personalities of the people concerned.*\n\n### Lesson 8 – The gender-based framing, historically and responsibly\n**8.1 What the table assumes.** The traditional matrix is organised as \"bride's Gana\" and \"groom's Gana\". It comes from a historical setting in which marriage was described from the groom's household's point of view and the bride was the one who moved. The asymmetry of the scores (for example 5 versus 6 for Deva with Manushya) is therefore recorded in gendered terms.\n\n**8.2 What the course does not claim.** The course does not claim to know why the table is asymmetric. Explanations offered in popular sources (about adaptation, household roles or temperament) are **hypotheses, not verified reasoning**, and none is endorsed here. The asymmetry is a property of the supplied table, and Lesson 5 shows that two tables can be exact transposes of one another, which warns against reading too much into the direction.\n\n**8.3 How to use the table responsibly today.**\n- Treat \"bride / groom\" as **labels for the row and column**. The calculator lets you use \"Person A (row) / Person B (column)\".\n- When the pair is not a bride and groom, or the tradition's roles do not apply, say which person was read as the row and why, and show the swapped result as well.\n- Never describe the direction as a statement about one person being \"better\" or \"more suited to adapt\".\n- Present the asymmetric result as \"this table gives different values for the two readings\", then discuss the people, not the Gana.\n\n**8.4 Language for reports.** Acceptable: \"Under Profile A (a supplied table, unverified), the pair scores 0 of 6 with the roles as entered and 1 of 6 with the roles swapped; the low-score flag is raised; exception categories are identified, not applied.\" Not acceptable: \"The groom is a Rakshasa, so the match is bad.\"\n\n## Examples\n\n### Worked examples (all calculated live by the engine)\nFive worked examples and five additional directional cases, with Profiles A, A0 and B and the swapped-roles score. Open each row for the five-step working.\n{{g7examples}}\n\n### Worked example in words\n**Rohini (bride) and Hasta (groom).** Step 1: Rohini is Manushya; Hasta is Deva. Step 2: Manushya bride, Deva groom. Step 3: matrix row Manushya, column Deva = **6**. Roles swapped (Deva bride, Manushya groom): 5. Flags: none. The same pair appears in Day 11 and Day 13, where the Gana contribution is 6 in the total of 25.\n\n**Krittika (bride) and Ashwini (groom).** Rakshasa bride, Deva groom = **0**; swapped = 1. Low-score flag and Rakshasa-mix flag both raised; five exception categories identified, not applied. Under Profile B the same pair scores 1, not 0.\n\n### Corrections to the supplied material shown in the Common Mistakes tab\nSee the References tab for the table of corrections.\n\n## Practice\n\n### Lesson 9 – Practical work\n**Guided steps.** (1) Learn the three lists of nine. (2) Do ten allocations by hand. (3) Score ten pairs from the 3 × 3 table, always saying which person is the row. (4) Swap the roles and compare. (5) Raise the flags and list exception categories without applying them. (6) Verify in the calculator. (7) Do the exercises and the final assessment.\n\n### Written task\nIn your Learning Journal write a six-line professional note on one pair that (a) names the profile and its status, (b) gives both Ganas and the row/column assignment, (c) states the score with the roles as entered and swapped, (d) states the flags separately, (e) lists exception categories as identified, not applied, (f) uses no statement about the real character of either person.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What does Gana (गण) mean?\nA: Group or class. In matching, one of three classes of Nakshatras: Deva, Manushya, Rakshasa.\n\nQ: Name the three Ganas.\nA: Deva (divine), Manushya (human), Rakshasa (fierce, traditionally \"demonic\").\n\nQ: How many Nakshatras are in each Gana?\nA: Nine in each: 27 in total.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Deva?\nA: Ashwini, Mrigashira, Punarvasu, Pushya, Hasta, Swati, Anuradha, Shravana, Revati.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Manushya?\nA: Bharani, Rohini, Ardra, Purva Phalguni, Uttara Phalguni, Purva Ashadha, Uttara Ashadha, Purva Bhadrapada, Uttara Bhadrapada.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Rakshasa?\nA: Krittika, Ashlesha, Magha, Chitra, Vishakha, Jyeshtha, Mula, Dhanishta, Shatabhisha.\n\nQ: What is the maximum Gana score?\nA: 6 of the 36 points in Ashtakoota.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra reference does Gana use?\nA: Each person's Janma Nakshatra (the Moon's Nakshatra at birth).\n\nQ: How is the matrix read?\nA: Row = bride's Gana; column = groom's Gana.\n\nQ: What is the traditional symbolic temperament of Deva?\nA: Gentle, generous, harmonious; a symbolic label, not a description of a person.\n\nQ: What is the traditional symbolic temperament of Manushya?\nA: Balanced, worldly, practical; symbolic only.\n\nQ: What is the traditional symbolic temperament of Rakshasa?\nA: Forceful, assertive, intense; symbolic only, and never an accusation.\n\nQ: What does the same-Gana pair score?\nA: 6 in every profile of this course.\n\nQ: What does a Deva bride with a Manushya groom score in Profile A?\nA: 5.\n\nQ: What does a Manushya bride with a Deva groom score in Profile A?\nA: 6.\n\nQ: What does a Deva bride with a Rakshasa groom score in Profile A?\nA: 1.\n\nQ: What does a Rakshasa bride with a Deva groom score in Profile A?\nA: 0.\n\nQ: What does a Manushya and Rakshasa pair score in Profile A?\nA: 0 in both directions.\n\nQ: Why is the matrix called asymmetric?\nA: Swapping bride and groom changes the value for Deva/Manushya (5 and 6) and Deva/Rakshasa (1 and 0).\n\nQ: What is Profile A?\nA: The active course profile: 6,5,1 / 6,6,0 / 0,0,6, supplied by the course author; its attribution is an unverified lead.\n\nQ: What is Profile A0?\nA: The earlier draft table (6,6,1 / 5,6,0 / 0,0,6), kept for comparison.\n\nQ: What is Profile B?\nA: The supplied Maitreya-style table (6,6,0 / 5,6,0 / 1,0,6); attribution unverified.\n\nQ: How are Profiles A and B related?\nA: A is exactly B with bride and groom swapped (the transpose); a lead about axis convention, not a proven explanation.\n\nQ: What is Gana Dosha?\nA: An unfavourable Gana combination, most often a Rakshasa with a Deva or Manushya; a flag for study, not a verdict.\n\nQ: Name the three Gana flags used in the course.\nA: Low-score flag, traditional (Rakshasa-mix) flag, research warning.\n\nQ: Is a Gana Dosha flag the same as a score of 0?\nA: No. The score, the flag and the exception status are three separate fields.\n\nQ: What may happen to a Dosha through exceptions?\nA: Categories some manuals mention are identified but not applied unless a school, a source and conditions are recorded; the raw score never changes.\n\nQ: Can a good Nadi or Bhakoot score cancel a Gana result?\nA: Not by itself. It may be listed as a category some manuals mention; it is not applied automatically.\n\nQ: Does Gana describe a real person's character?\nA: No. It is a traditional symbolic category; the course never uses it to judge a person.\n\nQ: Why must the direction be stated in every report?\nA: Because the table is asymmetric; the same two people can give two different values depending on which is the row.\n\nQ: How many ordered Nakshatra pairs are there and how many does each Gana cell cover?\nA: 729 pairs; each of the nine cells covers 81 pairs.\n\nQ: How should the Gana score be reported?\nA: Name the profile and its status, give both Ganas and the direction, give the score and the swapped score, state flags and exception status separately, and add the caution.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: Gana (गण) literally means:\nA) Sign\nB) Group or class\nC) Planet\nD) Star\nANS: B\nWHY: Gana means group or class; here one of three Nakshatra classes.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The three Ganas are:\nA) Deva, Manushya, Rakshasa\nB) Sattva, Rajas, Tamas\nC) Fire, Earth, Air\nD) Sampat, Vipat, Kshema\nANS: A\nWHY: Deva, Manushya and Rakshasa.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many Nakshatras does each Gana contain?\nA) Seven\nB) Nine\nC) Twelve\nD) Thirteen\nANS: B\nWHY: 27 Nakshatras divide into three groups of nine.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Gana Koota carries how many points?\nA) 4\nB) 5\nC) 6\nD) 7\nANS: C\nWHY: Gana is the sixth Koota, worth 6 of 36 points.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Gana is determined from:\nA) The ascendant\nB) Each person's Janma Nakshatra\nC) The seventh house\nD) The Navamsha\nANS: B\nWHY: The Moon's Nakshatra at birth is the reference.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra is Deva?\nA) Krittika\nB) Hasta\nC) Rohini\nD) Mula\nANS: B\nWHY: Hasta is in the Deva list; Rohini is Manushya; Krittika and Mula are Rakshasa.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra is Rakshasa?\nA) Pushya\nB) Bharani\nC) Chitra\nD) Revati\nANS: C\nWHY: Chitra is in the Rakshasa list.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra is Manushya?\nA) Ardra\nB) Ashlesha\nC) Swati\nD) Shatabhisha\nANS: A\nWHY: Ardra is Manushya; Ashlesha and Shatabhisha are Rakshasa; Swati is Deva.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: In the course matrix, the rows represent:\nA) The groom's Gana\nB) The bride's Gana\nC) The Moon's sign\nD) The Nakshatra lord\nANS: B\nWHY: Rows are the bride; columns are the groom.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Profile A scores a Deva bride with a Deva groom as:\nA) 0\nB) 1\nC) 5\nD) 6\nANS: D\nWHY: Same-Gana pairs score the full 6.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Profile A scores a Deva bride with a Manushya groom as:\nA) 5\nB) 6\nC) 1\nD) 0\nANS: A\nWHY: Row Deva, column Manushya = 5.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Profile A scores a Manushya bride with a Deva groom as:\nA) 5\nB) 6\nC) 1\nD) 0\nANS: B\nWHY: Row Manushya, column Deva = 6; the table is asymmetric.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Profile A scores a Rakshasa bride with a Deva groom as:\nA) 6\nB) 5\nC) 1\nD) 0\nANS: D\nWHY: Row Rakshasa, column Deva = 0.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Profile A scores a Deva bride with a Rakshasa groom as:\nA) 6\nB) 5\nC) 1\nD) 0\nANS: C\nWHY: Row Deva, column Rakshasa = 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Profile A scores a Manushya bride with a Rakshasa groom as:\nA) 0\nB) 1\nC) 5\nD) 6\nANS: A\nWHY: Row Manushya, column Rakshasa = 0.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Bride Ashwini, groom Bharani under Profile A:\nA) 6\nB) 5\nC) 1\nD) 0\nANS: B\nWHY: Ashwini is Deva and Bharani is Manushya: row Deva, column Manushya = 5.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Bride Rohini, groom Hasta under Profile A:\nA) 5\nB) 1\nC) 0\nD) 6\nANS: D\nWHY: Rohini is Manushya and Hasta is Deva: row Manushya, column Deva = 6.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Bride Krittika, groom Ashwini under Profile A:\nA) 0\nB) 1\nC) 5\nD) 6\nANS: A\nWHY: Krittika is Rakshasa and Ashwini is Deva: row Rakshasa, column Deva = 0.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Bride Mula, groom Dhanishta under Profile A:\nA) 0\nB) 1\nC) 6\nD) 5\nANS: C\nWHY: Both are Rakshasa; the same Gana gives 6, so a same-Gana Rakshasa pair is not a low-score pair.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: How are Profile A and Profile B related?\nA) Unrelated\nB) A is B with bride and groom swapped\nC) Identical\nD) A is B doubled\nANS: B\nWHY: The transpose of B's matrix equals A's matrix.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: What does the previous answer prove?\nA) That B is classical and A is not\nB) That a difference of axis convention is a lead, not a proof\nC) That both are verified\nD) That the scores never matter\nANS: B\nWHY: It suggests a possible convention difference; it must be checked against sources.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Profile A0 is:\nA) The active profile\nB) The earlier draft table kept for comparison\nC) A symmetric research model\nD) A Tamil system\nANS: B\nWHY: A0 is the earlier draft table, kept as a comparison profile.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: How many of the 729 ordered pairs score 6 under Profile A?\nA) 81\nB) 243\nC) 324\nD) 486\nANS: C\nWHY: Four cells score 6, each covering 81 pairs.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Under Profile A, a Gana Dosha low-score flag (threshold 1) is raised for:\nA) Deva bride, Manushya groom\nB) Rakshasa bride, Manushya groom\nC) Manushya bride, Deva groom\nD) Rakshasa bride, Rakshasa groom\nANS: B\nWHY: The score is 0; the others score 5 or 6.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Gana Dosha is best described as:\nA) A final verdict\nB) A flag for study and discussion\nC) A medical finding\nD) A guarantee of difficulty\nANS: B\nWHY: It is one traditional indicator and never decides suitability alone.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A score of 0 and a Dosha flag are:\nA) Always the same thing\nB) Separate fields\nC) Irrelevant\nD) Identical to the exception\nANS: B\nWHY: The course keeps score, flag and exception status separate.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: An exception category may be applied:\nA) Always\nB) Only when school, source and conditions are recorded\nC) Whenever the total is high\nD) Never discussed\nANS: B\nWHY: Otherwise it is reported as identified, not applied.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Applying an exception to a Gana result:\nA) Changes the raw score\nB) Leaves the raw score unchanged\nC) Changes the Nakshatra\nD) Changes the Gana\nANS: B\nWHY: Exceptions are reported in a separate field; the raw score is never altered.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Changing the scoring profile changes:\nA) The Gana allocation\nB) Only the scores and flags\nC) The Nakshatra\nD) The Moon's position\nANS: B\nWHY: Allocation is fixed; profiles only supply matrices.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which statement about the symbolism is correct?\nA) Rakshasa describes a cruel person\nB) It is a traditional category and not a description of a real person\nC) Deva people are always gentle\nD) Manushya people are ordinary\nANS: B\nWHY: The symbolism is traditional and never a claim about real people.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which phrase is acceptable in a report?\nA) \"The groom is a Rakshasa, so it is a bad match\"\nB) \"Under the named profile the pair scores 0 of 6; the flag is raised and exceptions are identified, not applied\"\nC) \"Success is guaranteed\"\nD) \"The marriage will fail\"\nANS: B\nWHY: It reports the status honestly without describing people.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Why must the bride/groom direction be stated?\nA) It is decorative\nB) The table is asymmetric, so the roles change the value\nC) It changes the Nakshatra\nD) It changes the Rashi\nANS: B\nWHY: Swapping roles can change a score by one or more points.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which reading of the asymmetry is acceptable?\nA) It proves one partner is more adaptable\nB) The cause is not verified here; explanations are hypotheses\nC) It is proved by Muhurta Chintamani\nD) It has no effect on scores\nANS: B\nWHY: No source is verified; popular explanations are unproven hypotheses.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which statement is correct?\nA) Gana alone decides suitability\nB) Gana Koota is one component of traditional analysis\nC) Gana is the same as Nadi\nD) Gana is the same as Tara\nANS: B\nWHY: The required caution says it cannot decide suitability alone.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. Why must the direction (row = bride, column = groom) be stated whenever a Gana score is given?\nMA: The Gana table is asymmetric: for example Deva bride with Manushya groom scores 5 while Manushya bride with Deva groom scores 6 in Profile A, and Deva with Rakshasa scores 1 or 0 depending on the direction. Without the direction the score cannot be reproduced.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Why are the score, the Dosha flag and the exception status kept as three separate fields?\nMA: They answer different questions. The score is a table value; the flag is a prompt for study based on a rule such as a low score or a Rakshasa mix; the exception status records whether a mitigating category was merely identified or applied under a named school and source. Merging them hides the differences between schools and may let an unsourced exception change a result.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual 3. How should a report describe a Rakshasa-with-Deva pair responsibly?\nMA: It should name the profile and its status, give both Ganas and the direction, state the score and the swapped score, state the flags and the exception status separately, add the caution that Gana is a symbolic temperament category, and avoid any statement about the real character of either person.\nLVL: Advanced\n\n## References\n### Source status (Day 7)\nNo classical verse or chapter has been verified for any Gana rule in this module.\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nine Nakshatras in each of three Ganas | B/C | Widely consistent in manuals; no verse verified |\n| Symbolic temperament of the three Ganas | C | Commonly stated; symbolism only |\n| Profile A matrix (6,5,1 / 6,6,0 / 0,0,6) | C/D | Supplied by the course author; attribution to *Muhurta Chintamani* / *Bharatiya Jyotish* is an unverified lead |\n| Profile B matrix (6,6,0 / 5,6,0 / 1,0,6) | C/D | Supplied \"Maitreya-style\" table; attribution unverified |\n| Profile A0 matrix | n/a | Earlier course draft; software record |\n| Profile A is the transpose of Profile B | n/a | Arithmetic observation about the supplied tables |\n| Exception categories | C/E | Mentioned in some manuals; no sourced rule verified |\n| Asymmetry rationale | E | No verified source; hypotheses only |\n| Regional comparison leads (for example a Telugu calendar-based comparison) | E | Unverified lead; no content adopted |\n\n### Corrections to supplied material\n| Supplied | Correct |\n|---|---|\n| An earlier course table: Deva bride with Manushya groom scored 6 and Manushya bride with Deva groom scored 5 | Profile A (the active, supplied table) gives 5 and 6 respectively. The earlier table is kept as Profile A0. Day 11 and Day 14 sample totals were recomputed: Day 11 and Day 14 pair 1 now total **25** (was 24; the Janma variant is 22, was 21), and Day 14 pair 3 now totals **33** (was 34) |\n| \"Rakshasa\" explained as demonic people | A neutral technical label; never applied to a real person |\n| A Dosha equals a score of 0 | Score, flag and exception status are separate fields |\n| An exception cancels a Dosha | Categories are identified, not applied, unless school, source and conditions are recorded; the raw score never changes |\n| Gana tables in all manuals agree | They differ; Profile A is the transpose of Profile B, which may be a convention difference (a lead) |\n\n### Research and source policy\nOnline explanatory pages may be used only as secondary support. For every classical passage the course would cite it must record: text name, author or attribution, chapter, verse, edition or translator, the exact rule supported, and the confidence of verification. Where the exact source cannot be verified, the statement is labelled **\"Traditional practice: source attribution requires verification.\"** Leads still to be checked: *Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra* (edition, chapter and verse), *Muhurta Chintamani* (the marriage passages), *Bharatiya Jyotish* (the supplied reference), *Daivajna Vallabha*, Maitreya as supplied, and regional manuals. No quotation, verse or chapter number is given here.\n\n## Three Ganas\n### Lesson 1 (interactive) – one learning page for each Gana\nChoose a Gana to see its meaning, its symbolic temperament, its role, what it does **not** mean and its nine Nakshatras.\n{{g7ganas}}\n\n## Allocation\n### Lesson 2 (interactive) – the 27 Nakshatras and their Ganas\nSearch, filter by Gana or hide the Gana column to test yourself.\n{{g7alloc}}\n\n## Nine Combinations\n### Lesson 3 (interactive) – the 3 × 3 matrix and the nine combinations\nChoose a profile, tap a cell, and see the reverse reading. The transposed view is for comparison only.\n{{g7nine}}\n\n## Profiles\n### Lesson 5 (interactive) – Profiles A, A0, B, C and D\nCompare the tables, see the difference report and edit your own Profile C.\n{{g7profiles}}\n\n## Dosha Explorer\n### Lessons 6 and 7 (interactive) – flags and exception categories\nChange the profile and the threshold; the score, the flags and the exception status stay separate.\n{{g7dosha}}\n\n## Matrix\n### The complete 27 × 27 Gana matrix\nAll 729 cells are generated by the engine. Rows are the bride's Nakshatra, columns the groom's. Search a Nakshatra, tap a cell for the working, show both directions, export CSV or print.\n{{g7matrix}}\n\n## Calculator\n### Gana Compatibility Calculator\nChoose two Nakshatras and a profile. The five-step calculation is always shown; the roles can be swapped with one button.\n{{g7calc}}\n\n## Exercises\n### Lesson 9 – five practical exercises\nAllocation; score reading; flags; direction and profile comparison; a case study.\n{{g7exercise}}\n\n## Final Assessment\n### Lesson 10 – the 30-question final assessment\n10 theory, 10 score-reading, 5 allocation and 5 interpretation questions. Pass mark 70%. A failed attempt never erases your completed lessons.\n{{g7final}}\n\n## Common Mistakes\nTen mistakes to recognise: the incorrect method, the corrected method and a worked example.\n| # | Mistake | Correct method | Worked example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Reading the matrix with the roles reversed | Row = bride, column = groom | Deva bride, Manushya groom is 5, not 6 |\n| 2 | Not stating the profile | Name the profile and its status | \"Profile A, unverified\" |\n| 3 | Treating Gana symbolism as personality | Symbolic category only | Never \"the groom is a Rakshasa\" |\n| 4 | Equating Dosha with a zero score | Keep score, flag and exception status apart | Deva bride, Rakshasa groom scores 1 and is flagged |\n| 5 | Calling Rakshasa–Rakshasa a Dosha | Same Gana scores 6 and the Rakshasa-mix flag is not raised | Mula and Dhanishta = 6 |\n| 6 | Applying an exception silently | Identify only; apply with school, source, conditions | \"Identified, not applied\" |\n| 7 | Changing the score because of an exception | The raw score is never altered | 0 stays 0 with the exception listed |\n| 8 | Transposing a table silently to reconcile sources | State the orientation of each source | Profile A is B transposed |\n| 9 | Using a wrong Gana because of a near name | Look it up | Ashlesha is Rakshasa; Ashwini is Deva |\n| 10 | Treating Gana as a verdict | One component only | Add the caution to every report |\n\n## Instructor Guide\n*For teachers and self-study students.*\n\n### Suggested time allocation (180 minutes)\n| Section | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Meaning, three Ganas and allocation | 35 |\n| Nine combinations and matrix | 35 |\n| Profiles and schools | 25 |\n| Dosha, exceptions and responsible interpretation | 30 |\n| Matrix, calculator and exercises | 35 |\n| Assessment and revision | 20 |\n\n### Teaching sequence\n1. Open with \"three groups of nine\". 2. Have students learn the lists and test them in the Allocation tab. 3. Build the 3 × 3 table cell by cell, asking each time \"who is the row?\". 4. Show the asymmetry. 5. Compare A, A0 and B and reveal the transpose relationship as a lead. 6. Separate score, flag and exception. 7. Discuss the gender-based framing and neutral labels. 8. Calculator, matrix and assessment.\n\n### Core definitions\nGana; Deva, Manushya, Rakshasa; allocation; relationship; row and column; profile; Dosha flag; exception status; raw score.\n\n### Common student mistakes\nSee the Common Mistakes tab.\n\n### Calculation demonstrations\nAshwini and Bharani both ways; Krittika and Ashwini both ways; Rohini and Hasta; Mula and Dhanishta; an Ardra and Chitra pair under three profiles.\n\n### Discussion questions\n\"Why might two published tables be exact transposes?\" \"What would a responsible sentence about a flagged pair sound like?\" \"What does it mean that an exception never changes the raw score?\" \"How should the course treat a couple who are not a bride and a groom?\"\n\n### Advanced interpretation notes\nDirectional asymmetry; independence of allocation and scoring; why a flag threshold is configurable; why unverified attributions must not appear as citations.\n\n### Case-study instructions\nUse Exercise 5: students write a reading that names the profile and its status, gives both Ganas and the direction, states score, swapped score, flags and exception status separately, and avoids any statement about real character.\n\n### Assessment answer key\nFinal assessment questions are generated and marked by the engine; the theory questions carry explanations after submission.\n\n## Validation\n### Technical validation report\nAutomatic tests of the Gana engine. Every row runs when this page opens.\n{{g7tests}}\n","08":"# Day 8 – Bhakoot Koota\n\n## Learn\n\n### Welcome to Day 8\n**Module 8: Bhakoot Koota (भकूट कूट) – the Moon-sign relationship, scored 7 or 0.** Day 8 of 14 · Foundation to Advanced · 150–180 minutes (self-paced) · Instructor: Anoopam Shukla.\n\nToday you learn Bhakoot as a **two-direction counting relationship between two Moon signs (Chandra Rashi, चंद्र राशि)**. You will count from the sign of Person A to the sign of Person B, count back from B to A, see why the two counts always add up to 14, classify the pair, and only then read the score. You will also study the famous *cancellation* claims honestly: what is claimed, who repeats it, and how little of it is verified.\n\n> **Central teaching principle.** Count first, classify second, score third, interpret last. The arithmetic never changes with the rule profile. Only the *reading* of a Dosha can change, and then only under a profile you chose and can name.\n\n### The three layers (keep them apart all day)\n1. **Calculation layer:** inclusive counting from sign to sign, forward and reverse. Pure arithmetic.\n2. **Scoring layer:** the relationship type gives a **raw score of 7 or 0**. The raw score is never edited.\n3. **Interpretation layer:** Bhakoot Dosha (भकूट दोष) and its *possible* exceptions (Parihara, परिहार). Every exception is an unverified research candidate with an evidence label, and an adjusted score exists only under a profile that explicitly allows it, always shown beside the raw score.\n\n### What you will be able to do by the end of the day\n1. Explain what Bhakoot compares and why Moon signs (not Nakshatras) are used.\n2. Name the twelve signs in order with their lords.\n3. Count inclusively, forward and reverse, between any two signs.\n4. State and prove why two different signs always give counts adding up to 14.\n5. Classify every pair into 1/1, 7/7, 3/11, 4/10, 2/12, 5/9 or 6/8.\n6. Name the three Dosha types: Dwirdwadasha (द्वितीय-द्वादश), Navapanchama (नव-पंचम) and Shadashtaka (षडाष्टक).\n7. Fill in the 12 × 12 matrix: 72 adverse and 72 favourable ordered pairs.\n8. Explain the same-lord (Ekadhipatya, एकाधिपत्य) and mutual-friendship claims, and their evidence status.\n9. Use the Navamsha-lord condition and the other additional conditions as *declared* inputs.\n10. Compare rule profiles A to E and explain what each does and does not change.\n11. Use the calculator, produce a report and read the validation report.\n12. Complete the exercises and the 30-question final assessment.\n13. Communicate a Bhakoot result responsibly, without fear or certainty.\n\n### Your learning route (ten stages)\nProgress → Introduction → Counting → Classification → Scoring → Bhakoot Dosha → Exceptions → Profiles → Calculator → Practical → Assessment. Tick each stage when you finish it; the tracker remembers where you stopped.\n{{b8dash}}\n\n### Prerequisites and quick revision\nYou should know: the twelve sidereal signs in order (Day 1); how the Moon's longitude gives its sign and Nakshatra (Day 2); and why a Koota is a *convention* with a rule profile, not a law (Day 1). Quick table:\n\n| No. | Sign (Rashi, राशि) | Hindi | Lord |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Aries | मेष | Mars |\n| 2 | Taurus | वृषभ | Venus |\n| 3 | Gemini | मिथुन | Mercury |\n| 4 | Cancer | कर्क | Moon |\n| 5 | Leo | सिंह | Sun |\n| 6 | Virgo | कन्या | Mercury |\n| 7 | Libra | तुला | Venus |\n| 8 | Scorpio | वृश्चिक | Mars |\n| 9 | Sagittarius | धनु | Jupiter |\n| 10 | Capricorn | मकर | Saturn |\n| 11 | Aquarius | कुंभ | Saturn |\n| 12 | Pisces | मीन | Jupiter |\n\n### How to study this module\nRead each tab in order, but always **try the calculation before opening the answer**. Use the Counting Ring to see the counts, the Calculator to test pairs, and the Matrix to see the whole picture. The Corrections note in the Examples tab matters: two supplied example titles carry the query \"6/6?\" for Taurus–Libra and Cancer–Sagittarius. Verified by counting, both are **6/8**.\n\n> **Responsible-use note.** Bhakoot is one conventional score in one tradition. It describes a Moon-sign relationship; it does not predict the success or failure of any marriage, and it is not a medical, legal or financial statement.\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### Lesson 1 – What is Bhakoot Koota?\n**Bhakoot (भकूट)**, also called Rashi Koota in some presentations, is the seventh Koota of the common 36-point Ashtakoota scheme and carries **7 points**, the second-highest weight after Nadi (8). It looks at the *signs* occupied by the two Moons. The idea, as commonly explained, is that the counted distance between the two Moon signs describes a kind of emotional and practical alignment. This is a symbolic reading, not a measurement.\n\nThree things to remember:\n- Bhakoot uses the **Moon sign (Rashi)**, not the Nakshatra. Tara (Day 4) and Yoni, Gana and Nadi use the Nakshatra.\n- It gives only **two possible raw scores: 7 or 0**. There is no half-point.\n- The relationship is **symmetric in score**: the score of A with B equals the score of B with A, even though the two *counts* differ.\n\n### Lesson 2 – Counting from sign to sign\nWe number the signs Aries 1 to Pisces 12. The **inclusive count** from sign A to sign B counts both ends: if A and B are the same sign the count is 1.\n\nThe formula: `count(A to B) = ((B - A + 12) mod 12) + 1`.\n\n| From → To | Working | Count |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aries → Aries | same sign, counting itself | 1 |\n| Aries → Taurus | 1, 2 | 2 |\n| Taurus → Aries | 2, 3, … 12, 1 | 12 |\n| Aries → Libra | Aries is 1, Libra is 7 | 7 |\n| Aries → Scorpio | Aries 1 … Scorpio 8 | 8 |\n| Scorpio → Aries | 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 1 | 6 |\n\n**Why the counts add up to 14.** Going from A forward to B covers `d` steps, and going on from B forward to A covers `12 - d` steps. Counting inclusively adds one to each, so the two counts are `d + 1` and `(12 - d) + 1`, giving a total of 14. This holds for **every pair of different signs**. For the *same* sign the counts are 1 and 1 (sum 2). Consequently **a pair such as \"6/6\" can never happen**: if one count is 6, the other must be 8.\n\n### Lesson 3 – The seven pair types\nBecause the counts add up to 14, only seven pair types exist. We write the smaller count first.\n\n| Pair | Name | Hindi | Class | Raw score | Ordered pairs of signs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1/1 | Same sign | एक राशि | favourable | 7 | 12 |\n| 7/7 | Samasaptaka | समसप्तक | favourable | 7 | 12 |\n| 3/11 | Tritiya-Ekadasha | तृतीय-एकादश | favourable | 7 | 24 |\n| 4/10 | Chaturtha-Dashama | चतुर्थ-दशम | favourable | 7 | 24 |\n| 2/12 | Dwirdwadasha (Dwitiya-Dwadasha) | द्वितीय-द्वादश | adverse | 0 | 24 |\n| 5/9 | Navapanchama (Nav-Pancham) | नव-पंचम | adverse | 0 | 24 |\n| 6/8 | Shadashtaka | षडाष्टक | adverse | 0 | 24 |\n\nTotals: 12 + 12 + 24 + 24 = **72 favourable** ordered pairs and 24 + 24 + 24 = **72 adverse** ordered pairs, 144 in all. Among the **66** unordered pairs of *different* signs, 36 are adverse and 30 are favourable.\n\n> The names of the favourable types (Tritiya-Ekadasha, Chaturtha-Dashama) are descriptive labels used in this course. Only the three adverse names are widely used in manuals.\n\n### Lesson 4 – Bhakoot Dosha (भकूट दोष)\nWhen the raw score is 0, manuals say that **Bhakoot Dosha** is present, and name it after the relationship: Dwirdwadasha, Navapanchama or Shadashtaka. The traditional associations (financial strain, emotional distance, health concerns, family obstacles) are *symbolic statements repeated in manuals*, not measured outcomes. This course teaches you to **report the Dosha, name the pair, show the counts and avoid any prediction**.\n\n### Lesson 5 – The 12 × 12 matrix\nTwelve signs for Person A and twelve for Person B give 144 ordered pairs. Row A, column B holds the pair type. The matrix is **symmetric in classification** (A,B and B,A are the same pair type) but **not in the count** (the count from A to B differs from the count from B to A). The Matrix tab lets you see all 144 cells, each with a symbol and a text label, never colour alone.\n\n### The central idea in one sentence\nBhakoot is simple arithmetic: **count both ways, notice the pair, read the score, and treat every exception as an unverified candidate.**\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### Rule 1 – The counting algorithm\n1. Convert each Moon sign to its number (Aries 1 … Pisces 12).\n2. Compute the forward count `f = ((B - A + 12) mod 12) + 1`.\n3. Compute the reverse count `r = ((A - B + 12) mod 12) + 1`.\n4. Check that `f + r = 14` (or both are 1 for the same sign).\n5. Write the pair as `min(f, r) / max(f, r)`.\n\n### Rule 2 – The scoring rule (common convention)\n| Pair | Score |\n|---|---|\n| 1/1 | 7 |\n| 7/7 | 7 |\n| 3/11 | 7 |\n| 4/10 | 7 |\n| 2/12 | 0 |\n| 5/9 | 0 |\n| 6/8 | 0 |\n\nThis is the common Tradition A convention (evidence grade C: widely published in manuals, no classical verse verified in this course). The engine stores it in `engine/profiles/tradition-A.json` and the Calculation Lab uses it for the Bhakoot row.\n\n### Rule 3 – Direction is recorded, the pair is symmetric\nReport both counts, for example \"A to B 8, B to A 6, pair 6/8\". The notation \"8/6\" and \"6/8\" describe the same pair; the course writes the **smaller count first** for the pair name and keeps the directional counts separate.\n\n### Rule 4 – Same sign\nIf both Moons are in the same sign, the counts are 1 and 1, the pair is 1/1 and the raw score is 7. Do not confuse this with the Nadi topic \"same Rashi, different Nakshatra\" (Day 9), which is a different Koota.\n\n### Rule 5 – Worked formulas you should be able to do by hand\n| Task | Method | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Forward count | `((B - A + 12) mod 12) + 1` | Leo (5) → Capricorn (10): 6 |\n| Reverse count | 14 minus the forward count (different signs) | Capricorn → Leo: 8 |\n| Pair | smaller / larger | 6/8 |\n| Score | 7 for 1/1, 7/7, 3/11, 4/10; 0 for 2/12, 5/9, 6/8 | 0 |\n\n### Rule 6 – Quick recognition table\nA fast way to recognise the pair without counting: the sign **2nd or 12th** from the other is Dwirdwadasha; **5th or 9th**, Navapanchama; **6th or 8th**, Shadashtaka; **3rd or 11th** and **4th or 10th** are favourable; the **7th** from the other (opposite sign) is Samasaptaka and favourable.\n\n### Rule 7 – Boundary and input care\nBhakoot uses the Moon's *sign*. If the Moon is within a few arc-minutes of a sign boundary, a small difference in birth time or ayanamsha (Day 1 and Day 2) can change the sign and therefore the whole result. The Calculation Lab warns about this. Verify the Moon's longitude before trusting a Bhakoot reading.\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### Lesson 7 – Why there are rule profiles\nDifferent manuals agree on the scores (7 or 0) but differ on **what to do with a Dosha**. Some treat 2/12 and 5/9 as mild and 6/8 as serious; some cancel the Dosha when the lords are the same or friendly; some add the Navamsha lords; some say nothing. The course does **not merge or average** these views. It keeps them as separate, selectable rule profiles. A drop-down in the Calculator lets you choose one; the raw score is identical in every profile.\n\n### The five course profiles\n| Profile | Name | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | Strict raw | Only the raw 7 or 0. No exception is ever applied (default). |\n| B | Same-lord exception allowed | Adjusted 7 only if both signs share one lord. |\n| C | Same lord or mutually friendly lords allowed | Adds mutual natural friendship of the two lords. |\n| D | Extended | Adds the Navamsha-lord condition and requires a declared \"Nadi different\". |\n| E | Research flags only | Lists every candidate with its evidence label; never adjusts. |\n\n> These are **software configurations for teaching**, not classical schools. No profile is claimed to represent a named author.\n\n### What a profile can and cannot change\n- A profile **can** decide whether an *adjusted* score is displayed.\n- A profile **cannot** change the counts, the pair type, the raw score or the Dosha identification.\n- An adjusted score is **always labelled ADJUSTED** and shown with the raw score.\n\n### How sources differ (observed, grade D)\nA web search for contemporary explanations (details in References) found these differences:\n- Most sites say the Dosha is *cancelled* when the lords are the same or mutual friends; one source says friendship only *reduces* it.\n- Some sites say 5/9 and 2/12 are easier to waive than 6/8, which then needs mutual friendship; others apply one rule to all three.\n- Some sites add Navamsha lords; many add \"strong Moon\" or other conditions.\nThese are *presentations on astrology websites*, not verified primary texts.\n\n### Choosing a profile in practice\nStart with Profile A to understand the calculation. Use B or C to see what a commonly described exception would change. Use E when you want the full list of candidates. Never mix results from two profiles in one sentence, and always state the profile you used.\n\n### Two sources, one question\nImagine two manuals reading the pair Aries–Leo (5/9). Manual X says \"5/9 is cancelled when Mars and the Sun are friends\". Manual Y says nothing about cancellation. Under Profile A both give raw 0. Under Profile C, Manual X's reading gives an *adjusted 7* (Mars and the Sun are mutual friends in the natural table), shown beside the raw 0. That visible difference is the reason profiles exist.\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### Lesson 6 – Bhakoot Dosha cancellation (Parihara, परिहार): a research chapter\nEverything in this lesson is **identified, labelled and not applied automatically**. We use the course evidence scale:\n\n| Label | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| A | Primary text verified (chapter and verse checked) |\n| B | Reputable edition or commentary |\n| C | Common published manual |\n| D | Contemporary web explanation |\n| E | Unsourced, regional or oral claim |\n| UNVERIFIED | Default status of any claim not yet checked |\n\n### The candidate exceptions\n| Candidate | Claim | Evidence | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Same lord (Ekadhipatya, एकाधिपत्य) | Dosha is cancelled when both signs have the same lord | D | UNVERIFIED |\n| Mutually friendly lords (मैत्री) | Dosha is cancelled when the two lords are mutual friends | D | UNVERIFIED |\n| Navamsha lords | Dosha is cancelled when the Navamsha lords of the two Moons are the same or mutual friends | E | UNVERIFIED |\n| Strong Moons | Dosha is reduced when both Moons are strong | E | UNVERIFIED |\n| Tara and Vashya | Dosha is eased when Tara and Vashya are favourable | E | UNVERIFIED |\n| Nadi prerequisite | Relief only if Nadi Dosha is absent | E | UNVERIFIED |\n| Same Rashi, different Nakshatra | A Nadi topic, not a Bhakoot cancellation | E | not applicable |\n\n### What the numbers say (computed, not claimed)\nOf the **36 unordered adverse pairs of different signs**:\n- **3** share a lord: Aries–Scorpio (Mars), Taurus–Libra (Venus), Capricorn–Aquarius (Saturn).\n- **14** have mutually friendly lords (natural table), for example Aries–Leo (Mars and Sun).\n- **9** have a *one-sided* friendship, which is **not** mutual (for example Cancer–Sagittarius: Jupiter regards the Moon as a friend, but the Moon is neutral to Jupiter).\n- **10** have none of these.\nSo Profile B would give a candidate for 3 pairs, and Profile C for 17 of the 36. A profile that \"cancels\" nearly half of all Doshas deserves a careful look at its evidence.\n\n### Pairs that share a lord but need no exception\nGemini–Virgo (Mercury) and Sagittarius–Pisces (Jupiter) share a lord but are **4/10**, which already scores 7. There is nothing to cancel. A common beginner mistake is to say \"Gemini–Virgo Bhakoot Dosha is cancelled by same lord\". It is simply absent.\n\n### The decision engine (how the software thinks)\n1. Count both ways, classify the pair, give the raw score.\n2. If the raw score is 7, stop: no Dosha, no exception candidates.\n3. If the raw score is 0, identify the Dosha by name.\n4. List the candidates (same lord, mutual friends, Navamsha lords if both longitudes are supplied).\n5. Apply a candidate **only if the chosen profile allows it**. Profile D also needs a declared \"Nadi different\".\n6. Show the **raw score unchanged** and, if any, the **adjusted score labelled ADJUSTED**.\n7. Show the evidence label and the disclaimer.\n\n### Mutual versus one-sided friendship\n\"Mutual\" means **each** planet regards the other as a friend in the natural table. If only one does, the friendship is one-sided and the software reports it separately; it does not count as mutual. The Moon regards Mercury as a friend, but Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy: one-sided.\n\n### Navamsha lords\nEach sign is divided into nine Navamsha parts of 3°20′. The Moon's Navamsha sign has a lord; the claim is that the two Navamsha lords being the same or mutually friendly eases the Dosha. This needs both Moon longitudes and is graded E. Use the Lords and Navamsha tab to see the working. A Moon near a 3°20′ boundary can change the Navamsha with a very small error.\n\n### Responsible communication\nNever say \"your Bhakoot Dosha is cancelled\" as a fact. Say: \"Under Profile B the pair would be adjusted to 7, because both signs share the same lord; the raw score remains 0, and this rule is not verified from a primary text.\" Never present the Dosha as a prediction.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Fifteen worked examples\nEach row is computed live from the engine. Study the counts first, then the pair, then the raw score, then the optional adjusted result.\n{{b8examples}}\n\n### Corrections to supplied material\n| Item | Supplied statement | Correct | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Example 5 | Taurus–Libra \"6/6?\" (marked as a query to verify) | **6/8** (Taurus → Libra 6, Libra → Taurus 8) | Counts for different signs add up to 14, so 6/6 is impossible |\n| Example 10 | Cancer–Sagittarius \"6/6?\" (marked as a query to verify) | **6/8** (Cancer → Sagittarius 6, Sagittarius → Cancer 8) | Same reason |\n| Method | Verify all sign distances | Done by computation for all 144 ordered pairs | The brief asked for wrong titles to be corrected, not preserved; the engine counts every pair in both directions |\n| Score effect | none | Both pairs still score 0 | 6/8 is Shadashtaka; the raw score is unchanged by the correction |\n\n### Reading Example 4 slowly: Aries–Scorpio\nAries → Scorpio is **8** (Aries 1 … Scorpio 8). Scorpio → Aries is **6**. The pair is **6/8**, Shadashtaka, raw score **0**. Both signs are ruled by Mars. Profile B would show an *adjusted* 7 beside the raw 0, because of the same-lord candidate (evidence D, unverified).\n\n### Reading Example 15 slowly: Gemini–Virgo\nGemini → Virgo is 4 and Virgo → Gemini is 10: pair **4/10**, raw score **7**, no Dosha. Both signs share the lord Mercury, but because there is no Dosha, no exception is needed or reported.\n\n### A Navamsha example\nTake Person A's Moon at 3°20′ of Aries and Person B's Moon at 16°40′ of Scorpio. The signs form Aries–Scorpio (6/8, raw 0, same lord). The Navamsha signs are Taurus (lord Venus) and Sagittarius (lord Jupiter). Venus and Jupiter are not mutual friends, so the Navamsha candidate is **not** satisfied. Check it in the Lords and Navamsha tab.\n\n## Practice\n\n### A reliable method\n1. Write the two signs and their numbers.\n2. Compute the forward count; compute the reverse count by subtracting from 14 (or recount to check).\n3. Name the pair; look up the score.\n4. If the score is 0, name the Dosha.\n5. Look up the lords. Same? Mutual friends? One-sided?\n6. State the profile and the result in a full sentence with the raw score first.\n\n### Six practice problems (try before you read the answers)\n| Problem | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Libra and Pisces | Libra → Pisces 6, Pisces → Libra 8: 6/8, raw 0 (Shadashtaka) |\n| Gemini and Sagittarius | Gemini → Sagittarius 7, back 7: 7/7, raw 7 |\n| Virgo and Scorpio | Virgo → Scorpio 3, Scorpio → Virgo 11: 3/11, raw 7 |\n| Leo and Taurus | Leo → Taurus: ((2 − 5 + 12) mod 12) + 1 = 10; Taurus → Leo = 4; pair 4/10, raw 7 |\n| Pisces and Aries | Pisces → Aries 2, Aries → Pisces 12: 2/12, raw 0 (Dwirdwadasha) |\n| Scorpio and Pisces | Scorpio → Pisces 5, Pisces → Scorpio 9: 5/9, raw 0 (Navapanchama); lords Mars and Jupiter are mutual friends |\n\n### Self-check habit\nAfter every calculation, add the two counts. If the total is not 14 (or 2 for the same sign), you made a counting error. This one-second check catches most mistakes, including the impossible \"6/6\".\n\n### Practice with the tools\n- Use the **Counting Ring** to watch the forward and reverse counts.\n- Use the **Matrix** to find all pairs with a given sign.\n- Use the **Calculator** to test your answers and compare profiles.\n- Use the **Exercises** tab for 17 graded checks, and the **Final Assessment** when ready.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What does Bhakoot Koota compare?\nA: The Moon signs (Chandra Rashi) of the two people.\n\nQ: How many points does Bhakoot carry?\nA: 7 of the 36 Ashtakoota points. The raw score is either 7 or 0.\n\nQ: How do you count between two signs?\nA: Inclusively, counting both ends. The formula is ((B − A + 12) mod 12) + 1.\n\nQ: What is the inclusive count from a sign to itself?\nA: 1.\n\nQ: What do the forward and reverse counts add up to for two different signs?\nA: Always 14.\n\nQ: Why can a pair never be \"6/6\"?\nA: Because the two counts add up to 14, a count of 6 forces the other to be 8. Taurus–Libra and Cancer–Sagittarius are 6/8.\n\nQ: Which pair types score 7?\nA: 1/1 (same sign), 7/7, 3/11 and 4/10.\n\nQ: Which pair types score 0?\nA: 2/12, 5/9 and 6/8.\n\nQ: What is Dwirdwadasha (द्वितीय-द्वादश)?\nA: The 2/12 relationship: each Moon sign is the 2nd or 12th from the other. Raw score 0.\n\nQ: What is Navapanchama (नव-पंचम)?\nA: The 5/9 relationship. Raw score 0.\n\nQ: What is Shadashtaka (षडाष्टक)?\nA: The 6/8 relationship. Raw score 0.\n\nQ: What is Samasaptaka (समसप्तक)?\nA: The 7/7 relationship: opposite signs. It is favourable and scores 7.\n\nQ: How many of the 144 ordered sign pairs score 0?\nA: 72; the other 72 score 7.\n\nQ: How many unordered pairs of different signs are adverse?\nA: 36 of 66 (and 30 are favourable).\n\nQ: Is the Bhakoot score symmetric?\nA: Yes. The score of A with B equals the score of B with A, although the counts differ.\n\nQ: Is a Bhakoot score of 0 a verdict on the marriage?\nA: No. It means Bhakoot Dosha is identified under the common convention. It is not a prediction.\n\nQ: What is Ekadhipatya (एकाधिपत्य)?\nA: Both signs having the same lord. A claimed cancellation of Bhakoot Dosha, evidence grade D, unverified.\n\nQ: Which adverse different-sign pairs share a lord?\nA: Aries–Scorpio (Mars), Taurus–Libra (Venus) and Capricorn–Aquarius (Saturn).\n\nQ: Do Gemini–Virgo and Sagittarius–Pisces need a cancellation?\nA: No. They share a lord but are 4/10, which scores 7.\n\nQ: What does \"mutual friendship\" of the lords mean?\nA: Each lord regards the other as a friend in the natural table. One-sided friendship is not mutual.\n\nQ: How does the Moon regard Mercury, and Mercury the Moon?\nA: The Moon regards Mercury as a friend; Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy. One-sided.\n\nQ: How many adverse pairs have mutually friendly lords?\nA: 14 of the 36 (plus 3 that share a lord) in the natural table.\n\nQ: Does an exception change the raw score?\nA: Never. The raw score is unchanged; at most an adjusted score is shown, labelled ADJUSTED.\n\nQ: What is the default status of every cancellation claim in this course?\nA: UNVERIFIED. None is verified from a primary text.\n\nQ: What does Profile A do?\nA: Reports only the raw 7 or 0 and applies no exception.\n\nQ: What does Profile E do?\nA: Lists every exception candidate with its evidence label and never adjusts the score.\n\nQ: What extra does Profile D require?\nA: A declared \"Nadi different\"; otherwise no adjustment is made.\n\nQ: How long is one Navamsha?\nA: 3°20′, one ninth of a sign.\n\nQ: Why must the Moon's longitude be checked near a sign boundary?\nA: A small error in birth time or ayanamsha can move the Moon to the next sign and change the whole Bhakoot result.\n\nQ: How does Bhakoot differ from Tara?\nA: Bhakoot counts between Moon signs (12 signs, 7 or 0). Tara counts between Nakshatras (27 stars, nine Taras).\n\nQ: What is the first thing to do after any Bhakoot calculation?\nA: Add the two counts: they must make 14 (or 1 and 1 for the same sign).\n\nQ: How should a Bhakoot Dosha be communicated?\nA: Name the pair, show the counts, state the profile and the raw score, and avoid any prediction or fear.\n\nQ: What three layers does the course keep apart?\nA: Calculation (counting), scoring (raw 7 or 0) and interpretation (Dosha and exceptions).\n\n## Assessment\nQ: Bhakoot Koota compares the:\nA) Ascendants\nB) Nakshatra lords\nC) Sun signs\nD) Moon signs\nANS: D\nWHY: Bhakoot uses the Moon sign (Rashi) of each person.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The maximum Bhakoot score is:\nA) 4\nB) 6\nC) 8\nD) 7\nANS: D\nWHY: Bhakoot carries 7 of the 36 points.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The inclusive count from Aries to Taurus is:\nA) 1\nB) 2\nC) 11\nD) 12\nANS: B\nWHY: Aries is 1 and Taurus is 2, counting both ends.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The inclusive count from Taurus to Aries is:\nA) 2\nB) 11\nC) 12\nD) 13\nANS: C\nWHY: Taurus 2 to Aries 1 wraps round the zodiac: ((1 - 2 + 12) mod 12) + 1 = 12.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: For two different signs the forward and reverse counts add up to:\nA) 12\nB) 13\nC) 15\nD) 14\nANS: D\nWHY: d + 1 and 12 − d + 1 sum to 14.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Taurus–Libra is:\nA) 6/6\nB) 6/8\nC) 5/9\nD) 7/7\nANS: B\nWHY: Taurus to Libra is 6 and Libra to Taurus is 8. A 6/6 pair is impossible.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which pair scores 7?\nA) 2/12\nB) 5/9\nC) 4/10\nD) 6/8\nANS: C\nWHY: 1/1, 7/7, 3/11 and 4/10 score 7.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Shadashtaka means the signs are:\nA) 2nd and 12th\nB) 6th and 8th\nC) 5th and 9th\nD) 3rd and 11th\nANS: B\nWHY: Shad means six and ashta means eight.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Navapanchama means:\nA) 5/9\nB) 6/8\nC) 2/12\nD) 4/10\nANS: A\nWHY: Nava is nine and Pancha is five.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The raw Bhakoot score for Aries–Libra is:\nA) 7\nB) 3.5\nC) 0\nD) 14\nANS: A\nWHY: Libra is the 7th from Aries (7/7), which is favourable.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The raw score for Aries–Scorpio is:\nA) 0\nB) 7\nC) 3.5\nD) 14\nANS: A\nWHY: Aries to Scorpio is 8 and Scorpio to Aries is 6: Shadashtaka.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many of the 144 ordered pairs score 0?\nA) 36\nB) 66\nC) 84\nD) 72\nANS: D\nWHY: 72 adverse and 72 favourable.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: How many unordered different-sign pairs are favourable?\nA) 30\nB) 36\nC) 66\nD) 72\nANS: A\nWHY: 66 pairs in total, 36 adverse, so 30 favourable.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Gemini and Virgo share Mercury. Their Bhakoot is:\nA) 0, cancelled by Ekadhipatya\nB) 6/8\nC) 7, with no Dosha\nD) 5/9\nANS: C\nWHY: Gemini–Virgo is 4/10, which scores 7: there is nothing to cancel.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which adverse different-sign pairs share a lord?\nA) None\nB) Gemini–Virgo, Sagittarius–Pisces\nC) Aries–Leo, Leo–Sagittarius\nD) Aries–Scorpio, Taurus–Libra, Capricorn–Aquarius\nANS: D\nWHY: Mars, Venus and Saturn each rule two signs forming an adverse pair; Gemini–Virgo and Sagittarius–Pisces are 4/10.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: \"Mutual friends\" means:\nA) Each lord regards the other as a friend\nB) One lord regards the other as a friend\nC) They rule the same sign\nD) They are neutral\nANS: A\nWHY: Both directions must be friendly; one direction is a one-sided friendship.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: In the natural table, the Moon regards Mercury as a:\nA) neutral\nB) friend\nC) enemy\nD) the same planet\nANS: B\nWHY: Moon's friends are the Sun and Mercury.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Mercury regards the Moon as a:\nA) friend\nB) neutral\nC) enemy\nD) ruler\nANS: C\nWHY: Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy, so the Moon–Mercury friendship is one-sided.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Under Profile A, Aries–Scorpio is reported as:\nA) Adjusted 7\nB) Raw 0 with no adjustment\nC) Raw 7\nD) 14\nANS: B\nWHY: Profile A never applies an exception.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Under Profile B, Aries–Scorpio is reported as:\nA) Raw 0 and adjusted 7, labelled ADJUSTED\nB) Raw 7 only\nC) Raw 14\nD) No information\nANS: A\nWHY: Same lord Mars; Profile B allows it; the raw 0 stays visible.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Under Profile B, Aries–Leo is:\nA) Raw 0 with no adjustment\nB) Adjusted 7\nC) Raw 7\nD) 14\nANS: A\nWHY: The lords differ (Mars and Sun), so Profile B does not apply. Profile C would, because they are mutual friends.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Cancer–Sagittarius under Profile C is:\nA) Raw 0 with no adjustment (one-sided friendship)\nB) Adjusted 7\nC) Raw 7\nD) Impossible\nANS: A\nWHY: Jupiter regards the Moon a friend but the Moon is neutral to Jupiter: not mutual.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: What is the evidence label of the same-lord claim in this course?\nA) A\nB) D, UNVERIFIED\nC) C\nD) B\nANS: B\nWHY: It is repeated on contemporary websites; no primary text was verified.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A raw Bhakoot score of 0 means:\nA) The marriage will fail\nB) The Moons are in the same sign\nC) Bhakoot Dosha is identified under the common convention; it is not a verdict\nD) The match is impossible\nANS: C\nWHY: A score is a convention, not a prediction.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: An exception to Bhakoot Dosha changes:\nA) The raw score\nB) Only the reading, shown as an adjusted score beside the raw score\nC) The counts\nD) The Moon sign\nANS: B\nWHY: The raw score and counts never change.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Profile E:\nA) Applies every exception\nB) Is the strictest school\nC) Lists candidates and never adjusts\nD) Changes the counts\nANS: C\nWHY: Profile E is for research flags only.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Profile D with Nadi declared the same:\nA) Adjusts to 7\nB) Raises the raw score\nC) Doubles the score\nD) Makes no adjustment\nANS: D\nWHY: Profile D needs a declared \"Nadi different\".\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A Moon at 3°20′ of Aries falls in which Navamsha sign?\nA) Aries\nB) Gemini\nC) Taurus\nD) Cancer\nANS: C\nWHY: 3°20′ starts the second Navamsha, whose sign is Taurus.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: True or false: the Bhakoot score of A with B can differ from B with A.\nA) True\nB) False\nANS: B\nWHY: The score is symmetric; only the directional counts differ.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: True or false: two different signs can have counts 6 and 6.\nA) True\nB) False\nANS: B\nWHY: The counts add up to 14, so 6 forces 8.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which response to an anxious client with a 5/9 result is most responsible?\nA) It will certainly cause hardship\nB) Predict a separation\nC) Hide the result\nD) Show the counts, state the profile and avoid fatalistic claims\nANS: D\nWHY: Bhakoot is one conventional score; communicate with care.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. Why do the forward and reverse counts of two different signs add up to 14?\nMA: Going from A to B covers d steps, going on from B back to A covers 12 − d steps; counting inclusively adds one to each, so the two counts are d + 1 and 13 − d, which add up to 14.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Why is the raw score never changed by an exception?\nMA: Calculation and scoring must stay separate from interpretation. If an exception edited the raw score, the same pair would give different arithmetic under different schools and the report could not show what was claimed. An adjusted score is shown beside the raw score and labelled.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Application 1. Taurus and Libra: give both counts, the pair, the raw score, and say whether Profile B would adjust it.\nMA: Taurus to Libra 6, Libra to Taurus 8; pair 6/8 Shadashtaka; raw 0. Both signs are ruled by Venus, so Profile B shows an adjusted 7 beside the raw 0, evidence D and unverified.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Application 2. Explain why a mutual-friendship exception for Cancer–Sagittarius is not applied.\nMA: The Cancer lord is the Moon and the Sagittarius lord is Jupiter. Jupiter regards the Moon as a friend, but the Moon regards Jupiter as neutral. One-sided friendship is not mutual, so Profile C does not apply it.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Evaluation 1. A website says Bhakoot Dosha is always cancelled when the lords are friends. How do you assess this claim?\nMA: Identify the claim, note that sources differ (some say friendship only reduces the Dosha), check for a primary text (none verified here), grade it D and UNVERIFIED, state which friendship table was used, and refuse to apply it automatically.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Evaluation 2. Write two sentences you would say to a student whose Bhakoot is 0.\nMA: \"Your Moon signs form a 6/8 relationship, which the common convention scores 0 and names Shadashtaka. This is one conventional score in one tradition; it is not a prediction, and we will look at the other Kootas and the full chart before drawing any conclusion.\"\nLVL: Intermediate\n\n## References\n\n### Source status (Day 8)\nNo classical verse or chapter has been verified for any Bhakoot rule in this module.\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bhakoot carries 7 points; 1/1, 7/7, 3/11, 4/10 score 7; 2/12, 5/9, 6/8 score 0 | C | Widely stated in manuals; no verse verified |\n| Inclusive counting; the counts add to 14 | n/a | Arithmetic consequence |\n| 72 adverse and 72 favourable ordered pairs | n/a | Arithmetic consequence |\n| Same-lord (Ekadhipatya) cancellation | D | Repeated on websites; unverified |\n| Mutual-friend lord cancellation | D | Repeated on websites; some say it only reduces the Dosha |\n| Navamsha-lord condition | E | Rare, regional; unverified |\n| Strong Moon, Tara and Vashya, Nadi prerequisite | E | Unverified |\n| Profiles A to E | n/a | Software configurations, not classical schools |\n\n### What a contemporary-source search found (grade D, not primary text)\nOne standard web search was made for how current sources describe Bhakoot cancellation. The pages below were returned in the results; the course relied on the search summary and did **not** read each page in full, so treat each as a lead that still requires verification.\n- [astromedha.in: Bhakoot Dosha in matching](https://astromedha.in/insights/vedic/bhakoot-dosha-in-matching)\n- [jagannathhora.com: Bhakoot Dosha complete guide](https://jagannathhora.com/bhakoot-dosha-complete-guide/)\n- [daanyam.in: Bhakoot Dosha meaning, cancellation, remedies](https://daanyam.in/blog/bhakoot-dosha-meaning-cancellation-remedies)\n- [onlinejyotish.com: Bhakoota Kootam](https://www.onlinejyotish.com/articles/marriage-matching/english/bhakoota-kootam.php)\n- [kundli.muthu.co: Bhakoot](https://kundli.muthu.co/koot/bhakoot)\nSummary of the pattern across these sources: same lord and friendly lords are the most repeated cancellations; some say friendship only *reduces* the Dosha; some say 5/9 and 2/12 are easier to waive than 6/8; some add Navamsha lords. No source verified in this course quotes a classical chapter and verse.\n\n### Bibliography (all entries require verification)\n| Work | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra | Named in manuals as a general reference for Jyotish; no Bhakoot chapter or verse claimed here. Requires verification. |\n| Muhurta Chintamani | Named in manuals for marriage-related Muhurta; no chapter or verse claimed here. Requires verification. |\n| Modern Ashtakoota manuals and calculators | Source of the common 7/0 convention; titles and editions not recorded here. Requires verification. |\n| Contemporary web pages listed above | Grade D leads. Requires verification. |\n\n> If you can consult a printed or scholarly edition, record the exact title, edition, page and wording in your Journal and ask your instructor to upgrade the grade only after checking.\n\n## Counting Ring\n\n### Lesson 2 in motion – count forward and reverse\nChoose two Moon signs. The ring numbers the signs Aries 1 to Pisces 12. Press **Play** to watch the forward count (F) from A to B, the reverse count (R) from B to A, or both one after the other. The summary table shows the two counts, their sum, the pair and the raw score.\n\nThings to try:\n1. Aries and Scorpio: forward 8, reverse 6.\n2. Taurus and Libra: forward 6, reverse 8, **not** 6 and 6.\n3. Aries and Aries: both counts are 1.\n4. Aries and Libra: both counts are 7.\n{{b8ring}}\n\n## Calculator\n\n### Calculate, compare profiles and generate a report\nSelect two Moon signs and a rule profile. The result is shown in three layers: calculation, scoring and interpretation. The comparison table shows profiles A to E side by side; the raw score is identical in every row. Use the optional declarations to see what Profile D needs. If you give both Moon longitudes (degrees inside the sign) the Navamsha candidate is also assessed.\n{{b8calc}}\n\n## Matrix and Dataset\n\n### The 12 × 12 matrix\nRows are Person A and columns are Person B. Each cell shows the count from A to B with a symbol (✓ favourable, ✗ adverse); use the drop-downs to show the pair or the score instead, or to display only adverse or only favourable cells. Tap a cell to see the working, the lords and the friendship status.\n{{b8matrix}}\n\n### The 144-record dataset\nEvery ordered pair of signs, with counts, pair type, raw score, lords and friendship. Filter it, search by sign and download it as CSV or JSON. Try the filter \"adverse with the same lord\": exactly six ordered records (three unordered pairs, in two directions).\n{{b8data}}\n\n## Lords and Navamsha\n\n### The natural friendship table\nThe same-lord and mutual-friendship candidates use the natural (Naisargika) table. It is directional: the Moon regards Mercury as a friend, but Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy. This table matches the one used in Day 6 and in the Calculation Lab; the course tests this agreement for all 144 sign pairs.\n{{b8friend}}\n\n### The Navamsha exception calculator\nEnter each Moon's sign and its position inside the sign. The tool computes the Navamsha sign (each Navamsha is 3°20′), its lord and whether the two lords are the same or mutual friends. This is a **grade E, unverified** candidate; the tool never changes the raw score.\n{{b8nav}}\n\n## Exercises\n\n### Seventeen graded exercises\nChoose an answer, press Check, and read the explanation. Your progress is saved on this device. Exercises 7 to 13 use the lords and the profiles.\n{{b8exercise}}\n\n## Final Assessment\n\n### The 30-question final assessment\nTen conceptual questions, ten calculation questions (freshly generated each attempt), five cancellation questions and five case questions. Pass mark 70%. A failed attempt never erases your completed lessons.\n{{b8final}}\n\n## Report\n\n### Student report generator\nCreate a short Bhakoot report for any pair of Moon signs. The report contains the counts, the pair, the raw score, the profile, any adjusted score (labelled) and the evidence-labelled candidates, and always ends with this statement: *This report is an educational calculation of the Moon-sign relationship under the selected rule profile. It is not a prediction, not a medical or legal statement, and not a decision about any marriage. Exception categories shown are unverified research candidates and have not changed the raw score.*\n{{b8report}}\n\n### Template for your own written report\n1. Names or initials, Moon signs and how the signs were obtained (chart, longitude, ayanamsha).\n2. The two counts and their sum.\n3. The pair and the raw score.\n4. Dosha (if any) and its name.\n5. Profile chosen and why.\n6. Candidates considered, their evidence labels, and which (if any) were applied.\n7. The raw score and any adjusted score, side by side.\n8. Limits and caution statement.\n\n## Software Specification\n\n### Purpose and layers\n`bhakoot.js` exposes `window.BhakootEngine`. All functions are pure (no DOM, no storage, no randomness), so the same input always gives the same output.\n\n### Function list\n| Function | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `getRashiIndex(x)` | Accept a name or a number 1 to 12 and return 1 to 12 |\n| `getRashiLord(r)` | Lord of a sign |\n| `forwardDistance(a, b)` | Inclusive forward count |\n| `reverseDistance(a, b)` | Inclusive count from b to a |\n| `classifyRelationship(a, b)` | Counts, pair, category, group, sum |\n| `rawScore(a, b)` | 7 or 0 |\n| `naturalRelationship(p, q)` | friend, neutral, enemy or same |\n| `checkSameLord(a, b)` | Same lord between different signs |\n| `checkMutualFriendship(p, q)` | Mutual and one-sided flags |\n| `navamshaSign(longitude)` | Navamsha sign 1 to 12 |\n| `navamshaLordCheck(lonA, lonB)` | Same or mutual Navamsha lords |\n| `additionalConditions(opts)` | Declared conditions, never invented |\n| `evaluateBhakootProfile(id, input)` | Result under profile A to E |\n| `generateBhakootReport(input, id)` | Text report with the disclaimer |\n| `generateDataset()` | 144 records |\n| `toCSV()` and `toJSON()` | Exports |\n| `runTests()` | 50 tests |\n\n### Input and output examples\n| Case | JSON |\n|---|---|\n| Input | `{\"a\":\"Aries\",\"b\":\"Scorpio\",\"nadiDifferent\":true}` |\n| Output (Profile B, abridged) | `{\"profile\":\"B\",\"raw\":0,\"adjusted\":7,\"relationship\":{\"forward\":8,\"reverse\":6,\"pair\":\"6/8\"},\"rawUnchanged\":true}` |\n| Dataset record | `{\"signA\":\"Taurus\",\"signB\":\"Libra\",\"forward\":6,\"reverse\":8,\"pair\":\"6/8\",\"raw\":0,\"sameLord\":true}` |\n\n### Pseudocode\n1. `a = getRashiIndex(inputA)` and `b = getRashiIndex(inputB)`; reject anything else.\n2. `f = ((b - a + 12) mod 12) + 1` and `r = ((a - b + 12) mod 12) + 1`.\n3. `pair = min(f, r) + \"/\" + max(f, r)`, or `1/1` if `a = b`.\n4. `raw = 7` if the pair is in `{1/1, 7/7, 3/11, 4/10}`, else `0`.\n5. If `raw = 0`, build the candidate list; apply only the candidates the profile allows.\n6. Return `raw`, `adjusted` (or none), the candidates with evidence labels and the disclaimer.\n\n### Error handling\n| Input | Error code |\n|---|---|\n| Empty or null sign | `MISSING` |\n| Sign number 0 or 13 | `OUT_OF_RANGE` |\n| Unknown name such as \"Foo\" | `UNKNOWN_SIGN` |\n| Fractional sign number | `NOT_INTEGER` |\n| Longitude not a finite number | `BAD_LONGITUDE` |\n| Unknown profile | `UNKNOWN_PROFILE` |\nErrors are typed (`BhakootError`) and `BhakootEngine.safe(fn)` converts them to `{ok:false, code, message}` for the interface.\n\n### Evidence-label data model\nEach exception has an `id`, `name`, `grade` (A to E) and `status` (\"UNVERIFIED\" by default). A candidate is never applied unless the active profile lists its id in `restore`.\n\n### Tests\nFifty automated tests cover counting, all pair types, the 144 and 72 totals, the sum of 14, lords, friendship, Navamsha boundaries, errors, profiles, the report disclaimer, exports and **agreement with the Calculation Lab for all 144 pairs**. See the Validation tab.\n\n## Instructor Guide\n\n### A 120-minute teaching plan\n| Minutes | Activity |\n|---|---|\n| 0–10 | Warm-up: recall signs and lords; ask \"what is a Koota convention?\" |\n| 10–25 | Counting method with the Counting Ring; students predict before Play |\n| 25–40 | Prove the sum of 14; show why 6/6 is impossible; classify the seven pair types |\n| 40–55 | Matrix: find the 72 and 72; pattern spotting |\n| 55–70 | Dosha names and responsible language; role-play with an anxious client |\n| 70–90 | Exceptions as a research chapter: evidence labels, same lord, mutual versus one-sided friendship |\n| 90–105 | Calculator and profile comparison; Navamsha example |\n| 105–115 | Exercises and mini-quiz |\n| 115–120 | Reflection: what is verified and what is not? |\n\n### Teaching points\n- Always have learners compute the reverse count *and check the sum*.\n- Use the Corrections table to teach that an error in a supplied source is a learning opportunity.\n- Never let a student say \"the Dosha is cancelled\" without naming the profile and the evidence grade.\n\n### Assessment rubric\n| Criterion | Excellent | Developing |\n|---|---|---|\n| Counting | Both counts, correct sum | One count or wrong sum |\n| Classification | Correct pair and name | Name only |\n| Exceptions | Evidence labels, profile stated | Exception applied without a label |\n| Communication | Calm, non-fatalistic | Predictive or alarming |\n\n### Common questions from learners\n1. \"Why 14?\" Show the proof in Lesson 2.\n2. \"Is 5/9 as bad as 6/8?\" Some sources distinguish them; the common score treats them equally (0). Say which profile you use.\n3. \"Is it cancelled?\" Raw is unchanged; adjusted depends on the profile and the evidence is unverified.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n### Twenty mistakes to avoid\nEach mistake is followed by the correction.\n1. **Writing Taurus–Libra as 6/6.** Correct: 6/8.\n2. **Writing Cancer–Sagittarius as 6/6.** Correct: 6/8.\n3. **Counting without including the starting sign.** Always count inclusively.\n4. **Forgetting to wrap round after Pisces.** Use the formula with `mod 12`.\n5. **Not checking that the counts sum to 14.** Always add them.\n6. **Using the Nakshatra instead of the Moon sign.** Bhakoot uses the Rashi.\n7. **Saying Aries–Libra is adverse.** It is 7/7, favourable.\n8. **Treating 4/10 as Dosha.** It scores 7.\n9. **Calling Gemini–Virgo a cancelled Dosha.** There is no Dosha (4/10).\n10. **Treating one-sided friendship as mutual.** Both directions must be friendly.\n11. **Using enemies instead of non-friends.** Neutral lords are not friends and not enemies.\n12. **Changing the raw score after an exception.** Never; show an adjusted score beside it.\n13. **Omitting the ADJUSTED label.** Always label it.\n14. **Saying \"cancelled\" without naming the profile.** State the profile and evidence.\n15. **Presenting grade D or E claims as classical rules.** State the label.\n16. **Mixing results from two profiles in one sentence.** Keep profiles separate.\n17. **Ignoring Moon sign boundaries.** Check the longitude near a boundary.\n18. **Confusing Bhakoot with Nadi's \"same Rashi\" exception.** Different Koota (Day 9).\n19. **Predicting divorce or failure from a 0.** It is a convention, not a prediction.\n20. **Assuming that signs sharing a lord always form a Dosha.** Only five pairs of different signs share a lord, and only three of them are adverse.\n\n## Validation\n\n### Automated validation report\nThe report runs the engine tests in your browser, including the comparison with the Calculation Lab for all 144 pairs. Any failure is shown in bold.\n{{b8tests}}\n","09":"# Day 9 – Nadi Koota\n\n## Learn\n\n### Welcome to Day 9\n**Module 9: Nadi Koota (नाड़ी कूट) – Nadi classification, Nadi Dosha, exceptions and advanced compatibility analysis.** Day 9 of 14 · Foundation to Advanced · 180–210 minutes (self-paced) · Instructor: Anoopam Shukla · Languages: English, Hindi, Gujarati.\n\nNadi carries the largest single weight in the conventional Ashtakoota: **8 points out of 36**. Today you learn far more than the familiar rule \"same Nadi scores zero\". You learn the 27-Nakshatra classification, how Nadi differs from Rashi-based Bhakoot, how the exception rules are *stated*, how little of them can be verified, and how to keep an astrological classification apart from any medical or genetic claim.\n\n> **Central teaching principle.** Classify first, score second, examine exceptions third, interpret last. The raw Nadi score is never overwritten, and an exception is never applied silently.\n\n### The one idea to keep in mind all day\nFour things must stay separate in every Nadi report:\n1. **The raw score:** same Nadi 0, different Nadi 8. Pure table lookup, the same in every profile.\n2. **The Dosha status:** whether a Nadi Dosha is flagged, and whether a selected rule cancels or mitigates it.\n3. **The exception rule:** the exact condition, the source, and the evidence quality. In this course **no exception rule is source-verified**.\n4. **The adjusted score:** it exists only if a profile restores points, and it is always labelled *adjusted* with the raw score still visible.\n\n### What you will be able to do by the end of the day\n1. Explain the meaning of Nadi (नाड़ी) in traditional horoscope matching.\n2. Name Adi (आदि), Madhya (मध्य) and Antya (अन्त्य) Nadi and place all 27 Nakshatras in them.\n3. Explain why Nadi is read from the Janma Nakshatra (जन्म नक्षत्र) and Bhakoot from the Moon Rashi (राशि).\n4. Calculate the conventional Nadi score and identify a same-Nadi combination.\n5. Explain Nadi Dosha (नाड़ी दोष) and its traditional significance without fear-based language.\n6. Distinguish Nadi Koota from Ayurvedic pulse examination and from any medical or genetic test.\n7. Examine same-Nadi pairs with different Rashis, with the same Rashi, and with the same Nakshatra.\n8. Use the Pada (चरण) correctly, including the nine Nakshatras that are split between two Rashis.\n9. Test Rashi-lord (राशि अधिपति), natural-friendship and Navamsha-lord (नवांश) conditions in both directions.\n10. Compare the exception claims, label their evidence, and separate cancellation from mitigation.\n11. Keep raw score, Dosha status and adjusted score apart.\n12. Compare the same pair under profiles A to F.\n13. Read the complete 27 × 27 Nadi matrix.\n14. Use the calculator, the Navamsha calculator and the report generator.\n15. Complete the sixteen exercises and the final assessment.\n\n### Module information\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Course | Professional Dashkoota Milan (ASTROSUTRAS) |\n| Day and module | Day 9 of 14 · Nadi Koota |\n| Main subject | Nadi classification and Nadi Dosha |\n| Level | Foundation to Advanced |\n| Instructor | Anoopam Shukla |\n| Mode | Self-paced learning with instructor-led support |\n| Tradition used for scoring | Tradition A, common published tables (grade C/D, not source-verified) |\n\n### Your learning route (ten stages)\nProgress → Introduction → Meaning of Nadi → The three Nadis → 27-Nakshatra table → Scoring and the matrix → Nadi Dosha → Exception research → Profiles and decision engine → Calculator and cases → Assessment. Tick each stage when you finish it; the tracker remembers where you stopped.\n{{n9dash}}\n\n### Prerequisites and quick revision\nYou should already know: the 27 Nakshatras in order; the Janma Nakshatra; the Moon's sidereal longitude; the Pada; the Rashi and its lord; the Navamsha of a Pada; and natural planetary friendship (Day 6). If any of these is shaky, revise Days 2 and 6 first.\n\n### Suggested pace (self-paced)\n| Block | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Meaning, scoring weight and the three Nadis | 25 |\n| The 27-Nakshatra table and the matrix | 35 |\n| Nadi Dosha and advanced relationships | 30 |\n| Exception research and evidence labels | 35 |\n| Profiles, calculator and the 15 cases | 35 |\n| Assessment and revision | 25 |\n| Total | 185 |\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### Lesson 1 – Meaning of Nadi\n**1.1 The word.** *Nadi* (नाड़ी) literally means a channel, a vessel or a pulse. In Jyotish it is used in more than one sense, so students must never assume the word means the same thing everywhere. In **Ashtakoota Milan** it names one of the eight Kootas: a three-fold classification of the 27 Nakshatras, used to compare two people.\n\n**1.2 Why the Janma Nakshatra?** Nadi is assigned from the **Janma Nakshatra** (जन्म नक्षत्र), the Nakshatra occupied by the Moon at birth. **Bhakoot** is different: it compares the two **Moon Rashis**. That is why two people in the *same Rashi* can still have *different Nadis*, and two people with the *same Nadi* can have *different Rashis*. Keep the question straight: Nadi asks \"which of three groups is each Nakshatra in?\"; Bhakoot asks \"how far apart are the two Rashis?\".\n\n**1.3 Position in the Ashtakoota.** Nadi is the eighth and heaviest of the eight Kootas.\n| Koota | Maximum points |\n|---|---|\n| Varna | 1 |\n| Vashya | 2 |\n| Tara | 3 |\n| Yoni | 4 |\n| Graha Maitri | 5 |\n| Gana | 6 |\n| Bhakoot | 7 |\n| Nadi | 8 |\n| Total | 36 |\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) What does the Nadi Koota compare, Nakshatra or Rashi? (b) Give two reasons two people in the same Rashi may differ in Nadi. (c) Which Koota is heaviest?\n\n### Lesson 2 – Why the weight, and why all or nothing\n**2.1 The highest weight.** Nadi receives 8 points because the conventional scheme treats it as the most weighty factor. That is a **feature of the traditional weighting system, not a scientific measurement** of relationship success. No point in the table is a probability.\n\n**2.2 All or nothing.** In the conventional framework Nadi is scored in only two ways: **different Nadi 8, same Nadi 0**. There is no half score. The weighting reflects how the scheme was built; it does not mean that a zero is twice as significant as a Bhakoot zero in any measurable way.\n\n**2.3 A high total does not cancel it.** With a Nadi score of 0 the highest possible total is 28 of 36 (36 − 8). Even a pair scoring that 28 has a Nadi Dosha flagged. Other Kootas never cancel it automatically. Only a documented exception rule, selected by you, can change the Dosha *status*, and even then the raw score stays zero.\n\n**2.4 A zero Nadi score cannot decide a marriage alone.** The Nadi score is one factor of a Moon-based screening. The complete horoscope (both charts, the seventh house and lord, Navamsha, timing) is a separate and larger study (Days 12 to 14).\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) Why is a score of 4 of 8 impossible in the conventional Nadi Koota? (b) Does a total of 28 of 36, the highest total possible with Nadi 0, remove a Nadi Dosha?\n\n### Lesson 3 – The three-fold classification\n**3.1 The three Nadis.**\n| Nadi | Hindi | Group code | Literal sense |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Adi | आदि नाड़ी | 1 | Beginning, first |\n| Madhya | मध्य नाड़ी | 2 | Middle |\n| Antya | अन्त्य नाड़ी | 3 | End, last |\n\n**3.2 Each group has nine Nakshatras.** 27 = 3 × 9. Every Nakshatra belongs to exactly one Nadi.\n\n**3.3 The Vata, Pitta and Kapha claim.** Many modern pages associate Adi with Vata, Madhya with Pitta and Antya with Kapha (the three Ayurvedic doshas). This association is **a circulated claim**: in this research it was seen only on secondary web pages (grade D) and **no primary text for it was verified**. Even if a source states it, its nature is symbolic. Do not equate the Nadi of a Nakshatra with an Ayurvedic Prakriti assessment. A Nakshatra-based assignment is **not a clinical examination**, and the three astrological groups are not three body types.\n\n**3.4 The arithmetic pattern.** Look at the zodiacal order: the Nadis run **Adi, Madhya, Antya, Antya, Madhya, Adi**, and that six-step pattern repeats (4½ times in 27). This is an *arithmetic property of the table*, a handy memory aid and a good checksum. It is not a classical statement and gives no reason for the grouping.\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) How many Nakshatras are in Antya? (b) Is Adi the same as Vata? Explain in one sentence.\n\n### Lesson 4 – Historical and symbolic basis\n**4.1 What can be said.** Nadi Koota is described in modern manuals as part of traditional marriage matching and is attributed to the **Muhurta Chintamani tradition** and related Vivaha (marriage) literature. In this research **no primary verse or commentary passage was verified**. One secondary page cites a verse number (34); other circulated references use other numbers (36, 6.37). Numbering differs across editions, so none of these is relied on.\n\n**4.2 Four kinds of statement.** Keep them separate:\n| Kind | Example in Nadi Koota | Status here |\n|---|---|---|\n| A. Textually supported principle | Same Nadi marriage is discouraged (a verse is cited by secondary pages) | Not verified from the text |\n| B. Traditional symbolic explanation | Nadi as a symbol of the continuity of the couple's life together | Symbolism, not evidence |\n| C. Later interpretative explanation | Links to constitution (Vata, Pitta, Kapha), progeny and health | Later tradition; not evidence |\n| D. Contemporary popular interpretation | Genetic compatibility, infertility, \"Nadi remedies\" | Not supported; see the next tab |\n\n**4.3 Reading same-Nadi combinations.** Secondary sources report that traditional writers discourage a same-Nadi marriage within the scheme; interpretations of *why* differ and are mostly later. Do not invent physical, reproductive or medical characteristics for any of the three groups.\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) Which kind (A to D) is the claim \"Nadi Dosha causes infertility\"? (b) Why is a verse number from a web page not enough?\n\n### Lesson 5 – Astrological Nadi versus medical terminology\nThis is the most important lesson of the day, and it has its own tab (**Nadi and Medicine**). In brief:\n- **Nadi Koota does not measure DNA compatibility.** Nadi Dosha is not a genetic test.\n- **Nadi Dosha cannot diagnose infertility**, establish inherited disease or predict a child's medical condition.\n- A medical or fertility concern must be assessed by **qualified healthcare professionals**.\n- Never use fear-based language. Say what the classification is and what it is not.\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### Lesson 6 – The complete 27-Nakshatra Nadi table\nThe initial reference profile is the conventional working classification (Tradition A), checked by the engine against the shared rule file. The table is in zodiacal order. Group codes: Adi = 1, Madhya = 2, Antya = 3.\n| No. | Nakshatra | Lord | Nadi | Group | Rashi coverage |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini (अश्विनी) | Ketu | Adi | 1 | Aries |\n| 2 | Bharani (भरणी) | Venus | Madhya | 2 | Aries |\n| 3 | Krittika (कृत्तिका) | Sun | Antya | 3 | Aries (Pada 1), Taurus (Padas 2–4) |\n| 4 | Rohini (रोहिणी) | Moon | Antya | 3 | Taurus |\n| 5 | Mrigashira (मृगशिरा) | Mars | Madhya | 2 | Taurus (Padas 1–2), Gemini (Padas 3–4) |\n| 6 | Ardra (आर्द्रा) | Rahu | Adi | 1 | Gemini |\n| 7 | Punarvasu (पुनर्वसु) | Jupiter | Adi | 1 | Gemini (Padas 1–3), Cancer (Pada 4) |\n| 8 | Pushya (पुष्य) | Saturn | Madhya | 2 | Cancer |\n| 9 | Ashlesha (आश्लेषा) | Mercury | Antya | 3 | Cancer |\n| 10 | Magha (मघा) | Ketu | Antya | 3 | Leo |\n| 11 | Purva Phalguni (पूर्वा फाल्गुनी) | Venus | Madhya | 2 | Leo |\n| 12 | Uttara Phalguni (उत्तरा फाल्गुनी) | Sun | Adi | 1 | Leo (Pada 1), Virgo (Padas 2–4) |\n| 13 | Hasta (हस्त) | Moon | Adi | 1 | Virgo |\n| 14 | Chitra (चित्रा) | Mars | Madhya | 2 | Virgo (Padas 1–2), Libra (Padas 3–4) |\n| 15 | Swati (स्वाति) | Rahu | Antya | 3 | Libra |\n| 16 | Vishakha (विशाखा) | Jupiter | Antya | 3 | Libra (Padas 1–3), Scorpio (Pada 4) |\n| 17 | Anuradha (अनुराधा) | Saturn | Madhya | 2 | Scorpio |\n| 18 | Jyeshtha (ज्येष्ठा) | Mercury | Adi | 1 | Scorpio |\n| 19 | Mula (मूल) | Ketu | Adi | 1 | Sagittarius |\n| 20 | Purva Ashadha (पूर्वाषाढ़ा) | Venus | Madhya | 2 | Sagittarius |\n| 21 | Uttara Ashadha (उत्तराषाढ़ा) | Sun | Antya | 3 | Sagittarius (Pada 1), Capricorn (Padas 2–4) |\n| 22 | Shravana (श्रवण) | Moon | Antya | 3 | Capricorn |\n| 23 | Dhanishta (धनिष्ठा) | Mars | Madhya | 2 | Capricorn (Padas 1–2), Aquarius (Padas 3–4) |\n| 24 | Shatabhisha (शतभिषा) | Rahu | Adi | 1 | Aquarius |\n| 25 | Purva Bhadrapada (पूर्वा भाद्रपद) | Jupiter | Adi | 1 | Aquarius (Padas 1–3), Pisces (Pada 4) |\n| 26 | Uttara Bhadrapada (उत्तरा भाद्रपद) | Saturn | Madhya | 2 | Pisces |\n| 27 | Revati (रेवती) | Mercury | Antya | 3 | Pisces |\n\n**Representation A – group-wise.**\n| Adi Nadi (आदि) | Madhya Nadi (मध्य) | Antya Nadi (अन्त्य) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ashwini | Bharani | Krittika |\n| Ardra | Mrigashira | Rohini |\n| Punarvasu | Pushya | Ashlesha |\n| Uttara Phalguni | Purva Phalguni | Magha |\n| Hasta | Chitra | Swati |\n| Jyeshtha | Anuradha | Vishakha |\n| Mula | Purva Ashadha | Uttara Ashadha |\n| Shatabhisha | Dhanishta | Shravana |\n| Purva Bhadrapada | Uttara Bhadrapada | Revati |\n\n**Representation B – the zodiacal sequence in one line.** Adi (1) Ashwini; Madhya (2) Bharani; Antya (3) Krittika; Antya Rohini; Madhya Mrigashira; Adi Ardra; Adi Punarvasu; Madhya Pushya; Antya Ashlesha; and so on. The interactive tab shows both forms.\n\n**Verification rules the engine runs.** Exactly 27 Nakshatras; each in exactly one Nadi; exactly nine per Nadi; none omitted or duplicated; the order is correct; the Nakshatra names, lords and Nadis agree with the shared rule file.\n\n### Lesson 7 – The mathematics of the score\n**7.1 Identification.** For each person, `Nadi_A = NadiMaster[Nakshatra_A]` and `Nadi_B = NadiMaster[Nakshatra_B]`.\n\n**7.2 The conventional formula.** If `Nadi_A == Nadi_B` then Nadi score = 0 and `NadiDosha = TRUE`; otherwise Nadi score = 8. Maximum = 8. This is the base calculation *before* any exception profile.\n\n**7.3 The 3 × 3 matrix.**\n| Person A ↓ / Person B → | Adi | Madhya | Antya |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Adi | 0 | 8 | 8 |\n| Madhya | 8 | 0 | 8 |\n| Antya | 8 | 8 | 0 |\n\n**7.4 Why the score is symmetric.** The basic Nadi calculation compares *group identity*: \"are the two groups the same?\". If Person A is Adi and Person B is Madhya, the score is 8 whichever is listed first. Unlike Tara, there is no directional counting. No directional Nadi scoring is used unless a documented regional method requires it (none is used here).\n\n**7.5 Facts about the 27 × 27 matrix.** 729 cells in total. Every row contains **nine zeros** (its own Nadi, including its own Nakshatra) and **eighteen eights**. So 27 × 9 = **243 zeros** and 27 × 18 = **486 eights**. The matrix is symmetric. Two random people share a Nadi one time in three (243 ÷ 729 = 1 ÷ 3), so a same-Nadi result is **common**, not rare. The matrix is generated from the Nadi table, not typed in.\n\n**7.6 Why zero does not by itself say whether an exception applies.** The matrix works at the level of Nakshatras. Exceptions need more: the Pada, the Rashi, the lords. Amber cells in the matrix mark same-Nadi pairs where some exception *could* become eligible under the selected profile.\n\n**Check yourself.** (a) How many zero cells does the Ashwini row have, and which Nakshatras are they? (b) Why is the matrix symmetric?\n\n### Lesson 8 – Nadi Dosha\n**8.1 How it is formed.** A Nadi Dosha is *flagged* when the two Janma Nakshatras belong to the same Nadi. That is all the mathematics says. The traditional reading treats a same-Nadi couple as sharing the same Nadi \"energy\", whose symbolic meaning varies by author.\n\n**8.2 The three combinations.**\n| Combination | Both persons in | Number of Nakshatra pairs (A, B) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Adi–Adi | Adi | 81 |\n| Madhya–Madhya | Madhya | 81 |\n| Antya–Antya | Antya | 81 |\n\n**8.3 Severity.** One secondary page says the cited verse lays particular emphasis on Madhya–Madhya, and the same page says other authorities differ. This could not be verified. **The conventional score treats all three same-Nadi combinations equally (0).** This course invents no severity hierarchy among Adi–Adi, Madhya–Madhya and Antya–Antya, and creates no unsupported medical or physical distinction between them.\n\n**8.4 Nadi Dosha and overall compatibility.** An astrologer looks at all eight Kootas, the Moon sign and Nakshatra, the Rashi-lord relationship, the seventh house and its lord, the Navamsha, any progeny-related factors (assessed *separately* under traditional progeny promise) and the overall marriage promise. **None of these cancels Nadi Dosha unless a documented rule says so.**\n\n### Lesson 9 – Advanced relationship analysis\nDo not merge the situations below. Each is its own case.\n| Situation | Same Nadi? | Raw score | Needs checking |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Same Nadi, different Nakshatras, different Rashis | Yes | 0 | Rashi-lord, friendship, Navamsha conditions |\n| Same Nadi, different Nakshatras, same Rashi | Yes | 0 | Same-Rashi / different-Nakshatra condition |\n| Same Nakshatra, different Rashis (split Nakshatra) | Yes | 0 | Same-Nakshatra / different-Rashi condition |\n| Same Nakshatra, same Rashi, different Padas | Yes | 0 | Pada condition (variant A) |\n| Same Nakshatra, different Rashis and different Padas | Yes | 0 | Pada condition (variant B) and the Rashi condition |\n| Same Nakshatra, same Pada | Yes | 0 | No Pada condition; some sources call same Pada a hindrance |\n| Same Rashi, different Nadi | No | 8 | Nothing: no Dosha |\n\n**9.1 Same Nadi, different Rashi.** Both Nakshatras are in the same Nadi and the Moon Rashis differ. The raw score is 0. Some traditions are said to treat the different-Rashi case as an exception; the exact condition differs and is unverified (see the Exceptions section). Example with a split Nakshatra: Krittika Pada 1 is in Aries, but Pada 3 is in Taurus.\n\n**9.2 Same Nadi, different Nakshatra.** The Nakshatras differ but are in one Nadi. Examples: Adi: Ashwini and Ardra. Madhya: Bharani and Mrigashira. Antya: Krittika and Rohini. The score is 0 before any exception. Whether *different Nakshatras* alone is a cancellation, or whether the *Rashi* condition must also hold, depends on the source; the circulated statement ties it to the same Rashi.\n\n**9.3 Same Rashi, different Nadi.** Nadi is Nakshatra-based, Bhakoot is Rashi-based. Within one Rashi the Nakshatras can belong to different Nadis, so a same-Rashi pair can score full 8. The engine counts them from the real boundaries:\n| Rashi | Nadis found in it |\n|---|---|\n| Aries, Cancer, Leo, Scorpio, Sagittarius, Pisces | all three (Adi, Madhya, Antya) |\n| Taurus, Capricorn | Madhya and Antya |\n| Gemini, Aquarius | Adi and Madhya |\n| Virgo | Adi and Madhya |\n| Libra | Madhya and Antya |\n\nSo six Rashis hold all three Nadis and the other six hold two. Example: Moon in Aries in Ashwini (Adi) and Moon in Aries in Bharani (Madhya): same Rashi, different Nadi, score 8, no Dosha.\n\n**9.4 Same Nakshatra and same Pada.** Both persons share the Nakshatra and the Pada; their Moons may still differ within that 3°20′ Pada. The Nadi is identical and the raw score is 0. No classical source listing a same-Nakshatra, same-Pada exemption was found; **do not assume any Pada exception applies to every Nakshatra**.\n\n**9.5 Same Nakshatra and different Pada.** A major research topic, treated in the Exceptions section. Three situations must be separated:\n1. **A.** Same Nakshatra, different Pada, same Rashi (for example Rohini Pada 1 and Pada 3).\n2. **B.** Same Nakshatra, different Pada, different Rashi (for example Krittika Pada 1 and Pada 3).\n3. **C.** Same Nakshatra, same Pada.\n\n**9.6 Rashi lords.** The claim: Nadi Dosha is \"mitigated\" if both Moon Rashis have the **same lord** (Aries and Scorpio: Mars; Taurus and Libra: Venus; Gemini and Virgo: Mercury; Sagittarius and Pisces: Jupiter; Capricorn and Aquarius: Saturn), or if the two lords are **mutual natural friends**. Check friendship in both directions. Distinguish mutual friendship from one-sided friendship; natural friendship from temporary friendship; and do not substitute Panchadha Maitri for natural friendship unless the selected source asks for it.\n| Planet | Natural friends | Neutrals | Enemies |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sun | Moon, Mars, Jupiter | Mercury | Venus, Saturn |\n| Moon | Sun, Mercury | Mars, Jupiter, Venus, Saturn | None |\n| Mars | Sun, Moon, Jupiter | Venus, Saturn | Mercury |\n| Mercury | Sun, Venus | Mars, Jupiter, Saturn | Moon |\n| Jupiter | Sun, Moon, Mars | Saturn | Mercury, Venus |\n| Venus | Mercury, Saturn | Mars, Jupiter | Sun, Moon |\n| Saturn | Mercury, Venus | Jupiter | Sun, Moon, Mars |\n\nOf the 21 pairs of different planets, **six are mutual friends** (Sun–Moon, Sun–Mars, Sun–Jupiter, Mars–Jupiter, Mercury–Venus, Venus–Saturn), **five are one-sided** (Sun–Mercury, Moon–Mars, Moon–Mercury, Moon–Jupiter, Mercury–Saturn) and ten are not friends. The interactive table in the Exception Research tab computes the same list.\n\n**9.7 Navamsha lords.** The Navamsha of the Moon is found from the Moon's longitude: Navamsha index = ⌊longitude ÷ 3°20′⌋ mod 12. In Nakshatra and Pada terms: counting the 108 Padas round the zodiac from Ashwini Pada 1, **Navamsha sign = (Pada number − 1) mod 12 + 1**. Its lord is the lord of that sign. Whether friendly or common Navamsha lords count as a Nadi cancellation is **not supported by any source found** (grade E); it is built as a separate, configurable calculator.\n\n**9.8 Other astrological factors.** Favourable Graha Maitri, Bhakoot, Gana, Tara or Vashya; a strong Moon; a strong seventh house or lord; a strong Navamsha; progeny-related combinations; and Gotra considerations are classified in the Exceptions section. **A general favourable chart factor is never called an automatic Nadi cancellation.**\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### Lesson 10 – Comparing rules, texts, schools and regions\nThis table is the course's honest map. \"Evidence\" uses the seven-level hierarchy of Lesson 11. Where no source could be verified the entry says so.\n| Rule | Text, school or region | Exact condition | Cancellation or mitigation | Score treatment | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Conventional Nadi scoring | Tradition A, common published tables | Same Nadi 0, different Nadi 8 | None | Raw score only | Widely consistent across published tables; no verse verified (grade C/D) |\n| Same Rashi, different Nakshatra | Attributed to the Muhurta Chintamani tradition by modern sources | Same Nadi, same Moon Rashi, different Nakshatra | Described as cancellation (the most often repeated) | Restoration unclear in sources | Level 6; wording and verse not verified |\n| Same Nakshatra, different Rashi | Same attribution | Same Nakshatra occupying different Rashis (nine split Nakshatras) | Called full by some pages, partial by others | Unclear | Level 6; not verified |\n| Same Nakshatra, different Pada | Same attribution plus later regional statements | Different Pada of one Nakshatra; scope disputed | Described as cancellation; at least one circulated source treats the same Pada (padavedha) as a hindrance | Unclear | Level 6; disputed, scope unverified |\n| Same Rashi lord | Attributed in the brief to Muhurta Ganapati and others | Different Rashis with one lord | Described as mitigation or cancellation | Unclear | Level 7; not located |\n| Mutual natural friendship of Rashi lords | Same | Both lords regard each other as friends | Same | Unclear | Level 7; not located |\n| Navamsha lords friendly or common | No source located | Navamsha lords same or mutual friends | Unknown | Unknown | Level 7 |\n| Specific Nakshatra exemptions | Lists said to come from the Narada Samhita and Piyushdhara | Both persons born in the same listed Nakshatra | Exemption | Unclear | Level 7; lists differ; none verified |\n| South Indian, Tamil, Telugu, Kerala practice | Regional Porutham systems | Not verified in this research | Not stated | Not stated | Not verified; do not generalise from one author or website |\n| B. V. Raman and other published authorities | Modern published works | Not consulted | Not stated | Not stated | Not verified |\n\n> **Never generalise.** One website is not a tradition; one author is not a region. A \"South Indian\" or \"North Indian\" Nadi profile cannot be built from this table. Profile E therefore refuses to run unless the user names the actual source.\n\n### Lesson 11 – The evidence hierarchy\nEvery exception in ASTROSUTRAS carries one of seven evidence levels.\n| Level | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Verified primary textual rule |\n| 2 | Recognised commentary |\n| 3 | Published traditional authority |\n| 4 | Documented regional practice |\n| 5 | Modern astrologer's interpretation |\n| 6 | Popular contemporary claim |\n| 7 | Unverified circulated rule |\n\nThe course grade letters (A verified text, B established practice, C later convention, D contemporary or software, E unverified or disputed) are used beside the level. In this research **every exception rule sits at level 6 or 7 (grade D or E)**; none could be placed higher because no primary or commentary text was seen.\n\n### Lesson 12 – The six rule profiles\nProfiles are **software configurations for transparent comparison, not six classical schools**.\n| Profile | Name | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | Conventional Nadi scoring | Same 0, different 8. No automatic exception; raw score shown on its own |\n| B | Muhurta Chintamani-attributed exceptions | Same Rashi/different Nakshatra and same Nakshatra/different Rashi may cancel (if acknowledged); the Pada condition is only a candidate because its scope is unverified |\n| C | Rashi-lord exception profile | Same lord of different Rashis, or mutual natural friendship, may cancel |\n| D | Extended traditional profile | Each check set to off, candidate, mitigate or cancel; default has the lord and Navamsha checks as mitigating |\n| E | Regional practice profile | You must name the actual source; empty until configured |\n| F | Research comparison | Shows every candidate with its evidence; applies none |\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### Lesson 13 – Classical cancellation and exception research\n**13.1 Muhurta Chintamani, Vivaha Prakarana.** The tradition most often named for the three Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada conditions is the *Muhurta Chintamani* (traditionally attributed to Rama Daivajna; this attribution is stated in many manuals and was not checked here).\n| Item | Status in this course |\n|---|---|\n| Original Sanskrit verse | **Not verified. No verse is quoted and none is reconstructed** |\n| Transliteration | Not given (no verified original) |\n| Literal translation | Not given |\n| Interpretative statement | \"Modern sources say the three conditions cancel the Dosha\" (secondary, grade D) |\n| Verse numbering | Circulated numbers include 34, 36 and 6.37; editions differ; none verified |\n| Does it cancel Nadi, Gana or both? | Some pages say Nadi and Gana; not verified |\n| Commentary | The Piyushdhara commentary is named by some sources; no passage was located |\n\n**13.2 Same Rashi, different Nakshatra.** The Moon Rashi is shared but the Janma Nakshatras differ. Modern pages call this the *most widely recognised cancellation* of all three Nadi Doshas. Why the shared Rashi matters is explained only symbolically. Whether the *raw score stays zero* depends on the reporting convention; sources do not say that the eight points are automatically restored. **In ASTROSUTRAS the raw score stays 0 in every case.**\n\n**13.3 Same Nakshatra, different Rashi.** This situation exists only for the **nine split Nakshatras**. The Pada decides the Rashi.\n| Nakshatra | Pada(s) in the first Rashi | Pada(s) in the second Rashi |\n|---|---|---|\n| Krittika | 1 in Aries | 2, 3, 4 in Taurus |\n| Mrigashira | 1, 2 in Taurus | 3, 4 in Gemini |\n| Punarvasu | 1, 2, 3 in Gemini | 4 in Cancer |\n| Uttara Phalguni | 1 in Leo | 2, 3, 4 in Virgo |\n| Chitra | 1, 2 in Virgo | 3, 4 in Libra |\n| Vishakha | 1, 2, 3 in Libra | 4 in Scorpio |\n| Uttara Ashadha | 1 in Sagittarius | 2, 3, 4 in Capricorn |\n| Dhanishta | 1, 2 in Capricorn | 3, 4 in Aquarius |\n| Purva Bhadrapada | 1, 2, 3 in Aquarius | 4 in Pisces |\n\nPages differ on whether this is a full or a partial cancellation, so the course reports it as an *eligible condition* with its evidence label.\n\n**13.4 Same Nakshatra, different Pada.** The three possibilities must stay separate: **A** same Rashi, **B** different Rashi, **C** same Pada (no exception). Several pages say a different Pada cancels Nadi Dosha; one circulated source says the opposite for the same Pada (padavedha). The scope (all Nakshatras? the same Rashi only?) is unclear. **Different Pada is therefore not treated as a universal exception.** In Profile B it is a candidate only and gives the status REVIEW_REQUIRED.\n\n**13.5 Rashi-lord friendship.** The brief attributes a rule about the same lord and mutual friendship of the Rashi lords to Muhurta Ganapati and other recognised authorities. **No such passage was located**, so it stays a level 7 claim. Whether it is an independent cancellation or needs extra conditions is not known.\n\n**13.6 Specific-Nakshatra exemptions.** Circulated lists name Nakshatras for which a *same-Nakshatra* pair is said to be exempt from Nadi Dosha. They are kept as **separate lists, never merged**:\n| List | Nakshatras | Attribution | Verified? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| List 1 | Rohini, Ardra, Magha, Pushya, Vishakha, Shravana, Bharani, Uttara Bhadrapada | Web pages attribute it to the Narada Samhita | No |\n| List 2 | List 1 plus Mrigashira, Krittika, Revati | Named in the course brief; attribution unknown | No |\n| Piyushdhara slot | (empty) | Commentary list not located | No |\nEach list applies only when **both people are born in the same listed Nakshatra**, and extra conditions, if any, are not known.\n\n**13.7 Regional variations.** See Lesson 10. Do not describe a whole region from one author.\n\n**13.8 What the research concludes.** (1) The 27-Nakshatra allocation is consistent across the published tables seen. (2) The three Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada exceptions are widely repeated and attributed to the Muhurta Chintamani tradition, but the verse, wording and scope need edition-level verification. (3) The lord, Navamsha and specific-Nakshatra rules are circulated claims. (4) Because of this, the engine keeps every cancellation separate and **never applies one without your acknowledgement**.\n\n### Lesson 14 – Raw score, Dosha status and adjusted score\n**14.1 Raw score.** Different Nadi 8 of 8; same Nadi 0 of 8. Always available.\n\n**14.2 Dosha status.**\n| Status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| NO_DOSHA | Different Nadis |\n| DOSHA_PRESENT | Same Nadi; nothing eligible or applied |\n| DOSHA_PRESENT_WITH_POTENTIAL_EXCEPTION | Eligible exception exists but was not applied (unacknowledged, or research profile) |\n| DOSHA_CANCELLED_BY_SELECTED_RULE | The profile applied a cancelling rule (evidence still unverified) |\n| DOSHA_MITIGATED | A mitigating rule applied; the Dosha stays flagged |\n| REVIEW_REQUIRED | A candidate whose scope is unverified is eligible; a person must decide |\n| INSUFFICIENT_DATA | The data needed (for example the Pada) are missing |\n\n**14.3 Two reporting methods.** *Method A: cancellation without score restoration.* The raw score stays 0 and the adjusted score stays 0. *Method B: cancellation with score restoration.* The raw score stays 0; the adjusted score may become 8 and is **labelled ADJUSTED** with the rule that produced it. Never silently replace the raw score.\n\n**14.4 A worked report.** Person A: Krittika Pada 1, Aries, Antya. Person B: Krittika Pada 3, Taurus, Antya.\n| Field | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Same Nakshatra | Yes |\n| Same Nadi | Yes |\n| Different Rashi | Yes (Aries and Taurus) |\n| Different Pada | Yes (1 and 3) |\n| Raw Nadi score | 0 of 8 |\n| Exceptions eligible | Same Nakshatra/different Rashi (level 6); same Nakshatra/different Pada, variant B (level 6, scope disputed) |\n| Profile B, acknowledged, Method A | DOSHA_CANCELLED_BY_SELECTED_RULE; adjusted score 0 of 8 |\n| Profile B, acknowledged, Method B | Same status; ADJUSTED score 8 of 8, raw score still 0 of 8 |\n| Profile B, not acknowledged | DOSHA_PRESENT_WITH_POTENTIAL_EXCEPTION; nothing applied |\n| Profile A | DOSHA_PRESENT; no exception examined |\n\n> **Caution.** A report that says only \"Nadi Dosha cancelled\" is incomplete. It must show the exact rule, the profile, the evidence level and the raw score.\n\n### Lesson 15 – Other factors are not automatic cancellations\nFor every factor below, ask which role it plays: **A** explicit Nadi cancellation, **B** school-specific exception, **C** general mitigating factor, **D** independent horoscope consideration, **E** unverified popular claim.\n| Factor | Role in this course |\n|---|---|\n| Favourable Graha Maitri, Bhakoot, Gana, Tara or Vashya | C: general mitigating factor; a claim that it *cancels* Nadi is E |\n| Strong Moon | D |\n| Strong seventh house or seventh lord | D |\n| Strong Navamsha | D (not the same as the Navamsha-lord exception) |\n| Favourable progeny-related combinations | D (separate traditional assessment) |\n| Different Gotra or lineage considerations | E |\n\n**Required caution (shown by the software).**\n> *Nadi Koota is a traditional component of Vedic horoscope compatibility analysis. Nadi Dosha is an astrological classification based on Janma Nakshatra and is not a medical or genetic diagnosis.*\n\n## Examples\n\n### The 15 worked cases (verified by the engine)\nEach case is computed live in the **Cases** tab. The table gives the Padas, Rashis and results; the \"eligible\" column is a research view and applies nothing.\n| # | Person A | Person B | Raw | Eligible conditions (research view) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini P1, Aries, Adi | Ardra P1, Gemini, Adi | 0 | Navamsha lords only (Mars and Jupiter) |\n| 2 | Bharani P1, Aries, Madhya | Mrigashira P1, Taurus, Madhya | 0 | Navamsha lords only (both Sun) |\n| 3 | Krittika P1, Aries, Antya | Rohini P1, Taurus, Antya | 0 | Navamsha lords only (Jupiter and Mars) |\n| 4 | Ashwini P1, Aries, Adi | Bharani P1, Aries, Madhya | 8 | None needed |\n| 5 | Rohini P1, Taurus, Antya | Ardra P1, Gemini, Adi | 8 | None needed |\n| 6 | Krittika P2, Taurus, Antya | Rohini P1, Taurus, Antya | 0 | Same Rashi, different Nakshatra |\n| 7 | Krittika P1, Aries, Antya | Krittika P3, Taurus, Antya | 0 | Same Nakshatra/different Rashi; Pada, variant B |\n| 8 | Rohini P1, Taurus, Antya | Rohini P3, Taurus, Antya | 0 | Pada, variant A; Rohini is on List 1 |\n| 9 | Ashwini P2, Aries, Adi | Ashwini P2, Aries, Adi | 0 | No Pada condition (same Pada); same Navamsha lord only |\n| 10 | Ashwini P1, Aries, Adi | Bharani P1, Aries, Madhya | 8 | Same Rashi, but different Nadi: no Dosha |\n| 11 | Mrigashira P3, Gemini, Madhya | Mrigashira P2, Taurus, Madhya | 0 | Same Nakshatra/different Rashi; Pada B; lords Mercury and Venus mutual; Navamsha lords mutual |\n| 12 | Ashwini P2, Aries, Adi | Jyeshtha P1, Scorpio, Adi | 0 | Same lord (Mars); Navamsha lords not friendly |\n| 13 | Bharani P2, Aries, Madhya | Purva Ashadha P3, Sagittarius, Madhya | 0 | Lords Mars and Jupiter mutual friends; Navamsha lords mutual |\n| 14 | Purva Phalguni P2, Leo, Madhya | Chitra P1, Virgo, Madhya | 0 | None: Sun and Mercury are one-sided; Navamsha lords also one-sided |\n| 15 | Bharani P2, Aries, Madhya | Chitra P3, Libra, Madhya | 0 | Navamsha lords only (Mercury and Venus mutual) |\n\n**Reading the cases.**\n- Cases 1 to 3 show the three same-Nadi groups with different Nakshatras. The raw score is 0 and no Rashi-based exception applies.\n- Case 4 and case 10 are the same pair: they show that same Rashi (Aries) does not create a Nadi Dosha.\n- Case 7 and case 8 show why the Pada rule needs variants: A (same Rashi) and B (different Rashi).\n- Case 9 has the same Pada, so the Pada condition is simply not met.\n- Case 14 is the lesson in one-sided friendship: the Moon-Mercury pair is even sharper (the Moon regards Mercury as a friend; Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy).\n- Case 15 is built to isolate the Navamsha condition: the Rashi lords Mars and Venus are neutral to each other, and the Navamsha lords Mercury and Venus are mutual friends. Profile D (default) mitigates it, Profile B ignores it, so the statuses differ while the raw score is the same.\n\n### How the statuses differ for the same pair\nTake case 7 with the evidence acknowledged and Method A: Profile A gives DOSHA_PRESENT; Profile B gives DOSHA_CANCELLED_BY_SELECTED_RULE (adjusted 0); Profile C gives DOSHA_PRESENT (the lords Mars and Venus are neutral); Profile F gives DOSHA_PRESENT_WITH_POTENTIAL_EXCEPTION. The raw score is 0 in all four. Use the **Profiles** tab to see this live.\n\n## Practice\n\n### Lesson 16 – Practical work\n**Guided steps.** (1) Learn the nine Nakshatras of each Nadi, using the Adi, Madhya, Antya, Antya, Madhya, Adi pattern as a checksum. (2) Do ten pair scores by hand before using the tools. (3) For a same-Nadi pair always note Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada. (4) Check each exception against its evidence label. (5) Compare profiles A, B, C and F for one pair. (6) Do the sixteen exercises. (7) Take the final assessment.\n\n### Practical assignments\nThe sixteen exercises cover: classifying all 27 Nakshatras; identifying a Nadi; scoring; same-Nadi and different-Nadi combinations; same Rashi/different Nakshatra; same Nakshatra/different Rashi; same Nakshatra/different Pada; Rashi lords; natural friendship; profile comparison; cancellation versus mitigation; why Nadi Dosha is not a medical diagnosis; split Nakshatras; Navamsha lords; and raw versus adjusted wording. They are in the **Exercises** tab.\n\n### Written task\nIn your Learning Journal write a six-line professional reading of one same-Nadi pair that (a) names the profile and whether you acknowledged the unverified rules, (b) gives the raw score, (c) names the Dosha status, (d) names any eligible exception with its evidence label, (e) states the adjusted score only if a restoring profile was used, (f) says plainly that Nadi is not a medical or genetic finding.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What does Nadi (नाड़ी) mean in Ashtakoota Milan?\nA: One of the eight Kootas: a three-fold classification of the 27 Nakshatras (Adi, Madhya, Antya), worth 8 of 36 points.\n\nQ: How many points does Nadi Koota carry?\nA: 8 out of 36, the highest single weight.\n\nQ: Which birth data does Nadi use?\nA: The Janma Nakshatra (जन्म नक्षत्र) of each person.\n\nQ: Which birth data does Bhakoot use?\nA: The Moon Rashi (राशि), not the Nakshatra.\n\nQ: Name the three Nadis with their Hindi names.\nA: Adi (आदि), Madhya (मध्य), Antya (अन्त्य).\n\nQ: How many Nakshatras are in each Nadi?\nA: Nine, because 27 = 3 × 9. Each Nakshatra is in exactly one Nadi.\n\nQ: Group codes for the three Nadis?\nA: Adi = 1, Madhya = 2, Antya = 3.\n\nQ: Conventional Nadi score for different Nadis?\nA: 8 of 8.\n\nQ: Conventional Nadi score for the same Nadi?\nA: 0 of 8, and a Nadi Dosha is flagged.\n\nQ: Why is the base Nadi matrix symmetric?\nA: It compares group identity, not direction. Adi with Madhya is 8 whichever person is listed first.\n\nQ: How many cells of the 27 × 27 matrix score 0 and how many 8?\nA: 243 zeros (27 rows × 9) and 486 eights (27 × 18), 729 in all.\n\nQ: What fraction of random pairs share a Nadi?\nA: One in three (243 ÷ 729). A same-Nadi result is common.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Adi?\nA: Ashwini, Ardra, Punarvasu, Uttara Phalguni, Hasta, Jyeshtha, Mula, Shatabhisha, Purva Bhadrapada.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Madhya?\nA: Bharani, Mrigashira, Pushya, Purva Phalguni, Chitra, Anuradha, Purva Ashadha, Dhanishta, Uttara Bhadrapada.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras are Antya?\nA: Krittika, Rohini, Ashlesha, Magha, Swati, Vishakha, Uttara Ashadha, Shravana, Revati.\n\nQ: What pattern do the Nadis follow round the zodiac?\nA: Adi, Madhya, Antya, Antya, Madhya, Adi, repeated. A memory aid and checksum, not a classical statement.\n\nQ: Is Nadi the same as an Ayurvedic Nadi examination?\nA: No. An astrological Nadi comes from the birth Nakshatra; it is not a pulse examination or any clinical assessment.\n\nQ: Does Nadi Dosha diagnose infertility or inherited disease?\nA: No. It is not a genetic test or medical diagnosis. Medical concerns need qualified healthcare professionals.\n\nQ: What is the Vata, Pitta, Kapha claim?\nA: A circulated claim (seen only on secondary pages) linking Adi, Madhya, Antya to the Ayurvedic doshas. Unverified; do not treat Nadi as Prakriti.\n\nQ: Can two people in the same Rashi have different Nadis?\nA: Yes. For example Ashwini (Adi) and Bharani (Madhya) are both in Aries: score 8.\n\nQ: In how many Rashis do all three Nadis occur?\nA: Six: Aries, Cancer, Leo, Scorpio, Sagittarius and Pisces. The other six contain two Nadis.\n\nQ: How many Nakshatras are split between two Rashis?\nA: Nine: Krittika, Mrigashira, Punarvasu, Uttara Phalguni, Chitra, Vishakha, Uttara Ashadha, Dhanishta, Purva Bhadrapada.\n\nQ: Which Rashis does Krittika occupy?\nA: Pada 1 in Aries; Padas 2, 3 and 4 in Taurus.\n\nQ: Which Rashis does Mrigashira occupy?\nA: Padas 1 and 2 in Taurus; Padas 3 and 4 in Gemini.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra lies wholly within Taurus?\nA: Rohini (Krittika and Mrigashira are split).\n\nQ: Name the three exception families attributed to the Muhurta Chintamani tradition.\nA: Same Rashi with different Nakshatras; same Nakshatra with different Rashis; same Nakshatra with different Padas. Attribution and wording are not verified.\n\nQ: Is the Pada exception a verified universal rule?\nA: No. Its scope is unclear and at least one circulated source treats the same Pada as a hindrance. It is a candidate only.\n\nQ: Which Rashi pairs share a lord?\nA: Aries and Scorpio (Mars), Taurus and Libra (Venus), Gemini and Virgo (Mercury), Sagittarius and Pisces (Jupiter), Capricorn and Aquarius (Saturn).\n\nQ: What is mutual natural friendship?\nA: Each planet regards the other as a natural friend. Check both directions.\n\nQ: Give an example of one-sided friendship.\nA: Moon regards Mercury as a friend, but Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy. Also Mercury regards the Sun as a friend while the Sun is neutral to Mercury.\n\nQ: How do you find the Navamsha sign of a Pada?\nA: Count the 108 Padas from Ashwini Pada 1 round the 12 signs: Navamsha = (Pada number − 1) mod 12 + 1. Equivalent to ⌊longitude ÷ 3°20′⌋ mod 12.\n\nQ: Is the Navamsha-lord exception supported by a source?\nA: No source was located (grade E). It is a separate, configurable calculator.\n\nQ: What is the difference between cancellation and mitigation?\nA: Cancellation changes the Dosha status to cancelled under a named rule; mitigation keeps the Dosha flagged and only eases its status. Neither changes the raw score.\n\nQ: May the software restore eight points silently?\nA: Never. Restoration only follows a profile and policy, is labelled ADJUSTED, and the raw score stays visible.\n\nQ: What does Profile F do?\nA: Lists every candidate exception with its evidence and applies none.\n\nQ: Does a high total Guna score cancel Nadi Dosha?\nA: No. Only a documented rule can change the status, and the raw score stays 0.\n\nQ: Is any Nadi exception source-verified in this course?\nA: No. Every exception is level 6 or 7 (grade D or E) and is applied only after you acknowledge that.\n\nQ: Do the three same-Nadi pairs differ in severity?\nA: The conventional score treats Adi–Adi, Madhya–Madhya and Antya–Antya equally (0). No verified severity hierarchy exists; do not invent one.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: Nadi Koota carries how many of the 36 Ashtakoota points?\nA) 6\nB) 7\nC) 8\nD) 4\nANS: C\nWHY: Nadi is the heaviest Koota with 8 points.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Nadi Koota is read from which data?\nA) The Moon Rashi\nB) The Navamsha lagna\nC) The ascendant\nD) The Janma Nakshatra\nANS: D\nWHY: Nadi is a Nakshatra-based classification.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Bhakoot is read from which data?\nA) The Moon Rashi\nB) The Janma Nakshatra\nC) The Pada\nD) The Nadi\nANS: A\nWHY: Bhakoot compares the two Moon Rashis; Nadi compares Nakshatra groups.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which list gives the three Nadis?\nA) Vata, Pitta, Kapha\nB) Adi, Madhya, Antya\nC) Deva, Manushya, Rakshasa\nD) Brahmin, Kshatriya, Vaishya\nANS: B\nWHY: Adi, Madhya and Antya are the three Nadis.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many Nakshatras belong to each Nadi?\nA) 7\nB) 8\nC) 9\nD) 10\nANS: C\nWHY: 27 = 3 × 9.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Ashwini belongs to which Nadi?\nA) Adi\nB) Madhya\nC) Antya\nD) None\nANS: A\nWHY: Ashwini is the first Adi Nakshatra.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Bharani belongs to which Nadi?\nA) Antya\nB) Adi\nC) Madhya\nD) None\nANS: C\nWHY: Bharani is Madhya.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Krittika belongs to which Nadi (whatever its Pada)?\nA) Adi\nB) Madhya\nC) Antya\nD) It depends on the Pada\nANS: C\nWHY: Nadi is assigned to the whole Nakshatra; the Pada changes the Rashi, not the Nadi.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra is Adi?\nA) Rohini\nB) Shravana\nC) Pushya\nD) Hasta\nANS: D\nWHY: Hasta is Adi; Rohini and Shravana are Antya, Pushya is Madhya.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra is Madhya?\nA) Chitra\nB) Swati\nC) Mula\nD) Revati\nANS: A\nWHY: Chitra is Madhya; Swati and Revati are Antya, Mula is Adi.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra is Antya?\nA) Anuradha\nB) Jyeshtha\nC) Vishakha\nD) Dhanishta\nANS: C\nWHY: Vishakha is Antya; Anuradha and Dhanishta are Madhya, Jyeshtha is Adi.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The conventional raw Nadi score for two people of the same Nadi is:\nA) 8\nB) 4\nC) 0\nD) It depends on the Rashi\nANS: C\nWHY: Same Nadi gives 0 before any exception.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The raw Nadi score for two different Nadis is:\nA) 0\nB) 8\nC) 4\nD) 2\nANS: B\nWHY: Different Nadi gives the full 8.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Ashwini (Adi) and Ardra (Adi): raw score?\nA) 0\nB) 8\nC) 4\nD) 1.5\nANS: A\nWHY: Both are Adi, so the Nadi is the same.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Rohini (Antya) and Hasta (Adi): raw score?\nA) 0\nB) 4\nC) 8\nD) Cannot be known\nANS: C\nWHY: Different Nadis give 8.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many of the 729 cells of the 27 × 27 matrix score 0?\nA) 243\nB) 81\nC) 486\nD) 27\nANS: A\nWHY: 27 rows × 9 same-Nadi cells = 243.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: How many zero cells does every row of the matrix contain?\nA) 3\nB) 18\nC) 9\nD) 27\nANS: C\nWHY: Each row has the nine Nakshatras of its own Nadi (including itself) at 0 and eighteen at 8.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The conventional Nadi matrix is:\nA) Symmetric\nB) Asymmetric\nC) Directional\nD) Random\nANS: A\nWHY: Group identity is compared, so swapping A and B changes nothing.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Two people share the same Rashi. Which is true?\nA) Their Nadi is always the same\nB) Their Nadi may differ, for example Ashwini and Bharani in Aries\nC) Their Nadi is always different\nD) Nadi cannot be calculated\nANS: B\nWHY: Nadi is Nakshatra-based, and one Rashi holds Nakshatras of different Nadis.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which Rashi contains Nakshatras of all three Nadis?\nA) Taurus\nB) Gemini\nC) Libra\nD) Aries\nANS: D\nWHY: Aries has Ashwini (Adi), Bharani (Madhya) and Krittika Pada 1 (Antya); Taurus, Libra and Gemini hold only two Nadis.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: How many Nakshatras are split between two Rashis?\nA) 6\nB) 7\nC) 9\nD) 12\nANS: C\nWHY: Krittika, Mrigashira, Punarvasu, Uttara Phalguni, Chitra, Vishakha, Uttara Ashadha, Dhanishta and Purva Bhadrapada.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Krittika Pada 1 lies in which Rashi?\nA) Aries\nB) Taurus\nC) Gemini\nD) Pisces\nANS: A\nWHY: Krittika begins at 26°40′ Aries; only its first Pada is in Aries.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Mrigashira Pada 3 lies in which Rashi?\nA) Gemini\nB) Taurus\nC) Cancer\nD) Aries\nANS: A\nWHY: Mrigashira Padas 1 and 2 are in Taurus, Padas 3 and 4 in Gemini.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra lies wholly in Taurus?\nA) Krittika\nB) Rohini\nC) Mrigashira\nD) Ardra\nANS: B\nWHY: Rohini lies completely in Taurus; Krittika and Mrigashira are split.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which pair of Rashis shares the same lord?\nA) Aries and Scorpio\nB) Aries and Taurus\nC) Leo and Cancer\nD) Gemini and Cancer\nANS: A\nWHY: Both are ruled by Mars.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which pair are mutual natural friends?\nA) Moon and Mercury\nB) Sun and Mercury\nC) Mars and Jupiter\nD) Moon and Mars\nANS: C\nWHY: Mars and Jupiter each list the other as a friend. The Moon regards Mercury as a friend but Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy, and the Sun is neutral to Mercury.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The Moon regards Mercury as a friend, but Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy. This is:\nA) Mutual friendship\nB) Mutual enmity\nC) One-sided friendship\nD) Neutrality\nANS: C\nWHY: Only one direction is friendly, so it does not qualify as mutual friendship.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The Navamsha lord is the lord of:\nA) The Moon's Rashi\nB) The ascendant\nC) The Nakshatra\nD) The Navamsha sign occupied by the Moon\nANS: D\nWHY: Navamsha lord is not Rashi lord; do not confuse them.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which statement about the exception rules in this course is correct?\nA) All are verified in primary texts\nB) None is source-verified and each carries an evidence label\nC) Only the Rashi rules are unverified\nD) They are applied automatically\nANS: B\nWHY: Every rule is level 6 or 7 and applied only after acknowledgement.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: When a profile restores points, the report must:\nA) Show both scores and label the new one as adjusted\nB) Hide the raw score\nC) Delete the exception\nD) Average the two\nANS: A\nWHY: The raw score is never overwritten; the adjusted score is labelled.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: An exception is eligible but the unverified rules were not acknowledged. The status is:\nA) DOSHA_CANCELLED_BY_SELECTED_RULE\nB) NO_DOSHA\nC) DOSHA_PRESENT_WITH_POTENTIAL_EXCEPTION\nD) DOSHA_MITIGATED\nANS: C\nWHY: The exception is reported but not applied.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Nadi Dosha is best described as:\nA) A genetic test\nB) A diagnosis of infertility\nC) A traditional astrological classification result\nD) An Ayurvedic pulse reading\nANS: C\nWHY: It is derived from the Janma Nakshatra and is not a medical or genetic finding.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A client with the same Nadi asks whether they can have healthy children. The best reply is:\nA) It is certain to cause trouble\nB) Nadi cannot answer this; please consult qualified healthcare professionals\nC) You must perform a remedy at once\nD) Nadi guarantees good health\nANS: B\nWHY: Nadi cannot diagnose fertility or predict a child's health, and fear-based language must be avoided.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Does a high total Guna score automatically cancel Nadi Dosha?\nA) Yes\nB) Only in Profile A\nC) Only above 28\nD) No\nANS: D\nWHY: Only a documented exception rule, selected by you, can change the status.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which statement about severity is correct?\nA) The conventional score treats all three same-Nadi pairs equally and no verified hierarchy exists\nB) Adi–Adi is the most severe\nC) Antya–Antya is the worst\nD) Madhya–Madhya is harmless\nANS: A\nWHY: One secondary page claims an emphasis on Madhya, but it is unverified; the course invents no hierarchy.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The same-Nakshatra, different-Pada exception is:\nA) A verified universal cancellation\nB) A circulated claim with unclear scope, kept as a candidate\nC) Valid for every Nakshatra\nD) Not discussed anywhere\nANS: B\nWHY: Several pages repeat it, one source says the same Pada is a hindrance, and the scope is unverified.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Ashwini Pada 2 and Ashwini Pada 2 (case 9). Which is true?\nA) The different-Pada condition is met\nB) The Nadi is different\nC) The Pada condition is not met; the pair has the same Nakshatra and the same Pada\nD) The score is 8\nANS: C\nWHY: Same Pada means the different-Pada condition fails; the raw score is 0.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Profile F (research comparison):\nA) Applies all rules\nB) Hides unverified rules\nC) Restores eight points\nD) Lists candidates with evidence and applies none\nANS: D\nWHY: Profile F is for comparison only.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A specific-Nakshatra exemption list is used only when:\nA) Any two people are compared\nB) Both people are born in the same listed Nakshatra\nC) The Nakshatras differ\nD) All 27 are listed\nANS: B\nWHY: The circulated lists apply to same-Nakshatra pairs, and are unverified.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: What is the pattern of Nadis round the 27 Nakshatras?\nA) Adi, Madhya, Antya, Antya, Madhya, Adi repeated\nB) Adi, Madhya, Antya repeated\nC) Random\nD) Antya, Madhya, Adi, Adi, Madhya, Antya repeated\nANS: A\nWHY: It is an arithmetic pattern of the table, useful as a checksum, not a classical statement.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. Explain, in four sentences, why Nadi Dosha is not a medical or genetic diagnosis.\nMA: Nadi is assigned from the Janma Nakshatra, a position of the Moon at birth. It is a three-group traditional classification (Adi, Madhya, Antya) with a conventional score (same group 0, different 8). It does not measure DNA, fertility or any clinical finding, and it cannot predict a child's health. Any medical or fertility concern needs qualified healthcare professionals.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Explain why raw score, Dosha status and adjusted score must be kept separate.\nMA: The raw score is the unchanged table result (0 or 8). The Dosha status records whether a selected rule cancels or mitigates the Dosha. The adjusted score exists only if a profile restores points and must be labelled adjusted. Merging them would hide the rule, the evidence and the source.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Calculation 3. Case 7 is Krittika Pada 1 and Krittika Pada 3. State the Nadis, the raw score, and the status under Profile B when the unverified rules are acknowledged and no restoration is used.\nMA: Both are Antya. Raw score 0 of 8. Same Nakshatra with different Rashis (Aries and Taurus) is eligible, so the status is DOSHA_CANCELLED_BY_SELECTED_RULE under Profile B, and the adjusted score remains 0 because Method A does not restore points.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Interpretation 4. Why is one-sided friendship not accepted as mutual friendship, and give an example.\nMA: A mutual-friendship rule needs each lord to regard the other as a friend. The Moon regards Mercury as a friend, but Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy, so only one direction is friendly. Sun and Mercury are similar: Mercury regards the Sun as a friend, but the Sun is neutral to Mercury.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Research 5. State two reasons the exception rules cannot be treated as settled.\nMA: No verse or commentary passage was verified; sources conflict on scope (for example the same Pada); and numbering and lists differ across editions and websites.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\n## References\n\n### Source status (Day 9)\nNo classical verse or chapter was verified for any Nadi rule in this module.\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nadi worth 8 of 36 in the Ashtakoota | C/D | Widely stated in manuals |\n| 27-Nakshatra allocation 9, 9, 9 | C/D | Consistent across published tables seen; matches the shared rule file; no verse verified |\n| Same Nadi 0, different Nadi 8 | C/D | Common published convention |\n| Association of the Nadis with Vata, Pitta, Kapha | E | Seen on secondary pages only; no primary source |\n| Same Rashi, different Nakshatra | D | Attributed to Muhurta Chintamani tradition by modern pages; wording not verified |\n| Same Nakshatra, different Rashi | D | As above; full or partial cancellation disputed |\n| Same Nakshatra, different Pada | E | Scope unclear; a circulated source treats same Pada as a hindrance |\n| Same Rashi lord and mutual friendship | E | Attribution to Muhurta Ganapati not located |\n| Friendly Navamsha lords | E | No source located |\n| Specific-Nakshatra exemption lists | E | List 1 (8 Nakshatras) attributed to Narada Samhita by web pages; not verified; Piyushdhara list not located |\n| Verse numbers (34, 36, 6.37) | E | Circulated; editions differ; none verified |\n| Natural friendship table | C | Standard seven-planet table |\n| Profiles A to F | n/a | Software configurations, not classical schools |\n\n### Notes on the supplied material\nThe brief was checked for arithmetic and listing errors. The 9, 9, 9 Nakshatra allocation, the nine split Nakshatras, the natural friendship table and cases 1 to 11 are correct and are reproduced exactly. Three observations:\n| Supplied | Note |\n|---|---|\n| Illustrative status POTENTIAL_DOSHA and cancellation status CANCELLED_BY_SELECTED_PROFILE in the sample schema | Replaced by the seven statuses of Part 9.2 (DOSHA_PRESENT_WITH_POTENTIAL_EXCEPTION, DOSHA_CANCELLED_BY_SELECTED_RULE and so on) so that the schema and the status list agree |\n| Case 4 (Ashwini and Bharani) and case 10 (Aries Moon in Ashwini and in Bharani) | The same pair: both Nakshatras lie in Aries. They are kept as two cases because they teach two lessons |\n| Case 1 asks for \"exception eligibility\" | Eligibility is computed live: Navamsha lords only, no Rashi-based exception |\n\n### Research and source policy\nThree standard web searches were run for this module. They returned astrology websites (grade D), listed below. Only the search summaries were read; **none of the pages was opened or checked in full**, so what they say is only a lead.\n- https://www.onlinejyotish.com/articles/marriage-matching/english/nadi-kootam.php (verse number 34; an emphasis on Madhya–Madhya; differing opinions)\n- https://www.scribd.com/doc/275297716/NadiDosha (conflicting statement about the same Pada)\n- https://kundli.muthu.co/koot/nadi and https://astromedha.in/insights/vedic/nadi-koota (classification and the Vata, Pitta, Kapha association)\n- https://gokshetra.com/?p=2286 (a page on exempted Nakshatras; list contents not checked)\n- https://www.hindu-blog.com/2024/10/ekanakshatra-vivaha-marriage-where.html (same-Nakshatra marriage; not checked)\nFor every classical passage the course would cite, the following must be recorded: text name, author or attribution, chapter, verse, edition or translator, the exact rule supported, and confidence of verification. Where the exact source cannot be verified, the statement is labelled **\"Traditional practice: source attribution requires verification.\"**\n\n### Bibliography (all entries require verification)\n| Work | Relevance | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Muhurta Chintamani, Vivaha Prakarana (traditionally attributed to Rama Daivajna) | The conditions on same Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada | Not independently verified; edition and verse unknown |\n| Piyushdhara commentary on the Muhurta Chintamani | Commentary on the above; a list of exemptions is claimed | Not located |\n| Narada Samhita | A list of exempted Nakshatras is attributed to it | Not located |\n| Muhurta Ganapati | Rashi-lord friendship rule attributed to it | Not located |\n| B. V. Raman, published work on Muhurtha and marriage matching | A recognised modern author | Not consulted |\n| Regional Porutham manuals (Tamil, Telugu, Kerala) | Possible regional practice | Not consulted; do not generalise |\n\n## Nadi and Medicine\n### Astrological Nadi versus medical and genetic terminology (read this twice)\n| Astrological Nadi | Modern medical or genetic terminology |\n|---|---|\n| A Nakshatra-based classification | A biological and clinical assessment |\n| Three traditional categories (Adi, Madhya, Antya) | Evidence-based medical classifications |\n| Used in traditional marriage matching | Used in healthcare and genetics |\n| A symbolic astrological framework | Laboratory, clinical and family-history evidence |\n\n### What Nadi Koota cannot do\n- It does not measure DNA compatibility. Nadi Dosha is **not a genetic test**.\n- It cannot diagnose infertility.\n- It cannot establish an inherited disease.\n- It cannot predict a child's medical condition.\n- A medical or fertility concern must be assessed by **qualified healthcare professionals**.\n\n### The Ayurvedic confusion\nThe word *Nadi* also means \"pulse\" in Ayurveda, where a physician reads the pulse of a living patient. Astrological Nadi is assigned from a Nakshatra at birth and involves no examination of anyone. The association of Adi, Madhya and Antya with Vata, Pitta and Kapha appears on many web pages, but this research found it only on secondary sources, and even then it is a symbolic association. It is not an assessment of anyone's Prakriti.\n\n### Language to use with clients\n| Avoid | Say instead |\n|---|---|\n| \"Your children will have problems.\" | \"Nadi is a traditional compatibility classification. It cannot say anything about children's health. For fertility or health questions please consult a qualified doctor.\" |\n| \"This is a serious Dosha; you must do a remedy.\" | \"The same Nadi gives zero in the conventional score. Some traditions mention exceptions; here is how each is labelled.\" |\n| \"The match is cancelled.\" | \"The raw score is 0 of 8. Your selected profile treats the pair as follows, and the evidence for that rule is unverified.\" |\n| \"Nadi Dosha is cancelled.\" (alone) | The rule, the profile, the evidence level and the raw score, all together |\n\n> **Instructor note.** Never teach Nadi Dosha as a medical or genetic diagnosis. Teach it as a traditional astrological compatibility classification, with its own mathematical rules and school-specific exceptions.\n\n## 27 Nakshatras\n### The 27-Nakshatra Nadi selector and tables\nChoose the zodiacal or the group-wise view, search a Nakshatra, tap one to see its lord, Nadi, four Padas and Rashi coverage, or export the table.\n{{n9table}}\n\n### The nine split Nakshatras\nThe Rashi of the Moon depends on the Pada for these nine. The table is computed from the Pada arithmetic.\n{{n9split}}\n\n## Matrix\n### The 3 × 3 summary and the complete 27 × 27 Nadi matrix\nAll 729 cells are generated from the Nadi table (nothing is typed in). Red means same Nadi (0). Green means different Nadi (8). Amber means a same-Nadi cell where an exception may need checking under the selected profile. Tap a cell for the Nadi of each person, the raw score and the exceptions that could become eligible.\n{{n9matrix}}\n\n## Exception Research\n### The evidence hierarchy, the rule registry, the profiles and the lists\nEverything here is computed from the engine. Read the evidence level and the grade of every rule before you rely on it.\n{{n9rules}}\n\n### Natural friendship in both directions\n{{n9friend}}\n\n## Profiles\n### Compare the same pair under every profile\nThe raw score is the same in every column. The statuses differ because the profiles differ. Tick the acknowledgement to see the unverified rules applied.\n{{n9compare}}\n\n### The decision engine in thirteen steps\n1. Identify the two Janma Nakshatras.\n2. Identify Adi, Madhya or Antya for each.\n3. Calculate the raw score: different 8, same 0.\n4. If the Nadis are the same, flag a potential Nadi Dosha.\n5. Compare the Moon Rashis.\n6. Compare the Nakshatras.\n7. Compare the Padas.\n8. Check whether the Rashi lords are the same.\n9. Check natural friendship in both directions.\n10. Check the Navamsha lords (only if the profile enables it).\n11. Check specific-Nakshatra exemptions (only if the profile enables a list).\n12. Check any other conditions the selected authority requires.\n13. Return the raw score, status, exception applied, source and evidence, conditions satisfied and not satisfied, adjusted score where permitted, and the interpretation and limits.\n\nThe engine never returns \"Nadi Dosha cancelled\" without the rule, its conditions and its evidence.\n\n## Calculator\n### Nadi Compatibility Calculator\nChoose two Nakshatras (and Padas), a rule profile, a restoration policy and whether to acknowledge that the exceptions are unverified. You can load any of the 15 worked cases. The calculation is always shown in full.\n{{n9calc}}\n\n### Navamsha exception calculator\nA separate calculator for the Navamsha-lord check. It never changes the raw Nadi score.\n{{n9nav}}\n\n## Cases\n### The 15 worked cases, live\nChoose a profile and see every case with its Padas, Rashis, Nadis, raw score, status and applicable exception.\n{{n9cases}}\n\n## Exercises\n### Sixteen practical exercises\nMarked automatically; written tasks show a model answer and a checklist.\n{{n9exercise}}\n\n## Final Assessment\n### The 30-question final assessment\n10 conceptual, 8 Nakshatra classification, 6 scoring, 4 exception and 2 case-study questions. Pass mark 70%. A failed attempt never erases your completed lessons.\n{{n9final}}\n\n## Software Specification\n### NadiKootaEngine (the module in nadi.js)\nFunctions: getNakshatraIndex, getNadiByNakshatra, getNakshatraPada, getMoonRashiFromLongitude, getMoonNavamshaRashi, getRashiLord, getNaturalPlanetaryRelationship, calculateRawNadiScore, identifyNadiDosha, checkSameRashiDifferentNakshatra, checkSameNakshatraDifferentRashi, checkSameNakshatraDifferentPada, checkRashiLordException, checkNavamshaLordException, checkSpecificNakshatraExemption, evaluateNadiRuleProfile and generateNadiReport. The engine has no user-interface code.\n\n### Pseudocode of the main functions\n1. `calculateRawNadiScore(a, b)`: `nadiA = NadiMaster[a]; nadiB = NadiMaster[b]; if nadiA == nadiB: return 0 else return 8`.\n2. `padaRashi(nakshatra, pada)`: `start = (nakshatra - 1) * 800 + (pada - 1) * 200` (arc-minutes); `return floor(start / 1800) + 1`.\n3. `navamshaOfPada(nakshatra, pada)`: `return ((nakshatra - 1) * 4 + (pada - 1)) mod 12 + 1`.\n4. `getNaturalPlanetaryRelationship(p, q)`: look up `p` to `q` and `q` to `p` in the friendship table; `mutual = both Friend`; `oneSided = exactly one Friend`.\n5. `checkSameNakshatraDifferentPada(A, B)`: conditions are same Nadi, same Nakshatra, different Pada; variant A if the Rashi is the same, B if it differs; the result lists conditions met, not met and missing.\n6. `evaluateNadiRuleProfile(input, profile, options)`: resolve and validate both persons; compute the raw score; run every check; for each check read the profile mode (off, candidate, mitigate, cancel); apply a rule only if it is eligible, its mode is cancel or mitigate and `acknowledgeUnverified` is true; derive the status; restore points only if the status is cancelled, the policy is RESTORE and the profile allows it; return the full result with a 13-step trace.\n\n### Data model\n| Data | Fields |\n|---|---|\n| Nakshatra master | number, name, Hindi name, lord, Nadi group |\n| Nadi master | id, name, Hindi name, group code 1 to 3 |\n| Pada boundaries | start in arc-minutes, Rashi, Navamsha sign |\n| Nakshatra to Rashi mapping | padaRashi for 108 Padas, split flag |\n| Rashi master and lords | 12 Rashis, lords |\n| Natural friendship | friends, neutrals, enemies for 7 planets |\n| Rule registry | id, title, family, evidence level, grade, verified flag, source, note |\n| Profile | id, name, rules with mode, restoreAllowed, source name (Profile E) |\n| Calculation result | see the schema below |\n\n### JSON examples\n- Nakshatra master entry: `{\"number\":3,\"name\":\"Krittika\",\"lord\":\"Sun\",\"nadiGroup\":3,\"rashis\":[\"Aries\",\"Taurus\"]}`\n- Nadi master entry: `{\"group\":3,\"id\":\"ANTYA\",\"name\":\"Antya\",\"hindi\":\"अन्त्य नाड़ी\"}`\n- Rule entry: `{\"id\":\"SAME_NAK_DIFF_RASHI\",\"evidenceLevel\":6,\"grade\":\"D\",\"verified\":false,\"source\":\"attributed by modern sources; not verified\"}`\n- Source reference: `{\"work\":\"Muhurta Chintamani, Vivaha Prakarana\",\"verse\":null,\"edition\":null,\"status\":\"requires verification\"}`\n- Calculation profile: `{\"id\":\"B\",\"rules\":{\"SAME_RASHI_DIFF_NAK\":{\"mode\":\"cancel\"},\"SAME_NAK_DIFF_PADA\":{\"mode\":\"candidate\"}},\"restoreAllowed\":true}`\n\n### Calculation result schema (improved)\nFields of the result: module, profile, personA and personB (nakshatra, pada, moonRashi, nadi, rashiLord, navamsha, navamshaLord), sameNadi, sameNakshatra, sameRashi, samePada, sameRashiLord, mutualFriendship, **rawScore**, maximumScore, **doshaStatus**, exceptionChecks (rule, eligible, conditionsMet, conditionsNotMet, conditionsMissing, mode, applied, reason, evidenceLevel, grade, source, sourceProfile), appliedRules, restorationPolicy, scoreRestored, **adjustedScore**, adjustedScoreLabel, acknowledgeUnverified, warnings, trace, interpretation and statement. Improvements over the starting schema: the status list is closed; every check carries its evidence; the raw score is separate from the adjusted score; and no value is null when it can be stated.\n\n### Error handling\n| Condition | Code | Behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| Missing Nakshatra | MISSING_NAKSHATRA | Stops with a message |\n| Invalid Nakshatra or Pada | INVALID_NAKSHATRA, INVALID_PADA | Stops |\n| Rashi does not match the Nakshatra or Pada | RASHI_INCONSISTENT | Stops |\n| Navamsha does not match | NAVAMSHA_INCONSISTENT | Stops |\n| Missing Pada | MISSING_PADA warning | Pada-dependent rules cannot be evaluated; status INSUFFICIENT_DATA, never a guess |\n| Missing Nadi classification | MISSING_NADI | Stops |\n| Missing profile, or Profile E without a named source | MISSING_SOURCE_PROFILE | Stops |\n| Unverified exception | (policy) | Reported, never applied without acknowledgement |\n| Missing Navamsha | (check) | Navamsha rule cannot be evaluated |\n\n## Common Mistakes\nTwenty-four mistakes to recognise.\n| # | Mistake | Correct understanding |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Confusing Nadi Koota with an Ayurvedic Nadi examination | Different things: one is a Nakshatra classification, the other a clinical pulse reading |\n| 2 | Treating Nadi as a genetic test | It is not; it cannot measure DNA compatibility |\n| 3 | Treating Nadi Dosha as a diagnosis of infertility | It is not; medical questions go to healthcare professionals |\n| 4 | Assuming every same-Nadi pair is automatically unacceptable | It is a flagged classification; the full horoscope matters and exceptions are studied separately |\n| 5 | Ignoring the 27-Nakshatra classification | The Nadi comes from the table, not from a guess |\n| 6 | Confusing Nadi with Gana | Gana has three types (Deva, Manushya, Rakshasa) and 6 points |\n| 7 | Confusing Nadi with Yoni | Yoni is an animal symbol and 4 points |\n| 8 | Confusing Nadi with Bhakoot | Bhakoot is Rashi-based, Nadi Nakshatra-based |\n| 9 | Treating the same Rashi as the same Nadi | Ashwini and Bharani share Aries but not Nadi |\n| 10 | Treating different Rashis as different Nadis | Ashwini (Aries) and Jyeshtha (Scorpio) share Adi |\n| 11 | Assuming different Nakshatras always cancel | Only a documented rule with its condition can |\n| 12 | Assuming different Pada always cancels | The scope is unverified and disputed |\n| 13 | Applying a Nakshatra exemption to every Nakshatra | Only to the same listed Nakshatra of both persons, and the list is unverified |\n| 14 | Confusing the Rashi lord with the Nakshatra lord | They are different tables |\n| 15 | Confusing the Navamsha lord with the Rashi lord | The Navamsha sign differs from the Rashi |\n| 16 | Treating one-sided friendship as mutual | Check both directions |\n| 17 | Treating a general favourable factor as a formal cancellation | Role C or D, never automatic |\n| 18 | Automatically restoring eight points | Restoration needs a profile and policy and is labelled adjusted |\n| 19 | Assuming a high Guna total cancels the Dosha | It does not |\n| 20 | Ignoring regional variation | Name the source; do not generalise |\n| 21 | Using a wrong Nakshatra-to-Rashi boundary | Use the Pada arithmetic; there are nine splits |\n| 22 | Not verifying the Pada | Without the Pada, split Nakshatras give INSUFFICIENT_DATA |\n| 23 | Treating one modern author as universal | Compare sources and label evidence |\n| 24 | Making medical or reproductive predictions from Nadi | Never |\n\n## Instructor Guide\n*For teachers and self-study students.*\n\n### Suggested time allocation (120 minutes)\n| Section | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Introduction and meaning of Nadi | 10 |\n| Historical and symbolic foundation | 15 |\n| Three Nadi classifications | 20 |\n| Complete 27-Nakshatra table | 15 |\n| Nadi scoring and Dosha | 10 |\n| Exceptions and cancellation research | 25 |\n| Practical case studies | 15 |\n| Recap and questions | 10 |\n\n### Teaching objectives\nStudents classify any Nakshatra, score a pair, identify a Nadi Dosha, state each exception with its evidence label, keep raw and adjusted scores apart, and never present Nadi as a medical finding.\n\n### Whiteboard demonstrations\n1. Draw the zodiac and write Adi, Madhya, Antya, Antya, Madhya, Adi under the first six Nakshatras; extend it round the circle.\n2. Draw the 3 × 3 matrix and shade the diagonal.\n3. Mark Aries with Ashwini, Bharani and a small piece of Krittika to show three Nadis in one Rashi.\n4. Draw the Krittika boundary at 30° to show Pada 1 in Aries and Pada 2 in Taurus.\n\n### Practical examples\nCases 4, 6, 7, 9, 12 and 14 on the board; then the others in the Cases tab.\n\n### Common student misconceptions\nSee the Common Mistakes tab. The four most frequent: same Rashi means same Nadi; Nadi is a health test; \"cancelled\" means eight points; a friendly planet is mutually friendly.\n\n### Student participation activities\nPair work: one student states a pair, the other gives the Nadi, the raw score and the status under Profile A and Profile F. Debate: \"What would make an exception verified?\"\n\n### Homework\nThe written task in the Practice tab and exercises 6 to 8, 10 and 13.\n\n### Assessment criteria\nCorrect Nadi and score; correct separation of raw, status and adjusted; evidence label named; no deterministic or medical claim; plain, non-fear language.\n\n### Special instructor note\n> Never teach Nadi Dosha as a medical or genetic diagnosis. Teach it as a traditional astrological compatibility classification, with its own mathematical rules and school-specific exceptions.\n\n## Report\n### The final Nadi Koota report\nThe report lists Person A and Person B (Nakshatra, Pada, Moon Rashi, Nadi), the raw score out of eight, the Dosha status, the same-Rashi, different-Nakshatra, same-Nakshatra, different-Rashi and different-Pada checks, the Rashi-lord and Navamsha-lord relationships, the specific-Nakshatra exemption check, the selected profile, the applicable cancellation, the evidence quality, the adjusted score where permitted, the interpretation, the source references and the disclaimer. Generate, print, copy or download it below.\n{{n9report}}\n\n### Mandatory statement\n> *Nadi Koota is a traditional component of Vedic horoscope compatibility analysis. Nadi Dosha is an astrological classification based on Janma Nakshatra and is not a medical or genetic diagnosis. Its interpretation depends on the selected astrological tradition, documented exception rules and the complete horoscope. It should not be used as the sole basis for a marriage decision or for predicting fertility, inherited conditions or health outcomes.*\n\n## Validation\n### Technical validation report\nAutomatic tests of the Nadi engine, including agreement with the shared rule file and the Lab engine for all 729 pairs and all 108 Padas. Every row runs when this page opens.\n{{n9tests}}\n","10":"# Day 10 – Varna, Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Rajju and Vedha\n\n## Learn\n\n### Welcome to Day 10\n**Module 10: the additional Kootas (अतिरिक्त कूट) and the comparison of ten-factor traditions.** Day 10 of 14 · Foundation to Advanced · 210–240 minutes (self-paced) · Instructor: Anoopam Shukla.\n\nToday finishes the Koota toolbox. You meet **Varna (वर्ण)**, the one-point Ashtakoota factor you have not yet studied, and three factors that belong to the wider Dashakoota (दशकूट) world: **Mahendra (महेन्द्र)**, **Stree Deergha (स्त्री दीर्घ)** and **Rajju (रज्जु)**, with its companion **Vedha (वेध)**. The most important lesson is not a table. It is that **\"Dashakoota\" is not one fixed list of ten**: each tradition chooses its own factors, its own calculation and its own way of scoring, and a rule from one tradition must never be silently borrowed by another.\n\n> **Central teaching principle.** First name the tradition. Then calculate. Then classify. Then interpret. Never add unlike factors into one invented total.\n\n### The one idea to keep in mind all day\nFour things must stay separate:\n1. **The tradition:** which ten (or eight) factors are in use, and who says so.\n2. **The calculation:** a count, a lookup or a comparison. This is arithmetic and never changes with the profile.\n3. **The profile:** a software setting such as a threshold or a direction. Profiles are configurations for transparent comparison, **not claims about a classical school**.\n4. **The interpretation:** what the tradition symbolises. It is never a medical, fertility, longevity or marriage prediction.\n\n### What you will be able to do by the end of the day\n1. Explain the difference between Ashtakoota and Dashakoota, and say why the ten factors are not universal.\n2. Name the ten factors of Tradition A and of the South Indian ten-Porutham comparison profiles.\n3. Explain the four Varna groups and calculate Varna for any two Moon signs in the correct direction.\n4. State clearly that Varna is a symbolic label of Moon signs and never a judgement of people.\n5. Count Mahendra inclusively and name the eight favourable positions.\n6. Explain why Mahendra's qualification is the same in both directions although the two counts differ.\n7. Count Stree Deergha from bride to groom and apply a named threshold profile.\n8. Recognise the boundary cases (count 13 and count 9) and the Revati-to-Ashwini wrap.\n9. Name the five Rajju groups and their body symbols, and read the 27-Nakshatra Rajju table.\n10. Identify Rajju Dosha as a flag, and Vedha pairs as a second flag.\n11. Explain why exceptions are never transferred from one Koota to another.\n12. Compare the same two horoscopes under several traditions without mixing their rules.\n13. Use the calculators, the four matrices and the report generator.\n14. Complete 20 exercises and the 40-question final assessment.\n\n### Your learning route (ten stages)\nIntroduction → Traditions → Varna → Mahendra → Stree Deergha → Rajju and Vedha → Comparison → Matrices → Cases and practice → Assessment. Tick each stage when you finish it.\n{{d10dash}}\n\n### Prerequisites\nYou should know the 27 Nakshatras in order, how to find the Moon sign, Nakshatra and Pada from a sidereal longitude (Day 2), inclusive counting (Day 4), and the idea of a rule profile (Days 3 and 4).\n\n### Suggested pace (self-paced)\n| Block | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Traditions and the Ashtakoota/Dashakoota comparison | 30 |\n| Varna | 30 |\n| Mahendra | 35 |\n| Stree Deergha | 35 |\n| Rajju and Vedha | 45 |\n| Comparison, cases and matrices | 35 |\n| Exercises and assessment | 30 |\n| Total | 240 |\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### Lesson 1 – Ashtakoota and Dashakoota\n**Ashtakoota (अष्टकूट)** is the eight-factor framework scored out of 36: Varna 1, Vashya 2, Tara 3, Yoni 4, Graha Maitri 5, Gana 6, Bhakoot 7, Nadi 8. **Dashakoota** means \"ten factors\", but the ten are chosen differently in different traditions. In this course:\n- **Tradition A** = the eight Ashtakoota factors (36 points) + **Rajju** + **Vedha** as pass/Dosha checks with no points. This is the frozen reference of the course.\n- **South Indian ten-Porutham profiles** (comparison only) = Dina, Gana, Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Yoni, Rasi, Rasi Adhipati, Vasya, Rajju, Vedha, as commonly listed by contemporary pages. They are judged factor by factor as matching or not matching, not summed to 36.\n\nA factor name can look the same in two traditions and still be a different rule. A score from one tradition cannot be moved into another.\n\n### Lesson 2 – Varna (वर्ण)\n*Varna* means \"class\" or \"colour\". In Koota Milan it is a symbolic grouping of **Moon signs** (Rashi) into four classes, used for a one-point check in Tradition A. The four traditional names are **Brahmin (ब्राह्मण), Kshatriya (क्षत्रिय), Vaishya (वैश्य) and Shudra (शूद्र)**, listed here from the highest to the lowest rank used by the comparison rule.\n\n> **Responsible teaching.** Varna Koota is a traditional symbolic matching label attached to Moon signs. It is not a judgement of caste, community, social status, occupation, intelligence, education or human worth. The software never infers any of these, and a report must never use Varna to label a person.\n\n### Lesson 3 – Mahendra (महेन्द्र)\n*Mahendra* is a Nakshatra-count factor. You count from one Nakshatra to the other (inclusively) and look at the number. Counts 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22 and 25 are the commonly cited **favourable positions**. Contemporary pages link it with growth, support, progeny or prosperity symbolism. That is **traditional symbolism only**: Mahendra cannot establish fertility, the birth of children or prosperity.\n\n### Lesson 4 – Stree Deergha (स्त्री दीर्घ)\n*Stree* means \"woman\" and *Deergha* means \"long\". It is a historical, gender-based factor in some regional systems: you count from the **bride's** Nakshatra to the **groom's** and check the count against a **threshold**. The threshold differs between sources (for example \"more than 13\", \"more than 9\", \"9 or more\"), so the lesson always names the profile. Its traditional symbolism concerns the bride's well-being and continuity. It must never be turned into a statement about a woman's health, longevity or marital destiny.\n\n### Lesson 5 – Rajju (रज्जु) and Vedha (वेध)\n*Rajju* means \"rope\" or \"bond\". The 27 Nakshatras are placed in **five groups named after body regions**: Pada (feet), Kati (waist), Nabhi (navel), Kantha (neck) and Siro (head). In Tradition A, **two people in the same group raise a Rajju Dosha flag**; different groups pass. *Vedha* means \"piercing\" or \"obstruction\": 14 listed pairs of Nakshatras are marked as mutually obstructing. Both are pass/Dosha checks. Neither adds points to the 36.\n\nThe body symbols are a traditional way of organising the list. They are **not** anatomy and not a medical or physical prediction.\n\n### Lesson 6 – Why the factors stay separate\nVarna uses the Moon sign. Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Rajju and Vedha use the Moon Nakshatra. Varna and Rajju/Vedha come from lookup tables; Mahendra and Stree Deergha come from counting. Varna and Stree Deergha depend on who is bride and who is groom; Rajju's base method does not. Because their natures differ, adding them together would need a source-defined weighting. **None is verified in this course, so the software creates no combined total.**\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### 10.1 Varna by Moon sign (Tradition A table)\n| Rashi | Element | Varna |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aries (मेष) | Fire | Kshatriya |\n| Taurus (वृषभ) | Earth | Vaishya |\n| Gemini (मिथुन) | Air | Shudra |\n| Cancer (कर्क) | Water | Brahmin |\n| Leo (सिंह) | Fire | Kshatriya |\n| Virgo (कन्या) | Earth | Vaishya |\n| Libra (तुला) | Air | Shudra |\n| Scorpio (वृश्चिक) | Water | Brahmin |\n| Sagittarius (धनु) | Fire | Kshatriya |\n| Capricorn (मकर) | Earth | Vaishya |\n| Aquarius (कुंभ) | Air | Shudra |\n| Pisces (मीन) | Water | Brahmin |\nThe rule is by element: Fire = Kshatriya, Earth = Vaishya, Air = Shudra, Water = Brahmin (grade C; no verse verified). Each Varna has exactly three signs. Other sources may classify differently; if one is adopted it gets its own table, never a merged one.\n\n### 10.2 Varna comparison and scoring\nRank used for the comparison: Brahmin 4, Kshatriya 3, Vaishya 2, Shudra 1. **Profile A (Tradition A):** score **1** if the groom's Varna is equal to or higher than the bride's, otherwise **0**. Maximum 1. The direction matters: bride Taurus with groom Aries scores 1, bride Aries with groom Taurus scores 0. The 4 × 4 matrix has 10 cells of 1 and 6 cells of 0.\n\n### 10.3 Mahendra counting and the favourable list\nUse zero-based Nakshatra numbers (Ashwini 0 … Revati 26). From A to B: **count = ((B − A + 27) MOD 27) + 1**. Adding 27 keeps the subtraction positive, MOD 27 wraps past Revati, and +1 makes the count inclusive, so the answer runs from 1 (same Nakshatra) to 27.\n| Count | Status (Profile M1) | Example from Ashwini (bride) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4 | Favourable position | Rohini |\n| 7 | Favourable position | Punarvasu |\n| 10 | Favourable position | Magha |\n| 13 | Favourable position | Hasta |\n| 16 | Favourable position | Vishakha |\n| 19 | Favourable position | Mula |\n| 22 | Favourable position | Shravana |\n| 25 | Favourable position | Purva Bhadrapada |\nAll other counts are simply **not qualifying**. They are not a Dosha, and no negative meaning is invented for them. Source status: grade D (contemporary pages agree on the list; no primary verse verified).\n\n**A discovery worth remembering.** The favourable counts are 4, 7, … 25, that is, every count that leaves remainder 1 when divided by 3, except 1 itself. When you count in the reverse direction you get 29 − c, and 29 − c also leaves remainder 1. So the list is **closed under reversal**: for example 4 reverses to 25, 7 to 22, 10 to 19, 13 to 16. The two counts differ, but a pair qualifies in one direction exactly when it qualifies in the other. Mahendra's qualification is therefore the same in both directions even though its counting method is directional. Each bride Nakshatra has exactly 8 qualifying grooms, so 216 of the 729 cells qualify.\n\n### 10.4 Stree Deergha counting and thresholds\n**count = ((Groom − Bride + 27) MOD 27) + 1**, counting from the bride's Nakshatra (counted as 1) to the groom's (included). For two different Nakshatras the bride-to-groom count and the groom-to-bride count always add up to 29, so the method is **directional** and its matrix is not symmetric.\n| Profile | Direction | Qualifies when | Source note (grade) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SD1 | bride → groom | count more than 13 | one contemporary page, vedicbirth.com (D) |\n| SD2 | bride → groom | count 13 or more | documented variant (D) |\n| SD3 | bride → groom | count more than 9 | one contemporary page, rahasyavedicastrology.com (D) |\n| SD4 | bride → groom | count 9 or more | one contemporary page, astrosight.ai (D); the same page calls 13 or more a stronger band |\n| SD6 | bride → groom | count 7 or more | variant used in earlier course material (E) |\n| SD5 | groom → bride | count more than 13 | research only, not attributed (E) |\n**Count 13 is a boundary case**: it qualifies under SD2, SD3 and SD4 but not under SD1. **Count 9 is a boundary case between SD3 and SD4.** A report must name the profile. The method is not replaced by a symmetric modern formula.\n\n### 10.5 The Rajju table (Tradition A)\n| No. | Nakshatra | Rajju | Body symbol |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini | Pada | Feet |\n| 2 | Bharani | Kati | Waist |\n| 3 | Krittika | Nabhi | Navel |\n| 4 | Rohini | Kantha | Neck |\n| 5 | Mrigashira | Siro | Head |\n| 6 | Ardra | Kantha | Neck |\n| 7 | Punarvasu | Nabhi | Navel |\n| 8 | Pushya | Kati | Waist |\n| 9 | Ashlesha | Pada | Feet |\n| 10 | Magha | Pada | Feet |\n| 11 | Purva Phalguni | Kati | Waist |\n| 12 | Uttara Phalguni | Nabhi | Navel |\n| 13 | Hasta | Kantha | Neck |\n| 14 | Chitra | Siro | Head |\n| 15 | Swati | Kantha | Neck |\n| 16 | Vishakha | Nabhi | Navel |\n| 17 | Anuradha | Kati | Waist |\n| 18 | Jyeshtha | Pada | Feet |\n| 19 | Mula | Pada | Feet |\n| 20 | Purva Ashadha | Kati | Waist |\n| 21 | Uttara Ashadha | Nabhi | Navel |\n| 22 | Shravana | Kantha | Neck |\n| 23 | Dhanishta | Siro | Head |\n| 24 | Shatabhisha | Kantha | Neck |\n| 25 | Purva Bhadrapada | Nabhi | Navel |\n| 26 | Uttara Bhadrapada | Kati | Waist |\n| 27 | Revati | Pada | Feet |\nGroup sizes: Pada 6, Kati 6, Nabhi 6, Kantha 6, Siro 3 (total 27). The table is stored as a **classification table** and checked against an independent statement in the engine tests. It is not generated by a formula. (After the fact one can see that it follows a nine-step zigzag Pada, Kati, Nabhi, Kantha, Siro, Kantha, Nabhi, Kati, Pada repeated three times. That is an observation that helps memory. It is not the source of the table.)\n\n### 10.6 The Rajju and Vedha rules\n**Same Rajju group = Rajju Dosha flag** (no points). Different groups = pass. The base method is symmetric: swapping the two people changes nothing. **Vedha:** if the two Nakshatras form one of the 14 listed pairs, a Vedha Dosha flag is raised (no points).\n| Vedha pair | Vedha pair |\n|---|---|\n| Ashwini – Jyeshtha | Ashlesha – Mula |\n| Bharani – Anuradha | Magha – Revati |\n| Krittika – Vishakha | Purva Phalguni – Uttara Bhadrapada |\n| Rohini – Swati | Uttara Phalguni – Purva Bhadrapada |\n| Ardra – Shravana | Hasta – Shatabhisha |\n| Punarvasu – Uttara Ashadha | Chitra – Dhanishta |\n| Pushya – Purva Ashadha | Mrigashira – Dhanishta |\nTwo findings from the engine: (1) **every listed Vedha pair is also a same-Rajju pair**, so in this data Vedha never flags a pair that Rajju did not already flag; (2) **no two different Nakshatras that share a Moon sign share a Rajju group**. Some manuals group Mrigashira, Chitra and Dhanishta differently (grade C).\n\n### 10.7 Procedure for the day\n1. Name the tradition and the profiles. 2. Varna from the Moon signs (bride row, groom column). 3. Mahendra and Stree Deergha counts from the Nakshatras. 4. Rajju group and Vedha pair check. 5. Record each result separately, with raw status and evidence grade. 6. Create no total except Tradition A's own 36 from the Calculation Lab.\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### Profiles at a glance\n| Koota | Profiles in the software | What changes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Varna | A, R, S | A: groom ≥ bride scores 1. R: bride ≥ groom (reversed variant). S: 1 only when equal (symmetric research profile) |\n| Mahendra | M1, M2, M3, M4 | M1 bride → groom. M2 groom → bride. M3 either direction. M4 exclusive count (a wrong method shown only to demonstrate the error) |\n| Stree Deergha | SD1 to SD6 | The direction and the threshold |\n| Rajju | A, R | A: same group = Dosha. R: same rule plus a list of candidate exceptions shown for study |\nProfiles are software configurations. They do not claim to reproduce a named classical school.\n\n### The ten-factor traditions\n| No. | Tradition A (this course) | South Indian (a) and (b) comparison profiles |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Varna (1) | Dina |\n| 2 | Vashya (2) | Gana |\n| 3 | Tara (3) | Mahendra |\n| 4 | Yoni (4) | Stree Deergha |\n| 5 | Graha Maitri (5) | Yoni |\n| 6 | Gana (6) | Rasi |\n| 7 | Bhakoot (7) | Rasi Adhipati |\n| 8 | Nadi (8) | Vasya |\n| 9 | Rajju (pass/Dosha) | Rajju |\n| 10 | Vedha (pass/Dosha) | Vedha |\nProfile (a) uses SD1; profile (b) uses SD4. Telugu, Kannada and Kerala practices exist, but no source that lists their ten factors is held here, so none is stated.\n\n### Selecting the course reference\nThe course keeps **Tradition A** as its reference for scoring because its tables are available for comparison with independent statements, its rules are consistent, it can be implemented transparently, and it suits teaching. This is a course configuration chosen by the course owner (eight Ashtakoota factors plus Rajju and Vedha), not a claim that the combination is universal or textual. The South Indian profiles are the comparison tradition for Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Rajju and Vedha.\n\n### Direction and gender\nVarna and Stree Deergha depend on who is bride and who is groom. Mahendra's qualification does not (see 10.3). Rajju's base method does not. Some manuals attach interpretations to a particular person's group (for example a group said to concern the husband and another the wife). These are interpretative statements, not directional scoring, and they are not universal (grade E).\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### Rule: exceptions are never transferred\nA Nadi exception is not a Rajju exception. A Bhakoot exception is not a Varna exception. A qualifying Stree Deergha does not cancel Rajju Dosha, and a favourable Mahendra does not cancel any Dosha. Every candidate exception below belongs to **one** Koota, carries an evidence level and is **not applied** by the software.\n\n### Varna\nNo standard cancellation is documented here. None is invented.\n\n### Mahendra and Stree Deergha\nA non-qualifying count is not a Dosha, so there is nothing to cancel. Boundary counts are decided by the profile, not by an exception.\n\n### Rajju and Vedha: candidate conditions (study only)\n| Candidate condition | Evidence | Status in the software |\n|---|---|---|\n| Same Rashi, different Nakshatras | E, unverified | Shown, never applied. In this table no such pair shares a Rajju group, so the condition cannot arise |\n| Same Nakshatra, different Pada | E, circulated for Nadi; not verified for Rajju | Shown, never applied |\n| Split Nakshatra, different Rashi | E, as above | Shown, never applied |\n| Same Moon-sign lord | E | Shown, never applied |\n| Mutual natural friendship of the two Moon-sign lords | E | Shown, never applied |\n| Natural friendship of the two Nakshatra lords | E (Rahu and Ketu lie outside the seven-planet table, so the check is undefined for them) | Shown, never applied |\n| Some traditions treat Rajju Dosha as not cancellable | E | Recorded as a statement of tradition |\nThe report always states: raw status, adjusted status (identical to raw in every Day 10 profile), exception applied (none), and evidence level.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example set 1 – Varna (ten pairs, profile A, with R and S for comparison)\n| # | Bride | Groom | Relationship | A | R | S |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Aries (Kshatriya) | Aries (Kshatriya) | equal | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n| 2 | Taurus (Vaishya) | Aries (Kshatriya) | groom higher | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| 3 | Cancer (Brahmin) | Aries (Kshatriya) | groom lower | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n| 4 | Gemini (Shudra) | Cancer (Brahmin) | groom higher | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| 5 | Cancer (Brahmin) | Gemini (Shudra) | groom lower | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n| 6 | Leo (Kshatriya) | Sagittarius (Kshatriya) | equal | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n| 7 | Virgo (Vaishya) | Capricorn (Vaishya) | equal | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n| 8 | Libra (Shudra) | Aquarius (Shudra) | equal | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n| 9 | Scorpio (Brahmin) | Pisces (Brahmin) | equal | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n| 10 | Capricorn (Vaishya) | Pisces (Brahmin) | groom higher | 1 | 0 | 0 |\nRows 2 and 3, and rows 4 and 5, show the direction effect.\n\n### Example set 2 – Mahendra (profile M1, every Nakshatra counted)\n| # | Bride → groom | Count | Reverse | Result | Counting sequence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini → Rohini | 4 | 25 | qualifies | 1 Ashwini, 2 Bharani, 3 Krittika, 4 Rohini |\n| 2 | Bharani → Pushya | 7 | 22 | qualifies | 1 Bharani, 2 Krittika, 3 Rohini, 4 Mrigashira, 5 Ardra, 6 Punarvasu, 7 Pushya |\n| 3 | Krittika → Uttara Phalguni | 10 | 19 | qualifies | 1 Krittika, 2 Rohini, 3 Mrigashira, 4 Ardra, 5 Punarvasu, 6 Pushya, 7 Ashlesha, 8 Magha, 9 Purva Phalguni, 10 Uttara Phalguni |\n| 4 | Mrigashira → Anuradha | 13 | 16 | qualifies | 1 Mrigashira, 2 Ardra, 3 Punarvasu, 4 Pushya, 5 Ashlesha, 6 Magha, 7 Purva Phalguni, 8 Uttara Phalguni, 9 Hasta, 10 Chitra, 11 Swati, 12 Vishakha, 13 Anuradha |\n| 5 | Punarvasu → Shravana | 16 | 13 | qualifies | 1 Punarvasu, 2 Pushya, 3 Ashlesha, 4 Magha, 5 Purva Phalguni, 6 Uttara Phalguni, 7 Hasta, 8 Chitra, 9 Swati, 10 Vishakha, 11 Anuradha, 12 Jyeshtha, 13 Mula, 14 Purva Ashadha, 15 Uttara Ashadha, 16 Shravana |\n| 6 | Hasta → Rohini | 19 | 10 | qualifies | 1 Hasta, 2 Chitra, 3 Swati, 4 Vishakha, 5 Anuradha, 6 Jyeshtha, 7 Mula, 8 Purva Ashadha, 9 Uttara Ashadha, 10 Shravana, 11 Dhanishta, 12 Shatabhisha, 13 Purva Bhadrapada, 14 Uttara Bhadrapada, 15 Revati, 16 Ashwini, 17 Bharani, 18 Krittika, 19 Rohini |\n| 7 | Anuradha → Purva Phalguni | 22 | 7 | qualifies | 1 Anuradha … 11 Revati, 12 Ashwini, … 21 Magha, 22 Purva Phalguni (wraps after Revati) |\n| 8 | Shravana → Mula | 25 | 4 | qualifies | 1 Shravana … 6 Revati, 7 Ashwini, … 24 Jyeshtha, 25 Mula (wraps after Revati) |\n| 9 | Ashwini → Mrigashira | 5 | 24 | not qualifying | 1 Ashwini, 2 Bharani, 3 Krittika, 4 Rohini, 5 Mrigashira |\n| 10 | Rohini → Hasta | 10 | 19 | qualifies | 1 Rohini, 2 Mrigashira, 3 Ardra, 4 Punarvasu, 5 Pushya, 6 Ashlesha, 7 Magha, 8 Purva Phalguni, 9 Uttara Phalguni, 10 Hasta |\n| 11 | Ashwini → Ashwini | 1 | 1 | not qualifying | 1 Ashwini |\nExample 6 versus 10 shows the reverse effect: Hasta → Rohini is 19 and Rohini → Hasta is 10. The counts differ, the verdict does not.\n\n### Example set 3 – Stree Deergha (bride → groom, SD1 / SD2 / SD4)\n| # | Pair | Nadi A/B | Count (reverse) | SD1 | SD2 | SD4 | Counting sequence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini → Punarvasu | Adi/Adi | 7 (22) | no | no | no | 1 Ashwini, 2 Bharani, 3 Krittika, 4 Rohini, 5 Mrigashira, 6 Ardra, 7 Punarvasu |\n| 2 | Chitra → Uttara Bhadrapada | Madhya/Madhya | 13 (16) | no | yes | yes | 1 Chitra, 2 Swati, 3 Vishakha, 4 Anuradha, 5 Jyeshtha, 6 Mula, 7 Purva Ashadha, 8 Uttara Ashadha, 9 Shravana, 10 Dhanishta, 11 Shatabhisha, 12 Purva Bhadrapada, 13 Uttara Bhadrapada |\n| 3 | Bharani → Uttara Ashadha | Madhya/Antya | 20 (9) | yes | yes | yes | 1 Bharani, 2 Krittika, 3 Rohini … 19 Purva Ashadha, 20 Uttara Ashadha |\n| 4 | Jyeshtha → Pushya | Adi/Madhya | 18 (11) | yes | yes | yes | 1 Jyeshtha, 2 Mula, 3 Purva Ashadha … 17 Punarvasu, 18 Pushya (wraps after Revati) |\n| 5 | Rohini → Hasta (bride earlier in the zodiac) | Antya/Adi | 10 (19) | no | no | yes | 1 Rohini … 10 Hasta |\n| 6 | Hasta → Rohini (groom earlier in the zodiac) | Adi/Antya | 19 (10) | yes | yes | yes | 1 Hasta, 2 Chitra, 3 Swati … 18 Krittika, 19 Rohini |\n| 7 | Ashwini → Revati | Adi/Antya | 27 (2) | yes | yes | yes | 1 Ashwini … 26 Uttara Bhadrapada, 27 Revati |\n| 8 | Revati → Ashwini | Antya/Adi | 2 (27) | no | no | no | 1 Revati, 2 Ashwini |\n| 9 | Ardra → Mula (same Nadi group) | Adi/Adi | 14 (15) | yes | yes | yes | 1 Ardra, 2 Punarvasu … 13 Jyeshtha, 14 Mula |\n| 10 | Jyeshtha P1 (Scorpio) → Pushya P3 (Cancer) | Adi/Madhya | 18 (11) | yes | yes | yes | as example 4 |\nExamples 5 and 6 are the same two people in opposite roles and give opposite verdicts under SD1: the method is directional. Example 10 qualifies under Stree Deergha while the Moon signs Scorpio and Cancer form a 5/9 pair, which the Calculation Lab scores 0 for Bhakoot: a qualifying factor does not rescue another Koota's adverse result.\n\n### Example set 4 – Rajju and Vedha (fifteen cases, profile R candidate list shown for study)\n| # | Person A (bride) | Person B (groom) | Rajju A / B | Rajju result | Vedha | Nadi A/B | Candidate conditions met (none applied) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini P1, Aries | Magha P2, Leo | Pada / Pada | Dosha flag | — | Adi/Antya | mutual Rashi-lord friendship |\n| 2 | Bharani P1, Aries | Pushya P2, Cancer | Kati / Kati | Dosha flag | — | Madhya/Madhya | Nakshatra-lord friendship |\n| 3 | Krittika P2, Taurus | Vishakha P1, Libra | Nabhi / Nabhi | Dosha flag | Vedha | Antya/Antya | same Rashi lord; Nakshatra-lord friendship |\n| 4 | Rohini P1, Taurus | Ardra P2, Gemini | Kantha / Kantha | Dosha flag | — | Antya/Adi | mutual Rashi-lord friendship |\n| 5 | Mrigashira P1, Taurus | Chitra P2, Virgo | Siro / Siro | Dosha flag | — | Madhya/Madhya | mutual Rashi-lord friendship |\n| 6 | Ashwini P1, Aries | Bharani P1, Aries | Pada / Kati | pass | — | Adi/Madhya | (same Rashi, different Nakshatra: no Dosha to relax) |\n| 7 | Ashlesha P2, Cancer | Mula P3, Sagittarius | Pada / Pada | Dosha flag | Vedha | Antya/Adi | none |\n| 8 | Ashwini P1, Aries | Magha P1, Leo | Pada / Pada | Dosha flag | — | Adi/Antya | mutual Rashi-lord friendship |\n| 9 | Krittika P1, Aries | Krittika P2, Taurus | Nabhi / Nabhi | Dosha flag (split Nakshatra) | — | Antya/Antya | same Nakshatra, different Pada; split Nakshatra, different Rashi |\n| 10 | Rohini P1, Taurus | Rohini P3, Taurus | Kantha / Kantha | Dosha flag (same Nakshatra) | — | Antya/Antya | same Nakshatra, different Pada; same Rashi lord |\n| 11 | Krittika P1, Aries | Krittika P3, Taurus | Nabhi / Nabhi | Dosha flag | — | Antya/Antya | same Nakshatra, different Pada; split Nakshatra |\n| 12 | Hasta P1, Virgo | Rohini P2, Taurus | Kantha / Kantha | Dosha flag; Nadi differs (Adi/Antya) so Nadi is favourable | — | Adi/Antya | mutual Rashi-lord friendship |\n| 13 | Ashwini P1, Aries | Ardra P1, Gemini | Pada / Kantha | pass; yet Nadi is the same (Adi/Adi), so Nadi Dosha is flagged elsewhere | — | Adi/Adi | none |\n| 14 | Ashwini P1, Aries | Magha P1, Leo | Pada / Pada | Tradition A flags Rajju; the plain eight-factor Ashtakoota profile in the Lab has no Rajju row, so it flags nothing | — | Adi/Antya | as case 8 |\n| 15 | Mrigashira P3, Gemini | Chitra P2, Virgo | Siro / Siro | Dosha flag; Mrigashira–Chitra is not on the common Vedha list, but some manuals group these three Nakshatras differently: **manual source-based review** | — | Madhya/Madhya | same Rashi lord |\nCase 14 shows that the same horoscope pair gives different Rajju outcomes under different traditions. Case 15 is a worked example of a point where the software cannot decide and a reviewer must consult a named source.\n\n## Practice\n\n### Exercise set 1 – Varna (bride, groom; Profile A)\n| # | Bride | Groom | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Aries | Cancer | |\n| 2 | Cancer | Aries | |\n| 3 | Taurus | Aries | |\n| 4 | Gemini | Libra | |\n| 5 | Scorpio | Capricorn | |\n\n### Exercise set 2 – Mahendra (bride → groom; count and verdict under M1)\n| # | Bride | Groom | Count | Qualifies? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini | Rohini | | |\n| 2 | Bharani | Pushya | | |\n| 3 | Ashwini | Mrigashira | | |\n| 4 | Hasta | Rohini | | |\n| 5 | Ashwini | Ashwini | | |\n\n### Exercise set 3 – Stree Deergha (bride → groom; verdict under SD1)\n| # | Bride | Groom | Count | Qualifies under SD1? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini | Punarvasu | | |\n| 2 | Chitra | Uttara Bhadrapada | | |\n| 3 | Bharani | Uttara Ashadha | | |\n| 4 | Revati | Ashwini | | |\n| 5 | Hasta | Rohini | | |\n\n### Exercise set 4 – Rajju and Vedha (bride, groom)\n| # | Bride | Groom | Rajju | Vedha |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini | Magha | | |\n| 2 | Bharani | Pushya | | |\n| 3 | Rohini | Ardra | | |\n| 4 | Mrigashira | Chitra | | |\n| 5 | Ashwini | Rohini | | |\n| 6 | Krittika | Vishakha | | |\n| 7 | Pushya | Purva Ashadha | | |\n| 8 | Hasta | Shatabhisha | | |\n\n### Exercise set 5 – responsible teaching\nWrite three sentences a practitioner could give a client who has read that \"Stree Deergha predicts the bride's lifespan\". Your note must say what the count is, what it is not, and which profile was used.\n\n### Answers\nSet 1: 1: 1 · 2: 0 · 3: 1 · 4: 1 · 5: 0.\nSet 2: 1: count 4, yes · 2: count 7, yes · 3: count 5, no · 4: count 19, yes · 5: count 1, no.\nSet 3: 1: 7, no · 2: 13, no (a boundary case: it qualifies under SD2) · 3: 20, yes · 4: 2, no · 5: 19, yes.\nSet 4: 1: Dosha (both Pada), no Vedha · 2: Dosha (both Kati), no Vedha · 3: Dosha (both Kantha), no Vedha · 4: Dosha (both Siro), no Vedha · 5: pass (Pada, Kantha), no Vedha · 6: Dosha (both Nabhi), Vedha yes · 7: Dosha (both Kati), Vedha yes · 8: Dosha (both Kantha), Vedha yes.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What is the difference between Ashtakoota (अष्टकूट) and Dashakoota (दशकूट)?\nA: Ashtakoota is the eight-factor framework scored out of 36. Dashakoota means ten factors, but the ten differ by tradition.\n\nQ: Are the ten Dashakoota factors the same in every tradition?\nA: No. Composition, calculation, scoring and interpretation differ by region and source, so the tradition must always be named.\n\nQ: What are the ten factors of Tradition A?\nA: The eight Ashtakoota Kootas (Varna 1, Vashya 2, Tara 3, Yoni 4, Graha Maitri 5, Gana 6, Bhakoot 7, Nadi 8 = 36) plus Rajju and Vedha as pass/Dosha checks with no points.\n\nQ: What are the ten Poruthams of the South Indian comparison profile?\nA: Dina, Gana, Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Yoni, Rasi, Rasi Adhipati, Vasya, Rajju, Vedha (as commonly listed; details unverified).\n\nQ: What does Varna (वर्ण) classify in Koota Milan?\nA: Moon signs, symbolically. Never people.\n\nQ: Which signs are Brahmin Varna in Tradition A?\nA: The water signs: Cancer, Scorpio, Pisces.\n\nQ: Which signs are Kshatriya, Vaishya and Shudra?\nA: Kshatriya: Aries, Leo, Sagittarius (fire). Vaishya: Taurus, Virgo, Capricorn (earth). Shudra: Gemini, Libra, Aquarius (air).\n\nQ: What is the Varna rule in Profile A?\nA: Score 1 if the groom's Varna is equal to or higher than the bride's, otherwise 0.\n\nQ: Why is Varna direction important?\nA: Bride Taurus with groom Aries scores 1, but bride Aries with groom Taurus scores 0.\n\nQ: What must the software never do with Varna?\nA: Infer caste, community, social status, occupation or human worth.\n\nQ: What is Mahendra (महेन्द्र) Koota?\nA: A Nakshatra-count factor: the inclusive count from one Nakshatra to the other should be one of the favourable positions.\n\nQ: Which counts are the favourable Mahendra positions?\nA: 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22 and 25.\n\nQ: What is the Mahendra counting formula?\nA: Count = ((B − A + 27) MOD 27) + 1 with zero-based Nakshatra numbers.\n\nQ: What is a non-qualifying Mahendra count?\nA: Any count outside the list. It means the favourable condition is not met. It is not a Dosha.\n\nQ: Why does Mahendra qualify in both directions?\nA: The reverse count is 29 minus the forward count, and the list is closed under that change (4 and 25, 7 and 22, 10 and 19, 13 and 16).\n\nQ: Does Mahendra carry points?\nA: No. No verified source defining points is held, so it is a qualitative factor.\n\nQ: How many grooms qualify for any one bride Nakshatra under Mahendra?\nA: Exactly eight (216 of the 729 cells).\n\nQ: What is Stree Deergha (स्त्री दीर्घ)?\nA: A historical, gender-based regional factor: the count from the bride's Nakshatra to the groom's is compared with a threshold.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatra is counted first in Stree Deergha?\nA: The bride's, counted as 1. The groom's Nakshatra is included.\n\nQ: What is the commonly cited Stree Deergha threshold?\nA: More than 13 (SD1). Other sources use more than 9 or 9 or more, and earlier course material used 7 or more.\n\nQ: Why is count 13 a boundary case?\nA: It does not qualify when the rule is \"more than 13\" but qualifies under \"13 or more\". The named profile decides.\n\nQ: What do the forward and reverse Stree Deergha counts add up to?\nA: 29 for two different Nakshatras (both are 1 for the same Nakshatra).\n\nQ: Is the Stree Deergha matrix symmetric?\nA: No. It is directional, so swapping bride and groom can change the result.\n\nQ: Does a non-qualifying Stree Deergha count mean a Dosha?\nA: No. It is a non-qualifying result, not a Dosha and not a prediction.\n\nQ: What does Rajju (रज्जु) mean?\nA: Rope or bond. The Nakshatras are grouped by body region.\n\nQ: What are the five Rajju groups?\nA: Pada (feet), Kati (waist), Nabhi (navel), Kantha (neck), Siro (head).\n\nQ: How many Nakshatras are in each Rajju group?\nA: Pada 6, Kati 6, Nabhi 6, Kantha 6, Siro 3.\n\nQ: Which Nakshatras form Siro Rajju?\nA: Mrigashira, Chitra and Dhanishta.\n\nQ: What is Rajju Dosha in Tradition A?\nA: A flag raised when both Nakshatras are in the same Rajju group. It adds no points.\n\nQ: Is the base Rajju method directional?\nA: No. It is symmetric.\n\nQ: Is the Rajju table calculated by a formula?\nA: No. It is stored as a classification table. It happens to follow a nine-step zigzag, but that is an observation, not the source.\n\nQ: What is Vedha (वेध)?\nA: Obstruction: 14 listed Nakshatra pairs are marked as mutually obstructing. It is a pass/Dosha flag with no points.\n\nQ: What is special about the 14 Vedha pairs?\nA: Every one is also a same-Rajju pair, so Vedha never flags a pair that Rajju did not already flag.\n\nQ: Can two different Nakshatras in the same Moon sign share a Rajju group?\nA: Not in this table: no such pair exists.\n\nQ: What is the status of Rajju exceptions?\nA: Candidates are shown with an evidence level (E, unverified). The software never applies them.\n\nQ: Can an exception from one Koota be used for another?\nA: No. Exceptions are never transferred between Kootas.\n\nQ: Does the software create a total across Varna, Mahendra, Stree Deergha and Rajju?\nA: No. Unlike factors need a source-defined weighting, and none is verified.\n\nQ: Where does Tradition A's 36-point total come from in the comparison?\nA: It is reproduced from the Calculation Lab for the eight scored Kootas. Rajju and Vedha add nothing.\n\nQ: How should Rajju's body symbols be understood?\nA: As a traditional way of organising the list. They are not anatomy and not a physical or medical prediction.\n\nQ: How should alarming traditional language about Rajju be treated?\nA: As historical context and traditional belief, not as established fact.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: Varna uses which input?\nA) Navamsha\nB) Moon Nakshatra\nC) Ascendant\nD) Moon sign\nANS: D\nWHY: Varna is read from the Moon sign.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: What is Varna's maximum in Tradition A?\nA) 1\nB) 2\nC) 3\nD) 4\nANS: A\nWHY: Varna carries 1 point.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which Varna do the fire signs have in Tradition A?\nA) Brahmin\nB) Kshatriya\nC) Vaishya\nD) Shudra\nANS: B\nWHY: Aries, Leo and Sagittarius are Kshatriya.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which signs are Brahmin Varna in Tradition A?\nA) Taurus, Virgo, Capricorn\nB) Aries, Leo, Sagittarius\nC) Cancer, Scorpio, Pisces\nD) Gemini, Libra, Aquarius\nANS: C\nWHY: The water signs are Brahmin.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Bride Leo, groom Libra: Varna score under Profile A?\nA) 0\nB) 1\nC) 0.5\nD) 2\nANS: A\nWHY: Groom Shudra is below bride Kshatriya.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Bride Libra, groom Leo: Varna score under Profile A?\nA) 0\nB) 1\nC) 0.5\nD) 2\nANS: B\nWHY: Groom Kshatriya is higher than bride Shudra.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which statement about Varna is correct in this course?\nA) It classifies people by social worth\nB) It is a symbolic grouping of Moon signs\nC) It decides marriage\nD) It is used to infer community\nANS: B\nWHY: The course teaches it as symbolism only, and the software never infers caste or status.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which counts are the favourable Mahendra positions?\nA) 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24\nB) 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25\nC) 1, 5, 9, 13, 17, 21, 25\nD) 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16\nANS: B\nWHY: The commonly cited list is 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22 and 25.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: What is the inclusive count from Ashwini to Rohini?\nA) 25\nB) 3\nC) 4\nD) 5\nANS: C\nWHY: Ashwini 1, Bharani 2, Krittika 3, Rohini 4.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Why does the Mahendra formula add 27 before MOD 27?\nA) To keep the subtraction positive across the Revati to Ashwini wrap\nB) To score the pair\nC) To make the count exclusive\nD) To reverse the direction\nANS: A\nWHY: Adding 27 avoids negative numbers when the groom's index is smaller.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: What is the inclusive count from Revati to Ashwini?\nA) 1\nB) 2\nC) 26\nD) 27\nANS: B\nWHY: Revati is 1 and Ashwini is 2.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A Mahendra count of 5 means:\nA) Mahendra Dosha\nB) Rejection of the match\nC) Only that the favourable condition is not met\nD) A score of zero out of eight\nANS: C\nWHY: Non-qualifying counts are not a Dosha.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: In Tradition A, Mahendra is:\nA) Part of Rajju\nB) Scored with 2 points\nC) A supplementary qualitative factor outside the 36\nD) A replacement for Nadi\nANS: C\nWHY: It is not one of Tradition A's ten factors.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Ashwini → Rohini has Mahendra count 4. What is the reverse count?\nA) 4\nB) 23\nC) 25\nD) 29\nANS: C\nWHY: The two counts add up to 29, and 25 is also a favourable position.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: How many grooms' Nakshatras qualify for one bride Nakshatra under Mahendra?\nA) 27\nB) 4\nC) 9\nD) 8\nANS: D\nWHY: One for each favourable count.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Why is Mahendra's qualification the same in both directions?\nA) Because the count is exclusive\nB) Because it uses the Moon sign\nC) Because the favourable list is closed under 29 minus the count\nD) Because it is not directional by definition\nANS: C\nWHY: The list is every count leaving remainder 1 modulo 3 apart from 1, and 29 minus such a count has the same remainder.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Stree Deergha is counted from:\nA) The groom to the bride\nB) The Sun to the Moon\nC) The ascendant\nD) The bride to the groom\nANS: D\nWHY: The commonly cited convention counts from the bride's Nakshatra.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Under SD1, which count qualifies?\nA) 13\nB) 12\nC) 14\nD) 9\nANS: C\nWHY: SD1 requires a count of more than 13.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Count 13 under SD1 and SD2:\nA) Qualifies in both\nB) Qualifies in neither\nC) Does not qualify under SD1, qualifies under SD2\nD) Qualifies under SD1, not under SD2\nANS: C\nWHY: \"More than 13\" excludes 13; \"13 or more\" includes it.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: What do the forward and reverse Stree Deergha counts add up to for two different Nakshatras?\nA) 27\nB) 28\nC) 29\nD) 30\nANS: C\nWHY: For example 4 and 25, or 14 and 15.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Bride Ashwini, groom Chitra: Stree Deergha count?\nA) 13\nB) 14\nC) 15\nD) 12\nANS: B\nWHY: Chitra is Nakshatra 14, and Ashwini is counted as 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Stree Deergha carries:\nA) 2 points\nB) 1 point\nC) No points in this course\nD) 36 points\nANS: C\nWHY: No verified source defining points is held.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which profile counts 9 or more as qualifying?\nA) SD1\nB) SD2\nC) SD3\nD) SD4\nANS: D\nWHY: SD4 is \"9 or more\"; SD3 is \"more than 9\".\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Is the Stree Deergha matrix symmetric?\nA) Yes\nB) No, because the counting is directional\nC) Yes, because the counts add up to 29\nD) Only for SD2\nANS: B\nWHY: Swapping bride and groom changes the count, so it can change the verdict.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: How many Rajju groups are there?\nA) 3\nB) 4\nC) 5\nD) 7\nANS: C\nWHY: Pada, Kati, Nabhi, Kantha, Siro.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which Rajju group has only three Nakshatras?\nA) Pada\nB) Nabhi\nC) Kantha\nD) Siro\nANS: D\nWHY: Siro holds Mrigashira, Chitra and Dhanishta.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Ashwini's Rajju group is:\nA) Kati\nB) Kantha\nC) Pada\nD) Siro\nANS: C\nWHY: Ashwini, Ashlesha, Magha, Jyeshtha, Mula and Revati are Pada.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Rohini's Rajju group is:\nA) Pada\nB) Kati\nC) Nabhi\nD) Kantha\nANS: D\nWHY: Rohini, Ardra, Hasta, Swati, Shravana and Shatabhisha are Kantha.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Rajju Dosha is flagged in Tradition A when:\nA) The Nakshatras are in the same Rajju group\nB) The Moon signs are adjacent\nC) The Nadis differ\nD) The Varnas differ\nANS: A\nWHY: Same group is the flag condition.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Rajju in Tradition A:\nA) Adds 2 points\nB) Is a pass/Dosha check with no points\nC) Replaces Nadi\nD) Is ignored\nANS: B\nWHY: Tradition A has 36 scored points plus two pass/Dosha checks.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The base Rajju method is:\nA) Directional (bride to groom only)\nB) Based on the Moon sign\nC) Symmetric: swapping the two people changes nothing\nD) Gender-specific in every school\nANS: C\nWHY: Same group is same group in either order.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which pair is a Vedha pair?\nA) Ashwini – Bharani\nB) Rohini – Hasta\nC) Ashwini – Jyeshtha\nD) Pushya – Magha\nANS: C\nWHY: Ashwini–Jyeshtha is on the list.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Bride Krittika, groom Vishakha shows:\nA) Neither flag\nB) Rajju only\nC) Vedha only\nD) Both a Rajju flag and a Vedha flag\nANS: D\nWHY: Both are Nabhi, and Krittika–Vishakha is a Vedha pair.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: What is true of the listed Vedha pairs in this table?\nA) None is a same-Rajju pair\nB) Every one is also a same-Rajju pair\nC) Half are same-Rajju pairs\nD) They are all in different Moon signs\nANS: B\nWHY: Vedha never flags a pair that Rajju did not already flag in this data.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: How is the Rajju table obtained in the software?\nA) Calculated from the Nakshatra number by a formula\nB) Typed in by each user\nC) Taken from the Moon's longitude\nD) Stored as a classification table and checked against an independent statement\nANS: D\nWHY: The zigzag is an observation, not the source.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Candidate Rajju exceptions in the software are:\nA) Applied automatically when their condition is true\nB) Used to restore points\nC) Shown with an evidence level and never applied\nD) Taken from Nadi rules\nANS: C\nWHY: They are unverified (grade E) and not transferable.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which factor is NOT computed in the Day 10 South Indian comparison profile?\nA) Mahendra\nB) Stree Deergha\nC) Rajju\nD) Dina\nANS: D\nWHY: Dina's regional counting rule is not defined or verified here.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Why does the software create no combined total across Varna, Mahendra, Stree Deergha and Rajju?\nA) Unlike factors need a source-defined weighting and none is verified\nB) The calculation is slow\nC) Totals are forbidden\nD) Only Rajju has points\nANS: A\nWHY: Tradition A's 36 is reproduced from the Calculation Lab; nothing else is invented.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A Nadi exception can be applied to Rajju when:\nA) The Nakshatras match\nB) Never automatically: exceptions are not transferred between Kootas\nC) The total is high\nD) Mahendra qualifies\nANS: B\nWHY: Each exception belongs to one Koota and carries its own evidence.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Profile M4 (exclusive counting) is included in the software to:\nA) Demonstrate how a wrong counting method changes the verdict\nB) Replace profile M1 in Tradition A\nC) Score Mahendra\nD) Represent the Telugu tradition\nANS: A\nWHY: It is labelled as a demonstration only.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. Why must the tradition be named before any Koota is calculated?\nMA: The ten factors, their calculation and their scoring differ between traditions, and a rule from one tradition cannot be moved into another. Naming the tradition makes the rules, the sources and the limits visible.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Explain, with a numerical example, why Mahendra's qualification is the same in both directions although its counts differ.\nMA: The reverse count is 29 minus the forward count. The favourable counts 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25 pair up as 4 and 25, 7 and 22, 10 and 19, 13 and 16, so if one direction gives a favourable count the other does as well. Example: Ashwini to Rohini is 4 and Rohini to Ashwini is 25.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual 3. A client says Stree Deergha predicts the bride's lifespan. What do you reply?\nMA: Stree Deergha is a count from the bride's Nakshatra to the groom's, compared with a named threshold. It is traditional symbolism used in some regional systems. It cannot predict health, longevity or marital destiny, and the result depends on the profile used.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 4. Why is count 13 a boundary case in Stree Deergha?\nMA: Under \"more than 13\" it does not qualify and under \"13 or more\" it does, so the same pair gives different verdicts. A report must name the profile and show both if the tradition is uncertain.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\n## References\n\n### Source status (Day 10)\nNo classical verse, chapter or page has been verified for any rule in this module. Statements below carry grades A (primary text verified) to E (unverified or disputed).\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Varna by element (fire Kshatriya, earth Vaishya, air Shudra, water Brahmin); groom ≥ bride scores 1 | C | Common manual convention; no verse verified |\n| Mahendra favourable counts 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25 (bride to groom, inclusive) | D | Contemporary pages agree on the list; no primary verse verified |\n| Stree Deergha threshold \"more than 13\" | D | One page; other pages give 9 as a baseline (more than 9, or 9 or more) |\n| Stree Deergha \"7 or more\" | E | Carried from earlier course material; not independently sourced |\n| Rajju groups (6/6/6/6/3) | B/C | Consistent across the pages found; no verse verified |\n| Rajju body-region names | D | Alternative names exist on some pages (for example a thigh region in place of the waist) |\n| Rajju symbolic consequences for particular people | E | Circulated claims; not endorsed |\n| Rajju exceptions | E | Unverified; never applied |\n| Vedha pair list (14 pairs) | C | Commonly listed; variants exist for Mrigashira, Chitra and Dhanishta |\n| Ten Poruthams listed as Dina, Gana, Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Yoni, Rasi, Rasi Adhipati, Vasya, Rajju, Vedha | D | Several contemporary pages agree; scoring and acceptance thresholds differ |\n| Aroha (ascending) and Avaroha (descending) terminology | E | Not confirmed by the pages found; the course uses \"ascending/descending\" only as a descriptive label |\n| Profiles A, R, S; M1 to M4; SD1 to SD6 | n/a | Software configurations, not classical schools |\n\n### Research notes for this day\nFour standard web searches were made. Only search-result summaries were read, not the full pages, so everything below is grade D and should be re-checked against the pages and, better, a printed manual.\n- The ten-Porutham list, the weighting remark that Rajju and Vedha are treated as most important in South Indian practice, and the \"at least seven of ten\" remark: [hindutone.com](https://hindutone.com/astrology/porutham-10-poruthams-south-indian-marriage-matching/), [hindu-blog.com](https://www.hindu-blog.com/2015/08/kalyana-porutham-10-porutham-meaning.html).\n- Mahendra counts and inclusive-counting remark: [vedicbirth.com Mahendra](https://www.vedicbirth.com/porutham/mahendra-porutham).\n- Stree Deergha thresholds: [vedicbirth.com](https://www.vedicbirth.com/porutham/stree-deergha-porutham) (more than 13; some traditions 9), [astrosight.ai](https://astrosight.ai/kundli/stree-deergha-significance-marital-bliss) (9 or more; 13 or more a stronger band), [rahasyavedicastrology.com](https://www.rahasyavedicastrology.com/stree-deergha/) (more than 9; that page uses Dosha language, which this course does not).\n- Rajju: [hindu-blog.com](https://www.hindu-blog.com/2018/05/rajju-in-marriage-rajju-porutham-for.html), [astrosight.ai](https://astrosight.ai/doshas/rajju-dosha-matchmaking). The search summaries gave only some group lists; one page's Siro list conflicted with the others (it included Rohini and Uttara Ashadha) and was not relied on. The full 27-Nakshatra table in this course comes from the common published table in the course data, cross-checked against an independent statement in the engine tests.\n\n### Corrections and observations on supplied material\n| Item | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| The brief asks whether Mahendra is directional (\"verify\") | Counting is directional, but qualification is identical in both directions because the list is closed under 29 minus the count |\n| The brief treats Mahendra, Stree Deergha and Rajju as possible members of one list | Tradition A has Varna, Rajju and Vedha; Mahendra and Stree Deergha are supplementary; the South Indian profile has Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Rajju and Vedha but not Varna |\n| The earlier Day 10 text called Mahendra and Stree Deergha \"for Day 11\" | They are now taught here in full. Day 11 applies them to compare schools |\n| The earlier Day 10 text said only \"a further refinement\" for Rajju | The ascending/descending refinement is recorded as unverified (E) and not applied |\n| Stree Deergha thresholds | Not one threshold: more than 13, 13 or more, more than 9, 9 or more, and 7 or more appear. Profiles keep them separate |\n\n### Research and source policy\nNo quotation, verse number, chapter or page is given. Leads still to be checked in printed editions: *Muhurta Chintamani* (Vivaha Prakarana), *Muhurta Ganapati*, *Prasna Marga* where relevant, recognised South Indian matching manuals and published works of B. V. Raman. Every rule that is not verified is labelled **\"Traditional practice: source attribution requires verification.\"**\n\n## Traditions\n### Part 1 – the ten-factor traditions and how to choose\nThe table compares the ten factors of each tradition held in the software. The custom slot lets you compose your own comparison tradition from the five modules of today.\n{{d10trad}}\n\n### What is documented and what is not\n| Tradition | Ten factors | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| North Indian Ashtakoota | Eight Kootas, 36 points | Common published tables (C/D) |\n| Tradition A (this course) | Eight Kootas + Rajju + Vedha | Course configuration |\n| South Indian / Tamil ten Poruthams | Dina, Gana, Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Yoni, Rasi, Rasi Adhipati, Vasya, Rajju, Vedha | Contemporary pages (D) |\n| Telugu, Kannada, Kerala practices | Not documented here | No source held; nothing asserted |\n| Published authorities (for example B. V. Raman) | Not verified here | Lead only |\n\n### Ashtakoota and Dashakoota: practical differences\n- **Scoring:** Ashtakoota scores to 36. South Indian practice judges matches factor by factor (a commonly repeated remark is that most of the ten should match) and puts weight on Rajju and Vedha. Point values differ between schemes.\n- **Counting:** the same Koota name may count differently. Dina is described as a one-direction count in Tamil practice while Tara in Tradition A is read in both directions (Day 4, Profile B).\n- **Mixing:** never copy a score or threshold from one tradition into another without recording the source.\n\n## Varna\n### Lesson 3 (interactive) – Varna calculator, twelve-sign table and matrices\nChoose the two Moon signs and a profile. The swapped-role result is shown for every profile. The matrices are generated from the engine.\n{{d10varna}}\n\n## Mahendra\n### Lesson 4 (interactive) – Mahendra calculator, favourable positions and the 27 × 27 matrix\nThe counting sequence is shown Nakshatra by Nakshatra. Profile M4 (exclusive) is a demonstration of a wrong method.\n{{d10mah}}\n\n## Stree Deergha\n### Lesson 5 (interactive) – Stree Deergha calculator, threshold profiles and directional matrix\nThe same pair is shown under every threshold profile so the boundary cases are visible.\n{{d10sd}}\n\n## Rajju and Vedha\n### Lesson 6 (interactive) – the Rajju table, the calculator and the 27 × 27 matrix\nThe table, the group-wise view, the calculator with the candidate exception list, and the matrix with Vedha marked.\n{{d10raj}}\n\n## Comparative Study\n### Lesson 7 – the four additional Kootas side by side\n| Feature | Varna | Mahendra | Stree Deergha | Rajju (and Vedha) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Primary input | Moon Rashi | Nakshatra | Nakshatra | Nakshatra |\n| Main calculation | Varna comparison | Inclusive count | Bride-to-groom count | Table lookup, same-group check |\n| Uses a counting formula | No | Yes | Yes | No |\n| Uses a classification table | Yes (12 signs) | No | No | Yes (27 Nakshatras; 14 Vedha pairs) |\n| Directional? | Yes (bride row, groom column) | Count is directional; qualification is not | Yes | No (base method) |\n| Needs bride/groom orientation | Yes | For the count only | Yes | No |\n| Numerical score | 1 in Tradition A | None | None | None |\n| Dosha? | No | No (non-qualifying is not a Dosha) | No | Rajju Dosha flag; Vedha Dosha flag |\n| Exceptions | None documented | None | None | Candidates, unverified, never applied |\nMoon-sign based: Varna. Janma Nakshatra based: Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Rajju, Vedha. Score-based: Varna only. Qualitative: Mahendra and Stree Deergha. Pass/Dosha: Rajju and Vedha.\n\n### Lesson 8 (interactive) – one horoscope pair, several traditions\nChoose two people and the traditions to compare. Every tradition is evaluated with its own rules. Differences are listed, and no total is created except Tradition A's own 36 from the Lab.\n{{d10cmp}}\n\n## Matrices\n### The four generated matrices\n| Matrix | Size | What each cell holds | Validation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Varna 4 × 4 | 16 | Score and relationship, bride Varna row, groom Varna column | 10 ones, 6 zeros |\n| Varna 12 × 12 | 144 | Score by Moon signs | Agrees with the Calculation Lab for all 144 pairs |\n| Mahendra 27 × 27 | 729 | Inclusive count and qualification | 216 qualifying, 8 per row, same qualification in both directions |\n| Stree Deergha 27 × 27 | 729 | Bride-to-groom count and qualification | Not symmetric; SD1 378, SD2 405, SD3 486, SD4 513, SD6 567 qualifying cells |\n| Rajju 27 × 27 | 729 | Same-group flag and Vedha marker | Symmetric; 153 same-Rajju cells (27 on the diagonal); agrees with the Lab for all 729 pairs |\nThe interactive matrices are in the Mahendra, Stree Deergha and Rajju and Vedha tabs, and the Varna matrices are in the Varna tab. Every matrix is generated by the engine, never typed in.\n\n## Worked Cases\n### Lesson 9 – twenty worked cases\nEvery Nakshatra, Pada and Moon sign below was checked by the engine tests. Tradition A profiles: Varna A, Mahendra M1, Stree Deergha SD1 (SD4 shown for comparison), Rajju A.\n| # | Case | Person A (bride) | Person B (groom) | Varna (A) | Mahendra (M1) | Stree Deergha (SD1 / SD4) | Rajju |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Same Varna | Rohini P2, Taurus | Hasta P1, Virgo | Vaishya/Vaishya: 1 | 10 qualifies (reverse 19) | 10: no / yes | Kantha/Kantha: Dosha flag |\n| 2 | Different Varna, favourable | Bharani P1, Aries | Pushya P4, Cancer | Kshatriya/Brahmin: 1 | 7 qualifies (reverse 22) | 7: no / no | Kati/Kati: Dosha flag |\n| 3 | Varna with bride/groom roles reversed | Pushya P4, Cancer | Bharani P1, Aries | Brahmin/Kshatriya: 0 | 22 qualifies (reverse 7) | 22: yes / yes | Kati/Kati: Dosha flag |\n| 4 | Mahendra count 4 | Ashwini P1, Aries | Rohini P1, Taurus | Kshatriya/Vaishya: 0 | 4 qualifies (reverse 25) | 4: no / no | Pada/Kantha: pass |\n| 5 | Mahendra count 7 | Bharani P2, Aries | Pushya P1, Cancer | Kshatriya/Brahmin: 1 | 7 qualifies (reverse 22) | 7: no / no | Kati/Kati: Dosha flag |\n| 6 | Mahendra count 10 | Krittika P3, Taurus | Uttara Phalguni P2, Virgo | Vaishya/Vaishya: 1 | 10 qualifies (reverse 19) | 10: no / yes | Nabhi/Nabhi: Dosha flag |\n| 7 | Mahendra count 13 | Mrigashira P3, Gemini | Anuradha P2, Scorpio | Shudra/Brahmin: 1 | 13 qualifies (reverse 16) | 13: no / yes | Siro/Kati: pass |\n| 8 | Mahendra count 16 | Punarvasu P4, Cancer | Shravana P3, Capricorn | Brahmin/Vaishya: 0 | 16 qualifies (reverse 13) | 16: yes / yes | Nabhi/Kantha: pass |\n| 9 | Mahendra count 19 | Hasta P2, Virgo | Rohini P4, Taurus | Vaishya/Vaishya: 1 | 19 qualifies (reverse 10) | 19: yes / yes | Kantha/Kantha: Dosha flag |\n| 10 | Mahendra count 22 | Anuradha P1, Scorpio | Purva Phalguni P3, Leo | Brahmin/Kshatriya: 0 | 22 qualifies (reverse 7) | 22: yes / yes | Kati/Kati: Dosha flag |\n| 11 | Mahendra count 25 | Shravana P2, Capricorn | Mula P1, Sagittarius | Vaishya/Kshatriya: 1 | 25 qualifies (reverse 4) | 25: yes / yes | Kantha/Pada: pass |\n| 12 | Mahendra non-qualifying count | Ashwini P3, Aries | Mrigashira P2, Taurus | Kshatriya/Vaishya: 0 | 5 no (reverse 24) | 5: no / no | Pada/Siro: pass |\n| 13 | Stree Deergha count below 13 | Ashwini P2, Aries | Ashlesha P1, Cancer | Kshatriya/Brahmin: 1 | 9 no (reverse 20) | 9: no / yes | Pada/Pada: Dosha flag |\n| 14 | Stree Deergha count exactly 13 | Chitra P3, Libra | Uttara Bhadrapada P4, Pisces | Shudra/Brahmin: 1 | 13 qualifies (reverse 16) | 13: no / yes | Siro/Kati: pass |\n| 15 | Stree Deergha count above 13 | Bharani P4, Aries | Uttara Ashadha P3, Capricorn | Kshatriya/Vaishya: 0 | 20 no (reverse 9) | 20: yes / yes | Kati/Nabhi: pass |\n| 16 | Stree Deergha boundary crossing (Revati to Ashwini) | Jyeshtha P1, Scorpio | Pushya P3, Cancer | Brahmin/Brahmin: 1 | 18 no (reverse 11) | 18: yes / yes | Pada/Kati: pass |\n| 17 | Same Rajju, same Nakshatra | Ashwini P1, Aries | Ashwini P3, Aries | Kshatriya/Kshatriya: 1 | 1 no (reverse 1) | 1: no / no | Pada/Pada: Dosha flag |\n| 18 | Same Rajju, different Nakshatra | Ashlesha P2, Cancer | Mula P3, Sagittarius | Brahmin/Kshatriya: 0 | 11 no (reverse 18) | 11: no / yes | Pada/Pada: Dosha flag; Vedha |\n| 19 | Different Rajju | Ashwini P2, Aries | Rohini P3, Taurus | Kshatriya/Vaishya: 0 | 4 qualifies (reverse 25) | 4: no / no | Pada/Kantha: pass |\n| 20 | Varna and Mahendra favourable, Rajju flagged | Ashwini P1, Aries | Magha P1, Leo | Kshatriya/Kshatriya: 1 | 10 qualifies (reverse 19) | 10: no / yes | Pada/Pada: Dosha flag |\nReading across the rows: no row is \"good\" or \"bad\" overall, because no combined score exists. Case 20 shows a favourable Varna and a qualifying Mahendra together with a Rajju flag. Case 4 shows a qualifying Mahendra with Varna 0. Case 16 shows a boundary-crossing count that qualifies although the Moon signs form a 5/9 pair (Bhakoot flagged in the Lab). Each result is a traditional classification and cannot decide suitability.\n\n{{d10cases}}\n\n## Software Specification\n### Part 14 – modular engines\nSix independent modules: **VarnaKootaEngine**, **MahendraKootaEngine**, **StreeDeerghaKootaEngine**, **RajjuKootaEngine**, **DashakootaTraditionEngine**, **DashakootaComparisonEngine**. In the code they are `DashakootaEngines.Varna`, `.Mahendra`, `.StreeDeergha`, `.Rajju`, `.Tradition` and `.Comparison`. The selected tradition decides which modules are included, how they are calculated and whether they give points or qualitative results.\n\n### Functions\n| Module | Functions |\n|---|---|\n| Varna | getRashiVarna, compareVarna, calculateVarnaScore, evaluateVarnaDirection, generateVarnaReport, matrix4, matrix12 |\n| Mahendra | getNakshatraIndex, calculateMahendraCount, checkMahendraFavourablePosition, evaluateMahendraProfile, generateMahendraReport, matrix |\n| Stree Deergha | calculateStreeDeerghaCount, checkStreeDeerghaThreshold, evaluateBrideGroomDirection, evaluateStreeDeerghaProfile, generateStreeDeerghaReport, matrix |\n| Rajju | getRajjuByNakshatra, compareRajjuGroups, identifyRajjuDosha, checkRajjuExceptions, evaluateRajjuProfile, generateRajjuReport, matrix, isVedha |\n| Tradition | getDashakootaTradition, getKootasInTradition, getKootaRuleProfile, calculateSelectedDashakoota, compareDashakootaTraditions, generateDashakootaReport |\n\n### Pseudocode\n1. `calculateMahendraCount(a, b)`: return ((b − a + 27) MOD 27) + 1, with zero-based indices.\n2. `checkMahendraFavourablePosition(count)`: return count is in {4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25}. Never return a Dosha.\n3. `evaluateMahendraProfile(bride, groom, profile)`: find both indices; compute both counts; choose the count the profile uses (bride to groom, groom to bride, or either); return status QUALIFIES or DOES_NOT_QUALIFY, the counting path, no score.\n4. `evaluateStreeDeerghaProfile(bride, groom, profile)`: count = ((groom − bride + 27) MOD 27) + 1 (or the reverse for SD5); qualifies = threshold(profile)(count); mark boundary cases 13 and 9; return no score.\n5. `calculateVarnaScore(brideRashi, groomRashi, profile)`: look up both Varnas; rank Brahmin 4, Kshatriya 3, Vaishya 2, Shudra 1; Profile A returns 1 when groom rank ≥ bride rank, else 0.\n6. `identifyRajjuDosha(a, b)`: look up both groups in the classification table; flag when equal.\n7. `checkRajjuExceptions(a, b, options)`: if options.applyUnverified is set, raise UNVERIFIED_EXCEPTION; otherwise return each candidate with its condition result, evidence level, active = false.\n8. `evaluateTradition(id, bride, groom)`: for each factor of the tradition, call its module, or return NOT_COMPUTED when the tradition's rule is not defined here; list absent factors as supplementary; create a total only when the tradition defines one and the Lab can reproduce it.\n\n### Data model\nDashakoota tradition master · Koota master · Koota-to-tradition mapping · Rashi master · Rashi Varna master · Nakshatra master · Nakshatra Pada master (Pada p of Nakshatra i lies in sign floor((4i + p − 1) / 9), zero-based) · Mahendra counting rule · Stree Deergha threshold rule · Rajju master · Rajju-Nakshatra mapping · Exception rule master · Source and reference master · Scoring profile · Calculation result · Learning material · Student assessment.\n\n### JSON examples\n- Selected tradition: `{\"id\":\"SI10a\",\"name\":\"South Indian ten-Porutham comparison profile (a)\",\"grade\":\"D\",\"verified\":false,\"total\":{\"defined\":false}}`\n- Ten-Koota list (start): `[{\"id\":\"dina\",\"type\":\"notComputed\"},{\"id\":\"gana\",\"type\":\"notComputed\"},{\"id\":\"mahendra\",\"type\":\"qualitative\"},{\"id\":\"streeDeergha\",\"type\":\"qualitative\"}]`\n- Varna classification: `{\"rashi\":\"Aries\",\"element\":\"Fire\",\"varna\":\"Kshatriya\",\"rank\":3,\"grade\":\"C\"}`\n- Mahendra rule: `{\"id\":\"M1\",\"direction\":\"bride_to_groom\",\"inclusive\":true,\"favourable\":[4,7,10,13,16,19,22,25],\"points\":null,\"grade\":\"D\"}`\n- Stree Deergha rule: `{\"id\":\"SD1\",\"direction\":\"bride_to_groom\",\"qualifiesWhen\":\"count > 13\",\"points\":null,\"grade\":\"D\",\"source\":\"one contemporary page\"}`\n- Rajju classification: `{\"nakshatra\":\"Ashwini\",\"rajju\":\"Pada\",\"body\":\"Feet\",\"grade\":\"B/C\"}`\n- Exception rule: `{\"id\":\"SAME_RASHI_LORD\",\"koota\":\"rajju\",\"evidence\":\"E\",\"active\":false,\"changesDoshaStatus\":false,\"restoresScore\":false}`\n- Calculation result: `{\"module\":\"DASHAKOOTA_DAY_10\",\"selectedTradition\":{\"id\":\"A\"},\"personA\":{\"moonRashi\":\"Aries\",\"nakshatra\":\"Ashwini\",\"pada\":1},\"personB\":{\"moonRashi\":\"Leo\",\"nakshatra\":\"Magha\",\"pada\":1},\"results\":{\"varna\":{\"rawScore\":1,\"max\":1},\"mahendra\":{\"status\":\"QUALIFIES\",\"count\":10,\"rawScore\":null},\"streeDeergha\":{\"status\":\"DOES_NOT_QUALIFY\",\"count\":10,\"profile\":\"SD1\"},\"rajju\":{\"status\":\"DOSHA_PRESENT\",\"exceptionApplied\":null}},\"overallStatus\":\"PROFILE_BASED_COMPARISON\",\"total\":null}`\n\n### Error handling\n| Code | Situation |\n|---|---|\n| MISSING_NAKSHATRA, INVALID_NAKSHATRA | No or unknown Nakshatra |\n| INVALID_PADA | Pada not 1 to 4 |\n| MISSING_RASHI, INVALID_RASHI | Moon sign missing or unknown |\n| INCONSISTENT_RASHI | The Moon sign does not match the Nakshatra and Pada (for example Krittika Pada 1 is Aries, not Taurus) |\n| MISSING_VARNA, MISSING_RAJJU | A table entry is absent |\n| MISSING_TRADITION, UNKNOWN_PROFILE | The tradition or profile does not exist |\n| UNVERIFIED_EXCEPTION | An unverified exception was requested |\n| INSUFFICIENT_DATA | Returned by the evaluate functions instead of guessing |\n| DATA_NOT_LOADED | The rule data has not been loaded |\nNo unsupported exception and no regional rule is ever silently substituted for another.\n\n## Exercises\n### Lesson 10 – twenty practical exercises\nVarna identification and direction, Mahendra counting, Stree Deergha counting and thresholds, Rajju groups, Vedha, and a combined case. Answers are computed by the engines.\n{{d10ex}}\n\n## Final Assessment\n### The 40-question final assessment\n10 conceptual, 10 Varna and Mahendra, 10 Stree Deergha and 10 Rajju and comparison questions. Pass mark 70%. A failed attempt never erases your completed lessons.\n{{d10final}}\n\n## Common Mistakes\nTwenty-five mistakes to recognise, with the correct method.\n| # | Mistake | Correct method |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Assuming all Dashakoota traditions use the same ten factors | Name the tradition and list its factors |\n| 2 | Mixing Ashtakoota and Dashakoota scores | Keep each tradition's results separate |\n| 3 | Adding Varna points to an unrelated ten-factor total | Create no total unless the tradition defines one |\n| 4 | Treating Varna as a caste judgement | It is a symbolic label of Moon signs only |\n| 5 | Treating Varna as a measure of intelligence or status | It measures nothing about a person |\n| 6 | Using an unverified Rashi-to-Varna table | Use a named, tabulated profile |\n| 7 | Reversing the Varna direction | Bride row, groom column in Profile A |\n| 8 | Counting Mahendra from the wrong Nakshatra | Start from the bride's in profile M1 |\n| 9 | Forgetting inclusive counting | The start is counted as 1 |\n| 10 | Calling every Mahendra count outside the list a Dosha | It is only non-qualifying |\n| 11 | Confusing Mahendra with Stree Deergha | Different thresholds and meaning, same counting skeleton |\n| 12 | Reversing bride and groom in Stree Deergha | The count runs from the bride |\n| 13 | Using an unverified threshold | Name the profile (SD1 to SD6) |\n| 14 | Forgetting the Revati-to-Ashwini wrap | Use MOD 27 |\n| 15 | Treating Stree Deergha as a woman's longevity prediction | It is a symbolic count, not a forecast |\n| 16 | Confusing Rajju with Nadi | Rajju has five body-region groups; Nadi has three |\n| 17 | Confusing Rajju with Gana | Gana has three temperaments (Day 7) |\n| 18 | Using an incorrect Rajju table | Use the stored, cross-checked table |\n| 19 | Treating all five Rajju groups as identical in meaning | They have separate symbolism, still unverified |\n| 20 | Making deterministic predictions of death or harm from Rajju | Never; traditional language is historical context |\n| 21 | Applying an exception from one Koota to another | Exceptions are not transferred |\n| 22 | Treating regional practice as universal | State the region, source and profile |\n| 23 | Giving points to qualitative factors | No points without a source-defined rule |\n| 24 | Ignoring the selected tradition | Every report shows it |\n| 25 | Producing an overall score without a verified framework | Show a structured profile instead |\n\n## Instructor Guide\n*For teachers and self-study students.*\n\n### 120-minute plan\n| Section | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Dashakoota tradition and comparison | 10 |\n| Varna Koota | 15 |\n| Mahendra Koota | 20 |\n| Stree Deergha Koota | 20 |\n| Rajju Koota | 25 |\n| Regional variations and exceptions | 15 |\n| Practical cases and recap | 15 |\n\n### Teaching sequence\n1. Ask: \"How many Kootas are there?\" and let students discover that the answer depends on the tradition. 2. Show the two ten-factor lists side by side. 3. Varna: draw the 4 × 4 matrix and show the direction effect; state the no-caste rule first. 4. Mahendra: count Ashwini to Rohini on the board in both directions, then show that 4 and 25 are both favourable. 5. Stree Deergha: count Chitra to Uttara Bhadrapada and ask \"does 13 qualify?\" to open the profile discussion. 6. Rajju: read the table by groups, then show the zigzag as a memory aid only. 7. Cases 14, 17, 18 and 20. 8. Recap with the comparison panel.\n\n### Whiteboard demonstrations\nThe 27-Nakshatra circle with the count arc from bride to groom; the reverse arc (adds to 29); the Rajju zigzag; the 4 × 4 Varna matrix.\n\n### Common student mistakes\nSee the Common Mistakes tab.\n\n### Student activities\nPairs choose two birthdays, find Nakshatras and Padas, and compute all four Kootas under two tradition profiles, then write a three-line neutral summary.\n\n### Homework\nComplete the 20 exercises, then write a one-page report with the selected tradition, each Koota result, the evidence level and the limitations.\n\n### Assessment criteria\nCorrect counts and tables; named tradition and profile; raw results kept separate from interpretation; no total invented; no medical, fertility, longevity or caste claims; limits stated.\n\n> **Special teaching note.** Do not treat Varna, Mahendra, Stree Deergha and Rajju as a universally fixed Dashakoota combination. Which of them belong to a tradition, how they are calculated and whether they give points is decided by that tradition and its source, and the software must show that choice.\n\n## Report\n### Part 18 – the additional Kootas report\nThe report shows the selected tradition and its source; both people's Nakshatra, Pada and Moon sign; the Varna result; the Mahendra count and result; the Stree Deergha count, threshold and result; the Rajju and Vedha results; raw status and adjusted status (identical here); exceptions applied (none); evidence level; limitations; and the statement below. It creates no total unless the selected tradition defines one.\n{{d10report}}\n\n> Dashakoota and additional Koota assessments follow specific regional and textual traditions. The composition, calculation and interpretation of these factors may differ between schools. ASTROSUTRAS displays the selected tradition and its rules transparently. These traditional astrological assessments should not be treated as scientifically established predictions or as the sole basis for a marriage decision.\n\n## Validation\n### Technical validation report\nAutomatic tests of the Day 10 engines. Every row runs when this page opens.\n{{d10tests}}\n","11":"# Day 11 – Comparative Schools of Thought\n\n## Learn\n\n### Today's learning question\n> How do I recognise which tradition a matching result belongs to, where its rules come from, and why two legitimate systems can give different results for the same couple, without forcing them into one table?\n\n### Central principle\n**A matching result is meaningful only when the tradition, source, calculation method and interpretation rules are identified.** Day 11 is not about declaring one tradition superior or about building a bigger score. It is about keeping differences accurate, documented and visible.\n\n### Learning objectives\nBy the end of this day you can:\n1. Name the principal matching systems and say which factors each one lists, with the status of the evidence.\n2. Distinguish Ashtakoota (अष्टकूट) from the regional ten-factor systems and explain why \"ten factors\" does not mean the same ten.\n3. Describe the commonly listed Tamil Pathu Porutham (पोरुत्तम) and run it as a separate profile.\n4. Recognise directional counting and apply the forward and reverse count formulas.\n5. Separate cancellation, mitigation, alternative interpretation and score restoration.\n6. Grade a rule with the seven-level evidence hierarchy and read a research register.\n7. Compare two reports, find the exact rule that makes them differ, and audit a software result.\n8. Generate a multi-tradition comparison report that never converts or adds scores.\n\n### Honest scope note\nThis module teaches the **method of comparison** and builds profiles only where identifiable rule data exists. Searches in preparation for this lesson found the **ten-Porutham list** documented on several websites, but did **not** find independent, verifiable rule tables for Telugu or Kannada practice, did **not** confirm the \"about 23 considerations\" claim for Kerala, and found **no software methodology documented** on the pages checked. Those rows therefore stay **PENDING_RESEARCH** or **implementation not independently verified**. Nothing is invented to fill a gap, and no classical verse, chapter or page is cited, because none was verified.\n\n{{c11dash}}\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### 11.1 Why horoscope matching is not one calculation\nReasons two competent astrologers can give different results, each following a recognised method:\n- different textual or regional foundations (Parampara, परम्परा);\n- different factor lists and different names for similar ideas;\n- different Nakshatra, Rashi and Yoni tables;\n- different counting directions and thresholds;\n- different scoring: weighted points, pass/fail counts, mandatory factors or purely qualitative reading;\n- different treatment of Dosha and of cancellation;\n- family custom versus published authority.\n\n### 11.2 Terminology\n| Term | Meaning as used here | Caution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ashtakoota (अष्टकूट), Guna Milan | Eight factors, 36 weighted points | Not a regional ten-factor system |\n| Dashakoota (दशकूट) | A ten-factor framework; the factors vary by source | There is no single verified ten-factor list |\n| Pathu Porutham | The Tamil \"ten agreements\" | Commonly listed as the ten below; sources differ on scoring |\n| Dashavidha Porutham | A Kerala and South Indian name for the ten Poruthams | Not verified as a separate rule set |\n| Porutham / Koota / Guna | \"Agreement\", \"factor\", \"quality\" | Not used identically across regions |\n| Anukulya (अनुकूल्य) | Favourable agreement; used for a broader assessment in some Kerala usage | Its factor count is not verified |\n| Papasamyam (पापसाम्यम्) | Balancing of malefic affliction between two charts | No verified numeric method found |\n| Chovva Dosham | A Kerala name for the Mars Dosha | Houses counted differ by source |\n| Vivaha Milan (विवाह मिलान) | Marriage matching in general | |\n| Parihara (परिहार) | Cancellation or relief | Four different ideas, see Exceptions |\n\n### 11.3 Ten factors do not always mean the same ten\nThe factor list commonly given for Tamil matching is **Dina, Gana, Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Yoni, Rasi, Rasyadhipathi, Vasya, Rajju, Vedhai**. It is **not** the eight Ashtakoota Kootas plus two:\n- Varna and Nadi are not in it (as listed on the websites found).\n- Tara is replaced by a counting factor called Dina.\n- Mahendra and Stree Deergha, which have no counterpart in the eight, take their places.\n- Rajju and Vedha are part of the ten (in Tradition A they are added as pass/Dosha checks).\nOther published ten-factor lists exist; each must be verified separately.\n\n### 11.4 Same name, different rule; different name, similar idea\n| Idea | Ashtakoota name | Tamil name | Caution |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nakshatra count | Tara | Dina | Different counting, remainder rule and scale |\n| Moon-sign relationship | Bhakoot | Rasi | The course's Tamil Rasi rule is a provisional mapping (Level 7) |\n| Sign-lord relationship | Graha Maitri | Rasyadhipathi | Thresholds differ; here derived (Level 7) |\n| Influence | Vashya | Vasya | Group tables differ |\n| Temperament | Gana | Gana | Matrices differ |\n| Animal symbol | Yoni | Yoni | Matrices differ |\n| Bond | Rajju | Rajju | Course profile: same group = Dosha |\n| Obstruction | Vedha | Vedhai | Pair lists differ |\nMatching names do not make rules identical.\n\n### 11.5 The seven-level evidence hierarchy\n| Level | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Primary textual evidence: a verifiable passage in a recognised classical text |\n| 2 | Recognised commentary with author, edition and textual basis |\n| 3 | Published traditional authority explaining the rule |\n| 4 | Documented regional practice (manual, Panchanga, recognised professional tradition) |\n| 5 | Contemporary professional convention with an identifiable methodology |\n| 6 | Popular contemporary claim without primary-source verification |\n| 7 | Unverified: origin or exact condition cannot be established |\nEvery rule in the engines carries one of these levels. A commercial website is evidence of current practice, never automatically of classical origin.\n\n### 11.6 Overlap\nSeveral factors depend on the same input (Moon sign or Moon Nakshatra), so a profile with ten factors is not ten independent tests.\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### 11.7 Orientation and counting formulas\nNakshatra indices run Ashwini = 0 to Revati = 26.\n- Forward inclusive count: `((End − Start + 27) MOD 27) + 1`\n- Reverse inclusive count: `((Start − End + 27) MOD 27) + 1`\n- For two different Nakshatras the two counts add up to **29**.\nThese are calculation tools. Each Koota states its own starting person, ending person, direction, threshold and whether the reverse is also checked.\n\n### 11.8 The factors that count (bride to groom, forward, inclusive)\n| Factor | Rule as published on websites (Level 5) | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dina | Divide the count by 9; remainder 2, 4, 6, 8 or 0 qualifies | One blog gives an explicit list of counts (2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 11, 13, 15, 18, 20, 24, 26); the two differ at counts 17, 22 and 27 |\n| Mahendra (महेन्द्र) | Counts 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25 qualify | A non-qualifying count is not a Dosha |\n| Stree Deergha (स्त्री दीर्घ) | Count greater than 13 qualifies | One source also mentions awarding points; a variant \"at least 7\" in earlier course text has no verified source |\nMahendra's qualifying set is closed under count → 29 − count (4↔25, 7↔22, 10↔19, 13↔16), so it gives the **same result in both orientations**; the counts themselves differ. Dina and Stree Deergha are genuinely directional.\n\n### 11.9 The other Tamil factors in this course\nGana, Yoni, Vasya and Rasyadhipathi use thresholds **derived by the course from its Tradition A tables** (Level 7, flagged in every result): Gana ≥ 5 of 6, Yoni ≥ 2 of 4, Vasya ≥ 1 of 2, Rasyadhipathi ≥ 3 of 5. Rasi uses a provisional mapping to the Bhakoot classes. Rajju qualifies when the groups differ. Vedhai qualifies when the pair is not in the course's list. These are the course's working assumptions, not published Tamil rules.\n\n### 11.10 Scoring styles\n| Style | Scale |\n|---|---|\n| 36-point weighted | 0–36 plus Dosha flags |\n| Ten-factor count | 0–10 qualifying; thresholds of 6 or 7 are quoted, sources differ |\n| Mandatory factors | Rajju and Vedhai are described as the most critical by several websites; shown as an optional check, not a score |\n| Qualitative | Dosha present / absent per chart |\nThese cannot be converted into one another without an explicitly defined and justified model; none was found, so conversion is **NOT_SUPPORTED**.\n\n## Different Schools\nUse the registry and the comparison engine below. Each profile is a separate rulebook with its own status.\n\n{{c11registry}}\n\n{{c11compare}}\n\n## Exceptions\n- A rule listed in one profile applies only within that profile.\n- An exception from one Koota or tradition is never transferred to another: a Nadi exception is not a Rajju exception; a Stree Deergha qualification does not cancel Rajju Dosha.\n- Where sources conflict, show the conflict; keep both as profiles.\n- Missing data is shown as PENDING_RESEARCH, never guessed.\n- **Cancellation** (the source removes the Dosha under stated conditions), **mitigation** (the Dosha remains but other factors lessen its weight), **alternative interpretation** (another tradition does not recognise the Dosha) and **score restoration** (the scoring method changes the number) are four different ideas.\n\n{{c11exc}}\n\n## Examples\n\n### Worked example – one pair, three profiles\n**Bride:** Moon in Rohini (Taurus). **Groom:** Moon in Hasta (Virgo). Tradition A (common tables):\n| Koota | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Varna | Vaishya and Vaishya = 1 |\n| Vashya | Chatushpada bride, Manava groom = 1 |\n| Tara | Counts 10 and 19, both Janma = 3 |\n| Yoni | Serpent and Buffalo = 1 |\n| Graha Maitri | Venus and Mercury mutual friends = 5 |\n| Gana | Manushya bride, Deva groom = 6 |\n| Bhakoot | Taurus to Virgo = 5/9, **Dosha**, 0 |\n| Nadi | Antya and Adi = 8 |\n| Rajju | Both Kantha, **Dosha** |\n| Vedha | Not a listed pair, pass |\n| **Total (scored)** | **25 of 36** |\n\n**Variants of Tradition A:** with \"Janma is inauspicious\", Tara becomes 0 and the total **22**.\n\n**Tamil profile A (separate scale):** Dina count 10 (remainder 1) does not qualify; Mahendra qualifies (count 10); Stree Deergha does not qualify (10 is not greater than 13; it would at \"at least 7\"); Gana qualifies; Yoni does not; Rasi does not (5/9, provisional mapping); Rasyadhipathi qualifies; Vasya qualifies; Rajju does not (both Kantha); Vedhai qualifies. **5 of 10 qualify.**\n\nThese are separate statements. \"25 of 36\" and \"5 of 10\" are not comparable numbers.\n\n{{c11tamil}}\n\n## Practice\n\n### Exercise set 1 – naming map\nComplete the table in 11.4 from memory and add a column \"what I still need to verify\".\n\n### Exercise set 2 – counts\n| # | Bride | Groom | Count | Mahendra? | Stree Deergha (> 13) | (≥ 7) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ashwini | Rohini | | | | |\n| 2 | Ashwini | Pushya | | | | |\n| 3 | Ashwini | Chitra | | | | |\n| 4 | Bharani | Hasta | | | | |\n| 5 | Revati | Ashwini | | | | |\n\n### Exercise set 3 – difference statement\nFor the worked example, write a five-line \"where the profiles differ\" statement without adding or converting scores.\n\n### Exercise set 4 – gap register\nCreate a table with rows Telugu, Kannada, Kerala and \"other regional\": columns Factors, Scoring, Source, Level. Write PENDING_RESEARCH where true.\n\n### Answers – set 2\n1: count 4, Mahendra yes, > 13 no, ≥ 7 no · 2: count 8, no, no, yes · 3: count 14, no, yes, yes · 4: count 12, no, no, yes · 5: count 2, no, no, no.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: Why keep regional systems as separate profiles?\nA: Same-named factors can have different rules, scales and tables; mixing them gives a hybrid no tradition recognises.\n\nQ: Can scores from two profiles be added or converted?\nA: No. Conversion is NOT_SUPPORTED; no justified model exists.\n\nQ: What is the central principle of Day 11?\nA: A matching result is meaningful only when the tradition, source, method and interpretation rules are identified.\n\nQ: Does \"ten factors\" mean the same ten factors everywhere?\nA: No. Lists differ; the Tamil ten are not the eight Ashtakoota plus two.\n\nQ: Which factors of Ashtakoota are absent from the commonly listed Tamil ten?\nA: Varna and Nadi.\n\nQ: Which Tamil factors have no counterpart among the eight Ashtakoota Kootas?\nA: Mahendra and Stree Deergha (and Rajju and Vedhai, which Tradition A adds as pass/Dosha checks).\n\nQ: Tara corresponds to which Tamil factor?\nA: Dina, with different counting and scale.\n\nQ: Bhakoot corresponds to which Tamil factor?\nA: Rasi; in this course the Tamil Rasi rule is a provisional mapping (Level 7).\n\nQ: What is Pathu Porutham?\nA: The Tamil \"ten agreements\" (पोरुत्तम), commonly listed as Dina, Gana, Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Yoni, Rasi, Rasyadhipathi, Vasya, Rajju, Vedhai.\n\nQ: Forward inclusive count formula?\nA: ((End − Start + 27) MOD 27) + 1, with Ashwini = 0.\n\nQ: Reverse inclusive count formula?\nA: ((Start − End + 27) MOD 27) + 1.\n\nQ: Forward plus reverse count of two different Nakshatras?\nA: 29.\n\nQ: Mahendra favourable counts?\nA: 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25.\n\nQ: Is a non-qualifying Mahendra count a Dosha?\nA: No. It simply does not qualify.\n\nQ: Why is Mahendra the same in both orientations?\nA: The favourable counts satisfy c ↔ 29 − c.\n\nQ: Stree Deergha common threshold?\nA: A bride-to-groom count greater than 13 (13 itself does not qualify at \"> 13\").\n\nQ: How many counts out of 27 qualify for Stree Deergha at \"> 13\"?\nA: 14 (counts 14 to 27).\n\nQ: Dina remainder rule?\nA: Divide the bride-to-groom count by 9; remainder 2, 4, 6, 8 or 0 qualifies.\n\nQ: Where does the Dina explicit list differ from the remainder rule?\nA: At counts 17, 22 and 27.\n\nQ: Is Mahendra part of Tradition A's 36 points?\nA: No. It is a supplementary qualitative factor.\n\nQ: What do several websites say about Rajju and Vedhai in Tamil practice?\nA: They are described as the most critical factors; this is a priority statement, not a score.\n\nQ: What threshold of qualifying Poruthams is quoted?\nA: At least 6 or at least 7 of 10; sources differ.\n\nQ: What is Papasamyam?\nA: A comparison of malefic affliction between two charts (Kerala usage); no verified numeric method was found.\n\nQ: What is Chovva Dosham?\nA: The Kerala name for the Mars Dosha; the houses counted differ between sources.\n\nQ: Can the app calculate Chovva Dosham from birth data?\nA: No. It has no planets other than the Moon, so the checker needs user-supplied Mars houses.\n\nQ: Was the claim of about 23 Kerala considerations confirmed?\nA: No. It is recorded as unconfirmed (Level 7).\n\nQ: What is the status of the Telugu and Kannada profiles?\nA: PENDING_RESEARCH: no independent rule tables were found.\n\nQ: What is evidence Level 1?\nA: A verifiable passage in a recognised classical text.\n\nQ: What is evidence Level 6?\nA: A popular contemporary claim without primary-source verification.\n\nQ: What is Level 7?\nA: Unverified: origin or exact condition cannot be established.\n\nQ: What does \"implementation not independently verified\" mean?\nA: A software's displayed result is not proof of the rule it uses; its methodology was not found documented.\n\nQ: Two programs disagree. First step?\nA: Separate the astronomical layer (ayanamsha, Moon longitude, time zone) from the rule layer (tables, directions, thresholds).\n\nQ: Name four things a software audit checks.\nA: Ayanamsha, Moon longitude, boundary Nakshatra/Pada/Rashi, the Koota rule tables, manual score recalculation, Dosha logic, cancellation conditions, report completeness.\n\nQ: Cancellation versus mitigation?\nA: Cancellation removes the Dosha under stated conditions; mitigation leaves it identifiable but lessens its weight.\n\nQ: Can an exception from one Koota be applied to another?\nA: No. Never without documentary support.\n\nQ: May an unverified exception be activated?\nA: Only by explicit user choice with acknowledgement; the raw result stays unchanged.\n\nQ: In the course data, can Rajju be favourable while Vedha is adverse?\nA: No. Every listed Vedha pair also shares a Rajju group; published lists may differ.\n\nQ: What must a comparison report never contain?\nA: A combined or converted score, or a statement that one tradition is superior.\n\nQ: What must a report name first?\nA: The selected tradition, source and rule profile.\n\nQ: What is the plug-in idea for Kootas?\nA: Each Koota is an independent module with its own input, classification, calculation, direction, exceptions, sources and tests.\n\nQ: Why is a high Ashtakoota total not a prediction of the Tamil result?\nA: The lists, scales, thresholds and tables differ.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: The course rule for regional systems is:\nA) Ignore them\nB) Merge them into one table\nC) Use the largest\nD) Keep separate rule profiles\nANS: D\nWHY: Merging creates a hybrid that no tradition recognises.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Dina in the Tamil list is the counterpart of:\nA) Nadi\nB) Varna\nC) Gana\nD) Tara\nANS: D\nWHY: Tara and Dina both use Nakshatra counting, though conventions differ.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Rasi in the Tamil list corresponds loosely to:\nA) Nadi\nB) Bhakoot\nC) Vashya\nD) Yoni\nANS: B\nWHY: Both concern the relation of the Moon signs; the course's Tamil Rasi rule is provisional.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Can scores from Ashtakoota and the Tamil profile be added?\nA) Yes\nB) Only for Nadi\nC) No\nD) Only for Yoni\nANS: C\nWHY: Scores from different profiles are never combined; conversion is NOT_SUPPORTED.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Mahendra favourable counts include:\nA) 2\nB) 5\nC) 6\nD) 4\nANS: D\nWHY: The list is 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Count from Ashwini to Rohini (inclusive)?\nA) 25\nB) 3\nC) 5\nD) 4\nANS: D\nWHY: Ashwini is 1 and Rohini is the 4th.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which two factors of the eight Ashtakoota Kootas are absent from the commonly listed Tamil ten?\nA) Vashya and Graha Maitri\nB) Gana and Yoni\nC) Tara and Bhakoot\nD) Varna and Nadi\nANS: D\nWHY: Varna and Nadi do not appear in the list found on the websites.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Stree Deergha for bride Ashwini, groom Chitra (count 14) at \"> 13\":\nA) Does not qualify\nB) Is a Dosha\nC) Qualifies\nD) Not applicable\nANS: C\nWHY: 14 is greater than 13.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Stree Deergha for bride Bharani, groom Hasta (count 12) at \"≥ 7\":\nA) Does not qualify\nB) Qualifies\nC) Is a Dosha\nD) Not applicable\nANS: B\nWHY: 12 is at least 7, although it fails \"> 13\".\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Count from Revati to Ashwini?\nA) 2\nB) 26\nC) 1\nD) 27\nANS: A\nWHY: ((0 − 26 + 27) MOD 27) + 1 = 2.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Reverse inclusive count from Ashwini to Bharani?\nA) 2\nB) 27\nC) 1\nD) 26\nANS: B\nWHY: ((0 − 1 + 27) MOD 27) + 1 = 27.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Forward and reverse counts of two different Nakshatras add up to:\nA) 27\nB) 29\nC) 28\nD) 30\nANS: B\nWHY: Forward + reverse = 29 whenever the Nakshatras differ.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which count qualifies under the Dina remainder rule (remainder 2, 4, 6, 8 or 0)?\nA) 5\nB) 12\nC) 11\nD) 10\nANS: C\nWHY: 11 divided by 9 leaves 2. The others leave 5, 3 and 1.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The Dina remainder rule and the explicit list differ at counts:\nA) They never differ\nB) 4, 7 and 10\nC) 13 and 16\nD) 17, 22 and 27\nANS: D\nWHY: Those counts have a qualifying remainder but are not in the explicit list.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Why does Mahendra give the same result for bride→groom and groom→bride?\nA) It ignores the count\nB) The qualifying counts satisfy c ↔ 29 − c\nC) It is symmetric by the definition of Nadi\nD) It is not a counted factor\nANS: B\nWHY: 4↔25, 7↔22, 10↔19, 13↔16; the counts differ but both lie in the set.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The worked pair (Rohini bride, Hasta groom) scores in Tradition A:\nA) 25\nB) 18\nC) 21\nD) 28\nANS: A\nWHY: 1 + 1 + 3 + 1 + 5 + 6 + 0 + 8 = 25.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: In the Janma-inauspicious variant, the same pair scores:\nA) 22\nB) 25\nC) 27\nD) 18\nANS: A\nWHY: Tara drops from 3 to 0, so 25 − 3 = 22.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: For the same pair the Tamil profile A gives:\nA) 5 of 10 qualify\nB) 22 of 36\nC) 25 of 36\nD) 10 of 10 qualify\nANS: A\nWHY: Mahendra, Gana, Rasyadhipathi, Vasya and Vedhai qualify; the others do not.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which statement about Telugu or Kannada profiles in this course is correct?\nA) They are fully documented\nB) They equal Kerala\nC) They equal Ashtakoota\nD) They are PENDING_RESEARCH\nANS: D\nWHY: No independent rule tables were found; none were invented.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The claim of \"about 23\" Kerala considerations is recorded as:\nA) Unconfirmed (Level 7)\nB) A Kerala standard\nC) A primary-text rule\nD) A Tamil rule\nANS: A\nWHY: The source and the factor list were not found.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A software program gives a different total from your table. What do you do?\nA) Trust the software\nB) Average them\nC) Identify the program's profile and compare factor by factor\nD) Delete one\nANS: C\nWHY: Differences come from tables, directions, thresholds or astronomical settings.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which source grade applies to a rule found only on a popular website with no methodology?\nA) Level 3\nB) Level 2\nC) Level 1\nD) Level 6\nANS: D\nWHY: A popular contemporary claim is Level 6.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A Nadi exception was listed in the Ashtakoota profile. Does it apply to Rajju?\nA) Yes, always\nB) Only for Siro Rajju\nC) Only if the total is above 18\nD) No, never without documentary support\nANS: D\nWHY: Exceptions are never transferred between Kootas.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: \"Cancellation\" means:\nA) A source says the Dosha is removed under stated conditions\nB) The score is multiplied\nC) The Koota is deleted from the list\nD) The total is rounded\nANS: A\nWHY: Mitigation and score restoration are different ideas.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The engine's treatment of an unverified exception is:\nA) Never shown\nB) Applied automatically when conditions match\nC) Applied only on explicit acknowledgement, raw result unchanged\nD) Always cancels the Dosha\nANS: C\nWHY: Unverified rules cannot be silently activated.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Chovva Dosham is checked in this course by:\nA) Birth-time calculation of Mars\nB) User-supplied Mars houses\nC) The Moon Nakshatra alone\nD) A fixed table\nANS: B\nWHY: The app has no planetary positions other than the Moon.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The two Chovva variants K1 and K2 disagree for Mars in house 2 from Lagna because:\nA) The count is reversed\nB) The Moon is involved\nC) One includes house 2 in its set, the other does not\nD) K1 uses Venus\nANS: C\nWHY: K1 uses houses 1, 7, 8; K2 uses 1, 2, 4, 7, 8, 12.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A report states \"Rajju favourable, Vedhai adverse\". In the course data this is:\nA) Common\nB) Impossible, because every listed Vedha pair shares a Rajju group\nC) Only for Siro\nD) Only for Kantha\nANS: B\nWHY: Published lists may differ; recorded as an open research item.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which layer do ayanamsha and time zone belong to?\nA) The rule layer\nB) The astronomical layer\nC) The scoring layer\nD) The interpretation layer\nANS: B\nWHY: They change the Moon longitude before any rule table is used.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Two sources disagree on a threshold. The course keeps:\nA) Both as separate profiles\nB) The larger value\nC) The newer source\nD) The average\nANS: A\nWHY: Conflicting rules coexist; no source is declared correct without evidence.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Papasamyam in this course is:\nA) Recorded as a research item; no numeric method was verified\nB) The same as Rajju\nC) Part of Tradition A\nD) Fully calculated\nANS: A\nWHY: No verified calculation method was found.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The sources found for Tamil matching say Rajju and Vedhai are:\nA) Unimportant\nB) Always scored 5\nC) Described as the most critical factors (a priority statement)\nD) Part of Ashtakoota\nANS: C\nWHY: It is not a score and it is not verified in a primary text.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A pending profile such as Telugu returns:\nA) PENDING_RESEARCH with no Koota results\nB) A guessed result\nC) The Tamil result\nD) An error that crashes the app\nANS: A\nWHY: The engine never fills gaps with assumptions.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many Mahendra counts out of 27 qualify?\nA) 8\nB) 9\nC) 4\nD) 13\nANS: A\nWHY: The counts 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22 and 25.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Two families follow different traditions and see different results. The correct report:\nA) Shows each result under its own profile with sources and reasons\nB) Hides the lower result\nC) Uses the higher result\nD) Averages the results\nANS: A\nWHY: Comparative reports preserve each system's original result.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which statement about the Tamil \"Rasi\" row is correct?\nA) It is a verified classical rule\nB) It is a provisional mapping to the Bhakoot classes (Level 7)\nC) It is a Nakshatra count\nD) It is identical to Rajju\nANS: B\nWHY: A verified Tamil Rasi rule was not found.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Gana, Yoni, Vasya and Rasyadhipathi in the Tamil profile use thresholds that are:\nA) Published Tamil rules\nB) From Muhurta Chintamani verses\nC) Derived by the course from its Tradition A tables (Level 7)\nD) Random\nANS: C\nWHY: They are flagged in every result as course-derived assumptions.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which of these is a Level 1 source?\nA) A software result\nB) A verifiable passage in a recognised classical text\nC) A popular website\nD) A forum post\nANS: B\nWHY: Level 1 is primary textual evidence; none is attached in this course.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which of these counts is in the explicit Dina list (2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 11, 13, 15, 18, 20, 24, 26)?\nA) 17\nB) 22\nC) 24\nD) 27\nANS: C\nWHY: 17, 22 and 27 have a qualifying remainder but are not in the list.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which factor is a pass/Dosha check without points in Tradition A?\nA) Yoni\nB) Gana\nC) Nadi\nD) Rajju\nANS: D\nWHY: Rajju and Vedha are pass/Dosha checks; the eight Kootas carry the 36 points.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: scoreConversion = NOT_SUPPORTED means:\nA) The conversion is optional\nB) The software has a bug\nC) No justified model converts one system's result into another's\nD) Only Tamil results can be converted\nANS: C\nWHY: Different lists, scales and weights make conversion unjustified.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Stree Deergha count 13 at the threshold \"> 13\":\nA) Qualifies\nB) Is a Dosha\nC) Cannot be evaluated\nD) Does not qualify\nANS: D\nWHY: 13 is not greater than 13; whether 13 qualifies is a profile decision.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. Why must a matching result always name its tradition and source?\nMA: Because the same word can hide a different rule, scale or table. Without the profile and its evidence level a reader cannot tell what was calculated, whether it is comparable with another report, or what its limits are.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Explain why \"7 of 10 Poruthams\" and \"25 of 36 Gunas\" cannot be compared.\nMA: They count different factor lists on different scales with different weights and rules. No verified or justified model converts one to the other, so each is read only within its own profile.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Calculation 3. Bride Bharani, groom Hasta. Give the Stree Deergha count, the result at \"> 13\" and \"≥ 7\", and the result after reversing orientation.\nMA: Count 12: does not qualify at \"> 13\", qualifies at \"≥ 7\". Reversed (bride Hasta, groom Bharani) the count is 17, which qualifies at \"> 13\": the factor is directional.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Research 4. How should a rule found only on one website be recorded?\nMA: With the website as a source, evidence Level 5 if a methodology is identified or Level 6 otherwise, a verification status of not verified in a primary text, and no attribution to a classical text.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Case 5. Two reports for the same pair differ on Rajju. List the checks you would make before choosing between them.\nMA: Identify each report's tradition and rule profile, compare the Rajju tables and the same-Rajju rule, check the Nakshatra and Pada inputs for boundary effects and the ayanamsha, and record both results without declaring one correct.\nLVL: Advanced\n\n## References\n| Statement | Level | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tamil ten-Porutham factor list | 5 | Several websites; primary text not verified |\n| Mahendra counts 4, 7 … 25 | 5 | Contemporary websites; variants exist |\n| Stree Deergha greater than 13 | 5 | One website; scoring statements conflict |\n| Stree Deergha at least 7 | 7 | Earlier course text; source unidentified |\n| Dina remainder rule | 5 | Websites; one search result did not confirm the exact list |\n| Dina explicit list | 6 | One blog summary |\n| Qualifying threshold 6 or 7 of 10 | 6 | Sources differ |\n| Telugu and Kannada independent tables | 7 | Not found; PENDING_RESEARCH |\n| Kerala about 23 considerations | 7 | Not confirmed |\n| Chovva variants K1, K2 | 7 | Single summaries; houses disagree |\n| Software methodologies | 5 | Not documented in the pages found |\n| Muhurta Chintamani, Prasna Marga, B. V. Raman | 7 | Leads only; no chapter, verse or page verified |\nNo textual references are verified; none are given.\n\n## Terminology\n{{c11register}}\n\n### Ashtakoota and Dashakoota compared\n| | Ashtakoota | Commonly listed Tamil ten |\n|---|---|---|\n| Factors | Varna, Vashya, Tara, Yoni, Graha Maitri, Gana, Bhakoot, Nadi | Dina, Gana, Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Yoni, Rasi, Rasyadhipathi, Vasya, Rajju, Vedhai |\n| Scale | 36 weighted points | Qualifying count out of 10 |\n| Thresholds quoted | 18 of 36 is the common reference | 6 or 7 of 10; sources differ |\n| Principal inputs | Moon Rashi and Moon Nakshatra | Moon Nakshatra and Moon Rashi |\n| Dosha logic | Nadi, Bhakoot, Gana flags | Rajju and Vedhai are described as most critical |\n\n## North Indian Ashtakoota\n### Historical and textual foundation\nAshtakoota is widely attributed in modern writing to marriage-matching passages of *Muhurta Chintamani*, and B. V. Raman wrote about marriage matching in his *Muhurtha*. These are **leads**: no chapter, verse or page was verified here, so the rules are labelled Level 5 (common published tables) and the attribution Level 7.\n### The eight Kootas\n| Koota | Maximum | Primary input |\n|---|---|---|\n| Varna | 1 | Moon Rashi |\n| Vashya | 2 | Rashi group |\n| Tara | 3 | Nakshatra |\n| Yoni | 4 | Nakshatra |\n| Graha Maitri | 5 | Moon-sign lords |\n| Gana | 6 | Nakshatra |\n| Bhakoot | 7 | Moon Rashi |\n| Nadi | 8 | Nakshatra |\n| **Total** | **36** | |\n### Scoring and interpretation\nThe commonly quoted reference of 18 of 36 is a convention. A total never overrides a Dosha and cannot replace the individual Koota results. Variant tables exist for Vashya, Yoni, Gana and Tara, and the course profile keeps them as named variants. The profile `NORTH_INDIAN_ASHTAKOOTA_BASE` reproduces the Calculation Lab on all 729 Nakshatra pairs.\n\n## South Indian Dashakoota\n### One list or a family?\nWebsites found treat \"Dashakoota\" and \"Dasha Porutham\" as a ten-factor family used in South India, and describe Tamil and Telugu practice as one family. Whether the name refers to one fixed list or several related lists is **not settled** by those sources.\n### Essential versus desirable\nSeveral sites call Rajju and Vedhai the most critical. Another says Dina, Gana, Rasi, Rajju and Yoni carry most practical weight while Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Rasi Athipathi, Vasya and Vedha count toward the total but are less often deal-breakers. These are priority statements from contemporary websites (Levels 5 and 6); different sources give different priorities, and none is presented as universal.\n\n## Tamil Pathu Porutham\n### The ten factors\n| # | Porutham | Input | Rule used by the profile | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Dina | Nakshatra | Count bride to groom; remainder 2, 4, 6, 8, 0 after dividing by 9 | Level 5 |\n| 2 | Gana | Nakshatra | Course Gana matrix ≥ 5 of 6 | Level 7 (derived) |\n| 3 | Mahendra | Nakshatra | Count 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25 | Level 5 |\n| 4 | Stree Deergha | Nakshatra | Count greater than 13 | Level 5 |\n| 5 | Yoni | Nakshatra | Course Yoni matrix ≥ 2 of 4 | Level 7 (derived) |\n| 6 | Rasi | Moon Rashi | Provisional mapping to Bhakoot classes | Level 7 |\n| 7 | Rasyadhipathi | Moon-sign lords | Graha Maitri score ≥ 3 of 5 | Level 7 (derived) |\n| 8 | Vasya | Rashi group | Course Vashya matrix ≥ 1 of 2 | Level 7 (derived) |\n| 9 | Rajju | Nakshatra | Different Rajju groups | Level 5 |\n| 10 | Vedhai | Nakshatra | Not a listed Vedha pair | Level 7 (course list) |\n### Scoring and priority\nThe result is the **count of qualifying Poruthams** and flags for the quoted thresholds (at least 6, at least 7). Rajju and Vedhai both qualifying is shown as an optional priority check. Weights are not asserted. No verdict is issued because the sources differ.\n### Single-switch variants\n`TAMIL_PATHU_PORUTHAM_PROFILE_A_DINA_LIST` changes only Dina; `..._SD7` changes only Stree Deergha. Each variant has one documented difference, so a result that changes can be traced to one rule.\n\n## Telugu Dasha Koota\nThe searches did not find a source that gives Telugu rule tables independent of the Tamil list; one site describes the Tamil and Telugu ten-factor system as one family, and another says Telugu families also emphasise caste and gotra alignment alongside horoscope matching, without saying this changes the Koota factors.\n**Status: PENDING_RESEARCH.** Regional practice documented, but a sufficiently verified independent rule profile is not yet established. Questions to research: which factors are included; whether Varna, Nadi or Gotra considerations enter; whether Rajju is mandatory; whether Vedha is included; counting directions and thresholds.\n\n## Kannada Dasha Koota\nNo source separating Kannada tables from the Tamil list was found. **Status: PENDING_RESEARCH.** Do not attribute a general South Indian rule to Kannada practice without a specific source. Open items: Nakshatra grouping tables, Rajju tables, Vedha pairs, Dina remainders, Mahendra and Stree Deergha thresholds, and Papasamyam practices.\n\n## Kerala Compatibility\n### What was found\nWebsites describe Kerala matching as Dasavidha Porutham with separate Rajju, Chovva (Mars) and Papasamyam checks. A Malayalam-language page is summarised as checking the Lagna and the 7th and 8th houses; an uploaded document of unknown authorship is summarised as counting malefics in houses 1, 2, 4, 7, 8 and 12 from Lagna, Moon and Venus. The two disagree, so they are kept as variants **K1** and **K2** (Level 7).\n### The \"23 considerations\" claim\nNot confirmed. It may reflect one astrologer's or software's counting; it is **not** a Kerala standard.\n### What the app does\nIt offers an **input-based** Chovva check: you supply Mars houses; it reports present or absent for each chart and a comparison status, and applies no balancing rule and no numeric Papasamyam value. *Prasna Marga* is listed as a lead only.\n\n{{c11kerala}}\n\n## Other Regions\nAndhra and Telangana variations, Tulu and coastal Karnataka, Maharashtra, Bengal, Gujarat, Himalayan and community-specific practices may exist. A profile needs identifiable evidence; a website claiming \"this is used in state X\" is not enough. For each region this course records: **Regional practice documented, but a sufficiently verified independent rule profile is not yet established.** Add a row to the register only when you can fill the actual name, textual or geographic association, factors, calculation, scoring, Dosha rules, source and evidence level.\n\n## Software Matching\n### How software changed matching\nAutomatic Moon and Nakshatra calculation, predefined tables, standardised scores, automatic Dosha flags, regional profile selectors and generated explanations make matching fast and hide the rule choices.\n### What was found\n| Product | What its pages show | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prokerala | A secondary article says users can choose Kerala, Tamil or Guna Milan systems | Methodology not documented in the pages found |\n| Drik Panchang | A matching form with an ayanamsha selector, stating Ashta Kuta | Methodology not documented there |\n| Jagannatha Hora (site article) | The site states its matching tool uses Lahiri sidereal longitudes | Vendor's own statement, not independent |\nEach is labelled **implementation not independently verified**. A displayed result is not proof of the rule used.\n### Why two programs differ\nAstronomical layer: ayanamsha, Moon longitude, birth-time rounding, time zone and daylight-saving handling, boundary precision. Rule layer: different tables, counting directions, thresholds, exception settings, scoring policies. Separate the two before judging a result.\n\n{{c11audit}}\n\n## Koota Matrices\n### Master comparative matrix\nRows exist for each Koota in each profile that has data, with the eighteen required columns. Telugu, Kannada and Kerala rows show PENDING_RESEARCH.\n\n{{c11master}}\n\n### Inclusion matrix\n\n{{c11incl}}\n\n## Scoring Comparison\n{{c11scoring}}\n\n## Directional Counting\n{{c11direction}}\n\n## Exception Comparison\nThe exception register is shown in the Exceptions tab. Every entry is Level 6 or 7 or PENDING_RESEARCH; none is applied automatically. A raw result is never overwritten, and any restoration would need a profile that defines it.\n\n| Case | What differs |\n|---|---|\n| Nadi, same Nakshatra, different Rashi | A claim attributed in modern sources to *Muhurta Chintamani*; verse numbering and scope not verified |\n| Nadi, same Rashi, different Nakshatra | Mentioned in some manuals; unverified |\n| Nadi, same Nakshatra, different Pada | Disputed |\n| Bhakoot, same or friendly lords | Mentioned in some manuals; unverified |\n| Rajju, Vedha regional relaxations | Exist in some manuals; no rule data attached |\n| Chovva balancing | Source-specific; method not verified |\n\n## Comparative Engine\nChoose the two horoscopes and the profiles; the same inputs go to every profile.\n\n{{c11compare}}\n\n## Comparative Cases\nTwenty-five cases are listed below. Each pair was **searched by the engines** against the stated condition, then compared under North Indian, Tradition A and Tamil A. Where a condition cannot be met in the course data, or needs chart data the app lacks, the case says so.\n\n| # | Category |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | High Ashtakoota total and few qualifying Poruthams |\n| 2 | Low Ashtakoota total and many qualifying Poruthams |\n| 3 | Nadi Dosha in Ashtakoota; the Tamil list has no Nadi |\n| 4 | Rajju Dosha under the Tamil profile with an Ashtakoota total of at least 18 |\n| 5 | Mahendra qualifies, Stree Deergha does not |\n| 6 | Dina qualifies, Rajju does not |\n| 7 | Rajju-favourable with Vedhai-adverse: impossible in the course data (documented gap) |\n| 8 | Rasi qualifies, Rasyadhipathi does not |\n| 9 | Gana qualifies, Yoni does not |\n| 10 | Orientation reversed changes a result |\n| 11 | Threshold (> 13 versus ≥ 7) changes a result |\n| 12 | Nadi Dosha with candidate exceptions (unverified, none applied) |\n| 13 | Rajju table: one table only in the course data |\n| 14 | Yoni table (Profile A versus earlier table) |\n| 15 | Vashya table (Profile A versus earlier table) |\n| 16 | Nadi: candidate cancellation against a profile with no Nadi |\n| 17 | Bhakoot Dosha with candidate same or friendly lord exception |\n| 18 | Papasamyam: illustrative chart inputs |\n| 19 | Chovva: K1 and K2 disagree |\n| 20 | Same Nakshatra, different Pada |\n| 21 | Same Rashi, different Nakshatra |\n| 22 | Same Nakshatra, different Rashi |\n| 23 | Adverse Koota matching; chart strength is a separate layer (discussion) |\n| 24 | Favourable Koota matching; chart concerns are a separate layer (discussion) |\n| 25 | Two families, two traditions, one pair |\n\n{{c11cases}}\n\n## Source Classification\n### Research method\nFor each source record: author, title, edition, publisher, year, chapter or section, language, translation, the exact rule, and any conflicting commentary. Give a verse or page **only if verified**. Prefer primary and commentary sources; treat modern websites and software documentation as evidence of current implementation, not of ancient authority. When two sources disagree, quote or summarise each rule, give dates and traditions, explain whether the difference is textual, regional or interpretative, keep both as profiles, and do not decide without sufficient evidence.\n### Register\nThe research register in the Terminology tab lists 18 rules with sources and levels. Every enabled software rule links to one of these records.\n\n## Software Specification\n### Architecture\n| Component | Role |\n|---|---|\n| MatchingTraditionRegistry | Lists profiles with status, version, region, evidence |\n| TraditionProfileEngine | Validates a profile (sources, Koota plug-ins, scoring consistency) |\n| KootaRuleRegistry | Independent Koota plug-ins |\n| KootaCalculationEngine | Runs one profile on two persons |\n| ScoringProfileEngine | Weighted total, qualifying count, qualitative; conversion NOT_SUPPORTED |\n| ExceptionRuleEngine | Candidates; explicit, acknowledged activation only |\n| SourceEvidenceRegistry | Source records and the research register |\n| ComparativeMatchingEngine | Same inputs to many profiles; differences and reasons |\n| MatchingReportEngine | Side-by-side report with the mandatory disclaimer |\n### Koota plug-in contract\n```\nKootaPlugin = {\n  kootaId, concept, name, kind,\n  input, direction,\n  def(cfg) -> {input, direction,\n               scale, rule},\n  calc(bride, groom, cfg) ->\n    {raw, max, status, label,\n     detail, evidence, sources}\n}\n```\n### Profile JSON (example)\n```\n{\n \"id\": \"TAMIL_PATHU_PORUTHAM_PROFILE_A\",\n \"status\": \"PROVISIONAL\",\n \"evidence\": 5,\n \"koota\": [\"dina\", \"gana_t\",\n   \"mahendra\", \"stree_deergha\",\n   \"yoni_t\", \"rasi\",\n   \"rasyadhipathi\", \"vasya\",\n   \"rajju_t\", \"vedha_t\"],\n \"excluded\": [\"varna\", \"nadi\"],\n \"cfg\": {\"dina\": \"rem9\",\n   \"sd\": {\"op\": \"gt\", \"n\": 13}},\n \"scoring\": {\"type\": \"count\",\n   \"max\": 10,\n   \"thresholds\": [6, 7]}\n}\n```\n### Pending profile\n```\n{\n \"id\": \"TELUGU_DASHAKOOTA_PROFILE_A\",\n \"status\": \"PENDING_RESEARCH\",\n \"koota\": null\n}\n```\n### Comparison output\n```\n{\n \"module\": \"COMPARATIVE_MATCHING\",\n \"traditionResults\": [],\n \"comparison\": {\n   \"differences\": [],\n   \"explanations\": [],\n   \"scoreConversion\":\n     \"NOT_SUPPORTED\"\n }\n}\n```\n### Exception record\n```\n{\n \"id\": \"NADI_SAME_NAKSHATRA_DIFF_RASHI\",\n \"koota\": \"nadi\",\n \"evidence\": 7,\n \"status\": \"UNVERIFIED\",\n \"activation\": \"explicit only\",\n \"rawResult\": \"never overwritten\"\n}\n```\n\n### Errors handled\nMissing or invalid Nakshatra or Pada; Nakshatra/Pada inconsistent with the Rashi; missing Koota plug-in; missing source record; conflicting scoring configuration; unsupported tradition; unverified or incomplete exception data; missing chart input. The engine never substitutes one regional rule for another.\n### Tests\nThe Validation tab runs more than 70 tests: tradition selection, counting, directions, thresholds, sources, comparison and boundary cases.\n\n## Exercises\n{{c11exercise}}\n\n## Final Assessment\n{{c11final}}\n\n## Common Mistakes\n1. Treating Ashtakoota and Dashakoota as identical.\n2. Assuming all ten-factor systems share the same ten.\n3. Treating Tamil Pathu Porutham as identical to every Telugu system.\n4. Treating Telugu and Kannada systems as identical.\n5. Treating Kerala practice as the Tamil profile with a different name.\n6. Assuming Varna belongs to every ten-factor list.\n7. Assuming Nadi belongs to every ten-Porutham list.\n8. Assuming Rajju is part of Ashtakoota.\n9. Assuming Vedha is part of Ashtakoota.\n10. Converting ten Poruthams into 36 points.\n11. Treating all ten factors as equally weighted.\n12. Ignoring a mandatory-factor or priority statement in the selected source.\n13. Ignoring veto-style statements about Rajju and Vedhai.\n14. Reversing bride and groom in directional counting.\n15. Applying one Dina remainder rule to every source.\n16. Using one universal Mahendra calculation without checking the direction.\n17. Applying one universal Stree Deergha threshold.\n18. Using the wrong Rajju table.\n19. Using an unverified Vedha pair list.\n20. Treating Nadi Dosha as a universal regional rule.\n21. Treating Bhakoot Dosha as equivalent to Rasi Porutham.\n22. Treating Rasyadhipathi as identical to Graha Maitri in every context.\n23. Applying Ashtakoota cancellation rules to Porutham.\n24. Treating a regional exception as a classical universal rule.\n25. Confusing score with qualitative compatibility.\n26. Assuming all software applications use the same calculation engine.\n27. Ignoring ayanamsha and boundary differences.\n28. Failing to disclose software rule settings.\n29. Treating one astrologer's custom as a whole region's tradition.\n30. Giving a definitive marriage verdict from one matching score.\n\n## Instructor Guide\n**Guiding principle:** *Never teach a regional system by borrowing a convenient rule from another system. First identify the tradition, then identify its source, then calculate according to that rule.*\n### 120-minute plan\n| Time | Section | Activity |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0–10 | Introduction to comparative schools | Show one pair under two profiles; ask why results differ |\n| 10–25 | North Indian Ashtakoota | Whiteboard the eight Kootas; scores never override Dosha |\n| 25–40 | South Indian Dashakoota | The ten are not the eight plus two; priority statements |\n| 40–55 | Tamil Pathu Porutham | Run the calculator; Dina remainder versus list |\n| 55–70 | Telugu and Kannada approaches | Show PENDING_RESEARCH; discuss what evidence would change it |\n| 70–85 | Kerala compatibility | Chovva K1 and K2; the unconfirmed 23-factor claim |\n| 85–95 | Software matching | Audit checklist; astronomical versus rule layer |\n| 95–105 | Comparative case studies | Pairs 5, 10, 11, 22 |\n| 105–120 | Research methodology and recap | Evidence levels; write a difference statement |\n### Misconceptions\nTen equals ten; one total fits all; a website equals a text; software output equals a rule; a favourable factor cancels another.\n### Discussion questions\nWhat would count as Level 3 evidence for a Kannada table? Why is Mahendra the same in both orientations? When is it honest to leave a row empty?\n### Homework\nComplete the 20 exercises; add one new regional row to the gap register with real evidence or mark it PENDING_RESEARCH.\n### Assessment criteria\nNames the profile; shows the rule; keeps scales separate; grades evidence; avoids conversion and winners.\n\n## Report\n{{c11report}}\n\n## Validation\n{{c11tests}}\n","12":"# Day 12 – Doshas, Exceptions and Cancellation Principles (दोष, अपवाद और परिहार)\n\n## Learn\n\n### Today's learning question\n> When one or more Kootas show a Dosha (दोष), how do I identify it, test every exception honestly, and still avoid turning \"a good score somewhere else\" into a shortcut that quietly erases a problem?\n\n### What this day is\nDays 3 to 11 gave you the individual Kootas and the regional systems. Day 12 is the **decision-making and synthesis module**. It teaches one discipline: a Dosha is *identified*, *classified*, *weighed*, *tested against its own exception rules*, and only then *interpreted* inside the complete horoscope.\n\n> **Identify before interpreting. Verify before cancelling. Examine the whole horoscope before concluding.**\n\n### Learning objectives\nBy the end of this day you can:\n- Identify the Dosha flag of each Koota and say whether it is a score deficiency, a recognised Dosha, or both.\n- Explain how a Dosha is formed and why a score deficiency is not the same thing as a separate Dosha interpretation.\n- Run the five-stage Dosha examination and explain why the software refuses to skip a stage.\n- Assess severity with the educational Level 0–5 framework and record it before and after exceptions.\n- Read a cancellation-rule record, test each of its conditions, and say whether it completely cancels, partly cancels, only mitigates, or is disputed.\n- Keep the **raw score**, the **Dosha status**, the **cancellation status** and the **adjusted score** in four separate fields.\n- Recognise double counting, overlap and independent corroboration when several Doshas appear together.\n- Compare profiles and schools side by side without averaging or merging them.\n- Move from Koota results to an honest, five-section compatibility report, without exaggerated language.\n\n### The seven conditions a Dosha can be in\nA Dosha is not simply \"there\" or \"cancelled\". Keep these seven conditions apart:\n| # | Condition | What it means |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Absent | The flag is not raised under the selected profile |\n| 2 | Present but weak | Raised, but only partly (for example a partial Tara score) |\n| 3 | Partially mitigated | A rule reduces its weight; the Dosha is still identifiable |\n| 4 | Cancelled under a recognised rule | A verified rule (class A to C) removes it. **No rule in this course reaches that level.** |\n| 5 | Cancelled only under a particular school | A school's unverified rule removes it; other schools disagree |\n| 6 | Unresolved | Identified, no rule applied, no rule fully tested |\n| 7 | Interpreted differently | Another tradition does not read the same combination as a Dosha |\n\n> Condition 4 cannot occur in this course because no cancellation rule found in the research is verified from a primary text. The engine can show condition 5, never condition 4.\n\n### The five meanings of the word \"Dosha\"\n| Label | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | A mathematical scoring deficiency | Varna 0 of 1 |\n| B | A traditional astrological incompatibility condition | Same Nadi |\n| C | A qualitative interpretative warning | Yoni enemy pair, read symbolically |\n| D | A condition that requires additional horoscope examination | Bhakoot 6/8 |\n| E | A condition a particular school treats as a veto or mandatory test | Rajju or Vedha in some regional systems |\nThe engine tags every Koota with the labels that apply (Varna: A; Nadi: A, B, D; Rajju: B, E).\n\n{{x12dash}}\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### 12.1 What is a Dosha (दोष)?\nIn classical Sanskrit, *Dosha* means a fault, a defect or an impurity. In compatibility analysis it names a **flagged unfavourable result in one Koota under a chosen profile**. Careful students separate five related words:\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Dosha (दोष) | The flagged unfavourable condition |\n| Dosha Bhanga (दोष भंग) | The \"breaking\" of a Dosha by a stated condition |\n| Parihara (परिहार) | A remedy or exception that sets the Dosha aside under stated conditions |\n| Shamana (शमन) | Pacifying or reducing the effect |\n| Mitigation | Other factors that support the assessment while the Dosha stays identifiable |\nA Dosha is **not** the same as an unfavourable score (a low number is a number), **not** a general weakness, and **not** a prohibition. Different texts and traditions give the same combination different weight, so every statement about a Dosha must name its profile.\n\n### 12.2 Dosha flags in Tradition A\n| Koota | Max | Basis | What raises a flag in this course |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Varna (वर्ण) | 1 | Rashi | No flag: only a score deficiency (0 of 1) |\n| Vashya (वश्य) | 2 | Rashi | No flag: only a score deficiency |\n| Tara (तारा) | 3 | Nakshatra | One direction in Tara 3, 5 or 7 (partial score) |\n| Yoni (योनि) | 4 | Nakshatra | Sworn-enemy pair (score 0) |\n| Graha Maitri (ग्रह मैत्री) | 5 | Moon-sign lords | No flag: only a score deficiency |\n| Gana (गण) | 6 | Nakshatra | Score 0 or 1 |\n| Bhakoot (भकूट) | 7 | Moon sign | 2/12, 5/9 or 6/8 |\n| Nadi (नाड़ी) | 8 | Nakshatra | Same Nadi |\n| Rajju (रज्जु) | – | Nakshatra | Same Rajju group (pass/Dosha, no points) |\n| Vedha (वेध) | – | Nakshatra pair | Listed Vedha pair (pass/Dosha, no points) |\n\n### 12.3 The five-stage examination\nEvery Dosha passes through the same five stages, in order.\n| Stage | Question | Output |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 Identification | Does the Dosha actually exist? | Raw scores and flags |\n| 2 Classification | What kind is it, on what basis, and which meanings (A–E) apply? | Type and basis |\n| 3 Severity | Is it isolated, repeated, intensified or weakened? | Level 0–5 (educational) |\n| 4 Exception and cancellation | Does any rule apply, and are all its conditions met? | Rule-by-rule verdicts |\n| 5 Final synthesis | What does the complete horoscope add? | Five-section report |\n**The software must not allow a jump from stage 1 straight to stage 4.** Try it below: the engine refuses.\n\n{{x12flow}}\n\n### 12.4 One Dosha or several? (individual versus cumulative)\nThink of six situations:\n1. One isolated Dosha.\n2. Two or more independent Doshas.\n3. Several Doshas that come from one underlying relationship (for example Bhakoot and a weak Graha Maitri both read the same Moon-sign pair).\n4. One Dosha seen in different analytical systems.\n5. A repeated unfavourable indication across Rashi, Nakshatra and Navamsha.\n6. Apparent repetition that is not independent evidence.\n\nThe same underlying factor must not be counted twice without a reason. The engine therefore labels each pair of Doshas as **overlapping** (one relationship, two readings), **same input, different table** (the same two Nakshatras read through two tables) or **independent** (different inputs and classifications).\n\n### 12.5 Why shortcuts are dangerous\n\"Nadi is zero but Graha Maitri is 5, so it is fine\" is a common sentence. It treats two different symbolic factors as interchangeable. It is valid only if a named school states that rule, with its conditions, and the software shows it. Even then the **raw Nadi score stays 0**.\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### 12.6 Raw, original, adjusted: four separate fields\n| Field | Meaning | Can it change? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Raw score | The Koota score from the profile | **Never** |\n| Original Dosha status | DOSHA_PRESENT or not | **Never** |\n| Cancellation status | The result of the rule examination (for example CANCELLED_UNDER_SELECTED_PROFILE) | Yes, by an applied rule |\n| Adjusted score | Exists **only** if the selected rule explicitly authorises restoration; always labelled ADJUSTED, shown beside the raw score | Yes, only then |\nIf a student writes \"Nadi 8 (cancelled)\" the engine would flag it: the raw score is 0 and the adjusted figure, if any, must be labelled.\n\n### 12.7 The cancellation-rule record\nEvery rule in the rulebook is a record with these fields:\n| Field | Required information |\n|---|---|\n| rule_id | A unique identifier such as `NADI-EX-001` |\n| dosha_type | The Dosha it concerns |\n| rule_name and tradition | The rule as circulated, and where it is attributed |\n| source_reference | text, author, chapter, verse, page, edition: **null unless independently verified** |\n| evidence_class | A to F (below) |\n| conditions and condition_logic | Each condition; ALL or ANY |\n| directional_requirement | Bride-to-groom, groom-to-bride, mutual or none |\n| cancellation_type | COMPLETE, PARTIAL, MITIGATION or DISPUTED |\n| score_restoration | Boolean, **false** by default |\n| limitations | What the rule does not cancel |\n| verification_status | VERIFIED, PARTIALLY_VERIFIED or UNVERIFIED |\nIf any required field is unknown, the rule is recorded as unverified and is **not applied** unless the practitioner explicitly acknowledges that.\n\n### 12.8 The evidence scale\n| Class | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| A | Primary textual evidence: identifiable text, verifiable wording and location |\n| B | Traditional commentary with identifiable author and edition |\n| C | A named modern authority (for example a published work) |\n| D | Regional or lineage practice without a verified primary source |\n| E | Contemporary popular rule: widely circulated, not established |\n| F | Unverified or disputed: wording, attribution or scope cannot be established |\nCrosswalk to the earlier days: Day 8, 9 and 10 grade D (contemporary web explanation) = class E here; their grade E (unsourced regional claim) = class D if a region is named with a documented practice, otherwise F; Day 11 Levels 1 to 4 = classes A to D; Levels 5 and 6 = class E; Level 7 = class F.\n**Never upgrade E or F to a classical rule because it is widely repeated.** In this course the research found no rule above class E: the rulebook holds 0 rules in classes A–C.\n\n### 12.9 Computed and practitioner conditions\nSome conditions are computed from the two Nakshatras and Padas: same Rashi, same lord, mutual friendship, Navamsha lords of the Moons, a favourable Koota. Others need data the app does not have, because it calculates only the Moon: Parivartana of the sign lords, Navamsha Lagna lords, Moon strength. These are **practitioner inputs**; until you state them, the engine reports UNKNOWN, never \"met\".\n\n### 12.10 Try the detector\nChoose a bride and groom (hypothetical people). The detector shows every Koota's raw score, its flag and its meaning labels. Nothing is cancelled at this stage.\n\n{{x12detect}}\n\n## Different Schools\n| Point | Variation |\n|---|---|\n| Which Dosha is gravest | Varies by school; no source in this course gives weights |\n| Which exceptions apply | Varies by school and region |\n| Complete or partial cancellation | Varies; the same condition is called full by one page and partial by another |\n| Whether points are restored | Software conventions differ; some restore, some keep the raw score |\n| Role of Navamsha and lords | Varies |\n| Whether Rajju is cancellable at all | Some pages list exceptions; others treat same Rajju as severe |\n\n### Teaching profiles T0 to T6 and RES\nTo let you compare how choices change the report, the engine offers **hypothetical teaching profiles**. They are *not* documented schools. No real school is named here, because no verified rule set was found.\n| Profile | What it enables |\n|---|---|\n| T0 | Record only (default): nothing applied |\n| T1 | Bhakoot same-lord exception only; status only |\n| T2 | Same-lord and mutual-friend exceptions for Bhakoot and Gana; Bhakoot points restored as an ADJUSTED figure |\n| T3 | The two Nadi Rashi/Nakshatra conditions; status only |\n| T4 | Extended Bhakoot rules, but Nadi Dosha must be absent first |\n| T5 | Gana mitigations only |\n| T6 | Mitigations and disputed candidates (Nadi lord rules, Gana same-Rashi, Rajju lord rule and Pada Rajju) |\n| RES | Research: examine everything, apply nothing |\nRegional systems (Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, Kerala) are compared in Day 11. A rule from one tradition is never silently imported into another.\n\n### Comparison mode\nSelect several profiles and compare. The tool shows the original scores, the Doshas, the accepted and rejected rules, and the final status under each profile, then lists areas of agreement and disagreement. It never produces an average.\n\n{{x12compare}}\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### 12.11 What an exception can and cannot do\n- A rule is **applied** only when (1) the selected profile enables it, (2) the practitioner has acknowledged that it is unverified, and (3) *every* required condition is met.\n- Even then, the raw score does not change. Cancellation changes the **status**. Restoration, if any, appears only as an ADJUSTED figure.\n- An exception for one Koota never extends to another Koota unless the rule itself says so. A Nadi exception is not a Rajju exception; a Bhakoot exception is not a Varna exception; Stree Deergha qualification does not cancel Rajju; Mahendra does not cancel any other Dosha.\n- A general favourable chart factor (strong seventh lord, friendly Navamsha) is **not** an automatic cancellation.\n\n### 12.12 Cancellation, mitigation, alternative interpretation, score restoration\nThese are four different concepts and are never interchangeable:\n| Concept | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Cancellation | A stated source says the Dosha does not operate under defined conditions |\n| Mitigation | The Dosha stays identifiable; other factors support or reduce its importance |\n| Alternative interpretation | Another tradition does not recognise the Dosha in the same way |\n| Score restoration | The scoring method changes the numeric result |\n\n### 12.13 Inspect a rule\nChoose a Dosha and a cancellation profile. Each rule appears as a card: its conditions (met, not met, unknown), its source and school, its evidence class, its type, and the reason it was or was not applied. There is no \"cancel all\" button.\n\n{{x12inspect}}\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example 1 – a pair with many flags (Day 14 pair 4)\nBride Krittika Pada 3 (Taurus), groom Vishakha Pada 2 (Libra); total **14.5 of 36**.\n- Bhakoot: Taurus to Libra is 6 forward and 8 reverse: **6/8 (Shadashtaka, षडाष्टक)**, raw 0. Both signs are ruled by Venus.\n- Nadi: both Antya, raw 0. Rajju: both Nabhi, Dosha. Vedha: Krittika–Vishakha is a listed pair, Dosha. Tara: partial (1.5).\n- Graha Maitri 5 (same lord), Gana 6 (both Rakshasa), Yoni 1, Varna 0, Vashya 1.\n\nRecording: \"Five Dosha flags (Tara partial, Bhakoot, Nadi, Rajju, Vedha). A same-lord exception category is identified for Bhakoot; under profile T0 it is not applied. No candidate rule is attached to Nadi, Rajju or Vedha. Raw total 14.5.\" No sentence says \"cancelled\".\n\n### Example 2 – a high total with a Nadi flag\nAshwini Pada 1 with Ashwini Pada 4: total **28 of 36**, but Nadi (same Nadi) and Rajju (same group) are both flagged. The conditions of the two circulated Nadi rules (different Nakshatra, or a split Nakshatra) are not met. Report both facts: \"Total 28 of 36; Nadi and Rajju flagged and unresolved.\" The total does not hide the flags, and the flags do not erase the total.\n\n### Example 3 – \"do not cancel by score\"\nWeak: \"Graha Maitri is 5, so the Bhakoot problem is cancelled.\" Strong: \"Graha Maitri is 5 because the lords are identical. A same-lord exception for Bhakoot is circulated by contemporary sources; no verified source is attached, so under T0 the Dosha is identified and not applied. Under T1 it would change the status only, with the raw score 0 unchanged.\"\n\n## Practice\n\n### Assignments (do at least ten)\n1. For three random pairs, list the Koota flags and say, for each, whether it is a deficiency, a Dosha or both.\n2. Count Aries–Scorpio, Taurus–Libra and Cancer–Sagittarius in both directions; confirm that each is 6/8 and the counts add to 14.\n3. Find all five same-lord sign pairs; state their categories (6/8, 6/8, 2/12, 4/10, 4/10); explain why 5/9 never occurs.\n4. Test friendship for ten planet pairs; separate mutual, one-sided and not friends.\n5. Write a rule record for NADI-EX-001 using the table in 12.7; mark unknown fields null.\n6. For Case 2, record the result under T1, T2 and T4 and explain each difference.\n7. Rewrite \"Nadi Dosha is cancelled because the Moon signs are friendly\" into an acceptable sentence.\n8. Take a favourable-Graha-Maitri pair with a Nadi flag and explain why Graha Maitri is not a cancellation.\n9. Choose one conflict from the register and present both sides without declaring a winner.\n10. Produce a full five-section report for any pair, naming the profile and stating the birth-time confidence.\n11. Audit a pair under two profiles and list which rule ID caused each difference.\n12. Explain, in four sentences, why a high total never cancels a Dosha.\n\n### Interactive exercises\n{{x12exercise}}\n\n### Answers to the written assignments (outline)\n- 2: forward 6 and reverse 8 for each pair; for any two different signs the counts add to 14, so a 6/6 pair cannot exist.\n- 3: Aries/Scorpio 6/8, Taurus/Libra 6/8, Capricorn/Aquarius 2/12, Gemini/Virgo 4/10, Sagittarius/Pisces 4/10.\n- 7 model: \"Nadi Dosha is identified. Friendly Moon-sign lords are noted as an exception category in some manuals; no verified source is attached, so it is not applied.\"\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What does \"Dosha identified\" mean?\nA: The unfavourable condition is present under the chosen profile; nothing has been cancelled. Explanation: identification is stage 1 and changes nothing.\n\nQ: Does cancellation change the raw score?\nA: No. Cancellation changes the status. Only an explicitly authorised restoration creates an ADJUSTED figure, shown beside the raw score.\n\nQ: Name the five stages of Dosha examination.\nA: Identification, classification, severity, exception and cancellation examination, final synthesis.\n\nQ: Why can the software not jump from identification to cancellation?\nA: Cancellation is meaningful only after the Dosha is classified and its severity noted; the engine raises STAGE_SKIPPED.\n\nQ: How many conditions of a Dosha does the module separate?\nA: Seven: absent, weak, partially mitigated, cancelled under a recognised rule, cancelled under one school, unresolved, interpreted differently.\n\nQ: Is a Dosha the same as an unfavourable score?\nA: No. A score is a number; a Dosha is a flagged condition with its own interpretation. Varna 0 is a deficiency, not a Dosha flag.\n\nQ: What are Dosha Bhanga and Parihara?\nA: Dosha Bhanga (दोष भंग) is the breaking of a Dosha by a stated condition; Parihara (परिहार) is the exception or remedy that sets it aside under conditions.\n\nQ: What is mitigation?\nA: The Dosha stays identifiable; other factors support the assessment or reduce its weight. It is not cancellation.\n\nQ: What is score restoration?\nA: A change to the numeric result by an explicitly enabled rule. It is not the same as cancelling the Dosha.\n\nQ: What are the six evidence classes?\nA: A primary text, B commentary, C named modern authority, D regional practice, E contemporary popular rule, F unverified or disputed.\n\nQ: How many rules in the rulebook reach class A, B or C?\nA: None. The research found only classes E and F (class D is available but unused).\n\nQ: What does score_restoration default to?\nA: false for every rule.\n\nQ: Which Kootas have no verified cancellation rule in this rulebook?\nA: Varna, Vashya, Tara, Yoni, Graha Maitri and Vedha. The engine prints: \"No sufficiently verified cancellation rule identified for this selected tradition.\"\n\nQ: How are two different signs' inclusive counts related?\nA: They add to 14. A 6/6 pair therefore cannot exist; Taurus–Libra and Cancer–Sagittarius are 6/8.\n\nQ: Which sign pairs share a lord?\nA: Aries/Scorpio, Taurus/Libra, Gemini/Virgo, Sagittarius/Pisces, Capricorn/Aquarius.\n\nQ: Which same-lord pairs fall in an adverse Bhakoot category?\nA: Aries/Scorpio and Taurus/Libra (6/8) and Capricorn/Aquarius (2/12). Gemini/Virgo and Sagittarius/Pisces are 4/10. None is 5/9.\n\nQ: What is mutual friendship?\nA: Each lord regards the other as a natural friend. Both directions must be checked.\n\nQ: What is one-sided friendship, and does it count as mutual?\nA: One lord regards the other as a friend while the reverse is not friendship. It never counts as mutual friendship.\n\nQ: Are Bhakoot and Graha Maitri independent?\nA: No. They read the same Moon-sign pair (counts and lords): overlapping, not independent confirmation.\n\nQ: Are Nadi and Rajju independent confirmations?\nA: No. They read the same two Nakshatras through different tables: \"same input, different table\".\n\nQ: What is the educational severity scale?\nA: Level 0 no Dosha, 1 minor deficiency, 2 recognised but limited, 3 with supporting concerns, 4 multiple independent indications, 5 serious unresolved concern under the selected tradition.\n\nQ: Is the severity scale classical?\nA: No. It is an educational and software framework with no numerical weights.\n\nQ: What must be recorded about severity?\nA: Severity before exceptions, after exceptions, remaining concerns, supporting evidence and confidence in the source.\n\nQ: Does a friendly Navamsha automatically cancel a Dosha?\nA: No. Supportive Navamsha is context unless a named rule says otherwise.\n\nQ: Can Dasha change a Koota score?\nA: No. Dasha is timing context and never alters a Koota score.\n\nQ: What must a manual override record?\nA: Reason, source or authority, practitioner note and date, with the original system result retained.\n\nQ: What happens to a saved report when the rulebook changes?\nA: It keeps its original result and notes the version difference; it is never silently recalculated.\n\nQ: Is there a universal pass/fail threshold?\nA: No. The module uses six descriptive reporting categories.\n\nQ: What does \"High\" mean in the decision matrix?\nA: A descriptive band for the exercise (28 or more of 36), not a pass mark.\n\nQ: Which planets does the app calculate?\nA: Only the Moon. Every other chart observation is practitioner-entered.\n\nQ: Why is Parivartana a practitioner input?\nA: It needs the positions of the two sign lords in the birth charts, which the app does not calculate.\n\nQ: What is the guiding principle of Day 12?\nA: Identify before interpreting. Verify before cancelling. Examine the whole horoscope before concluding.\n\nQ: What must a report never contain?\nA: Deterministic predictions of death, widowhood, divorce, infertility, illness or progeny.\n\nQ: Can Nadi Dosha be presented as medical evidence?\nA: Never. It is an astrological classification of the Janma Nakshatra, not a medical or genetic finding.\n\nQ: What is a teaching profile?\nA: A hypothetical rule configuration (T1–T6) used to show how choices change the report. It is not a documented school.\n\nQ: Why must the unverified acknowledgement be given?\nA: Because every rule is unverified; without acknowledgement no rule is applied, in any profile.\n\nQ: What is a conflict register for?\nA: To record a disagreement between rules or sources, the selected profile and the reason for the choice, without forcing a consensus.\n\nQ: How do you handle two cancellation rules that both apply to one Dosha?\nA: Count the effect once; two partial rules stay partial; a complete rule plus a disputed rule needs review.\n\nQ: What does the \"High total with a Dosha\" row say?\nA: Explain the unresolved Dosha: the total does not hide it and it does not erase the total.\n\nQ: Why must birth-time uncertainty be disclosed?\nA: Moon boundaries, Pada and Navamsha can change with the time, so some results may be inconclusive.\n\nQ: What is the sentence you may write after a valid-looking cancellation?\nA: \"Under profile X, rule ID Y applied: status changed; raw score unchanged; the Dosha is not removed from the record.\"\n\n## Assessment\nQ: Which statement is correct?\nA) A strong Koota automatically cancels a weak one\nB) All Doshas are equal\nC) Doshas do not exist\nD) Cancellation needs a documented rule, a school and its conditions\nANS: D\nWHY: A is the shortcut the course rejects; C and D contradict the classification and the absence of published weights.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: After any cancellation, the raw score is:\nA) Raised to the maximum\nB) Halved\nC) Deleted\nD) Unchanged\nANS: D\nWHY: Status is recorded separately; the other options all overwrite the raw score.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: \"Dosha identified\" means:\nA) Cancelled\nB) Present under the profile, nothing cancelled\nC) Not applicable\nD) Removed\nANS: B\nWHY: Identification only names the condition.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Varna 0 of 1 is:\nA) A recognised Dosha flag\nB) A veto\nC) A score deficiency without a Dosha flag\nD) A cancellation\nANS: C\nWHY: Varna carries meaning A only in this course; the other options give it a role it does not have.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which sign pair is 6/8?\nA) Aries–Cancer\nB) Aries–Leo\nC) Gemini–Libra\nD) Taurus–Libra\nANS: D\nWHY: Taurus to Libra is 6, Libra to Taurus is 8. Aries–Leo is 5/9, Gemini–Libra is 5/9, Aries–Cancer is 4/10.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: For two different signs the inclusive counts add up to:\nA) 12\nB) 13\nC) 15\nD) 14\nANS: D\nWHY: Forward plus reverse always gives 14, so 6/6 cannot exist.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which stage of the five-stage process examines exceptions?\nA) Stage 1\nB) Stage 2\nC) Stage 4\nD) Stage 3\nANS: C\nWHY: Stage 1 identifies, stage 2 classifies, stage 3 assesses severity.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which phrase would the course accept?\nA) \"Cancelled, since Graha Maitri is 5\"\nB) \"Identified; exception category noted; not applied\"\nC) \"Doesn't matter\"\nD) \"Fine because the total is high\"\nANS: B\nWHY: Only B names the Dosha and the state of the exception.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The severity levels 0 to 5 are:\nA) An educational and software framework\nB) A classical scale\nC) Weights that add up\nD) Mandatory in every school\nANS: A\nWHY: No source supplies numerical weights.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Evidence class E means:\nA) Primary text\nB) A contemporary popular rule\nC) Commentary\nD) Regional practice\nANS: B\nWHY: B is class A, C is class B, D is class D.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A rule is applied only when:\nA) Any one condition is met\nB) The profile enables it, the unverified status is acknowledged and every condition is met\nC) The total is high\nD) A website repeats it\nANS: B\nWHY: B ignores ALL logic, C and D are shortcuts.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which same-lord sign pair is 2/12?\nA) Aries/Scorpio\nB) Gemini/Virgo\nC) Capricorn/Aquarius\nD) Sagittarius/Pisces\nANS: C\nWHY: Capricorn and Aquarius are adjacent. Aries/Scorpio is 6/8; Gemini/Virgo and Sagittarius/Pisces are 4/10.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Mercury regards the Sun as a friend and the Sun regards Mercury as neutral. This is:\nA) Mutual friendship\nB) Same lord\nC) Enmity\nD) One-sided friendship\nANS: D\nWHY: Only Mercury's attitude is friendly; mutual friendship needs both.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Moon regards Mercury as a friend; Mercury regards the Moon as an enemy. Mutual friends?\nA) Yes\nB) No: one-sided\nC) Same lord\nD) Not defined\nANS: B\nWHY: Mercury's attitude is enmity, so the friendship is one-sided.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Bhakoot and Graha Maitri together are best described as:\nA) Two readings of one Moon-sign relationship\nB) Independent confirmations\nC) The same Koota\nD) Unrelated\nANS: A\nWHY: Both read the sign pair and its lords; counting both as separate misfortunes double-counts.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Nadi and Rajju both flagged for Ashwini and Ashwini. This is:\nA) Same input, different tables\nB) Independent confirmation\nC) Cancelled\nD) A Bhakoot effect\nANS: A\nWHY: They re-read the same two Nakshatras; this is not independent confirmation.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Under a profile that enables BHAK-EX-002 with restoration, the report must show:\nA) The raw score and the labelled ADJUSTED score\nB) Only the adjusted score\nC) Nothing\nD) An average of both\nANS: A\nWHY: The raw score is never replaced; averaging is never done.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which rule is practitioner-input only?\nA) BHAK-EX-001\nB) GANA-EX-003\nC) NADI-EX-001\nD) BHAK-EX-005 (Parivartana)\nANS: D\nWHY: Parivartana needs the sign lords' positions in the charts; the other conditions are computed.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which Kootas have no cancellation rule in the rulebook?\nA) Varna, Vashya, Tara, Yoni, Graha Maitri and Vedha\nB) Nadi and Bhakoot\nC) Gana only\nD) Rajju only\nANS: A\nWHY: Nadi, Bhakoot, Gana and Rajju have candidate rules; the others do not.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: For a Yoni enemy pair the engine reports:\nA) Cancelled by Graha Maitri\nB) Cancelled by Gana\nC) The exact \"no sufficiently verified cancellation rule\" statement\nD) A restored score\nANS: C\nWHY: No rule is held for Yoni; other favourable Kootas are context.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A high total (28) with Nadi and Rajju flagged should be reported as:\nA) Excellent: no concerns\nB) Average of 28 and 0\nC) Rejected\nD) Total 28 with two unresolved flags\nANS: D\nWHY: The report states both; A hides the flags, C is a verdict, D is an average.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Evidence class D is:\nA) Regional or lineage practice without a verified primary source\nB) Primary text\nC) Software documentation\nD) Commentary\nANS: A\nWHY: Software documentation is contemporary (class E); primary text is A; commentary is B.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: How many rules in the rulebook reach evidence class A, B or C?\nA) Five\nB) One\nC) None\nD) All\nANS: C\nWHY: The research found only contemporary and unverified claims (class E or F); B, C and D overstate the evidence.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which Kootas are score deficiencies only (no Dosha flag) in Tradition A?\nA) Nadi and Bhakoot\nB) Varna, Vashya and Graha Maitri\nC) Yoni and Gana\nD) Rajju and Vedha\nANS: B\nWHY: Nadi, Bhakoot, Yoni, Gana, Tara, Rajju and Vedha can raise a flag; Varna, Vashya and Graha Maitri cannot.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Cancellation versus mitigation: which is correct?\nA) Mitigation removes the Dosha\nB) They are identical\nC) Mitigation keeps the Dosha identifiable; cancellation says it does not operate\nD) Mitigation restores points\nANS: C\nWHY: A and D give mitigation powers it lacks; C ignores the distinction.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Two PARTIAL rules both apply to one Dosha. The status is:\nA) Complete cancellation\nB) Partially cancelled\nC) Identified only\nD) Restored\nANS: B\nWHY: Partial effects do not add up to a complete one.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A COMPLETE rule and a DISPUTED rule both apply to one Dosha. The engine returns:\nA) CANCELLED\nB) CONFLICT_REVIEW_REQUIRED\nC) MITIGATED\nD) IDENTIFIED_NOT_APPLIED\nANS: B\nWHY: Opposing claims need human review; picking one silently would hide the disagreement.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: In Case 2 (Ashwini P1 with Purva Phalguni P1), T2 gives total 24 raw and:\nA) 31 ADJUSTED, with 24 raw shown\nB) 24 only\nC) 36\nD) 17\nANS: A\nWHY: T2 restores the 7 Bhakoot points as an adjusted figure; the raw total remains 24.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: In Case 2, T4 produces:\nA) The same adjusted total as T2\nB) Nothing\nC) Cancelled status, no restored points, because T4 does not restore\nD) Nadi cancelled\nANS: C\nWHY: T4 applies the rule (Nadi absent) but has no restoration list.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: In Case 10 three teaching profiles disagree on the same Bhakoot Dosha. The correct presentation is:\nA) Show all three, name the rule IDs, and do not call one universal\nB) Choose the highest score\nC) Average them\nD) Hide two\nANS: A\nWHY: The disagreement is the finding.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Why does T4 block BHAK rules in Case 6?\nA) The profile requires Nadi Dosha to be absent, and the pair has same Nadi\nB) Bhakoot is absent\nC) The pair is 5/9\nD) Rules are verified\nANS: A\nWHY: Krittika and Vishakha are both Antya; the prerequisite is not met.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A friendly Moon Navamsha lord in a Gana case should be reported as:\nA) A supplementary observation unless a rule uses it\nB) A cancellation\nC) A restoration\nD) A Dosha\nANS: A\nWHY: No Gana rule in the rulebook uses the Moon Navamsha; GANA-EX-006 needs Navamsha Lagna lords.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which statement about Rajju is supported by the research?\nA) Same Rajju is always cancelled by friendly lords\nB) Sources disagree: some list lord exceptions, others call same Rajju non-cancellable\nC) Rajju adds eight points\nD) Rajju is a medical test\nANS: B\nWHY: The disagreement is recorded as conflict C04; A and C are false, D is an error.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Dasha may be used to:\nA) Alter the Koota score\nB) Cancel Nadi Dosha\nC) Give timing context in the horoscope layer\nD) Restore points\nANS: C\nWHY: Dasha is context only and never alters a Koota score.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: What does the engine do with a saved report when the rulebook version changes?\nA) Recalculate silently\nB) Keep the original result and note the version difference\nC) Delete it\nD) Merge\nANS: B\nWHY: Silent recalculation would change a past report.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which is a valid reason to select a rule profile?\nA) It gives the highest total\nB) It is the default in some software\nC) It matches the school the practitioner states and discloses\nD) It is shortest\nANS: C\nWHY: The profile must be chosen for its source and disclosed; A, C, D are not reasons.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Nadi Dosha in a report:\nA) May be described as a genetic risk\nB) Is a health test\nC) Predicts infertility\nD) Is an astrological classification, not a medical or genetic diagnosis\nANS: D\nWHY: The Nadi statement forbids A, C and D.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Case 6 (Krittika P3 with Vishakha P2) has five flags and severity level 5 before exceptions. Why?\nA) Level 5 is automatic for Day 14 pairs\nB) Varna is 0\nC) The total is below 18\nD) Three or more unresolved Doshas\nANS: D\nWHY: The framework assigns level 5 to three or more unresolved Doshas that are not just one overlapping relationship. B and C are true of the pair but are not the criterion; D is invented.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which of these does the module NOT define?\nA) Six reporting categories\nB) A Level 0 to 5 severity framework\nC) A universal pass/fail threshold\nD) A decision matrix\nANS: C\nWHY: The module deliberately defines no universal pass/fail threshold; the other three exist.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: In Case 9 (Ashwini P1 with Magha P1), T2 shows an ADJUSTED total of 27 from a raw total of 20. Which change supplies the 7 points?\nA) Gana\nB) Nadi\nC) Rajju\nD) Bhakoot, restored by BHAK-EX-002\nANS: D\nWHY: T2's restoration list contains only the Bhakoot rules. The Gana rule changes status only, Rajju carries no points, and Nadi is already 8.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. Distinguish cancellation, mitigation, alternative interpretation and score restoration in one sentence each.\nMA: Cancellation says the Dosha does not operate under stated conditions; mitigation leaves it identifiable but supported; alternative interpretation means another tradition does not recognise it that way; score restoration changes the numeric result and only under an explicit rule.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Why can a high total not cancel a Dosha?\nMA: The total sums different symbolic factors; a Dosha is a separate condition with its own rules. Only a documented rule with its conditions can change its status.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Calculation 1. Taurus (bride) and Libra (groom): give the counts, category and same-lord status.\nMA: Forward 6 and reverse 8: 6/8 Shadashtaka, raw 0. Both signs have Venus as lord, so the same-lord exception category is identified but not applied unless a profile enables it.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Interpretation 1. Explain why Nadi and Rajju flags on the same pair are not independent confirmation.\nMA: Both come from the same two Nakshatras read through two classification tables. The engine reports \"same input, different table\".\nLVL: Advanced\n\n## References\n| Statement | Grade (Day 12 scale) | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dosha flag conditions in Tradition A | C | Common manual conventions (grade C/D in Days 3–10) |\n| Same-lord and mutual-friend claims for Bhakoot and Gana | E | Contemporary web pages and software documentation |\n| Nadi same-Rashi / same-Nakshatra conditions | E / F | Attributed to the Muhurta Chintamani by modern pages; one page names the Jyotirnibandha; wording, edition and verse **not verified** |\n| Rajju lord and specific-Nakshatra claims | E / F | Conflicting pages and an anonymous upload |\n| Four-field recording model; severity levels 0–5; teaching profiles | n/a | Course design |\n| Natural friendship table | C | Standard seven-planet table |\nNo verse, chapter or page is given anywhere in this module. See the Bibliography tab for the source register and the research leads that still require verification.\n\n## Individual Koota Doshas\n\n### Using the rulebook\nFor each Koota the table gives: definition, calculation, scoring, what raises the flag, interpretation, whether it is a deficiency or a Dosha, exceptions and sources, and the software requirement.\n\n### Varna (वर्ण)\n| Item | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Definition | One of four symbolic classes assigned to the Moon sign: Brahmana, Kshatriya, Vaishya, Shudra |\n| Calculation | Course table by sign element; the groom's class is compared with the bride's (directional) |\n| Score | 1 if the groom's class is equal or higher, otherwise 0 |\n| Flag | Deficiency (meaning A) only |\n| Exceptions | None verified. Contemporary pages mention Rashi-lord friendship; no source was located |\n| Cancellation status | No sufficiently verified cancellation rule identified for this selected tradition. |\n| Caution | Varna is a symbolic matching label. It must never be used to infer caste, social worth, intelligence or capability |\n\n### Vashya (वश्य)\n| Item | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Calculation | Five Rashi groups (Chatushpada, Manava, Jalachara, Vanachara, Keeta); Profile A matrix; Sagittarius and Capricorn depend on the degree |\n| Score | 2, 1, 0.5 or 0 |\n| Flag | Deficiency only; Vashya itself has no standard Dosha |\n| Exceptions | None verified |\n| Software | State the active Vashya profile (A or A0) |\n\n### Tara (तारा)\n| Item | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Calculation | Count from each Nakshatra to the other, divide by 9; remainder 3, 5 or 7 is unfavourable; zero is read as 9 |\n| Score | 3 (both directions favourable), 1.5 (one direction unfavourable), never 0 for different Nakshatras |\n| Flag | Raised when the score is below 3 |\n| Exceptions | None verified |\n| Note | The compatibility Tara is not the same as Tara Bala used in predictive timing |\n\n### Yoni (योनि)\n| Item | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Calculation | Fourteen Yoni symbols by Nakshatra; Profile A is directional (bride row, groom column) |\n| Score | 4, 3, 2, 1 or 0 |\n| Flag | Raised for the seven sworn-enemy pairs (score 0) |\n| Exceptions | None verified. Other favourable Kootas are context, not cancellation |\n| Caution | Animal symbols are classification labels, not statements about behaviour, sexuality or fertility |\n\n### Graha Maitri (ग्रह मैत्री)\n| Item | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Calculation | Moon-sign lords and their natural friendship, both directions |\n| Score | Same lord 5; friend–friend 5; friend–neutral 4; neutral–neutral 3; friend–enemy 1; neutral–enemy 0.5; enemy–enemy 0 |\n| Flag | Deficiency only |\n| Role | Mitigation candidate for some Doshas; never an automatic cancellation |\n\n### Gana (गण)\n| Item | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Calculation | Deva, Manushya, Rakshasa by Nakshatra; Profile A matrix (bride row, groom column) |\n| Score | 6, 5, 1 or 0 |\n| Flag | Raised when the score is 0 or 1 |\n| Candidate exceptions | Same lords, mutual friends, favourable Graha Maitri or Bhakoot, same Rashi with different Nakshatras, Navamsha Lagna lords, Tara |\n| Status | All class E or F; none verified; they must not be fused into one formula |\n\n### Bhakoot (भकूट)\n| Item | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Calculation | Inclusive counts between the Moon signs in both directions |\n| Score | 7 for 1/1, 3/11, 4/10, 7/7; 0 for 2/12, 5/9, 6/8 |\n| Flag | Raised for 2/12, 5/9, 6/8 |\n| Candidate exceptions | Same lord, mutual friends, friend and neutral, one-sided friendship, Parivartana, Navamsha lords, Vashya and Tara, Nadi prerequisite |\n| Scope | Not established whether a rule covers all three adverse categories: test each pair separately |\n\n### Nadi (नाड़ी)\n| Item | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Calculation | Adi, Madhya, Antya by Nakshatra (nine each) |\n| Score | 8 if different, 0 if the same |\n| Flag | Raised for the same Nadi |\n| Candidate exceptions | Same Rashi with different Nakshatra, same Nakshatra with different Rashi, same Nakshatra with different Pada, lord rules, Navamsha lords, a listed-Nakshatra exemption |\n| Caution | Nadi here is an astrological classification, not a medical or genetic test |\n\n### Rajju (रज्जु) and Vedha (वेध)\n| Item | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Rajju | Five groups (Pada, Kati, Nabhi, Kantha, Siro) with 6/6/6/6/3 Nakshatras; same group is a Dosha; no points |\n| Vedha | A listed pair of Nakshatras; Dosha; no points |\n| Exceptions | Rajju: circulated lord, same-Rashi, specific-Nakshatra claims with conflicting evidence; Vedha: none verified |\n| Caution | No prediction of death, widowhood or harm may be made |\n\n## Severity Framework\n\n### Is every Dosha equally serious?\nA zero in a Koota with 8 points does not necessarily carry the same interpretative importance as a zero in a Koota with 1 point. Reasons differ: numerical weight, traditional importance, whether a school treats the factor as mandatory or as a veto, whether it has its own interpretation, whether it is repeated in the wider horoscope. **No source supplies numerical severity weights, and the course does not invent them.**\n\n### Comparative table\n| Koota | Max | Unfavourable condition | Independent Dosha status | Further examination |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Varna | 1 | 0 | No (deficiency) | Optional |\n| Vashya | 2 | 0 or 0.5 | No (deficiency) | Optional |\n| Tara | 3 | Below 3 | Yes (partial) | Yes |\n| Yoni | 4 | Enemy pair | Yes | Yes |\n| Graha Maitri | 5 | Low | No (deficiency) | Yes, if it overlaps Bhakoot |\n| Gana | 6 | 0 or 1 | Yes | Yes |\n| Bhakoot | 7 | 2/12, 5/9, 6/8 | Yes | Yes |\n| Nadi | 8 | Same Nadi | Yes | Yes |\n| Rajju | – | Same group | Yes (pass/Dosha) | Yes; veto in some schools |\n| Vedha | – | Listed pair | Yes (pass/Dosha) | Yes; veto in some schools |\n\n### A transparent severity scale (educational framework)\n| Level | Name | Criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | No Dosha identified | No flag and every scored Koota at maximum |\n| 1 | Minor score deficiency | No flag, but some scored Koota is below its maximum |\n| 2 | Recognised but limited Dosha | One flag, isolated, or all flagged Doshas limited by an applied mitigation |\n| 3 | Dosha with additional supporting concerns | One unresolved Dosha plus another Koota scoring zero, or two overlapping Doshas |\n| 4 | Multiple independent indications | Two independent unresolved Doshas |\n| 5 | Serious unresolved concern under the selected tradition | Three or more independent unresolved Doshas, or a Dosha the selected tradition treats as a veto |\nThese levels are a proposed educational framework, not an ancient classical scale. Record: severity before exceptions, severity after exceptions, remaining concerns, evidence and confidence in the source.\n\n### Repetition and reinforcement\n| Relation | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Repetition | The same indication stated again |\n| Independent confirmation | A different input and classification points the same way |\n| Corroboration by another Koota | A second table agrees; check whether it uses the same input |\n| Corroboration by the Rashi chart or Navamsha | Chart-layer support, kept in its own report section |\n| Contradiction | Indications point in opposite directions: report both |\nExample: Bhakoot 6/8 with Graha Maitri 0.5 is *one* Moon-sign relationship read twice, not two independent warnings.\n\n## Cancellation Rulebook\n\n### How to read this tab\nThe table lists every rule record in the engine. Each is an unverified, circulated claim. Filter by Dosha, read the conditions and limitations, and note the class. The JSON schema and one record are shown at the end.\n\n{{x12rules}}\n\n### What the rulebook proves\n1. **No rule is verified.** Classes A, B and C are empty.\n2. **Scope is rarely stated.** For Bhakoot, no source says whether a rule covers 2/12, 5/9 and 6/8 alike.\n3. **Direction is rarely stated.** The brief asked about bride-to-groom direction; no circulated rule specifies it.\n4. **Some rules conflict.** The register in the Regional Comparison tab lists twelve examples.\n5. **Some Kootas have no rule at all.** The engine says so in a fixed sentence and does not invent a rule.\n\n### Nadi cancellation research (ten items)\n| Item | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Same Rashi, different Nakshatra | NADI-EX-001, class E, \"complete\" as circulated; attribution to the Muhurta Chintamani by modern pages; wording not verified |\n| Same Nakshatra, different Rashi | NADI-EX-002, class E, full or partial depending on the page: DISPUTED |\n| Same Nakshatra, different Pada | NADI-EX-003, class F, scope unclear; one source treats the *same* Pada as the hindrance |\n| Different Padas within a shared Nadi | Not found as a separate rule; folded into the previous item |\n| Same Rashi lord | NADI-EX-004, class F, mitigation |\n| Mutual friendship | NADI-EX-005, class F, mitigation |\n| Favourable Rasi and Rajju together | NADI-EX-008, class F |\n| Regional Pada-based exceptions | Not documented with a source; not coded |\n| Special Nakshatra exceptions | NADI-EX-007: lists differ between pages; never merged |\n| Any other Parihara | None located |\nNone of these is sufficient on its own: each needs a source, a school and a profile.\n\n### Bhakoot, Gana, Yoni and Rajju\nSee the individual Koota tab and the labs. Yoni has no rule; Gana and Bhakoot have circulated candidates; Rajju's candidates conflict with the view that same Rajju is non-cancellable.\n\n## Complete, Partial and Mitigation\n\n### Five outcomes\n| Outcome | When it occurs | Engine status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Complete cancellation | A rule of type COMPLETE is applied | CANCELLED_UNDER_SELECTED_PROFILE |\n| Partial cancellation | Only PARTIAL rules apply | PARTIALLY_CANCELLED |\n| Mitigation | Only MITIGATION rules apply | MITIGATED |\n| Conditional cancellation | A rule needs two or more conditions together (ALL logic) or a prerequisite | Applied only if all hold |\n| Disputed cancellation | A DISPUTED rule applies | DISPUTED_CANCELLATION |\nTwo partial rules stay partial. A complete rule plus a disputed rule gives CONFLICT_REVIEW_REQUIRED. Duplicate rules from different sources count once.\n\n### Score restoration versus interpretative cancellation\nA software system must report: original Koota score, original Dosha status, cancellation rule applied, cancellation status, adjusted interpretative status, whether score restoration is authorised by the selected rule, and a final score only if the tradition provides one.\n\n### Six numerical representations of one Dosha\nThe pair is Krittika Pada 2 (Taurus) with Vishakha Pada 3 (Libra): Bhakoot 6/8, same lord Venus. The same rule is shown six ways; the raw total never changes.\n\n{{x12repr}}\n\n## Nadi Dosha Lab\nNadi is classified by Janma Nakshatra. Test every Nadi rule against any pair. The research view shows which candidate conditions the pair meets and why none is applied.\n\n{{x12labnadi}}\n\n## Bhakoot Dosha Lab\n\n### Sign counting, both directions\nCount inclusively from one sign to the other and back. The two counts add up to 14 for different signs. Aries–Scorpio, Taurus–Libra and Cancer–Sagittarius are all **6/8**, never 6/6.\n\n### The 12 × 12 reference matrix\n{{x12b12}}\n\n### Test a pair\n{{x12labbhakoot}}\n\n## Gana Dosha Lab\nGana Dosha is raised when the Gana score is 0 or 1. Many circulated exceptions exist; none is verified, and none is a single universal formula.\n\n{{x12labgana}}\n\n## Yoni and Rajju Labs\n\n### Yoni\nThe rulebook holds no Yoni cancellation rule. Favourable Graha Maitri, Gana or Bhakoot are context.\n\n{{x12labyoni}}\n\n### Rajju\nRajju is a pass/Dosha test in Tradition A. The research found both lord-based exceptions and a severe, non-cancellable reading.\n\n{{x12labrajju}}\n\n## Multiple Doshas\n\n### Two Doshas together\nDo not assume that one cancels the other. Examples: Nadi + Bhakoot, Nadi + Gana, Bhakoot + Gana, Yoni + weak Graha Maitri, Rajju + Vedha, Dina + Stree Deergha.\n\n### Three or more\nDistinguish: three independent Doshas; three Doshas from one common factor; two cancelled and one unresolved; one severe Dosha with several minor deficiencies; multiple Doshas with contradictory supporting factors.\n\n### Double-counting prevention\n| Relationship | Separate or overlapping? |\n|---|---|\n| Bhakoot and Graha Maitri | Overlapping: one Moon-sign relationship |\n| Bhakoot and Rashi-lord conflict | Overlapping when the lords are the issue |\n| Nadi, Gana, Yoni, Rajju, Vedha, Tara | Different tables; same Nakshatra input: not independent confirmation |\n| Rashi-based versus Nakshatra-based Doshas | Independent inputs |\nThe interaction matrix below marks a cell as a candidate only when a rulebook record names the other Koota in a condition. Otherwise it reads \"No established automatic cancellation\".\n\n{{x12multi}}\n\n## Case Studies\n\n### Ten hypothetical cases (all calculated by the engine)\nHypothetical people only. Each case runs under several profiles with the unverified status acknowledged, for demonstration. T1–T6 are teaching profiles, not documented schools.\n| # | Pair | Raw total | Question |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Punarvasu P1 with Hasta P1 | 24.5 | Does Graha Maitri 5 cancel Nadi Dosha? |\n| 2 | Ashwini P1 with Purva Phalguni P1 | 24 | How do T1, T2, T4 treat the same 5/9? |\n| 3 | Bharani P1 with Krittika P1 | 29 | Does a friendly Navamsha lord cancel Gana? |\n| 4 | Punarvasu P1 with Purva Phalguni P1 | 26.5 | Is there a rule for a Yoni enemy? |\n| 5 | Magha P1 with Jyeshtha P1 | 32 | What can be said about same Rajju? |\n| 6 | Krittika P3 with Vishakha P2 | 14.5 | What does the total hide? |\n| 7 | Ashwini P1 with Ashwini P4 | 28 | Does 28 settle the matter? |\n| 8 | Ardra P1 with Vishakha P4 | 14.5 | Can chart strengths change the Koota record? |\n| 9 | Ashwini P1 with Magha P1 | 20 | Why do rule variants give different totals? |\n| 10 | Ashwini P1 with Purva Ashadha P1 | 27 | How do you present conflicting rules? |\n\n### Reading the key cases\n- **Case 1.** Both Moon signs have Mercury as lord, so Graha Maitri is 5. Only T6 enables a Nadi lord rule, and it is a *mitigation*: the Nadi Dosha stays identified. Under T0 and T3 nothing applies. Favourable Graha Maitri does not cancel Nadi.\n- **Case 2.** Lords Mars and Sun are mutual friends but not the same lord. T1 does nothing; T2 applies the mutual-friend rule and restores 7 as an ADJUSTED total 31 (raw 24); T4 applies it as status only because Nadi is absent.\n- **Case 3.** Both Moons are in Aries, so rules that need two different signs fail. The Moon Navamsha lords (Sun and Jupiter) are friends but no Gana rule uses them. Only the disputed same-Rashi rule (T6) changes the status, to DISPUTED.\n- **Case 4.** Yoni is 0 (Cat and Rat are an enemy pair). No cancellation rule exists for Yoni; the engine prints the fixed statement.\n- **Case 5.** Magha and Jyeshtha are both Pada Rajju; lords Sun and Mars are mutual friends. T6 applies a disputed rule and a mitigation: status DISPUTED. Sources conflict (C04).\n- **Case 6.** Five flags. Nadi, Rajju and Vedha re-read the same Nakshatras. T1 cancels the Bhakoot status only; the other four stay identified. Under T4 nothing applies because Nadi is present.\n- **Case 7.** The total is high, the flags remain, and neither Nadi condition is met.\n- **Case 8.** Chart strengths (seventh lord, Venus, Jupiter, Navamsha, Dasha) are practitioner-entered and live in the horoscope layer. They do not rewrite the Koota record.\n- **Case 9.** The Lab's rule variants (Janma Tara inauspicious, earlier Vashya table, earlier Gana table, Gana Profile B) give totals of 20, 17, 20.5, 20, 19, 20. The differences are rule-table differences, not a different pair.\n- **Case 10.** T2, T3 and T4 disagree on the same Bhakoot Dosha. Present all three with the rule IDs.\n\n### Run the cases\n{{x12cases}}\n\n## Horoscope Verification\n\n### Three analytical layers\n| Layer | Question |\n|---|---|\n| A Koota score | What does the mathematical scoring system show? |\n| B Dosha and exception analysis | Which unfavourable conditions exist and which exceptions apply? |\n| C Complete horoscope | What do the individual and mutual charts add? |\nLayer C must never be used to falsify or silently rewrite Layers A and B.\n\n### What to examine\nLagna and its lord; Moon and Moon sign; seventh house and lord; Venus and Jupiter; dignity and aspects; Navamsha (D-9) Lagna, its lord, seventh house and lord, Venus, Jupiter; Dasha context; mutual chart factors (Moon–Moon, Venus–Mars, seventh-house links, Navamsha relationships).\n\n### Limits of this app\nThe app calculates only the Moon. Every other observation is **entered by the practitioner**. Planetary meanings differ between schools; do not give every planet the same meaning for both partners without naming the tradition. D-9 observations are supporting analysis, not a Koota cancellation. Dasha is timing context and never alters a Koota score.\n\n### Five independent sections\n1 Koota compatibility · 2 Individual Dosha examination · 3 Cancellation and mitigation · 4 Individual horoscope verification · 5 Final interpretive synthesis. They are never collapsed into one number.\n\n{{x12horo}}\n\n## Decision Framework\n\n### Relative importance\nAsk: Which Doshas need further examination? Are they repeated or independent? Does the selected tradition treat the factor as mandatory? Does an exception apply with every condition met? What remains unresolved? Is the birth time reliable?\n\n### Decision matrix and reporting categories\nThe tool lists the severity levels, the matrix, and the six reporting categories. These are non-deterministic categories, not predictions of marital success or failure. There is no universal pass/fail threshold.\n\n{{x12decision}}\n\n## Regional Comparison and Conflicts\n\n### Regional cancellation comparison\n| Rule or Dosha | North Indian | Tamil | Telugu | Kannada | Kerala |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Nadi | Circulated conditions, unverified | Not a separate Porutham | Requires verification | Requires verification | Requires verification |\n| Bhakoot / Rasi | Circulated same-lord and friend rules | Rasi Porutham with its own rule | Requires verification | Requires verification | Requires verification |\n| Gana | Circulated lord and Navamsha rules | Gana Porutham | Requires verification | Requires verification | Requires verification |\n| Yoni | None verified | Yoni Porutham; no verified rule | Requires verification | Requires verification | Requires verification |\n| Rajju | Not in Ashtakoota; circulated claims here | Rajju Porutham; sources conflict | Requires verification | Requires verification | Requires verification |\n| Vedha | None verified | Vedhai Porutham; none verified | Requires verification | Requires verification | Requires verification |\n| Mahendra, Stree Deergha | Supplementary | Porutham | Requires verification | Requires verification | Requires verification |\n| Other | – | – | – | – | Papasamyam and Chovva Dosham are chart-based and are covered in Day 11 |\n\"Requires verification\" means a documented regional profile was not located. Gaps are never filled with assumptions.\n\n### Why contradictions arise\nDifferent source texts and commentaries; regional traditions; different Nakshatra allocations; different counting directions; different friendship tables; different treatment of Padas; different cancellation conditions; different restoration conventions; different approaches to the whole horoscope.\n\n### The conflict-resolution protocol\n1. Identify the conflicting rules. 2. Identify each source. 3. Decide whether they belong to different traditions. 4. Verify the arithmetic. 5. Check mandatory conditions. 6. Decide whether they truly contradict or address different situations. 7. Apply the selected profile. 8. Display the alternative separately. 9. Record the reason.\n\n### Conflict register (12 examples)\n{{x12conflicts}}\n\n## Ethics and Professional Interpretation\n\n### Professional responsibility\n- Compatibility scoring is a traditional astrological framework, not a scientifically established predictor of marital success.\n- No single Koota should be used to frighten or pressure anyone.\n- No deterministic prediction of death, widowhood, divorce, infertility, illness or progeny may be made from a Dosha.\n- Astrological Nadi is never medical or genetic evidence.\n- No marriage decision should rest on a numerical score alone.\n- Birth-time uncertainty must be disclosed.\n- Different schools may legitimately produce different results.\n- The practitioner's role is to explain the method, the evidence and the limitations.\n\n### Language\nReplace \"Nadi Dosha is dangerous\" with \"Nadi Dosha is identified under the selected profile\". Replace \"this marriage will fail\" with \"several indications remain unresolved and need a fuller review\". Replace \"the Dosha is cancelled\" with \"under profile X, rule Y changed the status; the raw score is unchanged\".\n\n### Ethics checklist\n| Check | Yes / No |\n|---|---|\n| Profile, school and rule IDs named | |\n| Raw score and any ADJUSTED figure both shown | |\n| Birth-time confidence stated | |\n| No fear-based or deterministic language | |\n| No medical or genetic statement | |\n| Alternatives shown, not hidden | |\n| Open items listed | |\n\n## Quick Reference Sheets\nNine printable sheets: Dosha identification, severity, Nadi, Bhakoot, Gana, Yoni and Rajju, regional differences, horoscope verification, decision workflow.\n\n{{x12sheets}}\n\n## Final Examination\n\n### 100-mark paper\n| Section | Marks |\n|---|---|\n| Theory | 20 |\n| Rule identification | 20 |\n| Cancellation analysis | 25 |\n| Case studies | 25 |\n| Software interpretation | 10 |\nThe interactive paper below covers the objective part of every section: 10 theory questions (2 marks), 10 rule-identification questions (2), 5 computed cancellation questions (5), 5 computed case questions (5) and 5 software questions (2). The cancellation and case questions are generated from the engine, so they change each time. Pass mark: 70.\n\n{{x12final}}\n\n### Written paper (instructor marking)\n1. (4) Explain the seven conditions of a Dosha. 2. (4) Distinguish cancellation from mitigation and restoration. 3. (6) Take any case and write the five-section report. 4. (6) Present a conflict from the register without choosing a winner. 5. (5) Explain why Bhakoot and Graha Maitri overlap. 6. (5) Describe the evidence scale and apply it to NADI-EX-001. 7. (5) Explain how the engine prevents silent cancellation. 8. (5) Draft the ethics paragraph of a report.\n\n### Marking rubric\n| Criterion | Full marks when |\n|---|---|\n| Accuracy | Counts, scores and statuses match the engine |\n| Source discipline | Evidence class and verification status named; no invented citations |\n| Separation of layers | Raw, status, adjusted and chart layers kept apart |\n| Language | Non-deterministic, non-medical, non-fear-based |\n| Completeness | All required elements present |\nModel framework: state what was calculated; name the rule and class; say whether it was applied and why; state what remains.\n\n## Instructor Guide\n\n### 120-minute plan\n| Time | Topic | Method | Activity |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0–10 | Meaning of Dosha | Concept explanation | Dosha versus score deficiency |\n| 10–25 | Individual Doshas | Comparative tables | Koota identification |\n| 25–45 | Cancellation principles | Rulebook demonstration | Evaluate conditions |\n| 45–60 | Nadi and Bhakoot | Worked examples | Cancellation lab |\n| 60–75 | Gana, Yoni and Rajju | Comparative interpretation | Exception testing |\n| 75–90 | Multiple Doshas | Case-based learning | Combined analysis |\n| 90–105 | Complete horoscope | Chart demonstration | Rashi and Navamsha review |\n| 105–115 | Contradictory results | Group discussion | Regional profile comparison |\n| 115–120 | Summary | Questions | Key learning check |\n\n### Instructor notes\n- Open with the sentence \"A Dosha is not cancelled by a good score elsewhere.\" Ask students to predict the engine's answer before running it.\n- Whiteboard: the four fields (raw, original, cancellation, adjusted) in four columns.\n- Expected student mistakes: using \"cancelled\" for \"mitigated\"; counting Nadi and Rajju as two independent warnings; treating Parivartana as computed; assuming a 6/6 pair exists.\n- Discussion questions: When is a disputed cancellation better reported as unresolved? Who decides which profile a family follows?\n- Homework: assignments 5, 6, 9 and 10.\n\n> **Instructor guiding principle:** Never cancel a Dosha merely because another Koota scores well. First identify, then verify the rule, then examine the complete horoscope.\n\n## Software Specification\n\n### Core entities\nCompatibilityProfile, KootaDefinition, KootaCalculationRule, DoshaDefinition, DoshaDetectionRule, CancellationRule, CancellationCondition, SourceReference, TraditionProfile, EvidenceClassification, CompatibilityResult, DoshaAssessment, CancellationAssessment, PractitionerOverride, ConflictRegister, StudentCaseStudy, ReportTemplate.\n\n### Rule evaluation sequence\n1. Calculate the original Koota (Lab). 2. Detect the Dosha. 3. Load the selected profile. 4. Retrieve applicable rules. 5. Evaluate every mandatory condition. 6. Reject rules with unsatisfied conditions. 7. Record satisfied rules. 8. Determine complete, partial, mitigation or disputed. 9. Apply restoration only if explicitly authorised. 10. Generate the explanation. 11. Preserve the original score and status.\n\n```\nfunction evaluateCancellation(classified, cfg):\n  if classified.stage < 2: raise STAGE_SKIPPED\n  for each Dosha record r:\n    rules = rulebook.rulesFor(r.koota)\n    if rules is empty: status = IDENTIFIED_NOT_APPLIED; statement = NONE_STATEMENT; continue\n    for each rule in rules:\n      conds = evaluate every condition (MET, NOT_MET, UNKNOWN)\n      met = rule.logic == ALL ? all(conds MET) : any(conds MET)\n      applied = met and profile.enables(rule) and cfg.acknowledgeUnverified\n                and prerequisite(rule, profile) satisfied\n    applied = collapse_duplicates(applied)\n    status = resolve(applied)       # complete, disputed, partial, mitigation\n    restored = any(rule in profile.restore and rule.type == COMPLETE)\n    r.adjusted = restored ? koota.max : null    # always labelled ADJUSTED\n  rawTotal unchanged; adjustedTotal only if restored\n```\n\n### Example rule record (JSON)\n```\n{\n \"rule_id\": \"BHAK-EX-002\",\n \"dosha_type\": \"Bhakoot\",\n \"rule_name\": \"Moon-sign lords are mutual natural friends\",\n \"tradition\": \"Not established (circulated in contemporary sources)\",\n \"source_reference\": {\"text\": null, \"author\": null, \"chapter\": null, \"verse\": null, \"page\": null, \"edition\": null},\n \"evidence_class\": \"E\",\n \"conditions\": [{\"key\": \"mutualFriend\", \"desc\": \"Both lords natural friends of each other\", \"kind\": \"computed\"}],\n \"condition_logic\": \"ALL\",\n \"directional_requirement\": null,\n \"cancellation_type\": \"COMPLETE\",\n \"score_restoration\": false,\n \"limitations\": [\"Depends on which friendship table is used\", \"One-sided friendship is a separate rule\"],\n \"verification_status\": \"UNVERIFIED\"\n}\n```\n\n### Example result (abridged)\n```\n{\n \"module\": \"DOSHA_DAY_12\",\n \"rulebookVersion\": \"dosha-rulebook-1.0\",\n \"bride\": {\"nakshatra\": \"Krittika\", \"pada\": 3, \"moonRashi\": \"Taurus\"},\n \"groom\": {\"nakshatra\": \"Vishakha\", \"pada\": 2, \"moonRashi\": \"Libra\"},\n \"rawTotal\": 14.5,\n \"byKoota\": {\"Bhakoot\": {\"rawScore\": 0, \"rawStatus\": \"DOSHA_PRESENT\",\n   \"adjustedStatus\": \"CANCELLED_UNDER_SELECTED_PROFILE\", \"applied\": [\"BHAK-EX-001\"],\n   \"restored\": false, \"adjustedScore\": null, \"originalPreserved\": true}},\n \"adjustedTotal\": null,\n \"severity\": {\"before\": 5, \"after\": 5},\n \"finalCategory\": \"Significant unresolved concerns under the selected tradition\"\n}\n```\n\n### Multiple-rule handling, explainability and audit\nDuplicate rules from different sources collapse to one effect (`dupGroup`). A rule can be blocked by a prerequisite. Two partial rules are not added together. Every conclusion shows what was calculated, which Dosha was detected, which rules were examined, which were satisfied or rejected and why, the source, and what remains unresolved. The audit trail stores the original calculation, the selected profile, the rulebook and source-register versions, the cancellation outcome, any override and the final report version.\n\n### Controlled manual override\nRecord a practitioner's judgement with reason, source, note and date. The original system result stays visible and unchanged.\n\n{{x12override}}\n\n### Final Dosha analysis report\nReporting category, severity before and after, the four fields, the ethics paragraph, and the Nadi statement when Nadi is flagged. Print, download, or freeze with an audit trail.\n\n{{x12report}}\n\n## Common Mistakes\n1. Treating a high total as a cancellation.\n2. Writing \"cancelled\" when only mitigation applies.\n3. Restoring points automatically.\n4. Overwriting the raw score.\n5. Treating one-sided friendship as mutual friendship.\n6. Believing a 6/6 pair exists (Taurus–Libra and Cancer–Sagittarius are 6/8).\n7. Assuming the same-lord exception works for every adverse Bhakoot category.\n8. Counting Bhakoot and Graha Maitri as separate misfortunes.\n9. Counting Nadi and Rajju as independent confirmation.\n10. Treating favourable Graha Maitri as an automatic Nadi or Gana cancellation.\n11. Treating a friendly Navamsha as a cancellation.\n12. Applying a Nadi rule to Rajju, or any rule across Kootas, without a source.\n13. Merging exemption lists from different sources.\n14. Upgrading a widely repeated rule to a classical one.\n15. Assuming Parivartana or Navamsha Lagna lords can be calculated by the app.\n16. Letting Dasha change a Koota score.\n17. Averaging profiles or schools.\n18. Using a profile without disclosing it.\n19. Ignoring birth-time uncertainty.\n20. Using fear-based language.\n21. Making medical, genetic or fertility statements from Nadi.\n22. Reading Yoni symbols as literal behaviour.\n23. Using Varna as a judgement of caste or worth.\n24. Silently recalculating an old report after a rulebook change.\n25. Producing a single pass/fail verdict.\n\n## Bibliography\n### What this course holds\nNo primary classical text was consulted and no chapter, verse or page is given. Every rule is a contemporary circulated claim (class E or F). The leads below require verification against an accessible edition before any rule can be upgraded.\n\n| Lead | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Muhurta Chintamani, Vivaha Prakarana | Named by modern pages for Nadi conditions; edition, verse number and wording **not verified** |\n| Jyotirnibandha | Named by one web page for same-Rashi / same-Nakshatra conditions; **not verified** |\n| Narada Samhita; Piyushdhara commentary | Named for Nakshatra exemption lists; **not verified**, lists differ |\n| Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra; Jataka Parijata; Prasna Marga | Named in the brief as places to investigate; no cancellation rule located; **not verified** |\n| B. V. Raman, Muhurtha and published matching works | Named in the brief; **requires verification** against an edition |\n\n### Source register (contemporary documentation)\n| ID | Source | Use | Evidence limit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S1 | astrosight.ai, Bhakoot cancellation | Same-lord and friendly-lord claims | Class E website |\n| S2 | astrosight.ai, Gana rules | Gana mitigation claims | Class E website |\n| S3 | onlinejyotish.com, Gana Kootam | Lord and Navamsha claims | Class E website |\n| S4 | astrobix.com, cancellation of Dosha | Nadi, Bhakoot, Gana lists; one Jyotirnibandha attribution | Class E/F |\n| S5 | Jagannatha Hora, Bhakoot guide | Software treatment | Class E |\n| S6 | astrosight.ai, Rajju Dosha | Exceptions and a severe contrary view | Class E/F |\n| S7 | vedicbirth.com, Rajju Dosha | Same-lord / same-Rashi claims | Class E |\n| S8 | dekhopanchang.com, compatibility | Regional overview | Class E |\n| S9 | Scribd anonymous upload | A Tamil-named list for Rajju | Class F |\nThe links are listed in the Cancellation Rulebook tab. Websites and software documentation are evidence of current practice, not proof of classical authority.\n\n## Validation and Quality Control\n\n### Automated validation\n{{x12tests}}\n\n### Final quality-control checklist\n| Check | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Every major Koota addressed; Nadi, Bhakoot, Gana, Yoni, Rajju in depth | Done |\n| Regional traditions not merged; Day 11 holds them | Done |\n| Every rule has an evidence class, verification status, school and conditions | Done (class E/F, UNVERIFIED) |\n| Mandatory conditions explicit (ALL/ANY, prerequisites) | Done |\n| Complete cancellation distinguished from mitigation | Done |\n| Score restoration distinguished from cancellation; never automatic | Done |\n| Favourable Graha Maitri never an automatic cancellation | Done |\n| Supportive Navamsha never an automatic cancellation | Done |\n| Multiple Doshas not double-counted | Done |\n| High total never approval; low total never rejection | Done |\n| Counts checked independently (144 sign pairs, 729 Nakshatra pairs through the Lab) | Done |\n| No Sanskrit verse, chapter, page or attribution invented | Done |\n| Software output explainable; no silent cancellation | Done |\n| Hypothetical cases internally consistent and engine-checked | Done |\n| Every test case has an expected outcome | Done |\n","13":"# Day 13 – Integration with Complete Horoscope Analysis\n\n## Learn\n\n### Welcome to Day 13\n**Module 13: Beyond Dashakoota – The Complete Marriage Compatibility Framework (विवाह अनुकूलता का सम्पूर्ण ढाँचा).** Day 13 of 14 · Advanced · 240–300 minutes (self-paced) · Instructor: Anoopam Shukla · Brand: ASTROSUTRAS.\n\nKoota Milan (कूट मिलान) examines a few compatibility parameters that come from the **Moon**: the Moon sign and the Moon's Nakshatra. A marriage reading in Vedic astrology is wider. Today you place the Koota result **inside** a larger study: each horoscope on its own, the Navamsha (नवांश), the two charts compared, timing, and the practical conversation that no chart can replace.\n\n> **Central teaching principle.** Do not judge a marriage through Koota alone. Examine each horoscope independently, compare both charts carefully, evaluate timing separately and respect the realities of human relationships.\n\n### Required teaching note\nAstrology is used here as a **traditional interpretative framework**, not as a scientifically established method for predicting marital success. Nothing in this module predicts divorce, widowhood, death, illness, infertility or children. Nothing is a medical or psychological statement. Every observation is written as \"noted for discussion\", and every reading depends on the whole chart, the school followed and the quality of the birth data.\n\n### Five questions, kept apart\n1. **Marriage promise:** what does each individual horoscope indicate?\n2. **Marriage compatibility:** how do the two horoscopes relate to each other?\n3. **Dashakoota:** what do the selected traditional compatibility parameters indicate?\n4. **Timing:** which periods may be relevant under the selected dasha and transit framework?\n5. **Practical compatibility:** what do the two people themselves need to understand, discuss and decide?\n\n### What you will be able to do by the end of the day\n1. Explain the limitations of Koota Milan.\n2. Analyse the marriage-related indications in each horoscope separately (Layer 1).\n3. Explain the significance of the 7th house (सप्तम भाव), the 7th lord (सप्तमेश), Venus (शुक्र) and Jupiter (गुरु), and the supporting houses 2, 4, 5, 8 and 12, without fixed outcomes.\n4. Judge planetary strength: dignity, combustion, retrogression, Vargottama, functional nature and aspects, each under a declared profile.\n5. Calculate and read the Navamsha (Layer 2), and compare D-1 with D-9 without letting one erase the other.\n6. Compare two charts (Layer 3): Moon, Lagna, 7th lords, Venus, Jupiter and mutual aspects, using one declared aspect system.\n7. Use Jaimini factors (Chara Karakas, Darakaraka, Upapada) as a **separate** profile from Parashari.\n8. Integrate the Day 1–12 Koota findings (Layer 4) **without rewriting them**.\n9. Examine Vimshottari dasha and transit as timing (Layer 5), and say what timing cannot show.\n10. Hold a practical, respectful discussion list that is not an astrological calculation.\n11. Prepare an integrated report that keeps every layer separate and never produces one arbitrary number.\n12. Report mixed or contradictory findings responsibly.\n\n{{m13dash}}\n\n### The six analytical layers\nDay 13 is built around six layers. Tap a layer to see its question, its material and its limit.\n{{m13layers}}\n\n### What the software calculates and what you enter\nThis matters for everything that follows.\n- **Calculated by ASTROSUTRAS from the Moon:** the Moon's sidereal longitude from date, time and UTC offset, under one of four built-in ayanamshas, and its Rashi, Nakshatra, Pada and Navamsha.\n- **Entered by the practitioner:** the Lagna and every other graha longitude, taken from a named ephemeris or software. The chart record stores the ayanamsha and the source you name. ASTROSUTRAS does not compute planets other than the Moon.\n- **Calculated from the entered longitudes:** sign, house (whole sign), Nakshatra and Pada, D-9, dignity, combustion, aspects, conjunctions, lordships, Chara Karakas, Upapada, Vimshottari periods and transit observations.\n- **Not calculated:** Shadbala (accepted only as practitioner input), house systems other than whole sign, and every real-world factor in the practical layer.\n- **Case studies are schematic:** internally consistent entered longitudes, labelled as such. They are not real astronomical positions and not real people.\n\n### How Day 13 connects to Days 12 and 14\nDay 12 taught you to identify a Dosha, test its own cancellation rules and record the result. Day 13 places that finished Koota report inside the wider horoscope. Day 14 then applies everything in a full consultation. The Koota report you bring from Day 12 is **read-only** today.\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### 13.1 Promise versus compatibility\nThese are two different questions and must be examined separately.\n\n| Question | Asks | Material |\n|---|---|---|\n| A. Individual marriage promise | What does one person's horoscope indicate about marriage and partnership under the chosen tradition? | One chart at a time (D-1, D-9, dasha) |\n| B. Mutual compatibility | How do the two horoscopes relate to each other? | Both charts, Koota, cross-chart comparison |\n\nA person may have strong marriage-related indications and still be a challenging match with one particular partner. Equally, favourable mutual Koota results do not establish the marriage promise in either chart.\n\n### 13.2 What Koota cannot see\nKootas use only the Moon sign and the Moon Nakshatra. They ignore the Lagna, the 7th house and its lord, Venus, Jupiter, the Navamsha, planetary strength, aspects and dashas. A pair with a high total can have serious chart-level items to study; a pair with a low total can have supportive chart-level observations. Neither result is a verdict.\n\n### 13.3 Four analytical situations\nFor each situation: what can reasonably be said, what cannot, what to examine next, and how to word the report.\n\n| Situation | Can be said | Cannot be said | Examine next | Wording |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Strong promise in both charts, favourable Koota | The layers examined so far point the same way | That the marriage will succeed; that timing and practical matters are settled | Timing; practical discussion | \"Broadly supportive across the layers examined, subject to the limits of the method.\" |\n| 2. Strong promise in both, mixed Koota | The charts show individual strength; the Koota layer has flagged items | That chart strength cancels a Dosha | Each flagged item with its own rule (Day 12) | \"The charts are supportive; the Koota layer shows items to study under the selected tradition.\" |\n| 3. Different levels of support in the two charts, favourable Koota | Each chart has its own picture | That the stronger chart compensates for the other | Each person's chart separately; D-9 | \"The two charts differ in their indications; both are described.\" |\n| 4. Mixed promise and mixed compatibility | Several items need study | Any verdict | Priorities for discussion; birth-time reliability | \"Mixed findings requiring detailed review; nothing here is a prediction.\" |\n\n### 13.4 Promise is not a guarantee\nDistinguish four words: **promise** (what the chart shows in general), **possibility** (what may be supported), **tendency** (what a placement is commonly read to emphasise) and **timing** (when a period may activate it). One combination is never a universal rule, and different schools read the same placement differently. Never state that a single placement guarantees or denies marriage.\n\n### 13.5 Four kinds of statement\nEvery statement in a report is exactly one of these. The software keeps them in separate result objects.\n\n| Kind | Example | Can be checked? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Calculation | \"Moon is at Taurus 16.00°, Rohini Pada 2\" | Yes, against an ephemeris |\n| Rule application | \"Under Parashari graha drishti Saturn in Aries aspects Gemini, Libra, Capricorn\" | Yes, against the declared rule |\n| Interpretation | \"Noted as a supportive indication for discussion\" | Only as a school's reading |\n| Practitioner conclusion | \"Category chosen after reading all layers\" | It is a judgement |\n\n### 13.6 Why no combined number\nKoota points, 7th-house strength, Navamsha dignity, aspects, dasha support and practical fit are different kinds of evidence. Adding them would produce a figure such as 85% that has no unit and no source. If a school defines its own numerical method, present it separately with its source. Otherwise show a structured profile, never an invented total.\n\n### 13.7 Mangal (Kuja) Dosha, a note\nMany practitioners examine Mars from the Lagna, Moon or Venus as a separate factor. Houses counted, exceptions and cancellations **differ widely by school**. No rule is given here; if you use one, name the school and record it as a separate Layer 3 item.\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### R1. Whole-sign houses\nHouse number = ((sign of the planet − sign of the Lagna) mod 12) + 1, with Aries = 0 … Pisces = 11. Other house systems need ephemeris-grade cusp data and are not supported; the chart record declares \"whole sign\".\n\n### R2. Navamsha (D-9) formula\nEach sign has nine navamshas of 3°20′. **D-9 sign index = floor(longitude ÷ 3°20′) mod 12.** Equivalent rule: the first navamsha of a movable sign is that sign, of a fixed sign the 9th from it, of a dual sign the 5th from it. Checks: Aries 0° → Aries; Aries 3°20′ → Taurus; Taurus 0° (30°) → Capricorn; Gemini 0° (60°) → Libra; Pisces 29°59′ → Pisces. A planet in the same sign in D-1 and D-9 is **Vargottama (वर्गोत्तम)**.\n\n### R3. Dignity profile used here\nPriority: exalted or debilitated sign, then Moolatrikona range (from degree to below the end degree), then own sign, then the Panchadha relation with the sign lord (natural friendship combined with temporary friendship). Rahu and Ketu carry no assigned dignity because schools differ. This is a **contemporary teaching profile** (class E, unverified).\n\n| Planet | Exalted in (degree) | Own signs | Moolatrikona range (as commonly published) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sun | Aries (10°) | Leo | Leo 0°–20° |\n| Moon | Taurus (3°) | Cancer | Taurus 4°–20° |\n| Mars | Capricorn (28°) | Aries, Scorpio | Aries 0°–12° |\n| Mercury | Virgo (15°) | Gemini, Virgo | Virgo 16°–20° |\n| Jupiter | Cancer (5°) | Sagittarius, Pisces | Sagittarius 0°–10° |\n| Venus | Pisces (27°) | Taurus, Libra | Libra 0°–15° |\n| Saturn | Libra (20°) | Capricorn, Aquarius | Aquarius 0°–20° |\n\nDebilitation is the sign opposite the exaltation sign. Where a sign is exalted and a Moolatrikona range also applies (for example Moon in Taurus), the engine reports \"Exalted\" and adds the range as a tag.\n\n**Natural friendship** (the same table used since Day 6):\n\n| Planet | Friends | Neutrals | Enemies |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sun | Moon, Mars, Jupiter | Mercury | Venus, Saturn |\n| Moon | Sun, Mercury | Mars, Jupiter, Venus, Saturn | none |\n| Mars | Sun, Moon, Jupiter | Venus, Saturn | Mercury |\n| Mercury | Sun, Venus | Mars, Jupiter, Saturn | Moon |\n| Jupiter | Sun, Moon, Mars | Saturn | Mercury, Venus |\n| Venus | Mercury, Saturn | Mars, Jupiter | Sun, Moon |\n| Saturn | Mercury, Venus | Jupiter | Sun, Moon, Mars |\n\n**Temporary friendship:** a planet in the 2nd, 3rd, 4th, 10th, 11th or 12th sign from another is its temporary friend; elsewhere, its temporary enemy. **Panchadha combination:** friend + temporary friend = great friend; friend + temporary enemy = neutral; neutral + temporary friend = friend; neutral + temporary enemy = enemy; enemy + temporary friend = neutral; enemy + temporary enemy = great enemy.\n\n### R4. Combustion and retrogression\nA planet close to the Sun is called combust (अस्त). **Orbs differ between sources.** The common set used here: Moon 12°, Mars 17°, Mercury 14° (12° when retrograde), Jupiter 11°, Venus 10° (8° when retrograde), Saturn 15°. One contemporary source gives Mercury 13° and Venus 9°; that set is available as an alternative profile to show that tables differ. The Sun is never combust and the nodes have no orb. Retrograde status is **entered** by the practitioner, because ASTROSUTRAS has no planetary ephemeris.\n\n### R5. Aspect systems (never mixed)\n| System | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Parashari graha drishti | Every planet aspects the 7th sign from itself. Mars also the 4th and 8th, Jupiter the 5th and 9th, Saturn the 3rd and 10th. Rahu and Ketu: none (default) |\n| Parashari with nodes | As above, with Rahu and Ketu aspecting the 5th, 7th and 9th (accepted by some schools only) |\n| Jaimini rashi drishti | Movable signs aspect fixed signs except the adjacent one; fixed signs aspect movable signs except the adjacent one; dual signs aspect the other dual signs |\n\nCounting is inclusive: the 7th from Aries is Libra. Aspects here are **sign-level**: no degree orbs are applied.\n\n### R6. Functional nature\nA planet's nature depends on the houses it lords for the Lagna. Common teaching convention: kendras 1, 4, 7, 10; trikonas 1, 5, 9; dusthanas 6, 8, 12. A lord of both a kendra (4, 7, 10) and a trikona (5, 9) is called a **yogakaraka**; lords of 5th and 9th are functional benefic tendencies; lords of 3rd, 6th, 8th and 11th are functional malefic tendencies. These are **tendencies only**, and schools disagree about the 8th and 12th lords and about kendradhipati caveats. A natural benefic is not automatically a functional benefic.\n\n### R7. Chara Karakas (Jaimini)\nRank the planets by degree within their sign. Highest degree = Atmakaraka (आत्मकारक); lowest degree = Darakaraka (दारकारक), the spouse significator in this system. **7-karaka scheme:** Sun to Saturn, roles Atma, Amatya, Bhratru, Matru, Putra, Gnati, Dara. **8-karaka scheme:** Rahu is added with degree counted as 30° minus its degree in the sign, and a Pitru role is inserted. Whether Rahu counts is a **school choice**. Equal degrees are not broken by the software: the result is \"review required\".\n\n### R8. Upapada Lagna (उपपद लग्न)\nUpapada Lagna is commonly the **arudha** of the 12th house. Procedure: take the 12th sign from the Lagna; find its lord; count the signs from the 12th sign to the lord's sign (inclusive); count the same number again forward from the lord's sign. That sign is the arudha. Many sources then state that if the arudha falls in the house itself or in its 7th, the **10th from it** is taken; other sources apply no exception, and the description of the exception varies. ASTROSUTRAS always shows both the straight count and the exception result and lets you choose; it never silently applies one. Hand check: Aries Lagna, 12th Pisces, lord Jupiter in Cancer. Count Pisces→Cancer = 5; five signs on from Cancer (inclusive) = Scorpio. The straight count gives Scorpio; no exception is triggered.\n\n### R9. Vimshottari dasha arithmetic\nNine lords in order: Ketu 7, Venus 20, Sun 6, Moon 10, Mars 7, Rahu 18, Jupiter 16, Saturn 19, Mercury 17 years (total 120). Ashwini, Magha and Mula begin with Ketu; the Nakshatra index mod 9 gives the starting lord. **Balance at birth = (1 − fraction of the Nakshatra already covered) × the lord's years.** Antardasha length = (mahadasha years × antardasha years) ÷ 120, starting with the mahadasha lord. Pratyantardasha length = (MD × AD × PD years) ÷ 14,400. The **year length is a declared setting**: with 365.25 days, 120² ÷ 365.25 = 39.43 in the day-count form of the pratyantardasha divisor; with 360 days it is 40. Dates differ between the two, so always state which you used. If the birth time is unknown, the Moon's longitude itself is uncertain and every date is uncertain.\n\n### R10. Dashakoota import is read-only\nThe Koota report from Days 1–12 is imported as a frozen object. No Day 13 module may change it, restore points or cancel a Dosha.\n\n## Different Schools\n\n| Point | Variation |\n|---|---|\n| Marriage significators | Venus, Jupiter or both; historical conventions differ for male and female charts and are not universal |\n| Aspect system | Parashari graha drishti, Parashari with node aspects, Jaimini rashi drishti |\n| Ayanamsha | Lahiri, Raman, KP, Fagan-Bradley and others; one must be declared for both charts |\n| Combustion orbs | Several published sets |\n| Moolatrikona ranges | Commonly published table; sources differ in detail |\n| Functional nature | Treatment of 8th and 12th lords, kendradhipati caveats |\n| Chara Karakas | 7 or 8 karakas; Rahu counted as 30° minus its degree where used |\n| Upapada | Straight count or \"10th from it\" exception; some sources give other corrections |\n| Role of D-9 | Marriage chart, Dharma chart, strength chart; supplement to D-1 |\n| Dasha systems | Vimshottari used here; other systems exist |\n| House overlays | Contemporary comparative technique, no classical authority claimed |\n| Mangal Dosha | Methods vary widely; not included |\n\n**Never mix** Parashari and Jaimini rules in one algorithm. The workbench lets you choose the aspect system; the Jaimini panel always uses rashi drishti.\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### Birth-time uncertainty\n- **Unknown birth time:** Lagna, houses, house lords, D-9 Lagna, Upapada and functional nature are **unavailable**. They are not guessed from noon or from the Moon.\n- **Approximate time:** Lagna-dependent statements are flagged because a correction can change them.\n- **Any time uncertainty:** the Moon moves about 13° a day, so dasha dates are uncertain too.\n\n### Missing or inconsistent data\n- A planet whose longitude is not entered is marked **NOT_COMPUTED**; dependent items are listed as unavailable.\n- Two charts with different ayanamshas are flagged: cross-chart signs are not like-for-like.\n- Mercury entered more than about 28° from the Sun, or Venus more than about 47°, is flagged as impossible for those planets and should be checked.\n- A house system other than whole sign is rejected.\n\n### What an exception never does\n- It never rewrites the Koota result or cancels a Dosha.\n- A supportive Navamsha is an observation, not a formal Gana, Nadi or Bhakoot cancellation.\n- A dasha never alters a Koota result.\n- A transit never independently shows marriage promise.\n- A practical observation is never presented as a planetary calculation.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example 1 – the final synthesis sheet (independent columns)\n| Column | Content | Form of entry |\n|---|---|---|\n| Koota result | Profile name, Koota table, Doshas, exception status (Days 1–12), unchanged | Table |\n| Individual chart observation | 7th house and lord, Venus, Jupiter, houses 2, 4, 5, 8, 12 for each person | Words, with rule and school |\n| D-9 observation | D-9 Lagna, 7th, 7th lord, Venus, Jupiter, dignity, Vargottama | Words |\n| Mutual chart observation | Moon, Lagna, 7th lords, Venus/Jupiter, mutual aspects | Words |\n| Supporting factors | Items that point one way | Words |\n| Contradictory factors | Items that point the other way | Words |\n| Unresolved concerns | What remains to be examined | Words |\n| Source and school | For every rule used | Reference |\n| Practitioner conclusion | A category chosen by the practitioner | Words |\nNo cell contains a combined score.\n\n### Example 2 – phrasing an observation (hypothetical placements)\n*Hypothetical:* \"7th lord in the 2nd house with Jupiter's aspect on the 7th; Venus in own sign.\"\n- **Responsible:** \"The 7th lord is in the 2nd house, which links partnership to family matters; Jupiter's aspect on the 7th is generally read as supportive; Venus is dignified. These are noted as supportive indications and are weighed with the rest of the chart and the school followed.\"\n- **Not acceptable:** \"Therefore the marriage will be happy.\"\n- **Not acceptable:** \"Saturn is in the 7th, so marriage will be delayed or fail.\" (an isolated absolute rule)\n\n### Example 3 – a layered reading of one pair\nBride Rohini (Taurus Moon), groom Hasta (Virgo Moon). Under Tradition A the total is **25 of 36**, Bhakoot is **5/9** and Rajju is flagged (both Kantha). The Koota layer is therefore mixed and is **reported as it stands**. The reading then continues with: each chart's 7th house and lord; D-9; the relation of the two Moons (a trine from Taurus to Virgo, the same 5/9 that Bhakoot flags; some schools read a trine as supportive and Koota flags it as Dosha, and both statements are recorded); dasha timing; and practical discussion. The conclusion lists points for discussion. It does not announce success or failure.\n\n### Example 4 – calculating a D-9 sign by hand\nMoon at Taurus 10° = longitude 40°. 40 ÷ 3.3333 = 12.0, and 12 mod 12 = 0, so the D-9 sign is **Aries**. Check by the start rule: Taurus is fixed, so its first navamsha is the 9th from Taurus (Capricorn); the fourth navamsha (index 3, covering 10°–13°20′) is Capricorn + 3 = Aries. Both methods agree.\n\n### Example 5 – Vimshottari balance at birth\nMoon at Taurus 16° = 46°, inside Rohini (40°–53°20′). Covered: 6° of 13°20′ = 0.45. Rohini's lord is the Moon (10 years). Balance = (1 − 0.45) × 10 = **5.5 years** of Moon mahadasha remaining at birth; Mars (7 years) follows. With a 360-day year every date shifts, so the year length is always stated.\n\n### Example 6 – Chara Karakas (7-karaka scheme)\nDegrees within sign: Mercury 25.37, Venus 23.53, Sun 20.11, Moon 16.00, Saturn 12.19, Mars 10.23, Jupiter 8.41. Ranked high to low: Atmakaraka Mercury, Amatyakaraka Venus, Bhratrukaraka Sun, Matrukaraka Moon, Putrakaraka Saturn, Gnatikaraka Mars, **Darakaraka Jupiter**. If two degrees were equal to the arc-second the result would be \"review required\".\n\n### Eight D-1 / D-9 combinations (engine-checked)\n| # | Planet and position | D-1 dignity | D-9 sign and dignity | Vargottama | Study question |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Saturn, Libra 21° | Exalted (Strong) | Aries, Debilitated (Weak) | No | What needs qualification? |\n| 2 | Venus, Taurus 28° | Own sign (Strong) | Virgo, Debilitated (Weak) | No | What needs qualification? |\n| 3 | Jupiter, Capricorn 8° | Debilitated (Weak) | Pisces, Own sign (Strong) | No | What additional support exists? |\n| 4 | Mars, Aries 5° | Moolatrikona (Strong) | Taurus, Neutral (Moderate) | No | What is reinforced and what is only moderate? |\n| 5 | Moon, Taurus 10° | Exalted (Strong) | Aries, Neutral (Moderate) | No | What is reinforced and what is only moderate? |\n| 6 | Sun, Aries 10° | Exalted (Strong) | Cancer, Friend (Strong) | No | What is reinforced? |\n| 7 | Mercury, Virgo 16° | Exalted (Strong) | Taurus, Friend (Strong) | No | What is reinforced? |\n| 8 | Venus, Libra 2° | Moolatrikona (Strong) | Libra, Own sign (Strong) | **Yes** | What is reinforced? |\nThe matrix gives **questions**, not predictions. Genuine reinforcement means the same theme is supported by strength in both charts; superficial similarity means only the label matches.\n\n### Koota finding versus broader horoscope\n| Koota finding | Broader horoscope observation | What can be concluded | What cannot be concluded |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| High score, no Dosha | Supportive charts | The examined layers agree | A guaranteed outcome |\n| High score, no Dosha | Mixed charts | Koota is favourable; chart items need study | That the Koota result offsets the chart items |\n| Low score with Doshas | Supportive charts | Koota shows flagged items; charts show supportive observations | That charts cancel the Doshas |\n| Low score with Doshas | Mixed charts | Detailed review is needed | That the match is unsuitable |\n| Unresolved Nadi Dosha | Supportive D-1 and D-9 | The Dosha stays recorded; D-9 support is described separately | That D-9 cancels Nadi |\n| Bhakoot Dosha | Friendly lords, supportive mutual factors | A documented candidate exception may exist (Day 12 rule record) | That friendship cancels it |\n| Gana mismatch | Supportive Navamsha | Navamsha support is a supporting observation | That Navamsha cancels Gana |\n| Rajju flagged (in a tradition that uses it) | Any chart support | The flag stays; discuss it | That chart support removes it |\n\n## Practice\n\n### The twelve assignments\nUse the workbench (Chart Workbench tab) and the Exercises tab. Each assignment has a model answer computed from the loaded charts.\n1. **Individual chart analysis:** state Lagna, 7th house, 7th lord and its placement for each person.\n2. **Seventh-lord analysis:** house, sign, dignity, combustion and associations of the 7th lord.\n3. **D-9 examination:** D-9 Lagna, 7th and 7th lord, Venus and Jupiter.\n4. **D-1 / D-9 comparison:** fill the dignity-class matrix for the 7th lord, Venus and Jupiter.\n5. **Mutual Moon analysis:** both sign counts, lords, Nakshatras.\n6. **Seventh-lord comparison:** where each 7th lord falls in the other chart.\n7. **Venus / Jupiter comparison:** the sign relations of the four pairs.\n8. **Jaimini factor examination:** Darakaraka (7 and 8 karakas) and Upapada (straight count and exception).\n9. **Dasha comparison:** running periods for both people on a reference date; overlap of tagged periods.\n10. **Dashakoota integration:** import the Koota result and list what must be preserved.\n11. **Contradictory findings:** list three contradictions in the loaded pair and word each responsibly.\n12. **Complete synthesis report:** produce the report, choose a category and justify it in two sentences.\n\n### Exercise set 1 – layer assignment\nName the layer (1 to 6) for each item: (a) 7th lord in the 12th house; (b) Venus exalted in D-9; (c) bride's Moon in groom's 5th house; (d) Jupiter mahadasha running at age 27; (e) expected place of residence; (f) Nadi Dosha; (g) the final category.\n\n### Exercise set 2 – rewrite\nRewrite: \"Mars in the 7th, so divorce is certain.\" Then rewrite: \"High total, so no need to look at the charts.\"\n\n### Exercise set 3 – conflict\nA pair totals 31 of 36 with no Dosha, but one chart has a debilitated 7th lord and the D-9 is supportive. Write a balanced three-sentence summary.\n\n### Exercise set 4 – separation check\nFor each statement say whether it is a calculation, a rule application, an interpretation or a practitioner conclusion: (a) \"Venus is at Libra 8.5°\"; (b) \"Saturn aspects the 7th under Parashari graha drishti\"; (c) \"this is a supportive sign\"; (d) \"I choose 'mixed findings requiring detailed interpretation'\".\n\n### Answers\nSet 1: (a) 1 · (b) 2 · (c) 3 · (d) 5 · (e) 5 · (f) 4 · (g) 6.\nSet 2 model: \"Mars in the 7th is one factor traditionally examined for partnership friction; its effect depends on sign, aspects, strength and the rest of the chart and on the school used. It is noted for discussion and is not read as a prediction.\" / \"A high total describes only the Moon-based parameters. The charts are examined separately before any conclusion.\"\nSet 3 model: \"The Koota layer is high with no Dosha under the chosen profile. Person A's chart shows a debilitated 7th lord, with a supportive D-9 that adds context without removing the D-1 item. Both findings are reported, and the next step is to study dasha and the mutual chart.\"\nSet 4: (a) calculation · (b) rule application (the declared system) · (c) interpretation · (d) practitioner conclusion.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: Which input do all Kootas use?\nA: The Moon sign and the Moon Nakshatra only. They ignore the Lagna, the 7th house, Venus, Jupiter, the Navamsha, strength, aspects and dashas.\n\nQ: How many analytical layers does Day 13 use, and what are they?\nA: Six: individual marriage promise, Navamsha, mutual chart compatibility, Dashakoota, timing and practical compatibility, final synthesis.\n\nQ: What is the difference between marriage promise and mutual compatibility?\nA: Promise reads one chart on its own; compatibility reads how two charts relate. A strong promise does not establish compatibility with every partner.\n\nQ: Why is no combined score produced?\nA: Koota points, 7th-house strength, Navamsha dignity, aspects, dasha and practical fit are different kinds of evidence with no common unit. An invented percentage would carry no source.\n\nQ: What is the 7th house (सप्तम भाव) about?\nA: Partnership. In marriage study its sign, occupants, aspects received and lord are examined together with Lagna, 2nd, 4th, 8th and 12th links.\n\nQ: What is the 7th lord (सप्तमेश)?\nA: The lord of the 7th sign from the Lagna. Its house, sign, dignity, combustion, retrogression, associations and D-9 position are all examined.\n\nQ: Can the house of the 7th lord decide an outcome?\nA: No. A house placement gives commonly noted themes with supportive and challenging possibilities; modifiers such as dignity, aspects and D-9 change the picture.\n\nQ: Why is Venus examined?\nA: Many schools treat Venus as a general relationship significator. Its dignity, house, combustion, associations and D-9 position are examined, and historical male/female conventions are not applied as universal rules.\n\nQ: Is Jupiter always favourable?\nA: No. Jupiter is a natural benefic, but its functional nature depends on the Lagna, and its strength depends on dignity, placement and aspects.\n\nQ: What does the 2nd house add to marriage study?\nA: Family, speech and shared resources.\n\nQ: What does the 4th house add?\nA: Home, emotional base and domestic environment, read as the traditional house of comfort and not as a measure of well-being.\n\nQ: What does the 5th house add, and what is it not used for?\nA: Affection and romance. It is not used for fertility or child predictions.\n\nQ: What does the 8th house add, and what is it not used for?\nA: Shared resources, sensitivity and the need for adjustment. It is not used for statements about lifespan or widowhood.\n\nQ: What does the 12th house add?\nA: Privacy, expenses and distance, including living away from home.\n\nQ: What is a functional benefic?\nA: A planet whose lordships for the given Lagna lean supportive (for example lords of 5th and 9th). It can differ from the planet's natural nature.\n\nQ: What is a yogakaraka?\nA: By the common teaching convention, a planet that lords both a kendra (4, 7, 10) and a trikona (5, 9) for the Lagna.\n\nQ: What is dignity, and what is its limit?\nA: The condition of a planet in a sign: exalted, Moolatrikona, own, friend, neutral, enemy or debilitated. It is one input and never decides an outcome.\n\nQ: What is combustion (अस्त)?\nA: A planet within a set angular distance of the Sun. Orbs differ between sources, so the profile used must be declared.\n\nQ: What are the common combustion orbs used here?\nA: Moon 12°, Mars 17°, Mercury 14° (12° retrograde), Jupiter 11°, Venus 10° (8° retrograde), Saturn 15°.\n\nQ: Is Shadbala calculated by ASTROSUTRAS?\nA: No. It is accepted only as practitioner input from a named source, and a high value is never read as a guarantee.\n\nQ: What is the Navamsha (नवांश) formula?\nA: D-9 sign index = floor(longitude ÷ 3°20′) mod 12.\n\nQ: Where does the first navamsha of a sign begin?\nA: Movable signs begin from themselves, fixed signs from the 9th sign, dual signs from the 5th sign.\n\nQ: What is the D-9 sign of 0° Taurus?\nA: Capricorn.\n\nQ: What is Vargottama (वर्गोत्तम)?\nA: A planet in the same sign in D-1 and D-9.\n\nQ: Is D-9 a replacement for D-1?\nA: No. It is a supporting divisional chart that adds context; neither chart erases the other.\n\nQ: Does a supportive Navamsha cancel a Koota Dosha?\nA: Not automatically. A cancellation needs a named school's rule from Day 12; Navamsha support is a separate observation.\n\nQ: What does the D-1/D-9 matrix give?\nA: Study questions such as \"What needs qualification?\", not predictions.\n\nQ: What are the three aspect systems in the workbench?\nA: Parashari graha drishti, Parashari with node aspects (5, 7, 9) and Jaimini rashi drishti. They are never mixed.\n\nQ: Which houses does Mars aspect under Parashari graha drishti?\nA: The 4th, 7th and 8th from its position.\n\nQ: Which houses do Jupiter and Saturn aspect?\nA: Jupiter the 5th, 7th and 9th; Saturn the 3rd, 7th and 10th.\n\nQ: What do two different signs always add up to when counted both ways?\nA: 14 (for example 6 and 8).\n\nQ: What is a cross-chart house overlay?\nA: Reading one person's planets in the other person's houses. It is a contemporary comparative technique with no classical authority claimed.\n\nQ: What is the Darakaraka (दारकारक)?\nA: In the Jaimini scheme, the planet with the lowest degree within its sign, regarded as the spouse significator.\n\nQ: How is Rahu ranked in the 8-karaka scheme?\nA: By 30° minus its degree within the sign. Whether Rahu counts at all is a school choice.\n\nQ: What happens when two planets have equal degrees?\nA: The result is \"review required\"; the software applies no tie-break because the school's rule is needed.\n\nQ: What is the Upapada Lagna?\nA: Commonly the arudha of the 12th house. Count from the 12th sign to its lord, then the same distance again from the lord's sign.\n\nQ: How should the Upapada exception be handled?\nA: Show the straight count and the exception result (often \"10th from it\" when the arudha falls in the house itself or its 7th) side by side. Schools differ, and some apply none.\n\nQ: What is Karakamsha?\nA: The D-9 sign of the Atmakaraka in the Jaimini system; reading methods vary by school.\n\nQ: Why must Parashari and Jaimini be separate profiles?\nA: They use different aspect systems, karaka logic and conventions; blending them yields a rule that no school teaches.\n\nQ: What are the Vimshottari lords and their years?\nA: Ketu 7, Venus 20, Sun 6, Moon 10, Mars 7, Rahu 18, Jupiter 16, Saturn 19, Mercury 17 (120 in all).\n\nQ: How is the balance of the first dasha found?\nA: (1 − fraction of the Nakshatra already covered by the Moon) × the lord's years.\n\nQ: How long is an antardasha?\nA: (mahadasha years × antardasha years) ÷ 120, starting with the mahadasha lord.\n\nQ: Why must the year length be declared?\nA: Because 365.25-day and 360-day years give different dates.\n\nQ: Can a favourable dasha show compatibility?\nA: No. It is a timing observation; it never alters a Koota result.\n\nQ: How should transit be used?\nA: As an observation read alongside the natal promise and the dasha, never as independent confirmation of marriage or compatibility.\n\nQ: What happens to Lagna-dependent output if birth time is unknown?\nA: It is unavailable or flagged uncertain. The software never guesses a Lagna.\n\nQ: Why does the software flag different ayanamshas for the two charts?\nA: Cross-chart signs are not like-for-like when the zodiac reference differs.\n\nQ: Who chooses the final category?\nA: The practitioner, after reading every layer. The software proposes none.\n\nQ: What is a schematic case study?\nA: Internally consistent entered longitudes, labelled as such; not real astronomical positions and not real people.\n\nQ: What should a practitioner do when asked \"Should we marry on this report?\"\nA: Explain the method, evidence and limits, and encourage the two people to discuss practical matters and decide freely.\n\nQ: What are the three questions to answer for every finding?\nA: What was calculated, which rule and school was applied, and what remains to be examined.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: Koota Milan is calculated from:\nA) The full chart\nB) The Lagna only\nC) The Moon sign and Moon Nakshatra\nD) The Sun sign\nANS: C\nWHY: Kootas rest on the Moon alone. The full chart, the Lagna and the Sun sign are not Koota inputs.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Individual marriage promise asks:\nA) What each horoscope, read on its own, indicates about partnership under the chosen tradition\nB) How the two charts relate to each other\nC) Which Koota has the highest score\nD) Which dasha is running today\nANS: A\nWHY: Promise reads one chart; B is mutual compatibility, C is the Koota layer and D is timing.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: In Day 13 the layers are combined into:\nA) One percentage\nB) A simple average\nC) A weighted total\nD) Separate sections with no combined number\nANS: D\nWHY: The course forbids an arbitrary universal formula; A, B and C would all invent a number.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The Navamsha chart is also called:\nA) D-12\nB) D-9\nC) D-10\nD) D-7\nANS: B\nWHY: Navamsha is the ninth divisional chart, D-9.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which planet is commonly examined as a general relationship significator?\nA) Venus\nB) Mercury only\nC) The Sun only\nD) Rahu only\nANS: A\nWHY: Many schools use Venus; conventions differ and none is universal, and the other options are not the common choice.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The 7th house is traditionally the house of:\nA) Siblings\nB) Home\nC) Partnership\nD) Property\nANS: C\nWHY: The 7th is the partnership house; the others belong to the 3rd, 4th and other houses.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which wording is acceptable in a report?\nA) \"The marriage will fail.\"\nB) \"This placement is noted for discussion; its effect depends on the whole chart.\"\nC) \"Happiness is guaranteed.\"\nD) \"Nothing in the chart matters.\"\nANS: B\nWHY: Non-deterministic wording is required; A and C are absolutes, D dismisses the method without reason.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: If the birth time is unknown, Lagna-dependent statements are:\nA) Taken from noon\nB) Taken from the Moon sign\nC) Treated as fully reliable\nD) Unavailable or flagged uncertain\nANS: D\nWHY: The software never guesses a Lagna. Noon or the Moon sign would be invented inputs.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which of these does ASTROSUTRAS compute itself?\nA) Mars longitude\nB) Saturn longitude\nC) The Moon's sidereal longitude from date, time and UTC offset\nD) Shadbala\nANS: C\nWHY: Only the Moon is computed. Other longitudes are entered from a named ephemeris and Shadbala is practitioner input only.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A Koota Dosha and a supportive D-9 observation are:\nA) Reported separately\nB) Cancelled together\nC) Added into a total\nD) Deleted from the report\nANS: A\nWHY: Navamsha support is a separate observation; it is not a formal cancellation and never rewrites the Koota result.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The D-9 sign of Taurus 0° (30° longitude) is:\nA) Taurus\nB) Gemini\nC) Aries\nD) Capricorn\nANS: D\nWHY: Fixed signs begin from the 9th sign, which from Taurus is Capricorn. floor(30 ÷ 3.3333) mod 12 = 9 as well.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The D-9 sign of Aries 3°20′ is:\nA) Aries\nB) Taurus\nC) Gemini\nD) Capricorn\nANS: B\nWHY: It is the second navamsha of a movable sign, so Aries + 1 = Taurus.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A planet in the same sign in D-1 and D-9 is called:\nA) Vargottama\nB) Yogakaraka\nC) Combust\nD) Retrograde\nANS: A\nWHY: That is the definition of Vargottama; the other terms describe lordship, solar proximity and apparent motion.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: For an Aries Lagna the 7th lord is:\nA) Mars\nB) Mercury\nC) Venus\nD) Jupiter\nANS: C\nWHY: The 7th sign is Libra, ruled by Venus. Mars rules the 1st, Mercury the 3rd and 6th, Jupiter the 9th and 12th.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Under the common orb set, direct Mercury within how many degrees of the Sun is combust?\nA) 10\nB) 14\nC) 17\nD) 8\nANS: B\nWHY: 14° direct, 12° retrograde. 10 and 8 are Venus's orbs, 17 is Mars's.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Under Parashari graha drishti Jupiter aspects which houses from its position?\nA) 4th, 7th, 8th\nB) 3rd, 7th, 10th\nC) 2nd, 7th, 12th\nD) 5th, 7th, 9th\nANS: D\nWHY: A is Mars, B is Saturn, C belongs to no standard graha drishti list.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Counted inclusively in both directions, two different signs always give counts that add up to:\nA) 14\nB) 12\nC) 13\nD) 10\nANS: A\nWHY: The counts are d + 1 and (12 − d) + 1, which add to 14 (for example 6 and 8).\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Reading A's Venus in B's 7th house is:\nA) An established classical Ashtakoota rule\nB) A Koota\nC) A contemporary comparative technique\nD) A Dosha cancellation\nANS: C\nWHY: Cross-chart overlays have no classical authority claimed here; they are observations.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: In the 8-karaka scheme Rahu is ranked using:\nA) Its full longitude\nB) Its house\nC) Its Nakshatra\nD) 30° minus its degree within the sign\nANS: D\nWHY: Rahu moves backwards, so the commonly described method counts 30° minus its degree. Whether Rahu counts at all is a school choice.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: In the 7-karaka scheme the Darakaraka is the planet with:\nA) The highest degree\nB) The lowest degree\nC) The most aspects\nD) The strongest house\nANS: B\nWHY: Highest degree is the Atmakaraka and lowest is the Darakaraka; aspects and house do not rank karakas.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The Upapada Lagna is commonly the arudha of the:\nA) 7th house\nB) 12th house\nC) 2nd house\nD) Lagna\nANS: B\nWHY: The 12th house arudha is the usual definition; the arudha of the Lagna is the Arudha Lagna.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A Moon at 0° Aries begins which mahadasha at birth?\nA) Venus\nB) Sun\nC) Moon\nD) Ketu\nANS: D\nWHY: Ashwini is the first Nakshatra and its lord is Ketu (7 years); the other lords begin other Nakshatras.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: How long is the Jupiter antardasha inside the Venus mahadasha?\nA) 3.33 years\nB) 2.00 years\nC) 2.67 years\nD) 1.60 years\nANS: C\nWHY: 20 × 16 ÷ 120 = 2.667 years. 3.33 is the Venus-in-Venus length (20 × 20 ÷ 120).\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Vimshottari dates depend on, among other settings:\nA) The declared year length\nB) Only the Lagna\nC) The Koota total\nD) The aspect system\nANS: A\nWHY: 365.25 and 360 give different dates. The Lagna, the Koota total and the aspect system do not set dasha dates.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A favourable dasha period shows:\nA) Proven compatibility\nB) A cancelled Dosha\nC) A higher Koota score\nD) A timing observation only\nANS: D\nWHY: Dasha is Layer 5. It never alters or cancels a Koota result.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which system gives a movable sign such as Aries a sign aspect to Leo, Scorpio and Aquarius?\nA) Jaimini rashi drishti\nB) Parashari graha drishti\nC) Parashari with node aspects\nD) None of them\nANS: A\nWHY: Rashi drishti: a movable sign aspects the fixed signs except the adjacent one (here Taurus).\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which of these is NOT one of the six layers?\nA) The Navamsha\nB) A single combined score\nC) Timing\nD) Dashakoota\nANS: B\nWHY: The framework forbids a combined number; the others are layers.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A natural benefic planet and a functional benefic planet:\nA) Are always the same\nB) Are set by the Moon\nC) Can differ, because functional nature depends on the Lagna\nD) Are never compared\nANS: C\nWHY: Lordship of houses for the given Lagna changes a planet's tendency.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Shadbala in ASTROSUTRAS is:\nA) Practitioner input only\nB) Computed from the Moon\nC) Computed from D-9\nD) Hidden from reports\nANS: A\nWHY: It needs ephemeris-grade data; the software records it only if the practitioner supplies it from a named source.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: If the two charts use different ayanamshas:\nA) There is no effect\nB) The results are always better\nC) The longitudes should be averaged\nD) The difference is flagged, because cross-chart signs are not like-for-like\nANS: D\nWHY: One ayanamsha must be declared for both. Averaging would create an invented zodiac.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The Koota total is high, but Person A's 7th lord is debilitated in D-1. The report should:\nA) Hide the chart item\nB) Average the two findings\nC) State both, naming the chart item as a study question\nD) Declare the match unsuitable\nANS: C\nWHY: No layer rewrites another, and one chart item does not make a verdict.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Nadi Dosha is flagged and the D-9 is supportive. The report should:\nA) Cancel Nadi because of D-9\nB) Keep the Nadi finding, describe D-9 separately, and apply an exception only under a named school's rule\nC) Add 8 points to the total\nD) Remove Nadi from the table\nANS: B\nWHY: A, C and D silently rewrite the Koota layer; an exception needs a documented rule and evidence label from Day 12.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A planet is strong in D-1 and weak in D-9. It should be reported as:\nA) Strong\nB) Weak\nC) Average\nD) Both findings, with the difference named as a study question\nANS: D\nWHY: Neither chart erases the other, and averaging invents a value.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The commonly stated Upapada exception, when the arudha falls in the house itself, takes:\nA) The 10th from it, though schools differ and some apply no exception\nB) The 4th from it, in every school\nC) The 7th from it, in every school\nD) Nothing, in every school\nANS: A\nWHY: Sources describe the correction differently, so the lab shows the straight count and the exception result side by side and applies neither silently.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Two planets have exactly equal degrees when ranking Chara Karakas. The correct handling is:\nA) Alphabetical order\nB) Larger longitude first\nC) Flag \"review required\"; the school's tie rule is needed\nD) Ignore one of them\nANS: C\nWHY: The software applies no tie-break because the rule is school-dependent.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The Moon is at 6°40′ Aries, year length 365.25. The balance of the first mahadasha is:\nA) 7.00 years\nB) 3.50 years\nC) 1.75 years\nD) 5.25 years\nANS: B\nWHY: Half of Ashwini is covered, so half of Ketu's 7 years remain.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A pratyantardasha length in years is:\nA) (MD years × AD years × PD years) ÷ 14,400\nB) MD + AD + PD\nC) (MD years × AD years) ÷ 120\nD) The pratyantardasha lord's years\nANS: A\nWHY: C is the antardasha length. Dividing by 120 twice gives 14,400.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A 360-day year and a 365.25-day year give mahadasha end dates that:\nA) Are the same\nB) Differ only in the time of day\nC) Never differ\nD) Differ, so the year length must be declared\nANS: D\nWHY: Over a 20-year dasha the two conventions diverge by several months.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which statement about transit is acceptable?\nA) A Saturn transit proves delay\nB) A transit is read alongside the natal promise and the dasha, not independently\nC) A Jupiter transit guarantees marriage\nD) Transit replaces dasha\nANS: B\nWHY: A and C are absolutes; D confuses two timing tools.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which of these is a calculation and not an interpretation?\nA) \"Venus here is supportive\"\nB) \"The two will be compatible\"\nC) \"A's Moon sign is the 5th from B's Moon sign\"\nD) \"The pair is blessed\"\nANS: C\nWHY: Only C can be checked against the chart; the others are readings or judgements.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: \"Saturn always delays marriage\" is rejected because:\nA) It is an isolated absolute rule that ignores dignity, house, aspects, Lagna and school\nB) Saturn is always benefic\nC) Saturn has no aspects\nD) Delay is impossible\nANS: A\nWHY: One planet cannot be read in isolation; B, C and D are false.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The nine layer objects in the software are:\nA) Merged into the Navamsha object\nB) Merged into the dasha object\nC) Merged into the Jaimini object\nD) Independent, so no module overwrites another\nANS: D\nWHY: Separation is a design rule; the Koota object is frozen after import.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A schematic case study means:\nA) A real person's chart\nB) Internally consistent entered longitudes that are not real astronomical positions\nC) A published chart\nD) A chart computed from an ephemeris\nANS: B\nWHY: Schematic cases teach the method; every derived value is computed from the entered longitudes.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The imported Koota object is frozen because:\nA) It is large\nB) It is slow to compute\nC) Later layers must never alter it\nD) The Moon changes\nANS: C\nWHY: Preservation is a design rule, and freezing enforces it in code.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: The final category in the synthesis is:\nA) Computed from counts of supportive items\nB) Chosen by the software\nC) A percentage band\nD) Chosen by the practitioner after reading every layer\nANS: D\nWHY: Counting supportive items would be a hidden score; the software proposes no category.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Whole-sign houses mean:\nA) Houses of 30° beginning at the Lagna degree\nB) Cusps from a quadrant system\nC) House 1 is the Lagna sign, house 2 the next sign, and so on\nD) Houses of unequal size\nANS: C\nWHY: A describes equal houses from the Lagna degree, which is not the declared system here.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: In this lesson Karakamsha is:\nA) The D-9 sign of the Atmakaraka\nB) The D-1 sign of the Darakaraka\nC) The 7th sign from Upapada\nD) The Moon sign\nANS: A\nWHY: It is the Navamsha placement of the soul significator; reading methods vary by school.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Asked \"Should we marry on this report?\", the practitioner should:\nA) Say yes or no\nB) Explain the method and its limits and encourage the two people's own practical discussion\nC) Quote a percentage\nD) Predict the outcome\nANS: B\nWHY: Consent and choice remain with the two people; A, C and D claim an authority the method does not have.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Two software products disagree. The first step is:\nA) Choose the higher result\nB) Average them\nC) Reject both\nD) Compare the declared calculation profiles and settings\nANS: D\nWHY: Differences usually come from ayanamsha, house system, orbs, aspect system or dasha year length.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: Which statement about Day 13 and the Dashakoota result is correct?\nA) Day 13 places the Koota result in context and never rewrites it\nB) Day 13 replaces the Koota result\nC) Day 13 adds D-9 points to the total\nD) Day 13 cancels Doshas through the Navamsha\nANS: A\nWHY: The Koota layer is preserved unchanged; B, C and D would silently alter it.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Conceptual 1. Explain the difference between marriage promise and mutual compatibility, and why they are examined separately.\nMA: Promise reads one horoscope on its own; compatibility reads how two horoscopes relate. A strong promise does not prove a good fit with a particular partner, and favourable mutual results do not prove the promise in either chart, so the questions need different material and are reported separately.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual 2. Why does the software never produce a combined score?\nMA: The layers are different kinds of evidence with no common unit. Any percentage would be an invented number with no source, and it would hide contradictions between layers.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Calculation 3. Find the D-9 sign of Sagittarius 12°. Show the steps.\nMA: Longitude = 8 × 30 + 12 = 252°. 252 ÷ 3.3333 = 75.6, so the index is 75; 75 mod 12 = 3, which is Cancer. By the start rule, Sagittarius is dual so its first navamsha is the 5th from it (Aries); 12° falls in the fourth navamsha (10°–13°20′), so Aries + 3 = Cancer. Both methods agree.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Calculation 4. The Moon is at Scorpio 10° (220°). What are the nakshatra, the starting lord and the balance of the first mahadasha?\nMA: Scorpio 10° is in Anuradha (213°20′ to 226°40′). The covered fraction is (220 − 213.3333) ÷ 13.3333 = 0.5. Anuradha's lord is Saturn (19 years), so the balance is (1 − 0.5) × 19 = 9.5 years of Saturn mahadasha.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Application 5. A pair has a high Koota total. Person A's 7th lord is debilitated in D-1 but in its own sign in D-9. Write a balanced summary.\nMA: The Koota layer is high with no Dosha under the chosen profile. Person A's 7th lord is debilitated in D-1, which is noted as an item to study; its own-sign position in D-9 adds context without removing the D-1 item. Both findings are reported, and the next step is to read dasha and the mutual chart.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Application 6. Explain why a different ayanamsha for the two charts matters.\nMA: Sidereal longitudes shift with the ayanamsha, so signs, Nakshatras and D-9 positions can change. A cross-chart comparison with different ayanamshas is not like-for-like, so it is flagged and one ayanamsha must be declared for both.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Application 7. List three things that must stay unchanged when a supportive Navamsha is found beside a Koota Dosha.\nMA: The raw Koota scores, the Dosha flags and the cancellation status recorded under the selected rule profile. The Navamsha observation is added as a separate item.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Application 8. Write a responsible sentence for a 7th lord placed in the 8th house.\nMA: \"The 7th lord is in the 8th house, which links partnership with shared resources and adjustment; its dignity, aspects and D-9 position decide whether this is read as depth or as sensitivity, and the placement is noted for discussion rather than as a prediction.\"\nLVL: Advanced\n\n## References\n\n### Evidence labels used in Day 13\nThe same scale as Day 12: **A** primary text verified · **B** traditional commentary · **C** named modern authority · **D** regional or lineage practice with no verified primary source · **E** contemporary popular rule or teaching convention, which includes contemporary web and software documentation · **F** unverified or disputed. Nothing in Day 13 is graded A or B, and nothing is graded D because no regional manual or lineage source was read. Every rule and table in the engine is labelled **E, UNVERIFIED**.\n\n### Statements and grades\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Houses and planets commonly examined for marriage (7th, 7th lord, Venus, Jupiter, 2nd, 4th, 5th, 8th, 12th) | E | Widely taught; weighting is school-dependent; no verse is verified here |\n| D-9 as a supporting layer | E | Established practice; role differs between schools |\n| D-9 formula (3°20′ divisions, start rule for movable, fixed, dual signs) | E | Standard arithmetic; stated in contemporary texts; no primary text verified |\n| Dignity table, Moolatrikona ranges | E | Commonly published; sources differ in detail |\n| Natural and temporary friendship, Panchadha | E | As used since Day 6 |\n| Combustion orbs | E | Several sets published; one contemporary source differs for Mercury and Venus |\n| Parashari graha drishti | E | Standard teaching; node aspects are a school variant |\n| Jaimini rashi drishti | E | School-specific |\n| Chara Karakas, 7 or 8 schemes, Rahu as 30° minus degree | E | The scheme and Rahu's inclusion are disputed between schools |\n| Upapada as arudha of the 12th house | E | The exception rules are described differently by different sources |\n| Vimshottari arithmetic | E | Standard; year length is a declared setting |\n| Cross-chart house overlays | E | Contemporary comparative technique |\n| Mangal Dosha methods | E/F | Vary widely; not included |\n| No combined numerical formula | n/a | Course position |\n\n### Contemporary pages found during research (class E, contemporary documentation, not read in full)\nFour searches were made for this module. The pages below were **returned by the search and were not read in full**; they are listed only so you can check them yourself.\n- Upapada Lagna and arudha calculation, with differing exception rules: https://jagannathhora.com/?p=2192\n- Chara Karakas, 7 and 8 schemes: https://jagannathhora.com/?p=2110\n- Vimshottari balance at birth and antardasha formula: https://hindutone.com/astrology/vimshottari-dasha-explained-mahadasha-periods/\n- Combustion orbs: https://astromedha.in/insights/vedic/which-of-my-planets-are-combust\nThe searches also returned many other astrology sites, calculators and uploaded documents. They disagree on several points (for example the Upapada exception and Rahu as a Chara Karaka), which is why those points are settings in the lab.\n\n### Classical texts and authorities to investigate (not verified)\nBrihat Parashara Hora Shastra, Jataka Parijata, Phaladeepika, Saravali, Brihat Jataka, Jaimini Upadesha Sutras, Prasna Marga, Muhurta Chintamani (Vivaha Prakarana), and the published work of B. V. Raman on Muhurtha and marriage matching. **Status: requires verification.** No edition, chapter, verse or page is recorded for any rule in this module, and none is quoted.\n\n### Research and source policy\nOnline pages and software documentation are used only as **secondary evidence of current practice**. A rule becomes classical only when the text, author, edition or translator, chapter, verse, exact wording and commentary have all been checked. Where the exact source cannot be verified the statement is labelled **\"Traditional practice: source attribution requires verification.\"** If a traditional source and a popular claim disagree, both are shown and the difference is explained.\n\n## Chart Workbench\n\n### Lesson 2 (interactive) – the chart workbench\nEnter two charts, or load one of the twelve schematic cases. Declare the ayanamsha and the ephemeris or software that supplied the longitudes. Enter the Lagna and each graha as an absolute longitude (0–360) or as \"Sign degrees:minutes\". Mark retrograde planets yourself. Choose the aspect system and the combustion profile, then press **Apply**.\n\nThe workbench shows, for each person: the planet table (sign, Nakshatra, Pada, house, D-9 sign, D-1 and D-9 dignity), the house table with lords, combustion, conjunctions, aspects received by each house, functional nature, and North or South Indian charts for D-1 and D-9. **Moon only** mode computes the Moon from date, time and UTC offset and marks every other longitude NOT_COMPUTED. If the birth time is unknown, the Lagna is not used and every house-dependent item is unavailable.\n{{m13bench}}\n\n## Marriage Promise\n\n### Lesson 3 (interactive) – individual marriage promise (Layer 1)\nEach observation carries a tone (supportive, challenging, mixed or descriptive), the rule that produced it, the school, the evidence class and a limitation. The tone is a reading, never a prediction.\n{{m13promise}}\n\n### The seventh lord, house by house\nThe 7th lord's house is read as **commonly noted themes with supportive and challenging possibilities**, never as one outcome. The same table is shown live above.\n| House | Commonly noted | Possible supportive expression | Possible challenging expression |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1st | Partnership joined with self-identity and initiative | Partner-oriented attitude; active participation | Personal and partnership needs may press on one another |\n| 2nd | Family formation, speech, shared resources | Family-oriented outlook | Family or financial expectations may shape the partnership |\n| 3rd | Effort, communication, courage | Initiative and shared communication | Restlessness or differing communication styles |\n| 4th | Home, comfort, emotional base | Domestic focus | Tension between home and partnership demands; relocation themes |\n| 5th | Affection, courtship, creative expression | Warm, expressive bond | High expectations of romance |\n| 6th | Service, routine, adjustment | Practical cooperation | Disagreement or sustained adjustment (a commonly cited friction theme) |\n| 7th | The 7th lord in its own house | Partner prominence and committed outlook | Over-reliance on the partnership |\n| 8th | Shared resources, transformation, in-laws | Depth and resilience | Sensitivity and unexpected adjustments; read with care and never for lifespan claims |\n| 9th | Shared values, mentors, travel | Ethical or philosophical alignment | Differences in belief; distance from family |\n| 10th | Career and public life | Mutual support in work | Career demands versus personal time |\n| 11th | Gains, networks, aims | Shared aspirations | Many social commitments; high expectations |\n| 12th | Privacy, expenses, distant places | Privacy; living at a distance | Distance, withdrawal or expense themes recorded in some traditions |\n**Modifiers for every row:** dignity, relation with the Lagna lord, aspects, conjunctions, D-9 position and the school followed.\n\n### Venus (शुक्र) and Jupiter (गुरु)\nExamine for each person: sign and house; dignity in D-1 and D-9; combustion; conjunctions and aspects; dispositor and Nakshatra lord; relation with the 7th lord; functional nature for the Lagna. Many schools use Venus as a general relationship significator. Jupiter is a natural benefic and a significator in some schools. Historical conventions assign Venus and Jupiter differently for male and female charts; in this course the **same discipline is applied to both charts**, and those conventions are discussed only as history, not as universal rules. Do not assume Jupiter is favourable in every chart.\n\n### The other houses\n| House | Commonly examined | Not used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2nd | Family, speech, shared resources, family continuity | Statements about family stability |\n| 4th | Home, emotional base, domestic environment | Measuring well-being |\n| 5th | Affection, romance, courtship | Fertility or child predictions |\n| 8th | Shared resources, sensitivity, adjustment | Lifespan, widowhood or illness |\n| 12th | Privacy, expenses, distance, living away from home | Separation predictions |\nFor each: sign, lord, lord's house and dignity, occupants, aspects received and the D-9 echo.\n\n### Planetary associations (catalogue)\nEach association is read with: house and sign, Lagna-specific lordships, dignity in D-1 and D-9, and the aspects received under the declared system. **No association is read in isolation.** Phrases such as \"Saturn always delays marriage\" or \"Mars always damages marriage\" are rejected.\n| Association | Traditional reading | Possible supportive expression | Possible challenging expression |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7th lord with Lagna lord | A link between self and partnership | Self and partner themes support one another | The two sets of needs may be hard to separate |\n| 7th lord with Venus | Venus as general relationship significator | Harmony, affection, shared pleasures | Over-idealisation or indulgence themes, depending on dignity |\n| 7th lord with Jupiter | Wisdom, support, goodwill | Guidance and shared values | Over-optimism; Jupiter is not favourable in every chart |\n| 7th lord with Moon | Emotional attachment | Emotional responsiveness and care | Mood-dependent relationship needs |\n| 7th lord with Saturn | Structure, responsibility, time | Commitment, loyalty, steady responsibility | Reserve or slower closeness; not a rule that marriage is delayed |\n| 7th lord with Mars | Energy, drive, assertion | Vitality and shared activity | Impatience or friction in disagreements; not a rule that marriage is damaged |\n| 7th lord with Rahu or Ketu | Shadow points, school-dependent | Unconventional or cross-cultural themes; detachment or depth | Uncertainty, distance, unusual circumstances |\n| Venus with Mars | Attraction with drive | Passionate, energetic partnership | Heated disagreement or impulsiveness |\n| Venus with Saturn | Affection with restraint | Steadiness and lasting commitment | Emotional reserve |\n| Moon with Saturn | Emotion tempered by seriousness | Emotional maturity and stability | Emotional reserve or heaviness of mood |\n| Moon with Rahu or Ketu | Shadow influence on the mind, read differently by schools | Depth of feeling or unconventional outlook | Emotional uncertainty |\n| 7th-house occupants and their dispositors | The dispositor shows where results are directed | A strong, well-placed dispositor supports the occupant | A weak or afflicted dispositor may complicate it |\n{{m13assoc}}\n\n## Strength and Modifiers\n\n### Planetary dignity\nExalted, Moolatrikona, own sign, friend, neutral, enemy, debilitated, Vargottama, combust, retrograde (entered) and planetary war (not computed here). Dignity **contributes** to the assessment of a marriage-related planet and never decides the final outcome. The table and priority are in Detailed Rules (R3).\n\n### Shadbala\nThe six strengths are Sthana Bala, Dig Bala, Kala Bala, Cheshta Bala, Naisargika Bala and Drik Bala. They may be used as supporting evidence for the strength of relevant planets when taken from a named ephemeris-grade source. ASTROSUTRAS does **not** calculate them; a practitioner may record values. A high value is never read as guaranteed good results.\n\n### Functional benefic and malefic status\n| Label | Common teaching convention |\n|---|---|\n| Natural benefic or malefic | A planet's general nature (for example Jupiter and Venus benefic, Saturn and Mars malefic) |\n| Functional benefic tendency | Lord of the 5th or 9th (trikona) |\n| Functional malefic tendency | Lord of the 3rd, 6th, 8th or 11th only |\n| Yogakaraka | Lords a kendra (4, 7, 10) and a trikona (5, 9) |\n| Mixed lordship | Combines supportive and challenging lordships |\nThe same planet can behave differently for different Lagnas. Worksheet: for each Lagna write the houses lorded by Saturn, Mars, Jupiter and Venus and the tendency.\n\n### Aspects and conjunctions\nChoose one aspect system and state it. Conjunction here means the same sign; a \"close\" tag appears within 10° of arc. No orb is applied to aspects. Dispositor chains are read from the lord of the sign a planet occupies.\n\n## Navamsha (D-9)\n\n### Lesson 4 (interactive) – Layer 2\nThe D-9 sign is computed from the longitude. D-9 dignity here is sign-based (no Moolatrikona range). The matrix below is a set of **study questions**, not a formula.\n| D-1 dignity | D-9 dignity | Interpretative question |\n|---|---|---|\n| Strong | Strong | What is reinforced? |\n| Strong | Weak | What needs qualification? |\n| Weak | Strong | What additional support exists? |\n| Weak | Weak | What remains unresolved? |\n(Moderate combinations ask what is reinforced, what is only moderate and what remains undecided.)\n\n### What to examine\nD-9 Lagna and its lord; D-9 7th sign, its occupants and aspects; D-9 7th lord by sign, house and dignity; Venus and Jupiter in D-9; Vargottama planets; the relation between the D-1 and D-9 7th lords. D-9 is read as a divisional chart of marriage in some schools, a chart of Dharma in others, and a source of planetary strength in most; it is a **supplement**, and no rule is attributed to Parashara or any authority here without verification.\n{{m13d9}}\n\n## Mutual Chart\n\n### Lesson 5 (interactive) – Layer 3\nThe comparison reports **observations** under one declared aspect system.\n- **Moon to Moon:** sign counts both ways, lords, natural relation, Nakshatras. This repeats the Bhakoot count as an observation; the Koota result is in Layer 4.\n- **Lagna to Lagna:** sign counts, lords, and the relation of each Lagna lord to the other chart.\n- **Seventh-house links:** where one person's 7th lord falls in the other's chart, counted from the Lagna and the Moon (a modern comparative technique).\n- **Venus and Jupiter:** sign relations between the two Venuses, two Jupiters, Venus with Jupiter, and each with the other's Moon.\n- **Mutual aspects:** selected pairs (Moon-Moon, Moon-Venus, Moon-Mars, Venus-Mars, Venus-Saturn, Jupiter-Moon) at sign level.\n- **House overlays:** each person's planets in the other's houses; contemporary comparative technique, no classical authority claimed.\n- **D-9 Lagnas:** the sign counts of the two Navamsha Lagnas.\nDo not claim that Moon signs alone decide emotional compatibility, and do not assume a friendly sign relation guarantees a successful partnership.\n{{m13mutual}}\n\n## Jaimini\n\n### Lesson 6 (interactive) – Jaimini factors\nThis is an **optional, separate profile**. Chara Karakas use degrees within signs; Upapada uses the arudha method; Jaimini aspects are sign aspects. Choose the 7- or 8-karaka scheme and the Upapada exception rule. The panel shows both the straight count and the exception result, and reports ties as \"review required\".\n\nInterpreting a Darakaraka or Upapada **comparatively between two people** has no consistent traditional method verified here; it is labelled a modern comparative interpretation. Do not mix Parashari and Jaimini rules in one undifferentiated algorithm.\n{{m13jaimini}}\n\n## Dashakoota Integration\n\n### Lesson 7 (interactive) – Layer 4\nPreserve the original Koota report exactly: the selected tradition, each Koota score, the aggregate, the Doshas, cancellation rules applied, partial mitigation, disputed conditions and unresolved concerns. The import below is **frozen**; nothing on any other tab can change it. Choose which person is the bride, because several Kootas are directional.\n\nSupportive broader factors may change the overall **interpretation**, but they do not automatically cancel the original Koota Dosha. A cancellation needs a documented rule from the Day 12 rulebook, with its evidence label and conditions.\n{{m13koota}}\n\n### Integration worksheet\n| Column | What goes in it |\n|---|---|\n| Koota result | From the frozen import |\n| Individual chart observation | Person A and Person B, with rule and school |\n| D-9 observation | For each person |\n| Mutual chart observation | From the mutual panel |\n| Supporting factors | Items pointing one way |\n| Contradictory factors | Items pointing the other way |\n| Unresolved concerns | What remains to be studied |\n| Source and school | For every rule used |\n| Practitioner conclusion | A category chosen after reading all of the above |\n\n## Dasha and Transit\n\n### Lesson 8 (interactive) – timing\n**Promise, potential, timing and activation are different ideas.** Marriage promise is a reading of the natal chart; potential is what the chart may support; timing is when a period may be relevant; dasha activation and transit confirmation are tools, never proof. A favourable dasha is not proof of compatibility.\n\nThe panel computes Vimshottari mahadasha, antardasha and pratyantardasha for both people from the Moon, with a declared **year length**. For each running lord it lists **relevance tags** from the natal chart (7th lord, occupies the 7th house, lords the 2nd, 4th, 5th, 8th or 12th, Venus, Jupiter, conjunct the 7th lord, aspects the 7th sign). Tags are a checklist, not predictions and not scores. The overlap view lists antardasha periods in which **both** people have a tagged mahadasha and antardasha lord; it is a timing observation, not a compatibility result.\n\nTransit observations use longitudes you enter for a stated date. The panel reports each transit planet's house from the Lagna and from the Moon, and whether it occupies or aspects the natal Lagna sign, 7th sign, 7th lord's sign, Moon sign or Venus sign. Read a transit together with the natal promise and the dasha, never alone. Different birth-time accuracy affects every date.\n{{m13dasha}}\n\n## Practical Compatibility\n\n### Lesson 9 (interactive) – discussion prompts\nReal relationships involve things a chart cannot measure: personal expectations, communication, emotional maturity, shared values, financial expectations, family circumstances, lifestyle, career priorities, location and relocation, cultural and social expectations, personal boundaries, mutual consent, conflict resolution and long-term goals. The prompts below are **for the two people to discuss**. They are not a compulsory questionnaire, not an astrological calculation, and carry no score. The astrologer should not make assumptions about anyone's character or suitability from a planetary placement.\n{{m13practical}}\n\n### Professional checklist before the consultation ends\n- Birth data reliability and time accuracy are stated.\n- The ayanamsha and calculation profile are stated for both charts.\n- The school and aspect system are named.\n- Every observation shows its rule and limits.\n- Contradictions are reported, not hidden.\n- Consent and the couple's own decision are respected.\n\n## Final Synthesis\n\n### Lesson 10 (interactive) – six layers, one report, no total\nKeep these ten assessments independent: individual promise A; individual promise B; Navamsha A; Navamsha B; Dashakoota; additional horoscope compatibility; Jaimini (if selected); timing; practical and interpersonal; the final integrated interpretation.\n\n**Reporting categories** (descriptions, not predictions):\n1. Broadly supportive across examined layers\n2. Supportive with specific qualifications\n3. Mixed findings requiring detailed interpretation\n4. Significant unresolved concerns under the selected tradition\n5. Insufficient information for a reliable assessment\n6. Different traditions produce materially different conclusions\n\n**Handling contradictions:** strong D-1 with mixed D-9 (report both); mixed D-1 with supportive D-9 (D-9 adds context, it does not repair D-1); high Koota with challenging mutual observations (report both); low Koota with supportive mutual observations (report both); supportive individual promise with different timing (timing is separate); different Parashari and Jaimini results (show both profiles); different regional results (show each tradition separately).\n\n### The printable decision sheet\nA. **Birth-data reliability:** date, time, place, time accuracy, ayanamsha, house system, calculation profile. B. **Person A:** Lagna, 7th house, 7th lord, Venus, Jupiter, relevant houses, planetary strength, dasha context. C. **Person B:** same fields. D. **Navamsha:** D-9 Lagna, 7th house, 7th lord, Venus, Jupiter, dignity, D-1/D-9 consistency. E. **Dashakoota:** tradition, Koota scores, aggregate, Doshas, cancellation status, unresolved factors. F. **Mutual chart:** Moon, Lagna, 7th-house links, Venus/Jupiter, mutual aspects, Jaimini if selected. G. **Timing:** dasha, antardasha, transit, limitations. H. **Practical:** communication, expectations, values, family, lifestyle, consent. I. **Final synthesis:** supportive findings, challenging findings, contradictions, unresolved issues, selected tradition, practitioner explanation, limitations and disclaimer.\n{{m13synth}}\n{{m13report}}\n\n## Case Studies\n\n### Twelve schematic case studies\nEach case uses **schematic** entered longitudes: they are internally consistent and every Nakshatra, Pada, D-9 sign, dignity, aspect, karaka, Upapada and dasha value below is **computed from them by the engine**, but they are not real astronomical positions and not real people. All twelve use one declared ayanamsha (Lahiri) and the Vimshottari dasha with a 365.25-day year. The reference date for running periods is 2026-11-16. The aspect system and Jaimini settings are chosen in the lab. The Koota result is Tradition A, bride/groom as stated, preserved unchanged.\n\n| # | Pair (bride Moon / groom Moon) | Tradition A | Doshas flagged |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Rohini (Taurus) / Mrigashira (Taurus) | 36 | none |\n| 2 | Uttara Ashadha (Capricorn) / Bharani (Aries) | 29.5 | none |\n| 3 | Ashwini (Aries) / Ardra (Gemini) | 17 | Tara, Nadi |\n| 4 | Uttara Phalguni (Virgo) / Hasta (Virgo) | 27 | Nadi |\n| 5 | Ashwini (Aries) / Purva Ashadha (Sagittarius) | 27 | Bhakoot (9/5) |\n| 6 | Pushya (Cancer) / Ashlesha (Cancer) | 29 | Gana |\n| 7 | Ashwini (Aries) / Bharani (Aries) | 33 | none |\n| 8 | Uttara Bhadrapada (Pisces) / Revati (Pisces) | 34 | none |\n| 9 | Ashwini (Aries) / Uttara Bhadrapada (Pisces) | 22.5 | Tara, Bhakoot (12/2) |\n| 10 | Magha (Leo) / Purva Phalguni (Leo) | 30 | Gana |\n| 11 | Rohini (Taurus) / Hasta (Virgo) | 25 | Bhakoot (5/9), Rajju |\n| 12 | Ashwini (Aries) / Anuradha (Scorpio) | 25.5 | Tara, Bhakoot (8/6) |\n\n### Cases 1 to 12 at a glance\n| # | Theme | What the engine shows (Person A / Person B) | What can and cannot be said |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | High Koota, supportive layers | 7th lord Mars own sign in 7th / Saturn Moolatrikona in 7th, D-9 own sign. A: Jupiter exalted. B: Venus combust, D-9 Aries. Running: Jupiter/Jupiter/Jupiter and Jupiter/Venus/Mars | Favourable Koota with strong 7th lords; items still to study; no outcome |\n| 2 | High Koota, mixed individual findings | A: 7th lord Jupiter debilitated in D-1 (D-9 Aries). B: Saturn Moolatrikona, D-9 own sign | The Koota result does not offset A's debilitated 7th lord |\n| 3 | Low Koota, supportive chart observations | A: Mars own sign in 7th, Venus Moolatrikona, Jupiter exalted. B: Mercury own sign in 7th but D-9 Sagittarius (enemy), Venus own sign and D-9 Leo | Tara and Nadi remain; chart observations are reported beside them |\n| 4 | Nadi Dosha, favourable individual indicators | A: Jupiter own sign; 7th lord Moon in 9th. B: Saturn exalted in the 4th, D-9 own sign | Nadi identified (same Rashi, different Nakshatra): school-specific exceptions are Day 9 and Day 12 matters; none applied |\n| 5 | Bhakoot Dosha, friendly lords | Moon-sign lords Mars and Jupiter are natural mutual friends. A: Saturn exalted; B: Jupiter own sign, D-9 exalted | The friendship is a candidate documented in some manuals (class E, unverified), not applied automatically |\n| 6 | Gana mismatch, supportive Navamsha | A: 7th lord Venus, D-9 own sign. B: 7th lord Moon own sign, Jupiter D-9 Scorpio (great friend) | Navamsha support is not a formal Gana cancellation |\n| 7 | Strong D-1, weak D-9 | A: Saturn exalted in D-1, D-9 Aries (debilitated); Venus own sign in D-1, D-9 Virgo (debilitated). B: Saturn Moolatrikona | Both readings reported; neither erases the other |\n| 8 | Weak D-1, supportive D-9 | A: Jupiter debilitated and Venus debilitated and combust in D-1; both own sign in D-9. B: Mars own sign, D-9 exalted | D-9 adds context; it does not repair D-1 |\n| 9 | Different dasha periods | A: Venus/Ketu/Saturn on 2026-11-16. B: Ketu/Saturn/Mercury | Timing is not compatibility; the Koota result is unchanged |\n| 10 | Parashari and Jaimini | A: Atmakaraka Mars, Darakaraka Jupiter, Upapada Virgo. B: Atmakaraka Mercury, Darakaraka Venus, Upapada Scorpio (exception: Leo) | The two profiles are computed separately; never blended |\n| 11 | Regional systems differ | Tradition A: 25, Bhakoot 5/9 and Rajju flagged. Bride-to-groom count 10 (Mahendra count qualifies); reverse 19; Stree Deergha \"more than 13\" does not qualify | Different systems contain different factors and conventions; Day 11 holds the comparison |\n| 12 | Complete synthesis with contradictions | A: 7th lord Sun in the 1st, great-enemy sign; Venus exalted. B: 7th lord Jupiter, D-9 Aries. Running: Sun/Ketu/Jupiter and Ketu/Venus/Mercury | Tara and Bhakoot (8/6, same Moon-sign lord Mars) flagged; layers reported separately; practitioner chooses the category |\n**Case 11 convention:** the supplementary counts are bride Nakshatra to groom Nakshatra, inclusive, with the Mahendra counts 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25 and the Stree Deergha \"more than 13\" threshold. Both are conventions that differ between sources (Days 10 and 11) and are shown only to illustrate that systems differ.\n\n### Wording model for each case\nFor every case the lab shows **what can be said**, **what cannot be said** and a **responsible wording** sentence, next to the engine's computed facts. Example, Case 7: \"The D-1 and D-9 readings differ for Person A. Both are reported; the difference is a study question, not a verdict.\"\n{{m13cases}}\n\n## Quick Reference Sheets\n\n### Lesson 11 – ten printable sheets\n1. **Individual marriage promise:** read A and B separately; 7th house and lord; Venus; Jupiter; houses 2, 4, 5, 8, 12; strength and aspects; D-9; dasha context; observations, never predictions.\n2. **Seventh-house analysis:** sign and lord; occupants; aspects (declare the system); Lagna and Lagna lord; 2nd/4th and 8th/12th links; never one placement alone.\n3. **Seventh-lord analysis:** house and sign; dignity; combustion and retrogression; conjunctions and aspects; dispositor; Nakshatra lord; D-9 position; the house table gives possibilities, not outcomes.\n4. **Relevant houses:** 2nd family and resources; 4th home; 5th affection (no child predictions); 8th shared resources and adjustment (no lifespan statements); 12th privacy, expense, distance.\n5. **Venus and Jupiter:** natural versus functional nature; dignity in D-1 and D-9; combustion; associations; historical male/female conventions are historical, not universal.\n6. **D-9 Navamsha:** index = floor(longitude ÷ 3°20′) mod 12; Lagna, 7th, 7th lord, Venus, Jupiter; dignity classes; Vargottama; the matrix gives questions.\n7. **Mutual chart compatibility:** Moon to Moon; Lagna to Lagna; 7th lords; Venus/Jupiter; mutual aspects (one system); overlays are modern comparative; no combined number.\n8. **Jaimini marriage factors:** Chara Karakas (7 or 8); Darakaraka; Upapada (raw and exception results); Karakamsha; rashi drishti; keep apart from Parashari.\n9. **Dasha and transit timing:** declare year length; MD/AD/PD; relevance tags; overlap; transit as observation only; time uncertainty widens every date.\n10. **Final synthesis worksheet:** Koota (unchanged) | A | B | D-9 | mutual | timing | practical | supporting | contradictory | unresolved | school and source | practitioner category.\n{{m13sheets}}\n\n## Exercises\n\n### Twelve practical assignments (interactive)\nEach exercise uses the two charts currently loaded in the workbench (Case 1 by default). Work the exercise yourself first, tick it when done, then press **Show answer** to compare with the engine's computed answer. Changing the loaded pair changes the answers.\n{{m13exercise}}\n\n## Final Assessment\n\n### Lesson 12 (interactive) – the 100-mark examination\n| Part | Marks | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Theory | 15 | Limits of Koota; promise versus compatibility; separation; ethics |\n| Individual horoscope analysis | 20 | 7th lord, dignity, combustion, yogakaraka, house-wise reading |\n| Navamsha analysis | 15 | D-9 signs computed from longitudes; D-1/D-9 relation |\n| Mutual chart comparison | 20 | Sign counts, aspects, overlays, ayanamsha consistency |\n| Dasha and timing | 10 | Balance at birth, antardasha length, year length |\n| Integrated case study | 20 | Reporting a mixed pair responsibly |\n| **Total** | **100** | Pass mark 70 |\nQuestions with numbers are generated afresh each time, so recalling a previous answer will not help.\n\n### Marking rubric for written answers (model answer structure)\nFor a written integrated case, award marks for: (1) the Koota result stated and preserved (20%); (2) each individual chart read separately with rule and school named (20%); (3) D-9 read as a supplement (15%); (4) mutual observations under one declared aspect system (15%); (5) timing kept separate (10%); (6) contradictions named (10%); (7) responsible wording and limits (10%). **Expected reasoning:** identify, calculate, apply a declared rule, interpret cautiously, report separately. **Common errors:** merging layers, treating D-9 support as cancellation, mixing aspect systems, using a percentage, predicting an outcome. **Minimum professional reporting standard:** declared ayanamsha and profile, birth-time reliability, sources, contradictions, limitations and disclaimer.\n{{m13final}}\n\n## Software Specification\n\n### Engines (module names in `chart.js`)\n| Brief's module | ASTROSUTRAS object | Responsibility |\n|---|---|---|\n| BirthDataEngine | `Birth` | Parse and validate longitudes and settings; Moon from date, time, offset |\n| NatalChartEngine | `Natal` | Build the chart: signs, houses, Nakshatra, Pada, D-9 sign |\n| HouseLordEngine | `Lords` | House lords, houses lorded, functional nature (declared profile) |\n| PlanetaryStrengthEngine | `Strength` | Dignity, combustion, Vargottama; Shadbala flagged as practitioner input |\n| AspectEngine | `Aspects` | Three aspect systems, conjunctions |\n| DivisionalChartEngine | `Divisional` | D-9 sign arithmetic |\n| NavamshaEngine | `Navamsha` | D-9 chart, dignity matrix cell |\n| DashakootaEngine | Calculation Lab, `KootaImport` | Frozen Koota result |\n| DoshaAndCancellationEngine | `DoshaEngine` (Day 12) | Rule inspection; not altered by Day 13 |\n| MutualChartComparisonEngine | `Mutual` | Cross-chart observations |\n| JaiminiEngine | `Jaimini` | Chara Karakas, Darakaraka, arudha, Upapada, Karakamsha |\n| DashaEngine | `Dasha` | Vimshottari periods, tags, overlap |\n| TransitEngine | `Transit` | Transit observations from entered longitudes |\n| MarriageInterpretationEngine | `Interpretation` | Rule table and observations |\n| ReportGenerationEngine | `Synthesis`, `Report` | Layered report; no total |\n\n### Result objects (never overwritten)\n`promiseA`, `promiseB`, `navamshaA`, `navamshaB`, `mutual`, `dashakoota` (frozen), `jaimini`, `timing`, `practical`, plus `practitionerNotes` and `selectedCategory` (practitioner only). `Synthesis.hasNumericScore()` is a test that finds any numeric score, percentage, rank or total outside the preserved Koota layer; the expected result is **false**.\n\n### Interpretation rule data model\nEvery generated statement carries a rule record. Field names follow the Day 12 rule records so that both days can share one register.\n| Field | Example (rule IR-7L-01) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `rule_id` | IR-7L-01 | Unique identifier |\n| `subject` | Seventh lord: house placement | What is examined |\n| `school` | CONTEMPORARY_TEACHING_COMMON | The tradition or teaching convention |\n| `applicable_chart` | D1 or D9 | Which chart the rule reads |\n| `source` | text, author, chapter, verse, page, edition: all null | Filled only when independently verified |\n| `conditions` | lagna_known; seventh_lord_position_known | What must hold before the rule runs |\n| `required_calculation` | House of the 7th lord by whole-sign houses | The calculation behind the statement |\n| `interpretation` | Commonly noted themes with supportive and challenging possibilities | Never a fixed outcome |\n| `supporting_conditions` | 7th lord Strong in D-1 and D-9 | What points the same way |\n| `contradictory_conditions` | 7th lord Weak in D-1; 7th lord combust | What points the other way |\n| `limitations` | Contemporary teaching convention; no primary text verified; never read in isolation | Always shown with the statement |\n| `evidence_class` | E | A to F (Day 12 scale) |\n| `verification_status` | UNVERIFIED | VERIFIED, PARTIALLY_VERIFIED or UNVERIFIED |\n| `version` | 1.0 | A change of rule creates a new version; old reports keep their version |\nEvery Day 13 rule is class E and UNVERIFIED; none is VERIFIED.\n\n### Chart input and settings\n| Field | Example | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `name`, `role` | Person A (schematic bride), bride | Role matters for the directional Koota import |\n| `date`, `time`, `tzOffset` | 1996-03-12, 06:30, 5.5 | Needed only for the Moon and the dasha |\n| `place`, `timeAccuracy` | Schematic (no real place), exact | timeAccuracy is exact, approx or unknown |\n| `ayanamsha` | Lahiri (Chitra Paksha) | Must be declared; one for both charts |\n| `ephemerisSource` | Schematic (entered, not astronomical) | Required unless Moon only |\n| `houseSystem`, `nodeType` | WHOLE_SIGN, mean | Other house systems are rejected |\n| `longitudes` | Lagna: Taurus 8.00; Moon: Taurus 16.00; Sun: Aries 20.11 | Absolute 0–360 or Sign degrees:minutes |\n| `retro` | Saturn: true | Entered by the practitioner |\n\n### Result schema (excerpt)\n| Key | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `layers.promiseA`, `layers.promiseB` | Layer 1 observations, each with tone, statement, rule record, supporting and contradictory items, limitations |\n| `layers.navamshaA`, `layers.navamshaB` | Layer 2 observations and the D-9 chart |\n| `layers.mutual` | Layer 3: aspect system, items (label, kind, value, note, school), warnings |\n| `layers.dashakoota` | Layer 4: the frozen Koota import (profile, total, max, rows, doshas) |\n| `layers.jaimini` | Separate profile `JAIMINI_COMMON_TEACHING`: karakas, Upapada (raw and exception), Karakamsha |\n| `layers.timing` | Reference date, year length, running periods |\n| `layers.practical` | The discussion prompts and the practitioner's notes (no numeric field) |\n| `completeness` | SUFFICIENT_FOR_REVIEW or INSUFFICIENT_INFORMATION, with the missing items |\n| `categoryOptions`, `selectedCategory` | Six options; null until the practitioner chooses |\n| `contradictoryFlags` | Lists of contradictions to review; no counts or weights |\n| `disclaimer` | The responsible-use statement |\n\n### Pseudocode\nBuilding a chart:\n- Validate the input: ayanamsha and source named, house system supported.\n- For each graha, compute sign, degree, Nakshatra, Pada and D-9 sign from the longitude (integer milli-arcseconds).\n- If the Lagna is entered and the birth time is known, build whole-sign houses; otherwise mark the Lagna and houses UNAVAILABLE. Never guess.\n\nDignity of a planet:\n- If its sign is the exaltation sign, Exalted; else if the debilitation sign, Debilitated.\n- Else if the degree is inside its Moolatrikona range, Moolatrikona; else if its own sign, Own sign.\n- Else apply Panchadha: natural friendship with the sign lord combined with temporary friendship.\n\nArudha of a house (for Upapada, house 12):\n- Take the house sign `s` and its lord. Count `d` signs from `s` to the lord's sign, inclusive.\n- Raw arudha = the sign `d` signs on from the lord's sign, inclusive of the lord's sign.\n- If the raw arudha equals `s` or the 7th from `s`, the exception result is the 10th from the raw arudha; otherwise the two results are equal.\n- Return both. The selected exception rule decides which one feeds the lines derived from Upapada.\n\nVimshottari dasha:\n- Find the Moon's Nakshatra index `n` and the fraction `f` already covered.\n- First lord = ORDER[`n` mod 9]; balance = (1 − `f`) × that lord's years.\n- Mahadasha dates start from birth − `f` × years × year length; antardasha = MD × AD ÷ 120 years; pratyantardasha = AD length × PD years ÷ 120.\n\nSynthesis:\n- Keep every layer in its own object. Freeze the Koota import.\n- List contradictions between supportive and challenging items; do not count or weight them.\n- Set the category to null: the practitioner chooses it.\n\n### Error handling and uncertain data\nMissing ayanamsha or ephemeris source: error. Unsupported house system: error. Unknown birth time: Lagna, houses, lords, D-9 Lagna, Upapada, functional nature unavailable. Missing planet: NOT_COMPUTED. Different ayanamshas: flagged. Equal Chara Karaka degrees: review required. Impossible Mercury or Venus elongation: warning. No rule is silently substituted.\n\n### Test plan\nThe Validation tab runs the checks live (calculation, analytical, separation, missing or uncertain data). Categories: D-9 known answers and agreement with the Calculation Lab for 360 longitudes; whole-sign houses; dignity, combustion and aspect tables; Chara Karaka ordering and ties; Upapada hand-checked examples including exceptions; Vimshottari known-answer tests; interpretation text safety; frozen Koota import and unchanged synthesis; absence of any numeric score; unknown-time behaviour; different-ayanamsha flag; schematic case integrity.\n\n## Instructor Guide\n\n### Special teaching note\nDo not teach Koota as the complete marriage analysis. Teach it as one layer among six, with its own mathematics and its own limits, and never allow a chart-level observation to rewrite a Koota result.\n\n### 120-minute lesson plan\n| Time | Subject | Teaching method | Practical activity |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0–10 | Why Koota Milan is not the complete analysis | Concept explanation | Distinguish promise from compatibility |\n| 10–30 | Individual marriage promise | Chart demonstration | 7th house and 7th lord examination |\n| 30–45 | Relevant houses and significators | Comparative teaching | Individual chart worksheet |\n| 45–60 | Navamsha analysis | D-1/D-9 comparison | D-9 exercise |\n| 60–75 | Mutual chart compatibility | Comparative chart analysis | Moon and 7th-lord comparison |\n| 75–85 | Jaimini factors | Method demonstration | Darakaraka and Upapada exercise |\n| 85–100 | Dasha and transit | Timing analysis | Identify relevant periods |\n| 100–112 | Dashakoota integration | Case study | Layer-wise assessment |\n| 112–120 | Final synthesis | Discussion | Report preparation |\n\n### Whiteboard demonstrations\n(1) Six boxes, six arrows to a seventh box labelled \"report\", with the label \"no total\" on the report. (2) The D-9 table of movable, fixed and dual start signs. (3) The Upapada count on a 12-sign wheel, both with and without the exception. (4) A timeline of two people's mahadashas.\n\n### Expected student responses and misconceptions\n- \"A high Koota total means the charts are fine.\" Reply: Koota reads only the Moon.\n- \"A supportive D-9 cancels the Dosha.\" Reply: Only a named rule can; D-9 is a separate observation.\n- \"Saturn in the 7th delays marriage.\" Reply: An isolated absolute rule; read dignity, aspects, D-9 and the school.\n- \"We can average the layers.\" Reply: They are different evidence with no common unit.\n- \"The software knows the planets.\" Reply: Only the Moon; all other longitudes are entered.\n\n### Participation, homework and assessment\nPairs build the schematic Case 7 and Case 8 and explain the difference in one minute each. Homework: complete assignments 1 to 12 on a loaded pair and write a one-page layered report with a self-chosen category. Assessment criteria: layers kept separate; rule and school named; contradictions reported; ethical wording.\n\n## Ethics and Wording\n\n### Professional conduct\n- Astrology is a traditional interpretative framework, not a scientifically established method for predicting marital success.\n- A Koota score is never a guarantee.\n- One unfavourable placement must not be used to frighten or pressure anyone.\n- No predictions of divorce, widowhood, death, illness, infertility or children.\n- Indicators are never medical or psychological diagnoses.\n- Consent and individual choice remain central.\n- Practical compatibility is for the two people to discuss directly.\n- Birth-time uncertainty is disclosed.\n- Different schools may reach different conclusions; the practitioner explains the framework, evidence, limitations and uncertainty.\n\n### Responsible and irresponsible wording\n| Irresponsible | Responsible |\n|---|---|\n| \"This marriage will not last.\" | \"Several items in these charts need discussion; none is a prediction.\" |\n| \"Saturn in the 7th means delay.\" | \"Saturn's placement is one factor commonly examined; its effect depends on dignity, aspects, D-9 and the school.\" |\n| \"Your Nadi Dosha cancels your chances.\" | \"A Nadi Dosha is identified under Tradition A as a traditional classification; whether any school's exception applies is a separate question.\" |\n| \"The Navamsha is strong, so the Dosha is gone.\" | \"The Navamsha adds a supportive observation; the Dosha record is unchanged.\" |\n| \"85% compatible.\" | \"Each layer is reported separately; there is no combined figure.\" |\n| \"Mars in the 7th will damage the marriage.\" | \"Mars in the 7th is noted for discussion; effect depends on sign, aspects, strength and the school.\" |\n| \"The charts prove you should marry.\" | \"The charts offer observations to discuss; the decision is yours.\" |\n| \"The Moon is afflicted, so the mind is unstable.\" | \"The Moon's association is noted; no psychological statement is made.\" |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Merging layers** into one score or one paragraph.\n2. **Treating Koota as the complete marriage analysis.**\n3. **Rewriting the Koota result** after seeing a supportive chart.\n4. **Calling supportive Navamsha a cancellation.**\n5. **Reading one placement alone** (\"Saturn always delays\").\n6. **Mixing Parashari and Jaimini** rules.\n7. **Mixing aspect systems** in one comparison.\n8. **Different ayanamshas** for the two charts.\n9. **Guessing a Lagna** when the birth time is unknown.\n10. **Treating combustion orbs as a universal table.**\n11. **Forgetting to declare the dasha year length.**\n12. **Treating a favourable dasha or transit as proof** of compatibility.\n13. **Using gender stereotypes** to assign Venus or Jupiter different meanings.\n14. **Predicting** divorce, widowhood, illness, infertility or children.\n15. **Ignoring the practical conversation** between the two people.\n16. **Presenting an overlay as a classical rule.**\n17. **Applying a tie-break** to equal Chara Karaka degrees.\n18. **Silently applying the Upapada exception.**\n19. **Treating schematic charts as real positions.**\n20. **Hiding contradictory findings.**\n21. **Quoting an unverified source** as classical.\n22. **Choosing a category by counting supportive items.**\n\n## Validation\n\n### Final quality-control checklist\n- ✓ Individual marriage promise and mutual compatibility are separated.\n- ✓ Both horoscopes are analysed independently.\n- ✓ 7th house and 7th lord are treated in detail (house-wise table 1 to 12).\n- ✓ Venus and Jupiter are interpreted contextually.\n- ✓ The 2nd, 4th, 5th, 8th and 12th houses are addressed without deterministic claims.\n- ✓ Functional planetary nature is considered.\n- ✓ D-9 is not treated as a replacement for D-1; D-1 and D-9 calculations are internally consistent (tested against the Calculation Lab for 360 longitudes).\n- ✓ Mutual comparisons are separate from individual analysis.\n- ✓ Parashari and Jaimini are separate profiles.\n- ✓ Darakaraka and Upapada calculations are tested; the exception is shown, never silently applied.\n- ✓ Dasha and transit are not substitutes for natal analysis.\n- ✓ The Koota result is frozen and independently visible.\n- ✓ Practical compatibility is not an astrological calculation.\n- ✓ No combined numeric formula exists (tested).\n- ✓ Conflicting indications are reported, not hidden.\n- ✓ All case studies are internally consistent and labelled schematic.\n- ✓ Sources are recorded as class E (contemporary documentation) and unread in full; no verse, chapter or page is invented.\n- ✓ Software output is explainable: every statement shows its rule, school, evidence and limits.\n- ✓ Ethical and non-deterministic reporting standards are followed.\n**Limits to remember:** only the Moon is computed; all other longitudes are practitioner-entered; whole-sign houses only; Shadbala not computed; interpretation rules are contemporary teaching conventions (E, unverified).\n{{m13tests}}\n","14":"# Day 14 – Professional Matchmaking Practice and Final Synthesis\n\n## Learn\n\n### Today's learning question\n> How do I take two sets of birth details through the whole process, calculate and verify every Koota, test the Doshas and exceptions honestly, examine both horoscopes and their relationship, state the limits, and write a balanced professional report that I can explain and defend, without ever combining everything into one invented score?\n\n### Learning objectives\n- Collect and validate birth details, and state how reliable the birth time is.\n- Select one named tradition and a reproducible calculation profile.\n- Calculate every Koota with working, and cross-check the results.\n- Tell a low score from an independent Dosha, and test each exception against its own conditions.\n- Compare Tradition A with the documented Tamil provisional profile without converting or combining scores.\n- Examine each D-1 chart, each D-9 chart, the mutual chart, and the Dasha periods as separate layers.\n- Write a complete report (Sections A to L), a short executive summary, and a consultation in non-alarmist language.\n- Audit your own work before a report is issued, and handle a different result from another astrologer respectfully.\n\n### What today is, and is not\nToday is the **capstone**: you work through five teaching cases and then one unseen examination pair. The course gives you the inputs and keeps the answers in a separate instructor key.\n\n**Honesty about the data.** This app calculates only the **Moon** (an approximate engine; verify with a named ephemeris before professional use). The **Lagna and the eight other grahas in the cases are schematic teaching values**: they are internally consistent, and everything derived from them (sign, house, D-9, dignity, aspects, Dasha) is calculated correctly, but they are not astronomically calculated positions and must never be presented as real birth charts.\n\n### How to use today\nWork in the order of the tabs. Before you look at an engine result for a teaching case, do your own working and tick the confirmation box. Use the dashboard to track your progress.\n{{p14dash}}\n\n### The guiding principle of the course\n> Calculate correctly. Verify every rule. Understand both horoscopes. Compare with discrimination. Interpret without fear. Report with transparency.\n\n## Foundation Theory\n\n### 14.1 What the first thirteen days built\n| Days | Area | What you can now do |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Foundations, Moon position | Convert a sidereal Moon longitude into Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada; state boundaries |\n| 3–9 | Vashya, Tara, Yoni, Graha Maitri, Gana, Bhakoot, Nadi | Calculate each Koota and explain its limits |\n| 10 | Varna, Rajju, Vedha; Mahendra and Stree Deergha | Complete Tradition A; place the supplements outside the 36 points |\n| 11 | Schools of thought | Compare traditions as separate profiles; no score conversion |\n| 12 | Doshas and cancellation | Separate identified, mitigated and cancelled; keep the raw score |\n| 13 | Complete horoscope | Examine D-1, D-9, mutual chart and Dasha as separate layers |\n\nToday you combine these skills in one professional method.\n\n### 14.1a Key terms with their Hindi forms\n| Term | Hindi | Use today |\n|---|---|---|\n| Koota | कूट | One factor of the matching method |\n| Dosha | दोष | A named unfavourable condition |\n| Parihara | परिहार | An exception or cancellation, with its source and conditions |\n| Rashi, Nakshatra, Pada | राशि, नक्षत्र, चरण | The Moon's sign, star and quarter |\n| Graha Maitri | ग्रह मैत्री | Friendship of the Moon-sign lords |\n| Porutham | पोरुत्तम | The regional (Tamil) factor term |\n| Rajju, Vedha | रज्जु, वेध | Pass-or-Dosha checks with no points |\n| Lagna, Saptama Bhava | लग्न, सप्तम भाव | Ascendant and seventh house |\n| Navamsha | नवांश | The ninth-part chart (D-9) |\n| Ayanamsha | अयनांश | The offset between tropical and sidereal longitude |\n| Mahadasha, Antardasha | महादशा, अन्तर्दशा | Main and sub periods of Vimshottari |\n| Darakaraka | दारकारक | The Jaimini significator found by the lowest degree |\n\n### 14.2 Why a method matters\nTwo astrologers can give different results for the same couple and both be following a recognised method. A report is useful only when another trained person can see **which tradition, which settings, which rules and which sources** produced each statement. The method below makes that possible.\n\n### 14.3 The twelve stages\n1. Client information and birth-data collection.\n2. Birth-data validation.\n3. Calculation profile selection.\n4. Selection of the matching tradition.\n5. Individual Koota calculation.\n6. Dosha and exception examination.\n7. Individual horoscope examination.\n8. Navamsha examination.\n9. Mutual chart comparison.\n10. Timing and practical considerations.\n11. Final synthesis.\n12. Professional report and consultation.\n\nThe Workflow tab shows these as a diagram, with the check to make before moving on.\n\n### 14.4 The accuracy of the birth time\n| Category | Meaning | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Official birth record | Time from a hospital or civil record | Still check the time zone and daylight saving |\n| Family-recorded time | Remembered or written by the family | State it; compute the Moon at ±15 minutes |\n| Approximate time | \"About noon\", \"early morning\" | Compute the Moon at ±30 minutes; mark Lagna findings uncertain |\n| Rectified time | Adjusted by a practitioner from life events | Name the method; it remains an estimate |\n| Unverified time | Source unknown | Report Moon-based Kootas with a boundary warning; chart layers are not reliable |\n\nThe Moon moves about 13° in a day, roughly 0.55° in an hour. A Pada is 3°20′, so a one-hour error can change the Pada, and a few hours can change the Nakshatra.\n\n### 14.5 Layers, and why there is no combined score\nThe Koota total, the Dosha register, each person's promise, each D-9, the mutual chart, the timing and the practical matters are different kinds of evidence on different scales. No source defines a formula that adds them. A \"compatibility percentage\" would invent one. The final synthesis is therefore **qualitative and layered**: a short statement for each layer and an overall reporting category with its reasons.\n\n## Detailed Rules\n\n### 14.6 The Koota sheet (Tradition A)\n| Koota | Inputs | Result | Maximum |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Varna | Moon Rashi of each person | 1 or 0 (direction: groom's class equal or higher than bride's) | 1 |\n| Vashya | Rashi group of each person | 0 to 2 | 2 |\n| Tara | Nakshatra counts in both directions | 3, 1.5 or 0 | 3 |\n| Yoni | Yoni of each Nakshatra | 0 to 4 | 4 |\n| Graha Maitri | Lords of the Moon signs | 0 to 5 | 5 |\n| Gana | Gana of each Nakshatra | 0 to 6 | 6 |\n| Bhakoot | Moon-sign counts in both directions | 7 or 0 | 7 |\n| Nadi | Nadi of each Nakshatra | 8 or 0 | 8 |\n| Rajju | Rajju group of each Nakshatra | pass or Dosha | no points |\n| Vedha | Listed pair check | pass or Dosha | no points |\n\nThe scored total is the sum of the first eight, out of 36. Rajju and Vedha add nothing. Mahendra and Stree Deergha are shown separately as regional supplements.\n\n### 14.7 Dosha, score deficiency and the register\nA **recognised Dosha** is a condition the tradition names (for example the same Nadi, a 2/12, 5/9 or 6/8 Bhakoot pair, a Gana score of 0 or 1 in Profile A, the same Rajju group, a listed Vedha pair, a sworn-enemy Yoni pair). A **score deficiency** is only a lower number (for example Yoni 2 of 4). The register records each separately, with its original status. The total never overrides a Dosha.\n\n### 14.8 Exceptions and cancellation\nFor every Dosha, list the exception rules, their conditions, whether the conditions are met for this pair, the source and the evidence level. In this course every exception is **unverified** (evidence Level 7), so each is recorded as **identified, not applied**. Four different things must stay apart:\n| Concept | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Complete cancellation | A named source says the Dosha does not operate under stated conditions |\n| Partial cancellation | The Dosha is weakened but not removed |\n| Mitigation | Other factors support the pair; the Dosha remains |\n| Score restoration | The profile explicitly restores points; shown as \"adjusted\" beside the raw score |\n\nWhere no rule exists, write exactly: \"No sufficiently verified cancellation rule identified for this selected tradition.\"\n\n### 14.9 Settings you must declare\n| Setting | Declared value in the cases |\n|---|---|\n| Ayanamsha | Lahiri (Chitra Paksha), with a second ayanamsha for cross-checking |\n| Moon engine | ASTROSUTRAS approximate Meeus-based Moon |\n| House system | Whole sign from the Lagna sign |\n| Nodes | Mean Rahu; Ketu opposite |\n| Navamsha | Sign = floor(longitude ÷ 3°20′) mod 12, counted from Aries |\n| Dasha | Vimshottari, year of 365.25 days (360 days as a setting), dates in UT |\n| Aspects | Parashari graha drishti; Rahu and Ketu without aspects (a separate profile gives them 5, 7, 9) |\n| Dignity | Common table of exaltation, own sign, moolatrikona, natural friendship (grade C/D, not source-verified) |\n| Combustion | A declared orb table; orbs differ between sources |\n\n### 14.9a Mutual-chart rules\nClassical Koota rules and modern comparative techniques are kept apart. The Moon-to-Moon, Lagna-to-Lagna, seventh-lord, Venus, Jupiter and D-9 comparisons are reading aids. Cross-chart aspects by sign overlay are a modern comparative technique and are labelled so. Jaimini factors (Darakaraka) are a separate school and are never mixed into a Parashari conclusion.\n\n### 14.10 The report\nA complete report has twelve sections: A birth data, B Moon and Nakshatra, C Koota analysis, D Dosha analysis, E regional comparison, F Person A, G Person B, H mutual chart, I timing, J practical discussion, K final synthesis, L disclaimer. The Report writing tab builds it and exports a printable page and a structured JSON file that carries the report ID, version and calculation-profile metadata.\n\n## Different Schools\n\n### Choosing the tradition\nBefore calculating, name one tradition and its profile. The client's family tradition is the first thing to ask about.\n| Tradition or profile | Status in this course | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tradition A: Ashtakoota + Rajju + Vedha | Frozen course reference (common tables, grade C/D) | Full calculation with working |\n| North Indian Ashtakoota base (eight Kootas) | Implemented | Same eight Kootas without Rajju and Vedha |\n| Tamil Pathu Porutham | Provisional profile: ten factors documented on contemporary websites; several thresholds derived | Count qualifying factors out of ten; never convert |\n| Telugu Dasha Koota | Pending research | Regional practice documented, but a sufficiently verified independent rule profile is not yet established |\n| Kannada Dasha Koota | Pending research | Same sentence |\n| Kerala Anukulya | Pending research; Chovva Dosham is an input-based check with two unverified variants | State the variant; apply no cancellation |\n| Other regional or lineage method | Only if rule data and sources are documented | Otherwise pending research |\n\nNever borrow a convenient rule from another tradition. First identify the tradition, then its source, then calculate by that rule.\n\n### Software and other astrologers\nA different result from another tool can come from the ayanamsha, the Moon longitude, time-zone or daylight-saving handling, boundary precision, or a different rule table. Compare settings first. The Audit tab has a checklist.\n\n## Exceptions\n\n### Missing or uncertain data\n- Unknown birth time: Moon-based Kootas may still be calculated, with a stated confidence; Lagna, houses, D-9 and Dasha dates are not reliable.\n- Approximate time: compute the Moon at both ends of the range and report whether a Pada or Nakshatra boundary is crossed.\n- A daylight-saving record: confirm the historical rule; a wrong hour moves the Moon by about half a degree.\n- A second ayanamsha can move the Pada (and, near a boundary, the Nakshatra). Report it; do not hide it.\n\n### Rules that look the same but are not\n- Mutual friendship needs both directions. One-sided friendship is a different condition.\n- A supportive Navamsha is a separate observation. It is not a formal cancellation of a Koota Dosha.\n- A favourable Dasha does not change a Koota result.\n- A Tamil qualifying count is on a different scale from a 36-point total.\n\n### When two profiles give different results\nShow both, separately, with the rule that causes the difference. Do not average, convert or pick the higher one.\n\n## Examples\n\n### A worked demonstration\nThe demonstration pair below is **not** one of the five cases. It shows the whole sheet, the register, the exception candidates and the model language.\n{{p14demo}}\n\n### How to read it\nFollow the working line by line. Notice that the exception category is examined, found unsatisfied (one-sided friendship), and that even a satisfied category would be recorded as \"identified, not applied\". Notice that the Tamil count is reported on its own scale.\n\n## Practice\n\n### Exercise set 1 – the twelve assignments (for every case)\nFor each teaching case, complete these in order. Keep your own workbook; do not open the instructor key until you have finished.\n| No. | Assignment | What to submit |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Birth-data verification | Date, time, offset, coordinates, accuracy, calculation profile; time-sensitivity range |\n| 2 | Moon and Nakshatra | Moon longitude, Rashi, Rashi lord, Nakshatra, Pada, Nakshatra lord, with method |\n| 3 | Koota calculation | All Kootas: inputs, formula or lookup, intermediate working, result, maximum |\n| 4 | Favourable combinations | List with significance and limits |\n| 5 | Unfavourable combinations | Low scores, Doshas, regional concerns; mathematics kept apart from interpretation |\n| 6 | Cancellation and exceptions | For each Dosha: condition, rule, source, conditions tested, status, original score preserved |\n| 7 | Regional comparison | Tradition A and the Tamil provisional profile, side by side, not combined |\n| 8 | Individual horoscopes | Seventh house and lord, houses 2, 4, 5, 8, 12, Venus, Jupiter, dignity, Dasha |\n| 9 | Navamsha | D-9 Lagna, seventh house and lord, Venus, Jupiter, D-1/D-9 comparison |\n| 10 | Mutual chart | Moon, Lagna, seventh lords, Venus and Jupiter, mutual aspects |\n| 11 | Timing | Mahadasha and Antardasha at the analysis date; limits |\n| 12 | Final report | The complete report, Sections A to L |\n\n### Exercise set 2 – five additional supervised assignments\n| No. | Task | Expected submission | Assessment criteria |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S1 | Time-zone audit: find which hour error would change the Pada in Case 04 | A short table of the Moon at several offsets | Correct offsets; boundary reasoning |\n| S2 | Rule-borrowing review: find three places where a rule from one tradition could be wrongly applied to another | A list with the correct treatment | Names the tradition; no merging |\n| S3 | Rewrite exercise: turn ten alarmist sentences into professional wording | The rewritten sentences | No promise, no threat, no medical claim |\n| S4 | Different-software exercise: explain two reasons why a second program might show a different Pada | A one-page note | Separates astronomy from rules |\n| S5 | Disagreement role-play: a client's family follows a different school | A two-minute recorded or live explanation | Respectful, specific, no defensiveness |\n\n### Exercise set 3 – the final practical examination\nThe unseen pair is in the Final practical examination tab.\n\n## Flashcards\nQ: What is the first step of the professional workflow?\nA: Collect the birth details (date, exact time, place, coordinates, time zone, daylight saving) and record the source and accuracy of the time.\n\nQ: Why must a matching result name its tradition and profile?\nA: Different traditions use different factor lists, tables, thresholds and exceptions; without them the result cannot be reproduced or compared.\n\nQ: What are the widths of a Rashi, a Nakshatra and a Pada?\nA: 30°, 13°20′ and 3°20′.\n\nQ: How is a sidereal longitude obtained from a tropical one?\nA: Subtract the ayanamsha.\n\nQ: How are boundaries treated in the Lab?\nA: As half-open intervals: a start point belongs to the new division. A Moon very close to a boundary gets a warning.\n\nQ: How many points does Vashya carry, and what are its groups?\nA: 2 points. The groups are Chatushpada, Manava, Jalachara, Vanachara and Keeta.\n\nQ: How is Tara counted?\nA: Inclusively in both directions from each Nakshatra, divided by 9; remainders 3, 5 and 7 are inauspicious (1.5 points lost in each direction).\n\nQ: What is the Janma variant of Tara?\nA: A profile in which remainder 1 earns no points. It is shown separately and never merged with the main result.\n\nQ: How is the Yoni matrix read?\nA: Bride's Yoni is the row, groom's Yoni is the column; seven pairs are sworn enemies. It is symbolic, not a statement about behaviour.\n\nQ: What does Graha Maitri compare?\nA: The natural friendship of the two Moon-sign lords, in both directions; the same lord scores 5.\n\nQ: How is a friend and a neutral scored?\nA: 4 of 5 (one-sided friendship is not mutual friendship).\n\nQ: What is the Gana Dosha flag in Profile A?\nA: A Gana score of 0 or 1.\n\nQ: Which Bhakoot pairs score 0?\nA: 2/12, 5/9 and 6/8.\n\nQ: Why is Taurus–Libra 6/8 and not 6/6?\nA: For two different signs the forward and reverse inclusive counts always add up to 14.\n\nQ: What is Nadi, and what does a same-Nadi pair score?\nA: Adi, Madhya or Antya from the Nakshatra; the same Nadi scores 0 of 8.\n\nQ: May Nadi Dosha be called a medical finding?\nA: No. It is a traditional classification result from the Nakshatras, not a genetic or medical finding.\n\nQ: What happens when both persons have the same Rajju group?\nA: Rajju Dosha is recorded; Rajju adds no points.\n\nQ: How many listed Vedha pairs are there in the course data?\nA: Fourteen; a listed pair is recorded as Vedha Dosha and adds no points.\n\nQ: Which direction does Varna use in the course profile, and what does it not mean?\nA: The groom's class equal to or higher than the bride's scores 1. It is a symbolic classification, never a judgement of caste, worth or ability.\n\nQ: Which counts are the favourable Mahendra counts?\nA: 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22 and 25 (bride to groom, inclusive); a supplementary regional factor.\n\nQ: What are the Stree Deergha thresholds?\nA: Count bride to groom more than 13 (common statement), with at least 7 as a variant; both outside Tradition A.\n\nQ: Name the ten factors of the Tamil provisional profile.\nA: Dina, Gana, Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Yoni, Rasi, Rasyadhipathi, Vasya, Rajju, Vedha.\n\nQ: What is the status of the Telugu, Kannada and Kerala profiles?\nA: Pending research: regional practice is documented, but a sufficiently verified independent rule profile is not yet established.\n\nQ: Why can 36 points not be converted into ten Poruthams?\nA: The factor lists, scales and weights differ, and no verified model defines a conversion.\n\nQ: Distinguish cancellation, mitigation and score restoration.\nA: Cancellation: a named source says the Dosha does not operate. Mitigation: other factors help while the Dosha remains. Restoration: the profile explicitly restores points, shown as adjusted beside the raw score.\n\nQ: What does \"identified, not applied\" mean?\nA: The exception category exists and may be met, but no verified source attaches to it, so the Dosha status is unchanged.\n\nQ: Why does a high total not cancel a Dosha?\nA: The total sums different Kootas; each Dosha is tested against its own conditions.\n\nQ: How is a house numbered in the whole-sign system?\nA: By the distance of its sign from the Lagna sign: the Lagna sign is house 1.\n\nQ: How is the seventh lord found?\nA: It is the lord of the seventh sign from the Lagna sign.\n\nQ: Which special aspects do Mars, Jupiter and Saturn have (Parashari)?\nA: Mars 4, 7, 8; Jupiter 5, 7, 9; Saturn 3, 7, 10 (counted from the planet's house).\n\nQ: What are the exaltation signs of the seven planets?\nA: Sun Aries, Moon Taurus, Mars Capricorn, Mercury Virgo, Jupiter Cancer, Venus Pisces, Saturn Libra.\n\nQ: What is combustion?\nA: A planet very close to the Sun; the orb differs by source, so the table used must be declared.\n\nQ: How is the Navamsha sign calculated?\nA: floor(sidereal longitude ÷ 3°20′) mod 12, counted from Aries as 0.\n\nQ: What is Vargottama?\nA: A planet in the same sign in D-1 and D-9.\n\nQ: What is the role of D-9 in a report?\nA: A supplementary chart that adds context; it neither replaces D-1 nor cancels a Koota Dosha.\n\nQ: What is the Vimshottari order and total?\nA: Ketu 7, Venus 20, Sun 6, Moon 10, Mars 7, Rahu 18, Jupiter 16, Saturn 19, Mercury 17: 120 years.\n\nQ: How is the Dasha balance at birth found?\nA: The first lord's full years multiplied by the fraction of the Moon's Nakshatra still to be crossed.\n\nQ: Why is a Dasha period not a score?\nA: It is a timing observation under a declared year-length convention, not an event date and not a compatibility measure.\n\nQ: How should a transit be used?\nA: As a practitioner-entered observation read with the natal promise and the Dasha, never alone.\n\nQ: List the layers of the final synthesis.\nA: Koota, Dosha, each person's promise, each Navamsha, mutual chart, timing and practical matters.\n\nQ: Why is there no combined percentage?\nA: The layers use different scales and no source defines a formula; a percentage would be invented.\n\nQ: What does the birth-data validation check?\nA: Date, time, offset, daylight saving, coordinates, historical zone changes, and whether the Moon result is plausible.\n\nQ: Name the five categories of birth-time accuracy.\nA: Official record, family-recorded, approximate, rectified, unverified.\n\nQ: What does Section K of the report contain?\nA: Supportive findings, challenging findings, unresolved findings, school-specific differences and the final explanation with a qualitative category.\n\nQ: What is the professional way to describe Nadi Dosha?\nA: \"A traditional compatibility classification; it is not medical evidence.\"\n\nQ: How should you respond when a client follows another school?\nA: Ask which school and settings, compare rules and calculations respectfully, and keep both results visible.\n\nQ: What is the first item of the code of practice?\nA: Disclose the selected tradition and profile.\n\nQ: What does the audit check before issue?\nA: Birth data, offset, ayanamsha, Moon, Nakshatra, Pada, Koota scores, D-1, D-9, Dasha, aspects, rule sources, evidence levels, disclaimers and the absence of absolute claims.\n\nQ: How do you correct an error found after a report is issued?\nA: Issue a new version with the change recorded; never alter the issued report silently.\n\nQ: Who decides about a marriage?\nA: The two individuals, with mutual consent.\n\nQ: What must the second ayanamsha check tell you?\nA: Whether the Rashi, Nakshatra or Pada changes; if it does, the report says so.\n\nQ: What is a schematic teaching value?\nA: A position that is internally consistent for practice but not calculated from an ephemeris.\n\nQ: What are the three certificate categories?\nA: Participation, successful completion, and passing the final practical examination.\n\n## Assessment\nQ: The Tradition A scored total is the sum of:\nA) All ten Kootas, out of 40\nB) The eight Kootas from Varna to Nadi, out of 36\nC) The eight Kootas plus Rajju and Vedha points, out of 44\nD) Rajju and Vedha only\nANS: B\nWHY: Rajju and Vedha are pass or Dosha checks with no points.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which two Tradition A checks add no points?\nA) Varna and Vashya\nB) Nadi and Bhakoot\nC) Tara and Yoni\nD) Rajju and Vedha\nANS: D\nWHY: They are reported as pass or Dosha.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How large is one Pada of the Moon's path?\nA) 6°40′\nB) 1°20′\nC) 3°20′\nD) 13°20′\nANS: C\nWHY: A Nakshatra of 13°20′ has four Padas.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The Navamsha sign of a sidereal longitude L is:\nA) floor(L ÷ 3°20′) mod 12, counted from Aries as 0\nB) floor(L ÷ 13°20′) mod 12\nC) floor(L ÷ 30°)\nD) L mod 9\nANS: A\nWHY: Each 3°20′ is one Navamsha and the signs repeat every 12.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which Koota has the highest weight in the 36-point system?\nA) Bhakoot (7)\nB) Nadi (8)\nC) Gana (6)\nD) Graha Maitri (5)\nANS: B\nWHY: The weights are 1 to 8 from Varna to Nadi.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Why must a matching result always name its tradition and profile?\nA) Because different traditions use different factor lists, tables and thresholds, so the result cannot be reproduced or compared without them\nB) Because the profile name is a prediction\nC) Because every tradition gives the same answer\nD) Because the profile decides the marriage date\nANS: A\nWHY: Reproducibility is the purpose of the declaration.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: For two different signs, the forward and reverse inclusive counts always add up to:\nA) 12\nB) 14\nC) 13\nD) 15\nANS: B\nWHY: (d + 1) + (12 − d + 1) = 14.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which list matches the Tamil provisional Pathu Porutham profile of this course?\nA) The eight Ashtakoota Kootas\nB) Varna, Vashya, Tara, Yoni, Graha Maitri, Gana, Bhakoot, Nadi, Rajju, Vedha\nC) Tradition A plus Mahendra and Stree Deergha\nD) Dina, Gana, Mahendra, Stree Deergha, Yoni, Rasi, Rasyadhipathi, Vasya, Rajju, Vedha\nANS: D\nWHY: The ten-factor Tamil list differs from the Tradition A list; Varna and Nadi are not in it.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which counts (bride to groom) are the favourable Mahendra counts in the commonly quoted statement?\nA) 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25\nB) 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12\nC) 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24\nD) 1, 5, 9, 13, 17, 21, 25\nANS: A\nWHY: Counts increase by 3 from 4 to 25.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The five Rajju groups are:\nA) Aja, Gaja, Simha, Sarpa, Mesha\nB) Brahmin, Kshatriya, Vaishya, Shudra, Antya\nC) Deva, Manushya, Rakshasa, Adi, Madhya\nD) Pada, Kati, Nabhi, Kantha, Siro\nANS: D\nWHY: The groups are named after body regions as symbols; no medical meaning is intended.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many years does the full Vimshottari cycle cover?\nA) 120\nB) 100\nC) 144\nD) 108\nANS: A\nWHY: 7 + 20 + 6 + 10 + 7 + 18 + 16 + 19 + 17 = 120.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A sidereal longitude is obtained from a tropical longitude by:\nA) Adding the ayanamsha\nB) Dividing by 27\nC) Subtracting the ayanamsha\nD) Adding 13°20′\nANS: C\nWHY: The ayanamsha is the offset between the two zodiacs.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: How many Nakshatras are there in the course, and how wide is each?\nA) 27, each 12°\nB) 28, each 12°51′\nC) 12, each 30°\nD) 27, each 13°20′\nANS: D\nWHY: 360° ÷ 27 = 13°20′.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which statement about the Koota total is correct?\nA) It cancels every Dosha above 18 points\nB) It never overrides an identified Dosha\nC) It can be converted into a ten-Porutham count\nD) It predicts marriage success\nANS: B\nWHY: The total is a sum of different Kootas; each Dosha is tested separately.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: In this course Mahendra and Stree Deergha are:\nA) Part of the 36 points\nB) Replacements for Nadi\nC) Supplementary regional factors outside the 36-point Tradition A total\nD) Compulsory in every tradition\nANS: C\nWHY: They are shown separately with no points.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: The forward inclusive Nakshatra count from Ashwini to Pushya is:\nA) 7\nB) 8\nC) 9\nD) 21\nANS: B\nWHY: Ashwini is 1; Pushya is the 8th Nakshatra.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The reverse inclusive count from Pushya back to Ashwini is:\nA) 21\nB) 8\nC) 19\nD) 22\nANS: A\nWHY: The two counts add to 29: (8) + (21).\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A Tara count of 21 gives, after dividing by 9:\nA) Remainder 3, Sampat (auspicious)\nB) Remainder 3, Vipat (inauspicious)\nC) Remainder 9, Parama Mitra (auspicious)\nD) Remainder 2, Sampat (auspicious)\nANS: B\nWHY: 21 = 2 × 9 + 3, and remainder 3 is Vipat.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Moon signs Gemini and Scorpio form which Bhakoot pair, and what is the score?\nA) 6/8 (Shadashtaka), 0\nB) 5/9 (Navapanchama), 0\nC) 2/12 (Dwirdwadasha), 0\nD) 6/8 (Shadashtaka), 7\nANS: A\nWHY: Gemini to Scorpio is 6 and Scorpio to Gemini is 8.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Aries and Libra form which Bhakoot pair?\nA) 6/8, score 0\nB) 5/9, score 0\nC) 2/12, score 0\nD) 7/7, score 7\nANS: D\nWHY: Opposite signs count 7 in both directions.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Graha Maitri for Aries and Leo (lords Mars and Sun):\nA) 3\nB) 4\nC) 5 (mutual friends)\nD) 0\nANS: C\nWHY: Mars and the Sun regard each other as friends.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Graha Maitri for Gemini and Capricorn (lords Mercury and Saturn):\nA) 5\nB) 3\nC) 4 (Saturn regards Mercury as a friend; Mercury regards Saturn as neutral)\nD) 1\nANS: C\nWHY: A friend and a neutral score 4; this is one-sided friendship.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Nadi for Hasta (Adi) and Chitra (Madhya):\nA) 0, same Nadi\nB) 4\nC) 8, different Nadi\nD) 6\nANS: C\nWHY: Different Nadis score 8.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Nadi for Rohini and Magha (both Antya):\nA) 8\nB) 4\nC) 0, Nadi Dosha identified (a traditional classification, not a medical finding)\nD) Not applicable\nANS: C\nWHY: The same Nadi scores 0.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Rajju for Ashwini and Revati (both Pada):\nA) Same group (Pada): Rajju Dosha, no points\nB) Same group but no Dosha\nC) Different groups: pass\nD) Siro and Pada: pass\nANS: A\nWHY: The same Rajju group is recorded as Dosha.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Vedha for Ashwini and Jyeshtha:\nA) Not listed: pass\nB) A listed Vedha pair: Vedha Dosha, no points\nC) Score 2\nD) Gives 4 points\nANS: B\nWHY: The pair is on the course Vedha list.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Mahendra with bride Ashwini and groom Hasta:\nA) Count 12, not favourable\nB) Count 14, not favourable\nC) Count 13, favourable\nD) Count 4, favourable\nANS: C\nWHY: Hasta is the 13th Nakshatra from Ashwini, inclusive.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Stree Deergha with bride Hasta and groom Ashwini:\nA) Count 16, passes the more-than-13 threshold\nB) Count 11, passes\nC) Count 16, fails\nD) Count 3, fails\nANS: A\nWHY: ((0 − 12 + 27) mod 27) + 1 = 16.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Stree Deergha with bride Rohini and groom Ardra:\nA) Count 3, passes the at-least-7 variant\nB) Count 13, passes\nC) Count 25, passes\nD) Count 3, fails both the more-than-13 and the at-least-7 thresholds\nANS: D\nWHY: Ardra is the third Nakshatra counted from Rohini.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The Moon at Libra 20° (200°) falls in which Navamsha sign?\nA) Libra\nB) Scorpio\nC) Taurus\nD) Aries\nANS: D\nWHY: floor(200 ÷ 3.333) = 60, and 60 mod 12 = 0 (Aries).\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Which statement separates cancellation from mitigation?\nA) They are two words for the same thing\nB) Mitigation restores the points automatically\nC) Cancellation: a named source says the Dosha does not operate under stated conditions. Mitigation: other factors support the pair while the Dosha remains\nD) Cancellation happens whenever the total is above 18\nANS: C\nWHY: Mitigation never removes the Dosha.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: When may lost points be restored after a cancellation?\nA) Only if the selected profile explicitly authorises it, with the adjusted score labelled beside the raw score\nB) Always, after any cancellation\nC) Whenever the Moon signs are friendly\nD) Whenever the total is above 18\nANS: A\nWHY: Restoration is a separate policy.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Mercury regards Saturn as neutral; Saturn regards Mercury as a friend. For a rule that needs \"mutual friends\", this pair is:\nA) Mutual friends: the condition is met\nB) The same lord: the condition is met\nC) Enemies: the condition is met\nD) One-sided friendship: the condition is not met\nANS: D\nWHY: Both directions must be friendly.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: If no sufficiently verified cancellation rule exists for a Dosha in the selected tradition, the report says:\nA) The Dosha is cancelled by default\nB) \"No sufficiently verified cancellation rule identified for this selected tradition.\"\nC) Use a rule from another tradition\nD) The Dosha is ignored\nANS: B\nWHY: The gap is stated, not filled.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A report that also computed Tara under \"Janma inauspicious\" must:\nA) Hide the variant\nB) Replace the main result\nC) Average the two totals\nD) Show it as a separate variant and keep the main result unchanged\nANS: D\nWHY: Variants are never merged.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: \"Identified, not applied\" for an exception means:\nA) The exception category exists, but no verified source attaches to it, so the Dosha status is unchanged\nB) The score is restored\nC) The Dosha is cancelled\nD) The exception is proven\nANS: A\nWHY: It reports the status honestly.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: How should Nadi Dosha be described?\nA) A diagnosis of infertility\nB) A traditional classification result from the Nakshatras, not a medical or genetic finding\nC) A test of inherited disease\nD) A prediction about children\nANS: B\nWHY: Nadi is derived from the Nakshatra; it makes no medical statement.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A Yoni score of 2 of 4 is best recorded as:\nA) A score deficiency, not a flagged Dosha (a Dosha needs a sworn-enemy pair with score 0)\nB) A cancelled Dosha\nC) A Yoni Dosha\nD) A Rajju Dosha\nANS: A\nWHY: A lower number is not by itself a Dosha.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A cancellation rule repeated on many websites without a primary text is graded:\nA) Primary textual evidence\nB) Applied automatically\nC) Verified because it is common\nD) Unverified or a popular contemporary claim, never upgraded to a classical rule\nANS: D\nWHY: Repetition is not verification.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A pair scores 30 of 36 and has a Nadi Dosha. The correct report:\nA) States the Dosha cancelled by the high total\nB) States the total, lists the Nadi Dosha as identified, tests exceptions against their conditions and keeps the raw result\nC) Adds points to reach 36\nD) Gives only the total\nANS: B\nWHY: A high total never cancels a Dosha.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: For a Gemini Lagna, the seventh house is Sagittarius, so the seventh lord is:\nA) Mercury\nB) Venus\nC) Saturn\nD) Jupiter\nANS: D\nWHY: Jupiter rules Sagittarius.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: For a Capricorn Lagna, the seventh lord is:\nA) The Sun\nB) Saturn\nC) The Moon\nD) Mars\nANS: C\nWHY: The seventh sign from Capricorn is Cancer, ruled by the Moon.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: With Aries as the Lagna sign (whole-sign houses), a planet in Libra is in house:\nA) 6\nB) 8\nC) 7\nD) 1\nANS: C\nWHY: Libra is the seventh sign from Aries.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: In the Parashari system Jupiter aspects, counted from its own house:\nA) Houses 5, 7 and 9\nB) Houses 4, 7 and 8\nC) Houses 3, 7 and 10\nD) Houses 2, 6 and 12\nANS: A\nWHY: Jupiter's special aspects are 5 and 9, in addition to the 7th.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which pairing is correct?\nA) Saturn is exalted in Libra\nB) Mars is exalted in Cancer\nC) The Sun is exalted in Libra\nD) Venus is exalted in Virgo\nANS: A\nWHY: Venus is exalted in Pisces, Mars in Capricorn, the Sun in Aries.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Vargottama means:\nA) Retrograde in D-9\nB) A planet in the same sign in D-1 and D-9\nC) Exalted in D-1\nD) Combust\nANS: B\nWHY: The sign repeats in the Navamsha chart.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A planet is strong in D-1 and weak in D-9. The report:\nA) Lets the weak D-9 cancel the D-1\nB) States both, records the D-9 weakness as what needs qualification, and lets neither erase the other\nC) Lets the strong D-1 cancel the D-9\nD) Adds the two into a percentage\nANS: B\nWHY: Each chart says something different.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: A supportive Navamsha placement may be reported as:\nA) A separate supporting observation, not a formal cancellation of a Koota Dosha\nB) A cancellation of the Gana Dosha\nC) A restoration of Nadi points\nD) A proof of marital success\nANS: A\nWHY: D-9 is a supplementary chart.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nQ: In the seven-planet Jaimini scheme used here, the Darakaraka is the planet with:\nA) The highest degree within its sign\nB) The lowest degree within its sign among the seven planets\nC) The most exalted position\nD) The longest Dasha\nANS: B\nWHY: The planets are ranked by degree within the sign; the lowest is the Darakaraka.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: With the Moon at the very start of Bharani, the first Vimshottari Mahadasha and its balance are:\nA) Ketu, 7 years\nB) Sun, 6 years\nC) Venus, 20 years\nD) Venus, 10 years\nANS: C\nWHY: Bharani's lord is Venus and none of the Nakshatra has been crossed.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: The birth time is only \"approximate\". The professional response is:\nA) Refuse to calculate\nB) Treat it as exact\nC) State the accuracy, check whether the Moon changes Pada or Nakshatra within the range, and mark Lagna-dependent findings as uncertain\nD) Round it to noon\nANS: C\nWHY: Uncertainty is disclosed and measured.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: A client asks for one compatibility percentage. You:\nA) Give 85%\nB) Average the Koota total and the Tamil count\nC) Give 100% if the total is high\nD) Explain that the layers use different scales and no source defines a combined percentage\nANS: D\nWHY: Any such number would be invented.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Which wording is professional?\nA) \"This marriage will fail\"\nB) \"This factor is traditionally treated as a concern under the selected system\"\nC) \"Divorce is certain\"\nD) \"The Dosha is cancelled automatically\"\nANS: B\nWHY: It states the rule and its limit without a prediction.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Contradictory findings in different layers should be:\nA) Hidden\nB) Averaged\nC) Resolved by dropping the weaker layer\nD) Shown openly and explained\nANS: D\nWHY: The reader needs to see disagreement.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Practical compatibility (communication, finances, family, location) is:\nA) Calculated from the Moon\nB) Replaced by the Koota total\nC) Decided by the astrologer\nD) A set of matters for the two individuals to discuss, not an astrological calculation\nANS: D\nWHY: Astrology cannot measure these.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: After a report is issued, an error is found. You:\nA) Alter the issued report silently\nB) Issue a new version with the change recorded\nC) Ignore it\nD) Delete the report\nANS: B\nWHY: Issued reports are never silently altered.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: A client asks for a remedy for every Dosha. You say:\nA) A remedy is an optional personal practice; the report claims no outcome from it and no one is pressured\nB) Without it the marriage fails\nC) The remedy cancels the Dosha\nD) The remedy restores the points\nANS: A\nWHY: No outcome or pressure may be attached.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: Another astrologer reached a different result. You:\nA) Compare the settings and rules, show where they differ and keep both results visible\nB) Say the other is wrong\nC) Change your result\nD) Average both results\nANS: A\nWHY: Different valid settings can give different results.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nQ: To protect confidentiality a report uses:\nA) Full names on public pages\nB) Posting the chart online\nC) Sending it to relatives without consent\nD) Case identifiers and only the necessary details, shared with the people concerned\nANS: D\nWHY: Birth data is private.\nLVL: Basic\n\nQ: Who makes the decision about marriage?\nA) The astrologer\nB) The Koota total\nC) The two individuals, with mutual consent\nD) The family elders alone\nANS: C\nWHY: The report informs; it does not decide.\nLVL: Basic\n\nSA: Conceptual – How does a recognised Dosha differ from a score deficiency in a report?\nMA: A recognised Dosha is a condition the tradition names (for example the same Nadi); a score deficiency is only a lower number. They are recorded in separate rows of the register, and only the Dosha is tested against exception rules.\nLVL: Intermediate\n\nSA: Viva 1. Why is a matching result meaningful only when the tradition and profile are named?\nMA: Different traditions use different factor lists, tables, thresholds and exceptions. Without the profile, nobody can reproduce or check the result.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 2. A pair scores 30 of 36 but has Nadi Dosha. How do you report it?\nMA: Report the total, list the Nadi Dosha as identified, test each exception rule against its conditions, state that none is verified or applied, keep the raw score, and add the chart layers separately.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 3. A pair scores 15 of 36 and the charts look supportive. What do you say?\nMA: The Koota result stays 15 under the profile used. Supportive chart observations are recorded in their own layer; they do not rewrite the Koota result. Both are explained and the choice stays with the individuals.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 4. How do you handle an approximate birth time?\nMA: State the accuracy, compute the Moon at both ends of the time range, report whether a Pada or Nakshatra boundary is crossed, and mark Lagna-dependent findings as uncertain.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 5. Two software tools show different Nakshatras for the same person. What do you check?\nMA: Ayanamsha, Moon longitude, time-zone and daylight-saving handling, boundary precision, and whether the tools use the same Nakshatra and Pada rules. Astronomical differences are separated from rule differences.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 6. What is the difference between a Dosha and a score deficiency?\nMA: A flagged Dosha is a condition the tradition names (for example same Nadi); a score deficiency is only a lower number in a Koota. A deficiency need not be an independent Dosha.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 7. Explain the difference between cancellation and mitigation.\nMA: Cancellation means a named source states that the Dosha does not operate under defined conditions. Mitigation means other factors support the pair while the Dosha remains.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 8. When may points be restored after a cancellation?\nMA: Only when the selected profile explicitly authorises it. Even then the adjusted score is labelled adjusted and the raw score stays visible.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 9. A client asks you to say whether the marriage will succeed. What do you answer?\nMA: No horoscope can guarantee an outcome. Explain what each layer shows, what it cannot show, and the practical matters the two people should discuss.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 10. Why must Rajju and Vedha not be added to the 36-point total?\nMA: In Tradition A they are pass or Dosha checks with no points. Adding points would invent a scoring rule.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 11. What does it mean that Taurus–Libra is 6/8 and not 6/6?\nMA: Counts of different signs always add to 14, so one direction is 6 and the other is 8.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 12. How do you treat Mahendra and Stree Deergha in a Tradition A report?\nMA: They are supplementary regional factors, shown separately and outside the 36-point total, with the threshold variant stated.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 13. A client follows the Tamil Pathu Porutham. What do you do?\nMA: Use the documented provisional profile, show qualifying factors out of ten, state that it is not comparable with 36 points, and state which parts of the profile are unverified.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 14. What may you say about Telugu, Kannada and Kerala profiles in this course?\nMA: That regional practice is documented, but a sufficiently verified independent rule profile is not yet established; the profile is marked pending research.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 15. What is the role of D-9 in your report?\nMA: It is a supplementary chart: it adds context about dignity and the seventh house, but it does not replace D-1 and does not cancel a Koota Dosha.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 16. How is the D-9 sign of a longitude calculated?\nMA: Navamsha index = floor(longitude / 3°20') mod 12, with 0 = Aries, using the sidereal longitude.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 17. Why can a D-1 strong and D-9 weak planet not be summarised as good or bad?\nMA: Each chart says something different; the report states both and what needs qualification, without letting one erase the other.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 18. What do you do when one person's seventh lord is friendly to the other's but not mutually?\nMA: Report the two directions separately; one-sided friendship is not mutual friendship.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 19. What is the limit of the Vimshottari reading in your report?\nMA: It is a timing observation for the declared year-length convention; it is not an event date and does not change the Koota result.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 20. How do you present a transit?\nMA: As a practitioner-entered observation read together with the natal promise and the Dasha, never alone.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 21. What practical matters must always be left to the two people?\nMA: Communication, expectations, finances, family responsibilities, living arrangements, lifestyle, boundaries, long-term goals and mutual consent.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 22. How do you respond when another astrologer reached a different result?\nMA: Compare the settings and rules, not the people. Show where the calculation or the tradition differs, and keep both results visible.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 23. How should an error found after issuing a report be handled?\nMA: Correct it transparently: issue a new version with the change recorded; do not alter the issued report silently.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 24. What must be in the audit before a report is issued?\nMA: Birth data, offset, ayanamsha, Moon, Nakshatra, Pada, Koota scores, D-1 and D-9, Dasha, aspects, rule sources, evidence levels, disclaimers and absence of absolute claims.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 25. Why is there no combined compatibility percentage?\nMA: The layers measure different things on different scales; adding them would invent a formula that no source defines.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 26. What is the correct statement about Nadi Dosha and health?\nMA: Nadi Dosha is a traditional classification derived from the Nakshatra. It is not a genetic or medical finding and cannot diagnose anything.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 27. How do you explain a Gana mismatch responsibly?\nMA: As a symbolic classification result under the selected table, with the Dosha flag rule stated, and not as a statement about anyone's temperament or conduct.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 28. A client wants a remedy for every Dosha. What is your position?\nMA: A remedy is an optional personal practice; nothing in the report claims that it changes an outcome, and no one should be pressured.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 29. How do you keep client data confidential?\nMA: Use case identifiers, record only necessary details, share a report only with the people concerned and with their permission.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 30. What does 'identified, not applied' mean?\nMA: The exception category exists and its conditions may be met, but no verified source attaches to it, so the status of the Dosha is unchanged.\nLVL: Advanced\n\nSA: Viva 31. How would you tell a student who used a different recognised school that they are wrong?\nMA: I would not. A different documented school applied correctly and disclosed is acceptable; the marking checks method, disclosure and consistency.\nLVL: Advanced\n\n\n## References\n| Statement | Grade | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Koota tables used in the calculations | C/D | Common published tables in engine/profiles/tradition-A.json; not verified against a primary text |\n| Rajju, Vedha, Mahendra, Stree Deergha | C | Common statements; thresholds vary by source |\n| Tamil provisional profile | D | Ten-factor list reported by contemporary websites; some thresholds derived from course tables |\n| Telugu, Kannada, Kerala | – | Pending research; no verified rule profile |\n| Exception rules | E | All unverified; identified, not applied |\n| Dignity, aspects, combustion tables | C/D | Common teaching tables; orbs differ between sources |\n| D-9 formula | B | Standard 3°20′ division counted from the sign type |\n| Vimshottari arithmetic | B | Standard proportional division; the year-length convention is declared |\n| Moon calculation | – | ASTROSUTRAS approximate Meeus-based Moon; verify with a named ephemeris |\n| Report structure, workflow, audit sheets, language rules | – | Course design |\n\n### Corrections and decisions in this edition\n| Item | Earlier statement | Correct treatment |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sample report paragraph in the earlier edition | Called a pair of Moon-sign lords \"mutual friends\" although one of them regards the other as neutral | Mutual friendship needs both directions to be friendly; a one-sided friendship is a different condition, so the \"mutual friends\" category is not satisfied |\n| The five sample pairs | Defined only by a Moon longitude | Each case now has fictional birth data from which the app's Moon engine gives the same Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada; the Koota totals are unchanged |\n| Illustrative course map in the brief | Used illustrative titles | The Course synthesis tab uses the actual titles of Days 1 to 14 from the course index |\n| Case order | Pair 1 to 5 in the earlier list | The cases follow the order of the brief's case types; the Instructor guide lists which earlier pair each case uses |\n\n### Sources of authority\nNo verse, chapter or page reference is given. Traditional works (for example *Muhurta Chintamani*, *Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra*, *Jaimini Sutras*, *Prasna Marga*) and the published work of B. V. Raman are listed in the bibliography as leads that **require verification**; nothing in this day is attributed to them.\n\n## Workflow and Competencies\n\n### The twelve stages\nOpen any stage for what to do and the check before moving on.\n{{p14workflow}}\n\n### Progression\nLEARN → CALCULATE → VERIFY → INTERPRET → COMPARE → INTEGRATE → REPORT → COMMUNICATE\n\n| Step | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Learn | Know the rule and its limits |\n| Calculate | Obtain the number or classification with working |\n| Verify | Check by a second method, a second ayanamsha or a second tool |\n| Interpret | State what the result does and does not show |\n| Compare | Place the result beside another tradition, without merging |\n| Integrate | Add the chart layers, keeping each separate |\n| Report | Write it so another trained person can reproduce it |\n| Communicate | Explain it in calm, non-alarmist language |\n\n## Birth Data and Calculation Profile\n\n### Stage 1: what to collect\nCase identifier, date, exact time (local clock), place and country, latitude and longitude, standard time-zone offset, daylight-saving adjustment, source of the time and its accuracy. Use identifiers, not names, in working files.\n\n### Stage 2: validation checklist\n- Is the date a real calendar date, in the calendar the family uses?\n- Is the time the local clock time, and which offset applied on that date at that place?\n- Was daylight saving in force? A one-hour error moves the Moon by about 0.55°.\n- Are the coordinates plausible for the place?\n- Is the Moon result plausible, and does a second ayanamsha agree on the Rashi, Nakshatra and Pada?\n\n### Stage 3: record the calculation profile\nAyanamsha, ephemeris or engine, house system, node type, Nakshatra and Pada method, divisional method, Dasha system, aspect system and the matching profile. The report prints them.\n\n### Try it: validate birth data and see the Moon\n{{p14birth}}\n\n## Case Library\n\n### Student sheets\nEach case gives you inputs only. Do your own working, tick the assignments as you finish, and only then use the engine to check yourself.\n{{p14library}}\n\n### Design of the five cases\n| Case | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Case 01 | A pair whose layers are broadly supportive; explain why that is not a guarantee |\n| Case 02 | A high total beside an unresolved Dosha; an exception condition that is not met |\n| Case 03 | A lower total beside supportive chart observations; layers kept separate |\n| Case 04 | Several unfavourable Kootas, regional differences and a daylight-saving birth |\n| Case 05 | Mixed and contradictory findings, a variant-dependent Tara and different Dasha periods |\n\nThe examination pair is separate and unseen.\n\n## Koota Workbench\n\n### Calculate with working\nChoose your own pair, or a teaching case (after your own working). The sheet shows each Koota with its working, the register, the supplementary factors, the Janma variant on its own and the independent second check.\n{{p14koota}}\n\n### What the independent check means\nA second implementation inside the app recomputes the lookups and counts from the same rule tables. It checks arithmetic and lookups, not the correctness of the common tables themselves. For a true independent check, use a second named software or ephemeris.\n\n## Dosha and Cancellation Register\n\n### Register and cancellation table\n{{p14dosha}}\n\n### Regional comparison\n{{p14regional}}\n\n### How to write the cancellation table\n| Dosha | Rule examined | Required conditions | Conditions met | Cancellation status | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Name | The rule as stated | Every condition | yes or no, for this pair | Identified, not applied; or condition not satisfied | Source and evidence level |\n\nKeep the original score and the original Dosha record. Do not restore points unless the selected tradition explicitly authorises it.\n\n## Charts and Layers\n\n### Charts, D-1 and D-9\nThe planetary table gives the declared values. The D-9 is derived from them by the 3°20′ rule.\n{{p14charts}}\n\n### Individual, Navamsha and mutual observations\nThe observations are rule-based reading aids (a course teaching heuristic). They describe possibilities, never predictions.\n{{p14layers}}\n\n### Dasha tables\n{{p14dasha}}\n\n### Practical considerations\nMatters for the two people to discuss directly, not astrological conclusions: communication, expectations, career plans, financial priorities, family responsibilities, living arrangements, cultural expectations, lifestyle, personal boundaries, long-term goals, and mutual consent.\n\n## Report Writing and Template\n\n### The report generator\n{{p14report}}\n\n### Master template\n| Section | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| Cover | Branding, case reference, identifiers, date, tradition, calculation profile, practitioner |\n| A | Birth data and reliability of the time |\n| B | Moon, Nakshatra, Pada and lords |\n| C | Koota table with working, scored total, interpretation |\n| D | Dosha register, exceptions and cancellation table, remaining concerns |\n| E | Regional comparison, side by side |\n| F and G | Person A and Person B: D-1, seventh house and lord, relevant planets, D-9, Dasha, summary |\n| H | Mutual chart analysis |\n| I | Timing and limitations |\n| J | Practical discussion |\n| K | Supportive, challenging, unresolved and school-specific findings; final explanation |\n| L | Disclaimer |\n\n### Section L, the disclaimer\nThe findings represent an interpretation under the selected traditional astrological framework. They are not scientifically established predictions or guarantees of marital success or failure. Decisions about marriage belong to the individuals concerned and should consider mutual consent, personal circumstances and direct communication.\n\n### Qualitative categories\n- Broadly supportive across the examined factors.\n- Supportive with specific qualifications.\n- Mixed findings requiring further consideration.\n- Significant unresolved concerns under the selected tradition.\n- Inconclusive because of missing or uncertain information.\n- Different traditions produce different conclusions.\n\nThe suggested category is a reporting aid from the number of recognised Doshas, the balance of observations and the reliability of the birth time. It is not a score, and the practitioner confirms or changes it.\n\n## Consultation and Language\n\n### The consultation sequence\n1. Introduce the selected matching tradition.\n2. Confirm the accuracy of the birth data.\n3. Explain the Koota result.\n4. Explain favourable factors.\n5. Explain Doshas and exceptions.\n6. Discuss individual horoscope observations.\n7. Discuss mutual chart observations.\n8. Explain timing considerations.\n9. Identify practical matters for discussion.\n10. Summarise the findings.\n11. Explain the limitations.\n12. Answer questions.\n\n### Explaining difficult findings\n| Finding | A responsible way to explain it |\n|---|---|\n| Low total | \"This is the total under this profile; here are the individual results and what each does and does not show.\" |\n| Nadi Dosha | \"A traditional classification from the Nakshatras; not medical evidence.\" |\n| Bhakoot Dosha | \"A Moon-sign relationship the tradition marks as a concern; here are the exception categories and their status.\" |\n| Gana mismatch | \"A symbolic grouping; it says nothing about anyone's conduct.\" |\n| Yoni mismatch | \"A symbolic animal classification; it is not about intimacy or health.\" |\n| Rajju Dosha | \"A traditional classification; not a statement about lifespan.\" |\n| Different regional results | \"Each tradition counts different factors; here is each result separately.\" |\n| Uncertain birth time | \"The Moon's position may change Pada within this range; the Lagna-based findings are uncertain.\" |\n\n### Language transformation\nLearn the table and practise choosing the professional wording.\n{{p14lang}}\n\n### Client disagreement\n- A client follows a different tradition: ask which, compare rules and settings, show both results.\n- Another astrologer gives a different result: compare the calculation settings and rule tables, not the people.\n- A client questions a cancellation rule: show the rule, its source and its evidence level honestly.\n- The birth time is disputed: compute the range and report what changes.\n- A calculation differs from another software: check ayanamsha, longitude, time zone, boundaries, rule tables.\n- A client asks for a guarantee: explain that no horoscope can give one.\n\n### Consultation boundaries\nNo pressure to accept your conclusion. No fear-based persuasion. No guarantee. No deterministic prediction of divorce, death or illness. No medical or fertility claims. No disclosure of one person's private information without permission.\n\n## Audit and Quality Control\n\n### Audit sheets\n{{p14audit}}\n\n### Before issuing a report\n{{p14checklist}}\n\n### Version control\nEvery report keeps: report ID, date, calculation-profile version, Koota rule-profile version, cancellation-rulebook version, interpretation-profile version, practitioner changes and approval status. A revised rulebook never silently changes an issued report; a change creates a new version.\n\n## Workbench Software\n\n### The case manager\nSave your own cases on this device, move them through the eleven statuses, add practitioner notes and download the case record. Teaching cases can be copied as inputs.\n{{p14cases}}\n\n### Interface (specification)\nFourteen tabs: Client and Birth Data, Calculation Profile, Moon and Nakshatra, Dashakoota, Dosha and Cancellation, Regional Comparison, Person A Horoscope, Person B Horoscope, D-9 Comparison, Mutual Chart Analysis, Dasha and Transit, Final Synthesis, Professional Report, Audit and Verification. This course app implements the calculation, case, report and audit parts; a production workbench would add accounts, PDF export and administrator profile management.\n\n### Case status model\n| No. | Status | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Draft | Start |\n| 2 | Birth Data Pending | Birth data being collected |\n| 3 | Calculation Pending | Data valid; calculation not yet verified |\n| 4 | Calculation Verified | Needs valid birth data |\n| 5 | Koota Analysis Complete | Needs valid birth data |\n| 6 | Horoscope Analysis Complete | Chart layers examined |\n| 7 | Practitioner Review Pending | Audit sheets in progress |\n| 8 | Report Draft | Report written |\n| 9 | Report Approved | Practitioner approval |\n| 10 | Report Issued | Issued; later change creates a new version |\n| 11 | Archived | Terminal state |\n\nOne step at a time forward; return to an earlier stage for corrections; any state can be archived.\n\n### Main entities\nMatchmakingCase, PersonProfile, BirthData, CalculationProfile, TraditionProfile, PlanetaryPosition, D1Chart, D9Chart, NakshatraData, KootaResult, DoshaAssessment, CancellationAssessment, RegionalComparison, IndividualHoroscopeAssessment, MutualChartAssessment, DashaAssessment, TransitAssessment, PracticalDiscussionNote, FinalSynthesis, ProfessionalReport, AuditRecord.\n\n### Data integrity\n| Rule | Enforcement |\n|---|---|\n| Unique case ID | Generated when the case is created |\n| Valid date, time, coordinates, offsets | `validateBirth` rejects invalid data with a message |\n| Required calculation and tradition profile | Stored with every case |\n| D-1 and D-9 consistent | D-9 is always derived from the stated longitudes |\n| Original calculation preserved | Raw rows are never overwritten by an adjustment |\n| Traceable rule references | Every exception row carries its rule id and evidence level |\n\n### Report data model (illustrative example)\n| Field | Example |\n|---|---|\n| `reportId` | `ASR-C05-V1-20260102` |\n| `metadata.calculationProfile` | `{ayanamsha, moonEngine, houseSystem, nodes, divisionalMethod, dashaSystem, aspectSystem, version}` |\n| `C_kootaAnalysis` | `{rows, scoredTotal, maximum:36, note, independentCheck}` |\n| `D_doshaAnalysis` | `{register, exceptions, originalScoreRetained:true}` |\n| `E_regionalComparison` | `{tamil, other, scoreConversion:\"NOT_SUPPORTED\"}` |\n| `K_synthesis` | `{suggestedCategory, layerSummary, supportive, challenging, unresolved, noCombinedScore:true}` |\n| `L_disclaimer` | the Section L text |\n| `practitioner` | `{name, notes, approvalStatus}` |\n| `audit` | `{originalScoresPreserved:true, combinedScore:null, revisions:[]}` |\n\n### Explainability\nEvery conclusion in the report traces to the source calculation, the selected rule, the school or tradition, the evidence status and the practitioner's interpretation. The engine never produces an unexplained verdict.\n\n## Final Practical Examination\n\n### The task\nOne unseen pair. Verify the birth details, set the calculation profile, calculate the Moon and every Koota, identify Doshas and examine exceptions, compare Tradition A with the Tamil provisional profile, analyse both D-1 charts and the D-9, examine the mutual chart, work out the Dasha at birth and at the analysis date, identify contradictions and prepare the balanced report.\n\n### What to submit\n| Part | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| A. Calculation workbook | Birth-data verification, planetary table, Moon and Nakshatra, Koota calculations, D-1 and D-9 verification |\n| B. Dosha and exception register | Doshas, sources, rules, conditions, final status |\n| C. Horoscope analysis | Person A, Person B, Navamsha, mutual chart |\n| D. Timing analysis | Dasha, Antardasha, transit observations, limits |\n| E. Final professional report | Findings, contradictions, unresolved factors, balanced conclusion, disclaimer |\n| F. Presentation | A short oral or recorded explanation without alarmist or absolute statements |\n\n### The examination widget\nEnter your answers; the widget marks what it can check automatically and asks you to self-assess two parts. The locked key opens only after you submit.\n{{p14exam}}\n\n## Rubric and Bands\n\n### 100-mark framework\n| Component | Marks |\n|---|---|\n| Birth-data and calculation profile verification | 10 |\n| Moon, Nakshatra and Pada calculations | 10 |\n| Koota calculations and score verification | 15 |\n| Dosha and cancellation analysis | 15 |\n| Regional comparison | 10 |\n| Individual D-1 horoscope analysis | 10 |\n| D-9 Navamsha analysis | 10 |\n| Mutual chart compatibility | 10 |\n| Dasha and timing analysis | 5 |\n| Final report and professional communication | 5 |\n| **Total** | **100** |\n\n### Performance bands\n| Marks | Band |\n|---|---|\n| 85–100 | Advanced professional competency |\n| 70–84 | Competent with minor corrections required |\n| 55–69 | Developing competency; additional supervised practice recommended |\n| Below 55 | Further training and reassessment required |\n\nThese are course assessment bands, not measures of an astrologer's predictive accuracy.\n\n### Critical errors (substantial deductions)\n- Incorrect Moon Nakshatra.\n- Incorrect Pada.\n- Incorrect directional counting.\n- Wrong Koota matrix.\n- Mixing regional traditions without disclosure.\n- Inventing cancellation rules.\n- Treating mitigation as complete cancellation.\n- Restoring points without authority.\n- Inconsistent D-1 and D-9 calculations.\n- Incorrect planetary aspects.\n- Unsupported absolute predictions.\n- An arbitrary combined compatibility percentage.\n\n### Marking principle\nAccept more than one conclusion where documented traditions legitimately permit different interpretations. Do not mark a student wrong merely because they selected a different recognised school and applied it correctly with disclosure.\n\n### Sample feedback\n\"Your Koota sheet is correct and the working is clear. Your register separates Doshas from deficiencies. One cancellation row lacks the evidence level. Your report states the limits of the approximate birth time, which is professional. Add the second-ayanamsha result to Section A.\"\n\n## Revision and Viva\n\n### Quick reference manual\n| Topic | Remember |\n|---|---|\n| Birth data | Validate; state the accuracy; check offset and daylight saving |\n| Profile | Declare every setting |\n| Koota | Show the working; Rajju and Vedha add no points |\n| Dosha | Register separately from deficiencies |\n| Cancellation | Four different concepts; identified, not applied; no restoration without authority |\n| D-1 | Whole-sign houses; seventh lord; dignity; aspects |\n| D-9 | floor(L ÷ 3°20′) mod 12; supplementary |\n| Mutual chart | Label modern comparative techniques |\n| Dasha | Declared year length; a timing observation |\n| Synthesis | Layers separate; qualitative; no combined score |\n\n### Viva practice\nAnswer aloud first, then open the model answer.\n{{p14viva}}\n\n## Instructor Guide\n\n### 120-minute practical session\n| Time | Topic | Teaching method | Student activity |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0–10 | Complete professional workflow | Demonstration | Review the workflow |\n| 10–25 | Birth-data and calculation verification | Practical exercise | Validate case details |\n| 25–40 | Koota calculations | Calculation lab | Calculate the Kootas |\n| 40–55 | Dosha and cancellation | Rulebook exercise | Verify exceptions |\n| 55–70 | D-1 and D-9 analysis | Chart workshop | Analyse both individuals |\n| 70–80 | Mutual chart comparison | Demonstration | Compare chart relationships |\n| 80–90 | Timing and practical compatibility | Discussion | Identify limitations |\n| 90–105 | Report preparation | Writing workshop | Draft the final assessment |\n| 105–115 | Student presentation | Viva | Defend conclusions |\n| 115–120 | Course synthesis | Instructor review | Final reflection |\n\n### Instructor notes\n- Open each stage with the workflow diagram and one question from its check.\n- Common misunderstandings: treating a Tamil count as comparable with 36; reading one-sided friendship as mutual; treating D-9 as a cancellation; presenting schematic planets as real; hiding a boundary crossing.\n- Expected learning outcomes: a verified workbook, a register, a layered report and a calm explanation.\n\n### Cross-reference to the earlier sample list\n| Case | Earlier sample in the Lab menu |\n|---|---|\n| Case 01 | Pair 3 (Ashwini / Bharani) |\n| Case 02 | Pair 5 (Mrigashira / Shravana) |\n| Case 03 | Pair 2 (Pushya / Anuradha) |\n| Case 04 | Pair 4 (Krittika / Vishakha) |\n| Case 05 | Pair 1 (Rohini / Hasta) |\n\n### Instructor answer key\nThe key lists the twenty items of the model solution for each case, computed live. It is **not secured**.\n{{p14key}}\n\n## Course Synthesis\n\n### The fourteen days\n{{p14map}}\n\n### Final competency progression\nLearn, calculate, verify, interpret, compare, integrate, report, communicate: the sequence you followed, and the sequence every future report should follow.\n\n## Certificate Framework\n\n### Three categories\n| Category | Condition |\n|---|---|\n| Participation | Took part in the course; no assessment result is stated |\n| Successful completion | Lessons completed and assessments passed at the stated pass mark (70%) |\n| Passing the final practical examination | Final practical marked at 70 or more out of 100 under the published rubric |\n\n### Preview\n{{p14cert}}\n\n### What the certificate is not\nIt is not an external accreditation, a government recognition or a professional licence, and it makes no claim about the predictive accuracy of the holder. The course instructor is Anoopam Shukla.\n\n## Ethics\n\n### Professional responsibility\n- Traditional astrology is an interpretative framework, not a scientifically established predictor of marital success.\n- Koota scores do not guarantee a successful or an unsuccessful marriage.\n- Doshas must not be used to frighten or pressure anyone.\n- No deterministic prediction of divorce, death, widowhood, illness, infertility or children.\n- Astrological Nadi is not medical or genetic evidence.\n- No one should be rejected solely because of an unexplained number.\n- The two individuals keep full freedom of choice, and practical compatibility needs their direct communication.\n- Disclose birth-time uncertainty, and that different traditions can produce different results.\n- State the limits of your method.\n\n### Final professional disclaimer for the report generator\nThe findings represent an interpretation under the selected traditional astrological framework. They are not scientifically established predictions or guarantees of marital success or failure. Decisions about marriage belong to the individuals concerned and should consider mutual consent, personal circumstances and direct communication.\n\n### Final quality-control checklist\n- Five teaching cases and one unseen pair, each with sufficient birth data.\n- Moon values calculated by the engine; other positions labelled schematic.\n- D-1 and D-9 consistent; D-9 derived from the stated longitudes.\n- The selected tradition clearly identified; regional systems not merged.\n- Koota working shown; Doshas distinguished from deficiencies.\n- Cancellation rules with conditions and evidence levels; original scores preserved.\n- Student sheets separate from the instructor key.\n- An unseen examination pair; transparent criteria.\n- No unsupported certainty, no fear-based language, no combined percentage.\n\n## Validation\n\n### Engine and Lab tests\nThe report below runs the Day 14 engine tests and the Calculation Lab self-test in your browser.\n{{p14tests}}\n"};